    <node id="DISFAGIA OROFAR&#205;NGEA">
      <data key="d0" />
      <data key="d1">Disfagia orofar&#237;ngea (DOF), or oropharyngeal dysphagia, is a medical condition characterized by difficulty in swallowing, which can significantly impact a patient's nutrition and quality of life. This condition often necessitates dietary adaptations in texture and viscosity to ensure safe swallowing, as well as specific care strategies during feeding to prevent complications such as aspiration pneumonia, malnutrition, and dehydration. 

Disfagia orofar&#237;ngea is commonly observed in patients with various medical conditions, including stroke, and it requires a multidisciplinary approach for effective assessment, rehabilitation, and management. Healthcare professionals utilize various diagnostic tests, such as pH-impedance and manometry, to evaluate the severity of the condition. Early detection and treatment are crucial, as untreated DOF can lead to serious health consequences.

The management of oropharyngeal dysphagia involves not only dietary modifications but also rehabilitation techniques and specific nursing care to ensure safe oral feeding. It is relevant in hospital settings, particularly in food service and preparation, where understanding the dietary needs of affected patients is essential. Additionally, the condition is the focus of various health data collection and management systems, highlighting its significance in clinical practice and research.

Overall, oropharyngeal dysphagia is a complex swallowing disorder that requires comprehensive care strategies to enhance patient outcomes and maintain nutritional health.</data>
