    <node id="RADIOTERAPIA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">RADIOTERAPIA, or radiotherapy, is a treatment method that employs high doses of radiation to eliminate cancer cells. It is frequently utilized in the management of neoplasms and may be considered as a treatment option following surgery. While effective in targeting cancer, radiotherapy can have significant adverse effects on a patient's nutritional status. These effects can manifest as acute and chronic nutritional complications, which are influenced by factors such as the tumor's location and the radiation dose administered.

Patients undergoing radiotherapy may experience side effects like mucositis, which can hinder their ability to consume adequate nutrition. Additionally, the treatment can lead to oropharyngeal dysphagia, particularly in individuals with head and neck cancers, further complicating their nutritional intake. There is also a risk of damage to the pituitary gland, potentially resulting in hormonal deficiencies such as hypopituitarism.

Overall, while RADIOTERAPIA is a critical component of cancer treatment, it is essential to monitor and manage the nutritional implications that arise during the course of therapy to ensure the well-being of patients.</data>
      <data key="d2">0b901814a09392d24880be465dcbce6f,36cffc3e7c3a6fe82e50787e939f7537,3a1d1c4eb34995d440bd72c4b64e3c8d,40dcfaa6144c6424068ba46fdf288dd2,61890d3cb54fb752b95a4dfad8db8bb6,7c0d1f274b31d130e4feb8e46fbeb764,9b9d487a35d8197bfe9d1aa0fce19c32,9c3716c3860855ce045a1e5d9d30b9d1,b26b998479da515c234ce46b1bfa73db,df3e8fd56a2df522425984adc77392ab,e474ebdcd3e5392e4b2bed8434293849,ea0b88195d04b227bedf7fa3cb3b6513</data>
