      <data key="d6">169</data>
      <data key="d7">51c46d7e3733405297d51654762f6470</data>
    </node>
    <node id="REINO UNIDO">
      <data key="d0">GEO</data>
      <data key="d1">The United Kingdom, often referred to as "Reino Unido," is a country that has made significant contributions to medical research and public health. It suggests that transplantation should be considered only under specific medical conditions, particularly related to portal hypertension, liver cirrhosis, or fibrosis. The UK has recorded survival rates for patients with short bowel syndrome and has conducted extensive studies on the prevalence of gastrointestinal issues such as constipation and diarrhea.

In addition to these studies, the United Kingdom has examined the economic costs associated with malnutrition and its impact on public health. It has developed several guidelines for the refeeding of malnourished patients, incorporating recommendations from the Royal College of Psychiatrists and NICE (National Institute for Health and Care Excellence). Research in the UK has also focused on nutritional support and its economic implications, especially concerning malnutrition and home care.

Despite its advancements, the United Kingdom faces limitations in data collection for epidemiological studies, which affects the comprehensiveness of its health data. Notably, significant epidemiological studies on intestinal rhythm have been conducted within the country. Furthermore, the UK has identified a high prevalence of dysphagia among the elderly population and estimates that hereditary fructose intolerance occurs in approximately 1 in 18,000 individuals. The country is also recognized for its clinical guidelines that describe food textures and liquid viscosities, which are essential for patient care. Overall, the United Kingdom is a key player in health research, particularly in areas related to nutrition, gastrointestinal health, and public health policy.</data>
      <data key="d2">02b61e2d4f840772c76a05502aa4f171,0d083c309fb6f4d0d16c0e2b23ea5c8b,235520bdfb411a2997762c99ac421bf3,398be32cb0a16895225a8b29c9d118df,44f263c31f91c1bc594cbb28bd397c89,998342e0e27f4902274d51c0658d2f17,ada55dc10a07d6ad1d50199d5cb5f950,da6937e21690cfcb43a43b3438ec7586,e28f0577e97765bca9099d5dc4a4fcc5,e55543c6579d036d25f25eb2ff63fcf2,eab1daf48c7ca09f43fc115a3379cc10,f84386a9907d954b9cf46475d0d45243</data>
      <data key="d3">1930</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">17</data>
      <data key="d6">170</data>
      <data key="d7">834e73a062b54afaa446d4c6366529e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FRANCIA">
      <data key="d0">GEO</data>
      <data key="d1">FRANCIA, known as France, is a country that has been highlighted in various studies regarding health issues, particularly dysphagia among older adults, indicating a significant prevalence of this condition within this demographic. Additionally, France is included in the epidemiological study of NED (Nutritional and Eating Disorders), which underscores its relevance in the context of health research. Notably, Francia was one of the pioneering countries to regulate the funding of NED in 1988, demonstrating its proactive approach to addressing nutritional and eating disorders.</data>
      <data key="d2">e28f0577e97765bca9099d5dc4a4fcc5,eab1daf48c7ca09f43fc115a3379cc10,f84386a9907d954b9cf46475d0d45243</data>
      <data key="d3">1928</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">171</data>
      <data key="d7">9368d0ff0300474188b495b1eaffe801</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALEMANIA">
      <data key="d0">GEO</data>
      <data key="d1">Alemania, also known as Germany, is one of the eight countries that face limitations in data collection for epidemiological studies. Despite these challenges, Germany has reported a lower prevalence of dysphagia among its population, with only 43% of the elderly affected by this condition, which is comparatively lower than in other countries.</data>
      <data key="d2">e28f0577e97765bca9099d5dc4a4fcc5,eab1daf48c7ca09f43fc115a3379cc10</data>
      <data key="d3">1928</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">172</data>
      <data key="d7">266bfeccb357407ca3457f1eeb69e8c6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="JAP&#211;N">
      <data key="d0">GEO</data>
      <data key="d1">Japan is a country located in Asia, known for its unique dietary habits and health-related statistics. Among the elderly population, Japan has reported a prevalence of dysphagia, with figures ranging from 11% to 16% in various studies. To address this issue, Japan has established defined levels of food and liquid textures specifically for patients with dysphagia, ensuring that dietary needs are met.

In terms of nutrition, Japan is recognized for its high consumption of omega-3 fatty acids, with estimates reaching up to 2 grams per day. This dietary practice is linked to the country's notably low rates of coronary heart disease, attributed to the intake of alpha-linolenic acid (ALA) and other omega-3 fatty acids. Additionally, Japan recommends a daily salt intake of 6 grams, further reflecting its focus on health-conscious dietary guidelines.

Despite these positive health indicators, Japan is also one of the countries with limited data on home nutrition and has been identified as having restrictions in data collection for epidemiological studies. This limitation highlights the need for further research to better understand the nutritional landscape and health challenges faced by its population.</data>
      <data key="d2">0090177678aee58d08471d09d24d2842,0a43b350a248173236a96a64e266b744,398be32cb0a16895225a8b29c9d118df,84b542fb745825398f17e4f8f4756cb4,adb71bc02844276a2ecb4c4302938319,bfe7b32b596449644b274ed7486a507a,e28f0577e97765bca9099d5dc4a4fcc5,eab1daf48c7ca09f43fc115a3379cc10,f84386a9907d954b9cf46475d0d45243</data>
      <data key="d3">1376</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">173</data>
      <data key="d7">cef688951a384d22b922d3a8df8ebbc6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PA&#205;SES BAJOS">
      <data key="d0">GEO</data>
      <data key="d1">The Netherlands has also reported figures related to the prevalence of dysphagia among older adults.</data>
      <data key="d2">e28f0577e97765bca9099d5dc4a4fcc5</data>
      <data key="d3">953</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
