    <node id="DYSPHAGIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Dysphagia is a medical condition characterized by difficulty in swallowing, which can significantly impact a patient's quality of life and nutritional intake. It is commonly observed in patients with various conditions, including amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, dementia, and esophageal cancer. Dysphagia can lead to serious complications such as malnutrition and dehydration, necessitating multidisciplinary solutions for effective management. The condition is often studied in relation to various therapies and interventions, and it is the focus of numerous dietary guidelines and research efforts. Additionally, dysphagia can occur after a stroke, affecting patient outcomes and requiring thorough assessment and management. In 2010, a journal published an article discussing supranuclear control of swallowing, highlighting the ongoing research and interest in understanding and addressing this swallowing impairment.</data>
      <data key="d2">1d3c30089c42a02917c7625774aa29f2,242881af1a0439ab16c10b788cff01fd,28cd3f60e1ccd9129a7d4d386e241a93,37182848d30a82f99b7a617859ac6e0d,499132ff7f7da81d7effbadf776c5b54,4e83f9d1f09d1d6b2eb82c16a15f1928,694f957b0b008ab3339e5bf1fd57b81a,90aa696a6e48637e06acfcd7213699da,cb0b01ccd7736b620c0755a756a42e5b,cdb6aae3097a018e194a3fa9452146af,ce2e5f8df5ad472bb255174d496cc1d7,d590f931bbeb2d87392c751f2746e4da,e2aeed1cae3c67005a57b0695a907fbf,f943b9dd611368ee970abcd341b660a9</data>
