    <node id="SSA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">The SSA, or Standardized Bedside Swallowing Assessment, is a clinical tool utilized to evaluate swallowing function and identify dysphagia. It is designed to provide scientific rigor in the detection of dysphagia, making it a reliable assessment method. The SSA is particularly beneficial for non-expert professionals, enabling them to effectively detect oropharyngeal dysphagia.</data>
      <data key="d2">4befdcd21a98898cec30bbac7b402d4b,8d3134a53f89ef62070b5c3b2a7bc136,b44e720874efa74c538dd573ee936378</data>
      <data key="d3">157</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">298</data>
      <data key="d7">88e8f0c895f44cca84a7b483632386bc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FEES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">FEES, or Fiberoptic Endoscopic Evaluation of Swallowing, is an instrumental test designed to study and assess swallowing disorders. This procedure, also referred to as fibroendoscopy of swallowing, is utilized to evaluate the physiology of swallowing in patients experiencing dysphagia. Through this evaluation, healthcare professionals can gain insights into the mechanics of swallowing and identify any abnormalities or issues that may be present.</data>
      <data key="d2">4befdcd21a98898cec30bbac7b402d4b,e14475004a93d47a14d98dd4c5b5d02b</data>
      <data key="d3">1172</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">299</data>
      <data key="d7">2dcf5990c3dd4275838d3f3dadd75781</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VIDEOFLOUROSCOPIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">VIDEOFLOUROSCOPIA is a diagnostic imaging technique that plays a crucial role in evaluating swallowing function, particularly in patients with oropharyngeal dysphagia. This method allows for the observation of the swallowing process through real-time X-ray imaging, providing both lateral and anteroposterior projections. It is considered the gold standard for diagnosing dysphagia, as it comprehensively studies the three phases of swallowing. During the procedure, barium is used to enhance visualization, enabling healthcare professionals to assess the pharyngeal phase of swallowing effectively. Videofluoroscopy is particularly recommended when other assessment methods, such as Fiberoptic Endoscopic Evaluation of Swallowing (FEES), are not feasible. Overall, videofluoroscopy is an essential tool in the diagnosis and management of swallowing disorders.</data>
      <data key="d2">4befdcd21a98898cec30bbac7b402d4b,58af2bdd20eeb5647ecc8088732833d8,59a97e6bf8748a66eb335bec2852ce38,67cdbb6902307ad33e10fe5e37096083,a3444da30e339275da1056c3e13bcae5,d8fa59c6af96b4ebb96f2c6b4ddb5473,dae3d2cddf63b2af4067a27d8b9d7ab5</data>
      <data key="d3">908</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">11</data>
      <data key="d6">300</data>
      <data key="d7">f6eabeb16ddb4ab48b7cf904d6ef4ac7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AGUDA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Aguda refers to acute neurological conditions in patients that may affect swallowing.</data>
      <data key="d2">4befdcd21a98898cec30bbac7b402d4b</data>
