    </node>
    <node id="C1">
      <data key="d0">GEO</data>
      <data key="d1">C1 refers to the first cervical vertebra, which is relevant in the context of pharyngeal contraction during swallowing.</data>
      <data key="d2">eff38537223691079c4930d76cbb7427</data>
      <data key="d3">934</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">455</data>
      <data key="d7">91bcb0aef5e248faa8f33a956f5faad3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V&#205;A A&#201;REA">
      <data key="d0">GEO</data>
      <data key="d1">V&#205;A A&#201;REA refers to the airway, which is the passage that connects the nose and mouth to the lungs. This structure is essential for proper respiratory function and plays a critical role in swallowing safety. Issues related to swallowing can significantly impact the airway, potentially leading to complications. Therefore, maintaining the integrity of the airway is vital for both breathing and safe swallowing.</data>
      <data key="d2">63bd8dcdc323614f5359e4978e154e90,eff38537223691079c4930d76cbb7427</data>
      <data key="d3">934</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">4</data>
      <data key="d6">456</data>
      <data key="d7">65d39f70e91f4596b1036c46f1c4583f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BOL">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">BOL refers to the bolus, which is a mass of food or liquid formed in the mouth and subsequently swallowed. This process can be influenced by the viscosity of thickeners, which may alter the consistency of the bolus. The bolus plays a significant role in the study of swallowing mechanics, as it is the key element that is swallowed during the act of eating or drinking.</data>
      <data key="d2">c1fcea6b2c2290b4c90ae1a511ca05d4,eff38537223691079c4930d76cbb7427</data>
      <data key="d3">934</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">457</data>
      <data key="d7">7094a0c4655a4f74a29a130e12bd1596</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VALL&#201;CULA">
      <data key="d0">GEO</data>
      <data key="d1">The vallecula is a space at the back of the throat that plays a role in swallowing and can be a site for food residue.</data>
      <data key="d2">eff38537223691079c4930d76cbb7427</data>
      <data key="d3">934</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">458</data>
      <data key="d7">72dbc6de956b402eb1e1fab8e239da33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ESF&#205;NTER ESOF&#193;GICO">
      <data key="d0">GEO</data>
      <data key="d1">The esophageal sphincter is a muscle that controls the passage of food from the esophagus into the stomach, important for swallowing safety.</data>
      <data key="d2">eff38537223691079c4930d76cbb7427</data>
      <data key="d3">934</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">459</data>
      <data key="d7">8cc3899fd8b94b65bc65d318d6857059</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MOVIMIENTO HIOIDEO">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Hyoid movement refers to the motion of the hyoid bone during swallowing, which is essential for the proper function of the swallowing mechanism.</data>
      <data key="d2">eff38537223691079c4930d76cbb7427</data>
      <data key="d3">906</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">460</data>
      <data key="d7">224b59c1a9e44c9d9bf83a824ad93c16</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXCURSI&#211;N LAR&#205;NGEA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Laryngeal excursion is the upward and forward movement of the larynx during swallowing, crucial for airway protection.</data>
      <data key="d2">eff38537223691079c4930d76cbb7427</data>
      <data key="d3">906</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">461</data>
      <data key="d7">3de5892b203941b4a890ab2311ea0a8b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PENETRACI&#211;N">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Penetration is the entry of material into the airway above the vocal cords during swallowing, which can lead to aspiration if not managed properly.</data>
      <data key="d2">eff38537223691079c4930d76cbb7427</data>
      <data key="d3">964</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">462</data>
      <data key="d7">6e327b23edb3472896daab51d3220168</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALTERACIONES DE LA SEGURIDAD">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Safety alterations refer to changes in swallowing that increase the risk of penetration or aspiration.</data>
      <data key="d2">eff38537223691079c4930d76cbb7427</data>
      <data key="d3">906</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">463</data>
      <data key="d7">653b782078484806b1c0fc1fe06a2564</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OMNIPAQUE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Omnipaque is a non-ionic, monomeric, tri-iodinated, water-soluble radiological contrast medium used in diagnostic imaging.</data>
      <data key="d2">63bd8dcdc323614f5359e4978e154e90</data>
      <data key="d3">935</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">464</data>
      <data key="d7">63082365f54b41f5b1ad6e81a8395f52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ESF&#205;NTER ESOF&#193;GICO SUPERIOR">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">The "ESF&#205;NTER ESOF&#193;GICO SUPERIOR" is a muscle that plays a crucial role in controlling the entry of food into the esophagus. It is a functional unit involved in the process of swallowing, consisting of muscles that regulate the passage of food. This sphincter is particularly significant in the evaluation of oropharyngeal dysphagia, a condition that affects the ability to swallow.</data>
      <data key="d2">63bd8dcdc323614f5359e4978e154e90,dbe1af2b277e06c9d447af56351e496f</data>
      <data key="d3">935</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">465</data>
      <data key="d7">f82c9224b42146d9be0aec2e21d9d480</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PRUEBAS DIAGN&#211;STICAS INSTRUMENTALES">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Instrumental diagnostic tests are used to evaluate swallowing and identify any dysfunctions or abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d2">63bd8dcdc323614f5359e4978e154e90</data>
      <data key="d3">935</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">466</data>
      <data key="d7">e1c065d8e31d4897b3fc7f7d26aecaa0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RESIDUO">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">The term "RESIDUO" refers to the materials that remain in the body after the process of digestion, which can be significant in diagnostic studies of the digestive tract. Additionally, "residue" also pertains to the leftover food or liquid present in the pharynx following swallowing, which may indicate potential difficulties in swallowing. This dual definition highlights the importance of residue in both digestive health and the assessment of swallowing function.</data>
      <data key="d2">072912695b1cff9f390d4ead3b9ad742,63bd8dcdc323614f5359e4978e154e90</data>
      <data key="d3">935</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">467</data>
      <data key="d7">632f0234a9e840f4ac3305bdaa7636b5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FASES DE LA DEGLUCI&#211;N">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">The phases of swallowing include oral, pharyngeal, and esophageal phases, each critical for effective swallowing.</data>
      <data key="d2">63bd8dcdc323614f5359e4978e154e90</data>
      <data key="d3">935</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">468</data>
      <data key="d7">57d40eb5217d46928e15ff77ab7364a7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENFERMEDADES NEUROMUSCULARES">
      <data key="d0" />
      <data key="d1">ENFERMEDADES NEUROMUSCULARES refers to a category of diseases that impact the muscles and their direct nervous control. These conditions may necessitate specialized nutritional approaches, particularly in pediatric care, to support the health and development of affected individuals.</data>
      <data key="d2">5a69e2f952998ea5b7109ad2d51a7849,63bd8dcdc323614f5359e4978e154e90</data>
      <data key="d3">935</data>
