    <node id="ERGE">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">ERGE, or Enfermedad por Reflujo Gastroesof&#225;gico, also known as Esophageal Reflux Disease, is a medical condition characterized by the backflow of stomach contents into the esophagus. This condition leads to various symptoms, including heartburn and dysphagia, particularly in patients with a prolonged history of the disease. While manometry can be used to study ERGE, it is not typically employed to confirm the diagnosis of this condition.</data>
      <data key="d2">cfd217c3642e90cb9f3e1ff854830bd6,dbe1af2b277e06c9d447af56351e496f,f7c941bced546a7d607c30a9a5e9d887</data>
      <data key="d3">965</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">5</data>
      <data key="d6">502</data>
      <data key="d7">39b8a4f8a5884391b7a6f7a4b32ca5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PH-METR&#205;A">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">pH-metry is a diagnostic test used to measure the acidity in the esophagus, helping to evaluate conditions like GERD.</data>
      <data key="d2">f7c941bced546a7d607c30a9a5e9d887</data>
      <data key="d3">965</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">4</data>
      <data key="d6">503</data>
      <data key="d7">f81bcdae5116437f93def3199ba84f83</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TIRUZIA">
      <data key="d0">GEO</data>
      <data key="d1">Tiruzia is a geographical location mentioned in the context of esophageal studies, though not explicitly defined in the text.</data>
      <data key="d2">f7c941bced546a7d607c30a9a5e9d887</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">504</data>
      <data key="d7">b569b6c797d24738af099aae48c06f8d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="S&#205;NTOMAS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">The entity "S&#237;ntomas" encompasses a range of clinical manifestations experienced by patients across various medical conditions. These symptoms can vary significantly depending on the underlying health issues. For instance, in patients receiving enteral nutrition, symptoms such as abdominal pain or distension must be monitored daily to assess tolerance. In those following a ketogenic diet, nonspecific symptoms like fatigue and muscle weakness may indicate nutrient deficiencies.

Specific conditions present distinct symptomatology; for example, classic galactosemia is characterized by food rejection, vomiting, lethargy, and potential neurological complications. Patients experiencing dumping syndrome may report abdominal discomfort, nausea, profuse sweating, dizziness, and palpitations. Chronic pancreatitis can lead to symptoms such as abdominal pain, weight loss, and fat malabsorption.

Diabetes presents with symptoms including increased thirst, frequent urination, extreme fatigue, and blurred vision, which are crucial for diagnosis. Additionally, symptoms can correlate with reflux episodes during diagnostic evaluations, highlighting their importance in clinical assessments. In patients with cancer and its treatments, symptoms can significantly impact nutritional intake and status, necessitating specialized nutritional support. Lastly, individuals with urea cycle disorders may experience symptoms like vomiting, lethargy, and confusion, further illustrating the diverse manifestations associated with different health conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">0487e1a4525547575a1d284297280f9a,4bf9e20e18b101e95ebd6a9ca591fc54,4d84210c815fb41f2fa44f3e01aafa02,5e934738b2a8cb03468f4ebf4eec0cd2,65e3dfcb7a0c5a2a09a98342c597c020,803363886f385b089938bf122246dfa2,b58b0258891d9207b27237bab6a03946,bc394d70461777b6300d5449185a7859,f1bd89f4a1fcc385a19f5c5323ee2f89,f2f8594bddb0ab77444cdcd2a7799d59,f7c941bced546a7d607c30a9a5e9d887</data>
      <data key="d3">1529</data>
