      <data key="d6">589</data>
      <data key="d7">46cf154b37bc406791a36d1b4a35c7ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V&#211;MITOS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">V&#211;MITOS, or vomiting, is defined as the involuntary expulsion of the contents of the stomach through the mouth. This phenomenon can occur as a symptom of various underlying health issues, particularly in patients with gastrointestinal disorders. It is commonly observed in individuals suffering from conditions such as pancreatitis, cancer, and gastrointestinal disorders, including EPOC and gastroenteritis eosinof&#237;lica. 

Vomiting can significantly impact a patient's nutritional status, leading to malnutrition if it occurs frequently. In particular, it complicates the administration of enteral nutrition, as it may indicate intolerance to feeding, especially in infants. Additionally, vomiting can influence the nutritional and hydration needs of children, complicating their management in parenteral nutrition settings. 

Moreover, vomiting is a notable side effect of chemotherapy, which can hinder a patient's ability to maintain proper nutrition. It can also result in fluid and electrolyte loss, further affecting overall health. In summary, V&#211;MITOS is a critical clinical sign that can arise from various medical conditions and has significant implications for a patient's nutritional and hydration status.</data>
      <data key="d2">1ff94f3fcc6eff16c2fdae5eb9b3aefa,2ae5ac5b335a00d15c090777da386817,2bbe847617971cacfe15b2c970de2d21,37ca60bdc28109c9bec995e3c433c801,4dffad2ae717727b21f2294ac520f2be,693b5721c7fc52c8165c48b580777840,6c8b41c09b1ada129f0908d6e5d18185,79a751fb45e4b69c946d088100f43654,9562f39f9e78644164daeff3177a2f84,aacaabd084b7839bdedf5fbebfd7dcf4,beaa66c30582232126ce2c762a9c6bdb,c58712e2ee5757b5175054fcc54f4dd6,caf14765027dbf6266841af0571a522e,ddd48beea3c882bf1a651cb25283ba91,e12655d89e99e57ce1f6d87563adf674,e7a9d9aa3031b4c032d724047f80ebe9</data>
