      <data key="d6">592</data>
      <data key="d7">856d715396b44b858613f03dfc82eeb8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANOREXIA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Anorexia is a condition characterized by a significant loss of appetite or desire to eat, which can be linked to various psychological or medical issues. It is particularly prevalent among patients with chronic illnesses, including cancer, cystic fibrosis, and EPOC (chronic obstructive pulmonary disease). In cancer patients, anorexia can severely impact nutritional intake and overall health, leading to malnutrition. Similarly, in individuals with cystic fibrosis, the loss of appetite may arise due to the chronic nature of the illness and the side effects of treatment.

Anorexia can also manifest as a symptom in patients with urea cycle disorders, where it exacerbates their condition. Additionally, it is common in patients with dementia, where the lack of appetite can contribute to malnutrition. The condition may be influenced by biological factors related to the underlying pathological process or treatment, as well as emotional factors that cause suffering.

In critically ill patients, anorexia can lead to inadequate nutrient intake, further complicating their health status. It can also be triggered by inflammation in patients with inflammatory bowel disease. Overall, anorexia is a multifaceted issue that necessitates careful management, including potential interventions such as enteral nutrition to address the nutritional deficits it creates.</data>
      <data key="d2">0487e1a4525547575a1d284297280f9a,11cbdce47eee11a80ab609f3b786bcc1,1e997b2a6bf1c926a7a17d8b5ac1e699,2ae5ac5b335a00d15c090777da386817,397cb82cce268866351c44a5b38ccb8b,417548e99612a123d61f9fd10807ce34,6c8b41c09b1ada129f0908d6e5d18185,89c28a4a0410d8f4cbbbc0fbdf4b1c40,a87e8e113488b153c5b4b2d2fca712c0,b3760fcb9c0421b66d37c20b1dc68577,bd297992bd38c0b110dd5f0cae141f83,c9790e5e47712b100fb4870d35a3edfa,d3f53b7b7352e8ba2fd9dc8be977648c,ddd48beea3c882bf1a651cb25283ba91</data>
