      <data key="d7">959678b51c5540ea9901a9a788a302b1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OTTERY">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Ottery es un autor que modific&#243; la Valoraci&#243;n Global Subjetiva en 1994.</data>
      <data key="d2">e12655d89e99e57ce1f6d87563adf674</data>
      <data key="d3">1126</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">602</data>
      <data key="d7">91fcc81f56ad473398eee0294237e771</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ASPEN">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">The American Society for Parenteral and Enteral Nutrition (ASPEN) is a professional organization dedicated to providing comprehensive guidelines and recommendations for nutritional support in clinical settings, particularly focusing on parenteral and enteral nutrition. ASPEN emphasizes the importance of the Subjective Global Assessment (SGA) as the gold standard for nutritional evaluation and offers guidance on the screening and management of malnutrition, especially in oncology patients.

ASPEN develops clinical practice guidelines that address the nutritional needs of various patient populations, including those in critical care, surgical patients, and individuals with traumatic injuries. The organization has coordinated efforts with the European Society for Clinical Nutrition and Metabolism (ESPEN) to enhance the quality of nutritional care. It also provides specific recommendations for managing nutritional support in critically ill patients, including maintaining moderate blood glucose levels in ICU settings.

In addition to its focus on surgical patients, ASPEN has published guidelines for nutritional support post-surgery and has made modifications to commercial formulations of trace elements to prevent deficiencies in parenteral nutrition. The organization promotes standardized definitions and guidelines for nutritional care, ensuring that healthcare providers have the necessary resources to support patients with inadequate oral intake or those who cannot tolerate enteral nutrition.

ASPEN's commitment to evidence-based nutrition is reflected in its history, having published its first clinical practice guidelines in 1993. Overall, ASPEN plays a crucial role in advancing the field of nutrition, particularly in the context of parenteral and enteral nutrition, by providing essential guidelines and resources for healthcare professionals.</data>
      <data key="d2">01704aec4089b2f0f24dd0f0841b5ecf,07a16ed299a7f8bafcaa44a54495a9c3,0a742cf103e30cf0fadaf6ffabbbde2d,17444b83ec19bdcc65ff0722869e7b4b,195affa22ecd2f7b1cdce3d72fc1cba4,1bad878c6efa9d3f5719546bb51bccb6,25192e60a2c23bd74095f4602efeac4c,2b8c323c265f8f7f3d15bf988bf1f9ee,3159307e5633698722f72fde1e82702f,428ea9c156fe3f0571b20d5d81648868,44346a4b1b9a6d3ae2bc161314ff9efd,4c4d1882f0ac14b7c55941ec1c17b650,5549895a3a7f54651e6c54f5a5d7d833,58ae94a34f90df368b032f1618c7bcec,719fff2d16bc334d3d4b53f070373a10,7906ee47aaf0c271f072790eaea05866,7a389f3625ba13c74ce52213197b01ae,7ea112a550fd775a80c3eda15663fba5,814d394c907e59b29a5f0d9e82aed423,896ddfd9f06dc3ec8b1cfb986fae97fe,90fe1d34782402a1896d33ad5bb47614,a66b01654a2f495559fd1a24efc0e64e,a687eee1e8bfd2d98ce40a14628dd855,bb209dcfffac9c89dae0ad4ed2908d85,bd8df34b86026d71089bcf062570b70f,be7eb01a6daf673db6a127f66c79c96c,c07baf818737d704c290ca0f5eefd30f,c3182f51249513a920db6b935632fead,ceb66a898fed371e013171c43a87e1d1,d92f4ecdee41ca35485c997a95642c5b,e12655d89e99e57ce1f6d87563adf674,e596bbb9231cc72bade554b3d67804b8,e98937544f56006f5d327136c593c969,eae8ddef005974a2ffe71cf7c61b0634,f087e20586f6b263059597e194a04993,f75d1340b39e345875bf878dba07ab8c</data>
      <data key="d3">684</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
