      <data key="d6">698</data>
      <data key="d7">07d668e1b3bc45d6a7cbec6a65c922ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BEBIDA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Beverages that contribute to fluid intake, also listed in the context of hydration balance.</data>
      <data key="d2">186a95fdfe908123ad6f42d43b52ee1d</data>
      <data key="d3">531</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">699</data>
      <data key="d7">b8da818491a946adb6127d47ecfebfcb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ORINA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Urine, which is a significant output of fluid loss in the context of hydration balance.</data>
      <data key="d2">186a95fdfe908123ad6f42d43b52ee1d</data>
      <data key="d3">531</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">700</data>
      <data key="d7">ca838e70ed5e4180b0b1c78b5b5a5682</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PIEL">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">PIEL refers to the skin, which plays a crucial role in various physiological processes. In cases of hemochromatosis, the skin can exhibit signs of iron deposition, indicating an underlying health issue related to iron overload in the body. Additionally, the skin is essential for maintaining hydration balance, as it contributes to fluid loss through perspiration. This dual role highlights the skin's importance not only in health conditions like hemochromatosis but also in regulating the body's hydration levels.</data>
      <data key="d2">186a95fdfe908123ad6f42d43b52ee1d,45921035725d6b94d451399b0dac4079</data>
      <data key="d3">531</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">701</data>
      <data key="d7">a4a19e93c9b0457c99861adf354f681c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RESPIRACI&#211;N">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Respiration, which results in fluid loss through exhalation in the context of hydration balance.</data>
      <data key="d2">186a95fdfe908123ad6f42d43b52ee1d</data>
      <data key="d3">531</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">702</data>
      <data key="d7">d0452e40c2d6433485c3eac1dcf1f9dc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HECES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">HECES refers to feces or stool samples that are utilized in medical testing to analyze various health conditions. These biological waste samples are specifically examined for the presence of blood, which is an important aspect of medical diagnostics. Additionally, HECES are significant in assessing fluid loss, particularly in the context of hydration balance. They are also used to evaluate fat absorption, which is crucial for diagnosing conditions such as esteatorrea. In patients with cystic fibrosis, stool analysis plays a vital role in determining fat absorption levels, thereby guiding necessary adjustments in enzyme dosage. Overall, HECES encompass a range of analyses that contribute to understanding and managing various health issues.</data>
      <data key="d2">029a82f84eb8dfda723f9feabe2384d7,186a95fdfe908123ad6f42d43b52ee1d,4c37affdd97e7772d1ea27aee34533b8,c926a8237fba5d5bc497ac2165bbb039</data>
      <data key="d3">1052</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">5</data>
      <data key="d6">703</data>
      <data key="d7">bb10251dfa1d458880986ac632d89717</data>
    </node>
