    <node id="SAL">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">The entity "SAL" encompasses various aspects of salt, which is a mineral primarily composed of sodium and is a fundamental component in culinary practices and nutrition. Salt is widely recognized as the main source of sodium in the diet, commonly used for cooking and preserving food. It serves as a crucial seasoning that enhances the flavor of dishes, including those prepared in clinical settings, where it is utilized in hospital recipes to improve taste.

However, the consumption of salt is a double-edged sword. While it is essential for health, its intake must be monitored and controlled according to the recommendations of the World Health Organization (WHO) to mitigate health risks. High salt consumption has been linked to cardiovascular mortality and other health conditions, including cancer. Therefore, it is advised that individuals limit their salt intake to promote better cardiovascular health.

In clinical nutrition, SAL also refers to the salts used in products that affect the solubility of proteins and other ingredients, highlighting its importance beyond mere seasoning. Overall, while salt is a ubiquitous and vital dietary component, its consumption should be approached with caution to avoid adverse health effects.</data>
      <data key="d2">3dbf07634cee8d4d443f59ed50f98b54,5f2499e1d1e1a498084eb95bdfe634fb,7ce7a74ee3898be3e3f6d500d73afefd,91131751d701592a597bae807e00c1dc,a0ef28095dacaada520d8e6fbaf518f1,b68bac9cb298a49d19de8db2a2b640a1,b8fd422921866f53cdaa6c24db528ee5,c1ec1ae8298c71442c08a75a2e629469,cddf59ae158cab9962605b9e7d903544,dc38a37c30b175667931b84a9c9103bb,dcb29ef57f2b65cd420e728a3e29102d,f41688eecb31d8e55fc70e993e99c3e9,fa95290726c6f8f7c5947019c98a3e1d</data>
