    <node id="FIBROSIS QU&#205;STICA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Fibrosis Qu&#237;stica, also known as Cystic Fibrosis (CF), is a genetic disorder caused by a mutation in the CFTR gene, which primarily affects the lungs and digestive system. This condition leads to severe respiratory issues, including complications similar to chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), as well as significant nutritional challenges. Patients with Fibrosis Qu&#237;stica often experience malabsorption of nutrients, including vitamins A, E, and K, and may require specialized nutritional management, including enteral nutrition and pancreatic enzyme supplementation to aid digestion.

The disorder is characterized by its impact on exocrine glands, resulting in thick mucus production that can obstruct airways and pancreatic ducts. This obstruction can lead to chronic respiratory infections, lung damage, and diabetes due to pancreatic damage and insulin secretion issues. Malnutrition is a common concern, necessitating careful caloric intake calculations and nutritional support to ensure adequate growth and health.

Fibrosis Qu&#237;stica is diagnosed through clinical criteria and specific tests, and it often requires multidisciplinary management to address the various complications associated with the disease. The condition is autosomal recessive, meaning that both parents must carry the mutated gene for a child to be affected. It is the most common cause of chronic pancreatitis in children and is associated with a range of complications that can worsen the patient's prognosis, including diabetes and malnutrition. 

Overall, Fibrosis Qu&#237;stica is a complex genetic disorder that necessitates ongoing medical care and support to manage its multifaceted challenges effectively.</data>
