      <data key="d7">cf16be70eebe430e94e68f4c57abeb47</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NRS-2002">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">NRS-2002 is a comprehensive nutritional screening tool designed to assess malnutrition and disease severity, particularly in clinical settings. It is recommended by the European Society for Clinical Nutrition and Metabolism (ESPEN) for evaluating the nutritional status of hospitalized patients. NRS-2002 is utilized in various studies, including the Predyces study, to identify malnutrition among diabetic patients. This tool serves as a preferred method for assessing nutritional risk, helping to predict potential surgical complications and ensuring that patients receive appropriate nutritional care. Overall, NRS-2002 is an essential instrument for identifying patients at risk of malnutrition in hospital environments.</data>
      <data key="d2">14039baea6555ffc156768e503ff0b90,1bad878c6efa9d3f5719546bb51bccb6,4734ce2075bc72c352950c018bab7185,7906ee47aaf0c271f072790eaea05866,87e62f930efe0b81911357297ccd271c,bf0cc16dc61df5b90a5a1e961322391a,f2846d08229fda837153207946e29831,fbbb8c2530f7ac5635ecf5110ea186b2</data>
      <data key="d3">702</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">14</data>
      <data key="d6">1698</data>
      <data key="d7">95a5313fa8fc4857afff4262b8978f76</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VSG">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">VSG, or Valoraci&#243;n Subjetiva Global, is a comprehensive screening tool utilized primarily in hospital settings to assess the nutritional status of patients. It has demonstrated a strong capacity to detect malnutrition, particularly within surgical populations. The method involves a subjective global assessment that incorporates both the patient's clinical history and a physical examination. As a diagnostic test, VSG effectively determines the presence and severity of malnutrition, making it an essential tool for healthcare professionals in evaluating and managing patients' nutritional needs.</data>
      <data key="d2">14039baea6555ffc156768e503ff0b90,4734ce2075bc72c352950c018bab7185,7906ee47aaf0c271f072790eaea05866,c51fc76876398ca4252c2585fb1dbb6b,fab4b91ddb77bd16a93a3808a9c12b4c</data>
      <data key="d3">1165</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">5</data>
      <data key="d6">1699</data>
      <data key="d7">ae62e5726c77411288e9feaf48c94706</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MNA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">The MNA, or Mini Nutritional Assessment, is a validated screening tool specifically designed for assessing the nutritional status of individuals aged 65 and older. It is widely utilized in geriatric settings to evaluate the risk of malnutrition among elderly patients. The MNA incorporates various aspects, including physical, social, and dietary factors, making it a comprehensive method for identifying undernutrition in frail elderly individuals. Recommended by the European Society for Clinical Nutrition and Metabolism (ESPEN), the MNA serves as an essential resource for healthcare professionals to detect malnutrition risk and ensure appropriate nutritional care for the elderly population.</data>
      <data key="d2">07b1884f11a85f8feb424b7cc369f822,14039baea6555ffc156768e503ff0b90,17f48ff2bbbb22ecd24983f41739c88b,1bad878c6efa9d3f5719546bb51bccb6,4734ce2075bc72c352950c018bab7185,56da8c0bffe9c7811cc9790d767f4972,6458fe5a14762d5f8c1561f80acb844a,76c4ef07f382585bf943bfaf57372c86,7906ee47aaf0c271f072790eaea05866,79b6030cb061514423118a8c4214b5e4,7d1eed82db6d0ad62418f3f965316c39,8e8a6418528a5c33a9ddf7eb94066a04,8e8aac45bfe71fa006f622690dfdbbb8,cc6ee3d7bc9bfaf4a06f3f02c4907eb0,e596bbb9231cc72bade554b3d67804b8,ef8194ca70c613876b927087bf7554f5,f2846d08229fda837153207946e29831,fab4b91ddb77bd16a93a3808a9c12b4c,fbbb8c2530f7ac5635ecf5110ea186b2</data>
      <data key="d3">708</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">31</data>
      <data key="d6">1700</data>
      <data key="d7">b988aa30879047398085f5019451164e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MNA-SF">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">MNA-SF, or Mini Nutritional Assessment Short Form, is a simplified version of the original Mini Nutritional Assessment (MNA) specifically designed for assessing nutritional status in older adults. It serves as a predictor of frailty in hospitalized elderly patients and is recognized as a validated screening tool for evaluating nutritional status across various healthcare settings. The MNA-SF is characterized by its ease of application and quick implementation, allowing healthcare professionals to efficiently screen for malnutrition risk in the elderly population while maintaining high sensitivity for detecting malnutrition. Overall, the MNA-SF is an effective and practical tool for nutritional screening, ensuring that the nutritional needs of older adults are adequately addressed.</data>
      <data key="d2">07b1884f11a85f8feb424b7cc369f822,17f48ff2bbbb22ecd24983f41739c88b,4734ce2075bc72c352950c018bab7185,54633fef79a422c0fada846ff2b861ba,8e8a6418528a5c33a9ddf7eb94066a04,e596bbb9231cc72bade554b3d67804b8,ef8194ca70c613876b927087bf7554f5</data>
      <data key="d3">708</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">1701</data>
      <data key="d7">086f033a0aed422db18e69b8ae57ee87</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRIBADO DEFINITIVO">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">The Cribado Definitivo is a screening process that assesses nutritional status and severity of illness based on specific criteria and scoring.</data>
      <data key="d2">c639615d7681ffeb945a996d231bb7aa</data>
      <data key="d3">729</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">1702</data>
      <data key="d7">4bddcf3219fb42dca0106d740d887b8a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AGE">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">AGE encompasses several critical aspects related to nutrition and health. It refers to the essential fatty acids provided by lipid emulsions, particularly linoleic acid, which are vital for various physiological functions. Additionally, age plays a crucial role in dietary recommendations, particularly concerning liquid and protein intake for pediatric patients. In nutritional assessments, age is a significant factor, with individuals over 70 years receiving an additional point in scoring, highlighting its importance in evaluating nutritional status. Furthermore, age significantly influences the prevalence of sarcopenia and frailty, with higher rates observed in older populations. For instance, in the context of risk classification according to the AJCC guidelines, the age of a patient, such as one who is 50 years old, is a key factor in determining their risk classification. Overall, age is a multifaceted element that impacts nutritional needs, health assessments, and risk evaluations across different populations.</data>
      <data key="d2">2a351a5c3cb70af0293c0897583049cc,87da0f3b46604498629565db45667b3b,a830f202c73ba48c11fb8863e0e4a642,b1ce0d940909277d321ee55bffcc69b3,c639615d7681ffeb945a996d231bb7aa</data>
      <data key="d3">1020</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">1703</data>
      <data key="d7">1522a4ed375e45f2aa14768e21490dc8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NUTRITIONAL TREATMENT PLAN">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">A plan initiated for patients identified as malnourished or at risk of malnutrition based on their assessment scores.</data>
      <data key="d2">c639615d7681ffeb945a996d231bb7aa</data>
      <data key="d3">1020</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">1704</data>
      <data key="d7">4dcbe52508774d6b9992dc040723067a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SEVERITY OF ILLNESS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Severity of illness is evaluated in the context of increased metabolic stress and its impact on nutritional requirements, as outlined in the Cribado Definitivo.</data>
      <data key="d2">c639615d7681ffeb945a996d231bb7aa</data>
      <data key="d3">704</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">1705</data>
      <data key="d7">af427f2506fe4acc89e1622d3d4c7ca6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WEIGHT LOSS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Weight loss refers to the process of reducing total body mass, which is often achieved through dietary changes and lifestyle modifications. It is a significant decrease in body weight that plays a critical role in assessing nutritional risk, particularly in identifying potential malnutrition or health issues. This is especially relevant for cancer patients, such as those undergoing treatment for esophageal cancer, who frequently face challenges related to weight loss.

In the context of health management, weight loss is crucial for improving insulin sensitivity and managing diabetes, making it a common recommendation as part of treatment plans. Various diets, including Very Low-Calorie Diets (VLCD), Ornish, and Pritikin, specifically aim to achieve weight loss through caloric restriction.

Weight loss is also a primary outcome measured in clinical studies, such as the SCALE studies, which evaluate the effectiveness of liraglutide treatment, and the BLOSSOM, BLOOM, and BLOOM-DM studies, which assess lorcaserin and its combinations. Overall, weight loss is a multifaceted issue that encompasses health, nutrition, and treatment strategies across different populations.</data>
      <data key="d2">032fc2148da74e586c8fd91e073c2be6,1388342730ee9177144f78f9e9d7816e,1f2b31f5a0bba00ecee5e3a8fb2c83ea,2b5b30d7b9371f8fe9e686c89dde3cb7,58ae94a34f90df368b032f1618c7bcec,623ef060e116bc05259d5e6d3d8c0996,64821313d6eb9af473ab515aff2ba3c7,7855ffa74481ba85f44e9700279250e7,90c684dca4542bbe1177afd26e78581e,bef1f848ac9b922f7361b740210a8314,c639615d7681ffeb945a996d231bb7aa,ce2e5f8df5ad472bb255174d496cc1d7,d0061877457bb078516d0e43dd6d8131</data>
      <data key="d3">738</data>
