    <node id="L&#205;PIDOS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">L&#237;pidos, or lipids, are a group of organic compounds that include fats and oils, playing a crucial role in nutrition and health. They are classified as macronutrients that provide energy, contributing significantly to the dietary needs of individuals, including patients requiring nutritional support. In parenteral nutrition, l&#237;pidos serve as a vital source of energy and essential fatty acids, accounting for 25%-40% of the non-protein caloric intake. It is recommended that l&#237;pidos constitute 30-35% of the total energy value of a diet, with an emphasis on healthy fatty acids, particularly for the elderly.

L&#237;pidos are absorbed in the small intestine through a process involving emulsification and digestion by pancreatic lipase and colipase. They are essential for various bodily functions, including energy storage, cellular structure, and nutrient absorption. In the context of athletic performance, l&#237;pidos are a significant energy source, especially during prolonged exercise, and are important in pediatric nutrition as well.

The analysis of l&#237;pidos is crucial for diagnosing medical conditions such as chylothorax and chylous ascites. They must be consumed as part of a balanced diet, ideally with a heart-healthy profile that minimizes saturated fats. Additionally, l&#237;pidos are a key topic in discussions about diet and health, particularly concerning low-fat diets and their implications for obesity and metabolic health. Overall, l&#237;pidos are indispensable nutrients that support energy needs and prevent deficiencies in essential fatty acids.</data>
      <data key="d2">06ffde2dc989dcf66c55a8058f4f51d9,0ebd0c517a3d578a6823dc39797e4da1,2f548ddee569586dbe82ca124f2695e9,37ca60bdc28109c9bec995e3c433c801,39da0855b83941d97766254aa36ed2fd,422dbd193843eb5098307e57ba002ced,43883071d6db17a632d928bc976776fd,4a88c4a9d485f88fca1d0f03e2e277a5,4b5441741b392c27b7f7c29cc79e13a5,507eeea555c496a467930836fc4c1a39,622b4827c8894dba02debfade4a9f817,638446780cdd61df83a06944c4e15c15,658ee56030a35ceeeec05d15cfa49842,7ba6c80b1b1919de63502e93c6b9f6fa,89b0bde0358432506a0e03d4691dd2ab,8d4057f6ab3a5d00b2ae7609742cdf86,9fda43c414d32c5a8a3bb7354bfb1704,c69834419bc4c69bcafd7a81ed8535d4,df73c75d84a055e0a67bc7f14d35e9d5,eff56271e184ab2f11877bfab7f715c4,f718747b68f5c2d9d67148f05fd2d4ef</data>
      <data key="d3">671</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">23</data>
      <data key="d6">1729</data>
      <data key="d7">8001055c9dfa4246a3225cf087c52ba1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALBUMINA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Albumina, also known as albumin, is a vital protein found in blood plasma that plays several crucial roles in the body. It is primarily produced by the liver and is essential for maintaining oncotic pressure, which helps regulate fluid balance within the bloodstream. Albumina binds to various substances, including copper, free fatty acids, and calcium, facilitating their transport throughout the body. 

The levels of albumina in the blood are commonly measured to assess nutritional status, liver function, and kidney function. It serves as a significant marker in nutritional assessments, particularly in patients undergoing surgery, those with cancer, and individuals with chronic kidney disease. Low levels of albumina can indicate compromised nutritional status, while increases in albumina levels may reflect improvements in nutritional intake, such as with enteral nutrition.

In clinical settings, albumina is monitored as part of liver function tests and is used to evaluate the caloric-protein status of patients, including those on dialysis. It is also recognized as a prognostic marker in various studies, including those related to patient outcomes in chronic illnesses. Overall, albumina is a critical protein that not only reflects nutritional health but also plays a significant role in various physiological processes within the body.</data>
      <data key="d2">0597a142067272f25431b8b42cb5288f,0c6c9eb4aba5c4642a75d76bd7ea08ba,0f2aa9d202fbabe2b0f310000e6987e7,169eceabd2bd24183f24faccb2c00088,30676865841b1573426895ff17c40ab4,38c7f97d650c77986c2dc9132aa20fdb,3c2bb3d9d6534668e58f571710c9bb02,45ae0837b0632b026fac1053b7d9d4e8,49d3f1ca700a7974db89e684f3744a40,500e56f18da6e8b6ed0e1eed4af927e9,541c13a95526e4d68903f0901042f292,58af2bdd20eeb5647ecc8088732833d8,61564a330848cb173088bd08e1a9a181,6458fe5a14762d5f8c1561f80acb844a,6bbb4df989ed7353f621a7d44dbc7972,78482a90281ee54a4aac11bb36bd6e36,7906ee47aaf0c271f072790eaea05866,7c10030e29c1434df3b10b1533710486,7e539a13a598e4ff2d2d4d6641a044d5,850572c4d004fc52f65ced939eda06bf,94481f05649e1ec83f8cc2e4a70b660b,9562f39f9e78644164daeff3177a2f84,9944c8e8d3d7769c5297f8dfb9b6f664,b00c312f46e790b32fe99743480f9e47,bf0cc16dc61df5b90a5a1e961322391a,c69834419bc4c69bcafd7a81ed8535d4,cba5d81f1acff7cbf84e85ef35420521,d1e7cd9fdc3093bc5902030d654d0763,d23c564df025cf16e37f2ef2be83ec34,d66eff5b0a0e16993c167d3a6ffef395,d87b6185e9826c5338c079056e2019a9,e34642244b0c7f309ab1c1be1e81da31,f0d279ccc92a98dc953edafb1080630b</data>
      <data key="d3">1155</data>
