    <node id="MACRONUTRIENT DISTRIBUTION">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Macronutrient distribution refers to the recommended proportions of carbohydrates, proteins, and fats in a patient's diet, which are essential for meeting their nutritional needs. For diabetic patients, this distribution involves specific recommendations tailored to help manage their condition effectively. Proper macronutrient distribution is crucial for maintaining optimal health and managing diabetes, ensuring that patients receive the right balance of nutrients to support their overall well-being.</data>
      <data key="d2">8413d4414414c5cb139198857cce327a,bfe875ea294b9a390193b5de532ff71b</data>
      <data key="d3">1248</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">1775</data>
      <data key="d7">f405652d89424a008ac29f40d7056198</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARBOHYDRATES">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Carbohydrates are a macronutrient that plays a crucial role in dietary management, particularly in relation to blood sugar levels. They are a significant focus in dietary recommendations for diabetes management, as careful management of carbohydrate intake is essential for diabetic patients to prevent spikes in blood sugar. Generally, carbohydrates should constitute 60-70% of total daily caloric intake, with a minimum recommended intake of 130 grams per day. They serve as a major source of energy in the diet, making their intake recommendations vital for overall health management. Additionally, carbohydrates are assessed for their dextrose equivalent value and functional properties in nutritional products, highlighting their importance in both dietary planning and food formulation.</data>
      <data key="d2">018644ab78da6f804c57546f08ff6a65,440978f75b135fa97eb7b99ca379e9a4,7180911fea4c085adf139e1c4cd43151,bfe875ea294b9a390193b5de532ff71b,c98ed25e58194d76463e3553fb3f3c15,e173daca5490a19b057aecb1ee4edca2</data>
      <data key="d3">1248</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">1776</data>
      <data key="d7">899822fe8bc4425292996600b53cbccb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROTEINS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Proteins are essential macronutrients that play a critical role in the body's repair, growth, and overall health. They are composed of amino acids and are vital for maintaining the body's structure and function. In dietary contexts, proteins are emphasized as crucial nutrients, particularly for recovery after bariatric surgery, where they are recommended to constitute 15-20% of total caloric intake, adjusted according to the patient's weight and metabolic stress. 

In addition to their importance for post-surgical recovery, proteins are also significant for patients undergoing dialysis, as their intake is carefully managed to ensure adequate nutritional support. For diabetic patients, similar recommendations apply, with proteins also constituting 15-20% of their total caloric intake. 

Furthermore, proteins are particularly important for older adults, who require higher protein intake to prevent fragility and support recovery from illness. Their nutritional quality and amino acid profiles are evaluated in the development of nutritional products, highlighting their role in maintaining nitrogen balance and tissue maintenance. Overall, proteins are monitored in various patient populations to ensure sufficient nutritional intake during dietary treatment, underscoring their importance in health and recovery.</data>
      <data key="d2">214cb2b8ddec52ff69849d87feb8e141,440978f75b135fa97eb7b99ca379e9a4,5d87d36fec170e242e5e50305a1bec49,6517add809ee588bede013f59f20b0ef,77c2b5963fab84c30e6eb596b49336bf,7ff62e1dc423be2f4b2fe08546751d0d,8413d4414414c5cb139198857cce327a,97a728c18b63007d07b6a4a5fc22d5e4,ae99604da4e0a7da3a6e99e197205ab5,bfe875ea294b9a390193b5de532ff71b,cf34fdea5374cdee9a8be8f93c63e830</data>
      <data key="d3">1247</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">11</data>
      <data key="d6">1777</data>
      <data key="d7">8c2029369f0b478eaca4f123dbcfe6ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FATS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Fats are a macronutrient that should be limited in the diet to reduce health risks, particularly for individuals with specific health concerns such as diabetes. It is recommended to monitor the intake of both saturated and unsaturated fats to mitigate potential health issues, including obesity and cardiovascular problems. This dietary guidance emphasizes the importance of managing fat consumption to promote overall health and well-being.</data>
      <data key="d2">8413d4414414c5cb139198857cce327a,bfe875ea294b9a390193b5de532ff71b</data>
      <data key="d3">1248</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">1778</data>
      <data key="d7">6c2b06ce4e8743a184304cd0312fb73a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FIBER">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Fiber is a crucial dietary component that plays a significant role in digestive health and the regulation of blood sugar levels, making it particularly beneficial for both diabetic and non-diabetic patients. It is recommended to be consumed at a rate of 14 grams per 1000 kilocalories to achieve optimal health benefits. Fiber is included in various nutritional products, such as Fresubin 2 kcal Fibre Drink, which supports digestive health. 

In addition to its general health benefits, fiber may also be included in nutritional formulas for critically ill patients; however, its use is not routinely recommended in certain conditions, such as diarrhea. The selection of fiber is based on its type and quantity, as it can impact the viscosity of nutritional products. Furthermore, fiber derived from whole grain cereals has been associated with reduced urinary calcium excretion and is relevant in the dietary management of kidney stones.

It is important to note that fiber intake can be affected by the elimination of gluten-containing cereals, necessitating an increase in fiber consumption from alternative sources such as fruits and vegetables. Overall, fiber is a vital nutrient that supports various aspects of health and well-being.</data>
      <data key="d2">440978f75b135fa97eb7b99ca379e9a4,5236affa95aaf73b0f2d08e839e4db54,550574bce077f34348bd11c78dc814a1,7180911fea4c085adf139e1c4cd43151,77c2b5963fab84c30e6eb596b49336bf,8413d4414414c5cb139198857cce327a,8d9c5b85d058d0d3cde4479b5589a7d1,a3d1ea55509c69a04f3268ea20a33ba4,bfe875ea294b9a390193b5de532ff71b,e173daca5490a19b057aecb1ee4edca2</data>
      <data key="d3">1248</data>
