      <data key="d5">11</data>
      <data key="d6">1779</data>
      <data key="d7">10949a23a0864502885cd50dbfeb1c9e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WATER NEEDS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">WATER NEEDS refer to the essential hydration requirements for individuals, which vary based on specific health conditions. For adults, the general recommendation for water intake is between 30 and 40 mL/kg/day, a guideline that is particularly important for maintaining hydration in hospitalized patients. Additionally, water needs for diabetic patients are generally comparable to those of the general population, although there may be specific considerations based on other health conditions that could influence their hydration requirements.</data>
      <data key="d2">8413d4414414c5cb139198857cce327a,bfe875ea294b9a390193b5de532ff71b</data>
      <data key="d3">752</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">1780</data>
      <data key="d7">e2b0da483cac4ce3a93605839f7bc101</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENERGY REQUIREMENTS">
      <data key="d0" />
      <data key="d1">Energy requirements encompass the total caloric intake necessary for various patient populations, including those with specific health conditions. For diabetic patients, energy requirements are comparable to those of the general population; however, adjustments must be made for weight, particularly in cases of obesity. In a hospital setting, the typical energy requirements for patients range from 2200 to 2400 kcal. Additionally, for patients suffering from burns or critical conditions, energy requirements are crucial for maintaining health and supporting recovery, highlighting the importance of tailored caloric intake based on individual health needs.</data>
      <data key="d2">4e7560a799f319bdc8ae25737acc4d05,8413d4414414c5cb139198857cce327a,bfe875ea294b9a390193b5de532ff71b,fb0c6cd346ca91e15f8ff1cc07570d9d</data>
