      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">1782</data>
      <data key="d7">2be55a5bfd4044029050e21a9c7e2b70</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HARRIS BENEDICT">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">The Harris Benedict equation is a widely recognized formula utilized to calculate the basal metabolic rate (BMR) and energy requirements of individuals, particularly in patients requiring nutritional support. This equation takes into account several key factors, including sex, age, weight, and height, to determine the resting energy expenditure. By integrating these variables, the Harris Benedict formula provides a comprehensive assessment of an individual's energy needs, making it an essential tool in clinical nutrition and metabolic studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">140611dbb8a68fc40712c946de92b73f,285343ba92b89a1761b72d9d789ba8f6,43883071d6db17a632d928bc976776fd,93ddfb3a9423ac76f9303638f6465d25</data>
      <data key="d3">1132</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">1783</data>
      <data key="d7">2fe4dca562a54dc095a71865c9c61e12</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MACRONUTRIENTES">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">"MACRONUTRIENTES" refers to essential nutrients that the body requires in large quantities, including carbohydrates, proteins, and fats. These macronutrients play a crucial role in providing energy and supporting various bodily functions. Their absorption primarily occurs in the small intestine, and they are vital for overall health and nutrition. In particular, macronutrients are significant in the dietary management of specific health conditions, such as cancer and diabetes, where a balanced intake is necessary for optimal patient nutrition. Additionally, macronutrients are important in pediatric nutrition and in the management of metabolic disorders like McArdle disease. Overall, macronutrients are fundamental components of a healthy diet, necessary for energy production and the maintenance of bodily functions.</data>
      <data key="d2">06ffde2dc989dcf66c55a8058f4f51d9,12281055b260e7dc55fcaeeab7eefde6,24e49b0b93b2307e2c4e7ac34f4d7f4b,43883071d6db17a632d928bc976776fd,4464b0da291e94ba7b7f5b51134b4a75,7b13bde26d33b4316c3183dd027ed909,8d7280a9f9085fe677f5993b12910ea2,93ddfb3a9423ac76f9303638f6465d25,abf638322cfb1c522bfbee1852e28a19,c2a8dd9d28d1992a73de2834131b69c9,d028f23d86d7ed32eebe35c5d90765f2,d667e6abc204bbfa6aba210705db6d56,df410977169e29218f41ff919bd4e44b</data>
      <data key="d3">1183</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">18</data>
      <data key="d6">1784</data>
      <data key="d7">a1f54f1b363b4480905b760e49f408da</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICRONUTRIENTES">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Micronutrientes are essential nutrients, including vitamins and minerals such as zinc, copper, selenium, iron, and folic acid, that are required in small amounts for various bodily functions. They play a crucial role in metabolic processes and are particularly important for critically ill patients, as deficiencies can lead to significant clinical consequences. In the context of parenteral nutrition, it is vital to monitor the levels of these micronutrients to prevent both deficiencies and excesses.

Micronutrients are necessary for the proper functioning of the human nervous system and are critical in the dietary management of specific patient populations, including those with chronic kidney disease, cancer, inflammatory bowel disease (IBD), and post-transplant patients. For instance, cancer patients may experience deficiencies in these nutrients, and while supplementation is important, high doses are generally discouraged due to potential harm. Similarly, patients with IBD may require careful management of their micronutrient intake to avoid deficiencies.

In pediatric dietary recommendations, micronutrients are essential for growth and development, while in athletes, they support overall health and performance, particularly iron and calcium. Additionally, micronutrients are important for the recovery of patients with eating disorders, as deficiencies can worsen their conditions. In elderly populations, these nutrients may help in preventing cognitive decline and supporting overall health.

Overall, micronutrients are vital components of a balanced diet, and their adequate intake is crucial for maintaining health across various patient groups, including those who are hospitalized, critically ill, or recovering from surgery or illness.</data>
      <data key="d2">06ffde2dc989dcf66c55a8058f4f51d9,111edb0e21c873322871e35619a1ebf5,12281055b260e7dc55fcaeeab7eefde6,16887bf1589e9515213d2e392bbc8c69,1adf391b13598f3ffe28ae596ff49438,2c60ced31055ac58d76da9c1e90c8e9d,46912552a886d029c4aa16e3ea9a0e27,5fb0e4d2f6efd6c6abcd9f7b7583ed7f,6289e9e0b5871fb9a2f2f691429bb4f8,7b13bde26d33b4316c3183dd027ed909,81bb0be389c10331fb2a5568b6c379dc,89c28a4a0410d8f4cbbbc0fbdf4b1c40,93ddfb3a9423ac76f9303638f6465d25,a687eee1e8bfd2d98ce40a14628dd855,b5f705e0ba650f5ad1f558446be0191e,bb9a5c32408b601f9ecedc2a2b82d6b6,bf76380d2417653bab17a6a9b91d85bc,c06e5bcd7933ed338cd1257d11c709cf,c9790e5e47712b100fb4870d35a3edfa,d600b483c44c0647e40e4775e0936044,df73c75d84a055e0a67bc7f14d35e9d5,eaa2ba81b06f325e284b5dbdc45b5c9f,f7871bbaa57d50bb5f5675cd580cb0af,fe69a5b07baa1ca7328e65ff39f7719a</data>
      <data key="d3">673</data>
