    </node>
    <node id="ACEITE DE PESCADO">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">ACEITE DE PESCADO, or fish oil, is utilized primarily as a source of omega-3 fatty acids. It plays a significant role in parenteral nutrition, providing essential nutrients for individuals who cannot consume food orally. Additionally, ACEITE DE PESCADO is incorporated into various dietary formulations, further emphasizing its importance as a rich source of omega-3 fatty acids, which are beneficial for overall health.</data>
      <data key="d2">a6a4604a5fc2c91b714b6ba568bb0090,aea37ea74c52c952285b3ae7db228572,ee5838dfb7aae573c969c1510fbf2d09</data>
      <data key="d3">1763</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">1884</data>
      <data key="d7">94d7ac6888c94f49a9f4e2904b895e8a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRIGLIC&#201;RIDOS DE CADENA MEDIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">"TRIGLIC&#201;RIDOS DE CADENA MEDIA" (Medium-Chain Triglycerides, MCT) are a type of fatty acid that is metabolized differently from long-chain fats, leading to a neutral to positive effect on atherogenesis and no effect on thrombogenesis. These triglycerides are primarily derived from coconut oil and are known for their rapid metabolism, making them a valuable energy source in specific dietary plans. MCTs are particularly beneficial for patients with conditions such as steatorrhea and Adrenoleukodystrophy, as they can be included in low-fat diets and specialized nutritional formulas to enhance energy intake and improve the absorption of fat-soluble vitamins. Additionally, medium-chain triglycerides are utilized in second-generation lipid emulsions for parenteral nutrition, highlighting their versatility in medical dietary management. Overall, MCTs serve as important supplements in diets aimed at improving nutrition and addressing certain medical conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">3d053ac744015b40d8790ff91b5d02f2,5fb0e4d2f6efd6c6abcd9f7b7583ed7f,609140cabbcd0c7297a7029f5365d186,86450d0e00138163bc3427ff406aa842,8a7b2d5e0440750e36603c8d3f93a68a,8d7a18d8258ab74a0e56129f937a8a84,9927d054458e409309b9e917cbb6a10c,aea37ea74c52c952285b3ae7db228572,c4abdcd9f1628aa02ca6ed662b795fd6,e55003dbf85cc1fb81cac90716ce0e87</data>
      <data key="d3">650</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">1885</data>
      <data key="d7">d72a09f6b150419e80f86701dd89eb25</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ARGININA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">ARGININA, also known as arginine, is a basic amino acid that plays a crucial role in various physiological processes within the body. It is often included in enteral diets due to its health benefits, particularly for patients undergoing surgery for head and neck tumors, where it supports recovery and immune function. Arginina acts as a nitrogen transporter, primarily synthesized in the kidneys, and is involved in multiple metabolic pathways.

One of the significant functions of arginina is its role as a precursor to nitric oxide (NO), a molecule essential for vascular relaxation. This property makes arginina important in the context of conditions such as MELAS (Mitochondrial Encephalomyopathy, Lactic Acidosis, and Stroke-like episodes), where it is presumed to improve the availability of nitric oxide and potentially aid in symptom management.

In addition to its therapeutic applications, arginina is recognized as an immunonutrient that enhances T lymphocyte numbers and function, contributing to immune responses. It is also involved in the metabolism of proline, another amino acid.

Arginina is frequently discussed in the context of nutritional requirements, particularly regarding its effects on patient mortality rates and its use as a supplement among athletes to improve performance and recovery after exercise. However, the addition of arginina to nutrition in certain patient groups remains a topic of debate due to a lack of conclusive studies.

Furthermore, specific supplements, such as Resource ArginAid, contain L-arginine and are designed to support nutritional needs, highlighting its importance in both clinical and athletic settings. Overall, arginina is a versatile amino acid with significant implications for health, recovery, and performance.</data>
      <data key="d2">194ecedd60f53d4157bd44eac52cbcd8,33815f13a51d2a716356eaca97176390,3e8ab018917854d76a0137102e0392bd,43883071d6db17a632d928bc976776fd,6bcc5beeb4b20475f24131743313fb30,7c43869fff0960219e47d176597b35b8,9b853d8ac0d48f1111932f52fb0749db,aea37ea74c52c952285b3ae7db228572,c6d3d43de4b87527989dd0a7d9bda8e0,d2f16314e7098030a6780411d2e03023,fae6538f87888794da1ff0aaf0d842fe</data>
      <data key="d3">1945</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">11</data>
      <data key="d6">1886</data>
      <data key="d7">dd743ac2937b4a4cb79d0ddec8638dc1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLUTAMINA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">GLUTAMINA, or glutamine, is a non-essential amino acid that can become conditionally essential in certain stress situations, particularly during metabolic stress such as severe burns, post-surgical recovery, and in critically ill patients. It plays a crucial role in various metabolic processes, including nitrogen transport, immune response modulation, and serving as an energy source for enterocytes and intestinal cells. 

This amino acid is often added to enteral diets to support gut health and is suggested to improve glycemic control in diabetic patients when supplemented in nutritional therapy. In the context of critical care, glutamine may be supplemented to manage nutritional needs, particularly in patients with burns or those undergoing chemotherapy and surgery, as it has been shown to help prevent nutritional problems and improve postoperative complication rates.

Research has indicated that glutamine can enhance intestinal barrier function, improve insulin sensitivity, and reduce hospital complications. It is also recognized for its potential benefits in patients with malabsorption, as it may aid in the absorption of water and electrolytes. However, the recommendations for its use in parenteral nutrition are not uniform across studies, and while it has been previously recommended for routine use, recent studies have raised questions about its efficacy.

In specific medical contexts, such as hematopoietic stem cell transplantation, glutamine has demonstrated beneficial effects, and it has been speculated to help alleviate symptoms of hepatic veno-occlusive disease in transplant patients. Additionally, glutamine is noted for its role as an immunomodulator, which can optimize recovery post-exercise and protect against mucositis during chemotherapy and radiotherapy treatments.

Despite its potential benefits, the use of glutamine in clinical practice is sometimes questioned due to inconclusive evidence regarding its effectiveness, and it is not included in standard parenteral nutrition solutions due to its instability. Nonetheless, it remains a significant nutrient in the nutritional management of various patient populations, particularly those experiencing metabolic stress or undergoing intensive medical treatments.</data>
      <data key="d2">0b02ff4978fb09a0b2a360ae8c661337,125acd19edb76fa23986de56fc2517e8,194ecedd60f53d4157bd44eac52cbcd8,202e4fa915a698c9d9164c3fcc61534e,25192e60a2c23bd74095f4602efeac4c,2bbe847617971cacfe15b2c970de2d21,33815f13a51d2a716356eaca97176390,3e6ae05671fed21e332ee155332df21e,4f6c697d19c8a4459b3accd91f111bfd,546d2f3d8a308b461c44f6b0e1351ee6,57ec3d3d82d22e19c09ea2b735ef7779,6a12bee5b54e4c1706548bca972516e3,71dd228326087dc7aaa0d310d49b6f42,7b34ed1eb4b09b2e0762c7e917f8a516,84aebe8ee79b67a8fe16d40b6c07331d,86a4647fb1fa580f7b72aa4833965ecb,896ddfd9f06dc3ec8b1cfb986fae97fe,916d4d35de887923f4ea5b05928f13e3,a687eee1e8bfd2d98ce40a14628dd855,aea37ea74c52c952285b3ae7db228572,beaa66c30582232126ce2c762a9c6bdb,c58712e2ee5757b5175054fcc54f4dd6,c70d301baddcdcdefbba9b024ad4f3c5,ce8f5f24dbbf8624e49a6f85c2ad0d04,d147df5b06fa62d87b7edd3f20dcd246,d2f16314e7098030a6780411d2e03023,eae8ddef005974a2ffe71cf7c61b0634,f333e5a9af59dd8504607b2559ac35dd,fae6538f87888794da1ff0aaf0d842fe</data>
      <data key="d3">644</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">34</data>
      <data key="d6">1887</data>
      <data key="d7">64051f66e56b48f799db7be757568f3c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NUCLE&#211;TIDOS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">NUCLE&#211;TIDOS are compounds included in enteral diets that support cellular function and immune response. They are classified as immunonutrients and serve as precursors of RNA and DNA, which enhances T lymphocyte function.</data>
      <data key="d2">aea37ea74c52c952285b3ae7db228572,d2f16314e7098030a6780411d2e03023</data>
      <data key="d3">644</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">1888</data>
      <data key="d7">b1ff86b3666046b8a70a3163c3232a57</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACEITES VEGETALES">
      <data key="d0" />
      <data key="d1">ACEITES VEGETALES, or vegetable oils, are fats derived from plants that must be clearly identified in ingredient lists. They are generally considered healthier alternatives to saturated fats and are associated with cardiovascular benefits. Specifically, vegetable oils are sources of omega-6 fatty acids and are recommended for inclusion in a healthy diet, as endorsed by the American Heart Association. Additionally, these oils, particularly those rich in unsaturated fats, are emphasized in the Mediterranean diet for their positive impact on heart health. However, it is important to note that vegetable oils are not permitted in certain modified diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">347e3d175ef7f30e3dcad731f84fb375,633a3a90a82e02d05096b361ddba3ad7,7796f48230d374753b744d8e5fb1e8ae,aea37ea74c52c952285b3ae7db228572,bfe7b32b596449644b274ed7486a507a,c7af1c950a6caab8be5d180a2889a25a,e04d44fa9d8f83cab42b04dd086695fc,ed7bd985e5e4a3d5642e4b401b8e3e17</data>
      <data key="d3">1183</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">5</data>
      <data key="d6">1889</data>
      <data key="d7">c78d2ddb889d45ebb1aff091a657702a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GASTROINTESTINALES COMPLICACIONES">
