    <node id="HIPERGLUCEMIA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">HIPERGLUCEMIA is a medical condition characterized by elevated blood glucose levels. This condition can occur in various patient populations, particularly in those receiving nutritional therapy, such as enteral and parenteral nutrition. It is commonly seen in diabetic patients, critically ill individuals, and those undergoing surgical procedures, as well as in newborns with very low birth weight due to excessive glucose intake.

In patients receiving parenteral nutrition, hyperglycemia is a frequent complication that arises when nutrient intake exceeds the body's metabolic clearance capacity. It can also complicate the diagnosis of other conditions, such as hyponatremia, and is notably prevalent in patients suffering from pancreatitis, where blood glucose levels can become abnormally high. Additionally, hyperglycemia can affect recovery in surgical patients and is a concern in the management of critically ill patients.

The condition is also associated with the use of steroids in transplant patients, which can contribute to elevated blood glucose levels. Overall, hyperglycemia is a significant metabolic complication that requires careful monitoring and management in various clinical settings.</data>
      <data key="d2">241d358b60724c07a75223b79c9e5f41,4f6c697d19c8a4459b3accd91f111bfd,507eeea555c496a467930836fc4c1a39,6289e9e0b5871fb9a2f2f691429bb4f8,7469cc60475806b519e91abe2796aead,81bb0be389c10331fb2a5568b6c379dc,86aa5f95aae53ab120c5c63eda5486bc,88dcf64f61e33adc3702b1ec401e2d4e,9480cb55d00db88cfe1decdf011cb41f,c06e5bcd7933ed338cd1257d11c709cf,c58712e2ee5757b5175054fcc54f4dd6,c73e5e558cab6ae4736ce1619142c0a4,cd16cc82ca1357af8c9c1ab73ea55c5a,cff2f16dff598dbf0bff544091c77837,d42b833940d845abc98aa8e8ea05d6ae,d936cb199b788cfcddbfb6e51b1e6e48,e41a9b2827462263a11ef60ce74c160b</data>
