    <node id="HIPERTRIGLICERIDEMIA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Hipertrigliceridemia, also known as hypertriglyceridemia, is a medical condition characterized by elevated levels of triglycerides in the blood. This condition can arise from various causes, including genetic disorders and acquired conditions. It is particularly concerning for patients with Glycogen Storage Disease I, as well as those receiving parenteral nutrition, where excessive lipid administration can lead to elevated triglyceride levels. 

Management of hipertrigliceridemia often involves dietary adjustments to help control triglyceride levels. In patients on parenteral nutrition, careful monitoring is essential, as excessive lipid intake can exceed the body's ability to clear triglycerides, potentially leading to complications such as pancreatitis and pseudohyponatremia. Overall, hipertrigliceridemia is a significant health concern that requires attention to dietary management and monitoring, especially in vulnerable patient populations.</data>
      <data key="d2">1f2182c320403c424563ba1a084c00d5,3f46d79bcfab2143f045659e11be171b,6289e9e0b5871fb9a2f2f691429bb4f8,948f7e888e5bb06b347a80a3fbf87ae7,a9ee55a5371c88622e278b2543fa7317,c06e5bcd7933ed338cd1257d11c709cf,c73e5e558cab6ae4736ce1619142c0a4,c78125c02ea4d5afdf5cb5ac4baeeb27,d936cb199b788cfcddbfb6e51b1e6e48</data>
      <data key="d3">1800</data>
