      <data key="d7">61956b2ff4ac43cf910845f48ed82a4b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HEPATOPAT&#205;A">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">HEPATOPAT&#205;A refers to a range of liver diseases characterized by liver dysfunction, which can lead to various complications such as hepatic encephalopathy, cirrhosis, and nutritional deficiencies. This condition may necessitate dietary modifications to support liver function and manage protein intake, particularly in patients receiving home parenteral nutrition or those with intestinal failure. Hepatopathy can arise from multiple factors, including excessive glucose or lipid administration, especially in individuals on long-term parenteral nutrition.

Additionally, hepatopathy is associated with metabolic disorders, such as Adrenoleukodystrophy and Wilson's disease, and can be a contraindication for certain weight loss medications. It is important to note that hepatopathy can affect the body's metabolism and may require specific dietary management, including considerations for ketogenic diets. Overall, hepatopathy encompasses any disease of the liver that impacts its function and structure, necessitating careful monitoring and treatment to prevent further complications.</data>
      <data key="d2">0a9b42ff082f2d0bb036ec3365d9a6e9,1911ce26c118381473eccb438875b29c,1f2182c320403c424563ba1a084c00d5,20491afa9a4770e93e6cff9034f8b0f0,2f96bbc5eafd39b659620da7282a2079,488970234520652b063db88e8346031c,5310010ed05fb1b56f667b44eb14106d,75a4b2d504de480eaef247aa682bfd05,7ad48347b2499729451a184bae719445,c4abdcd9f1628aa02ca6ed662b795fd6,c6406ac5c0c7b03d4ee61ca311fd783f,e55543c6579d036d25f25eb2ff63fcf2,ea816809115cc37c571bd36191042123,ecc0b96af9066f424f1c114102063682</data>
      <data key="d3">597</data>
