      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2134</data>
      <data key="d7">31119c8930d440c9b3e47384449247b8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NEOGLUCOG&#201;NESIS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Neoglucogenesis is the metabolic process of generating glucose from non-carbohydrate substrates, particularly during stress.</data>
      <data key="d2">33815f13a51d2a716356eaca97176390</data>
      <data key="d3">1389</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2135</data>
      <data key="d7">30c4aea91bf0499c84554846b4937663</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TCCR">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">TCCR refers to a specific condition where protein intake may need to be increased to 2-2.5 g/Kg/d&#237;a.</data>
      <data key="d2">33815f13a51d2a716356eaca97176390</data>
      <data key="d3">1389</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2136</data>
      <data key="d7">94ec1f98590f4b0a9a45d729726c0f6b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACIDOS GRASOS ESENCIALES">
      <data key="d0" />
      <data key="d1">Essential fatty acids, known as "&#225;cidos grasos esenciales," are vital nutrients necessary for the proper functioning of the human body. These include linoleic and linolenic acids, which are crucial for maintaining health and must be obtained through diet, as the body cannot synthesize them. A deficiency in essential fatty acids can lead to various health issues, including conditions such as hepatic steatosis. Therefore, ensuring an adequate intake of these fatty acids is important for overall well-being.</data>
      <data key="d2">0cdbb308e3359fd3ddbb1d828d12dc04,33815f13a51d2a716356eaca97176390,d76cb2d50e736da37be7fe2a415a7281,e41a9b2827462263a11ef60ce74c160b</data>
      <data key="d3">1389</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">4</data>
      <data key="d6">2137</data>
      <data key="d7">526e660812de4669b4b6c682880f0bee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CALORIMETR&#205;A INDIRECTA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">CALORIMETR&#205;A INDIRECTA is a method used to estimate and measure energy expenditure in patients, making it a crucial tool for managing nutrition, particularly in acute pancreatitis and critically ill patients. It is considered the gold standard for calculating energy requirements in these populations. This method involves measuring oxygen consumption and carbon dioxide production to estimate caloric expenditure, which is essential for determining nutritional needs in various patient groups, including post-operative and oncological patients. 

Indirect calorimetry is particularly beneficial for evaluating the energy needs of obese patients and is preferred for calculating nutritional requirements in critically ill individuals. However, despite its advantages, indirect calorimetry is often unavailable in hospitals due to cost and time constraints, limiting its widespread use. Overall, CALORIMETR&#205;A INDIRECTA serves as a valuable tool in clinical settings for assessing patients' energy expenditure and guiding nutritional interventions.</data>
      <data key="d2">111edb0e21c873322871e35619a1ebf5,125acd19edb76fa23986de56fc2517e8,195affa22ecd2f7b1cdce3d72fc1cba4,3e40e9a9e041bbf1e7ca6eaa14080666,43883071d6db17a632d928bc976776fd,445e6aeaae0e8edb3faf2d36be2073ce,d667e6abc204bbfa6aba210705db6d56,d92f4ecdee41ca35485c997a95642c5b,f5ac70db902f1361fd681d1d109828c0,fb23af50b771da28e73b9545fa4f09b0</data>
      <data key="d3">1133</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">2138</data>
      <data key="d7">8c00343a173d42068399c2f6f18786ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLUCEMIA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">GLUCEMIA refers to the concentration of glucose in the blood, a critical parameter in the management and diagnosis of diabetes, prediabetes, and related complications. Regular monitoring of blood glucose levels is essential for patients undergoing treatment for diabetes and malnutrition, as well as for those receiving parenteral nutrition. It is particularly important to assess glucemia before, during, and after exercise for individuals with diabetes, as dietary intake significantly influences glucose levels. 

In the context of diabetes management, glucemia is crucial for diagnosing conditions such as diabetes and cetoacidosis diab&#233;tica, and it plays a key role in assessing metabolic health, especially in patients with specific conditions like MELAS syndrome. Blood glucose levels are also tested during the initial assessment of patients with eating disorders to monitor metabolic health and potential complications. 

Monitoring glucemia is vital for maintaining glucose levels within normal ranges, particularly for patients with Glycogen Storage Disease I. In clinical practice, blood sugar levels are typically controlled every 4 to 6 hours during the initial days of fasting to detect hypoglycemia, although the level of glucemia may not directly correlate with the effectiveness of dietary interventions. Overall, maintaining appropriate glucemia is essential for the health and management of individuals with diabetes and related metabolic disorders.</data>
      <data key="d2">043ed163c427b880fe39884076cae7ea,1f2b31f5a0bba00ecee5e3a8fb2c83ea,21339c051dc2f47471026673b3ac6436,241d358b60724c07a75223b79c9e5f41,3dfc700a2eaec232c05b0a53f2089229,3ed179f6e734d9fe4921fb0be0b018e4,3f685a05a9682db6dcde958d1918cc77,43883071d6db17a632d928bc976776fd,48e690b80c196e397d9ab9527b38c3d4,4c37affdd97e7772d1ea27aee34533b8,4d84210c815fb41f2fa44f3e01aafa02,4f6c697d19c8a4459b3accd91f111bfd,6a2d5ced0ad16871fec539845797e0ac,7cc3d3c5826ce81977ed8ad725e1ddbe,8bcf09481326ab5017cc3b45317538a3,948f7e888e5bb06b347a80a3fbf87ae7,9889a9000d5a82ffba9e0f6c92da918a,a81765e6d24ccef0c86801f71673de98,b7a2c1e4dd77f47605fdb33f4a2c2bdf,e3370b71146ad5063d6b1e9c3d8a3bd2,e82ce4aa73097ca70936be74cdafc12b,eb1f89320202c337496dbad5ca168356,f1bd89f4a1fcc385a19f5c5323ee2f89</data>
      <data key="d3">1600</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">22</data>
      <data key="d6">2139</data>
      <data key="d7">4e1202754ab14a62bb1471bad0bc1729</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GL&#218;CIDOS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">GL&#218;CIDOS, also known as carbohydrates, are macronutrients that serve as a primary source of energy and are included in nutritional recommendations for patients. However, in the context of a ketogenic diet, the intake of GL&#218;CIDOS is restricted to maintain the desired ketogenic ratio. This highlights the dual role of GL&#218;CIDOS in nutrition, where they are essential for energy but may be limited in specific dietary plans to achieve particular health goals.</data>
      <data key="d2">1592143354d1ef792f8618ec03a32a16,43883071d6db17a632d928bc976776fd</data>
