    <node id="CIRROSIS HEP&#193;TICA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Cirrhosis hep&#225;tica, or hepatic cirrhosis, is a late-stage liver disease characterized by extensive scarring (fibrosis) of the liver, which is often irreversible. This chronic condition can arise from various liver diseases and conditions, including hepatitis, chronic alcoholism, and glucogenosis due to prolonged liver damage. Cirrhosis can lead to significant complications, such as ascitis quilosa, a condition involving fluid accumulation in the abdominal cavity, and digestive hemorrhage, particularly complicating medical procedures like the placement of gastrostomy tubes. 

Additionally, cirrhosis can affect the body's ability to clear prolactin, which may have further implications for health. Management of cirrhosis often requires dietary modifications, such as salt restriction, to help induce a negative sodium balance and manage symptoms. Overall, cirrhosis hep&#225;tica represents a serious health concern that necessitates careful monitoring and treatment to mitigate its complications.</data>
      <data key="d2">2b6e6d589cd4e83086e44c77205b3740,2eeab2238556165bbea897f24c5b1c7a,71dd228326087dc7aaa0d310d49b6f42,76bdd5f53571ff68dd1e439219572347,82adde9844cf0b1ccd052f91b304fb6e,84b542fb745825398f17e4f8f4756cb4,9426220ccd162a5796b1bb2cb373e29f,9610fe47fea8bd5de48277db4233764c,c77caafffd9b5d42c51cb2359ad9cc6d,eb8877823f796fc9a02f5103dc4650cc,f7b4cba3bbdeec64513c036fd4166835</data>
      <data key="d3">1817</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">26</data>
      <data key="d6">2213</data>
      <data key="d7">b8244794c91c4cd6a8d583297af42e72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARCINOMA HEPATOCELULAR">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Carcinoma hepatocellular, also known as hepatocellular carcinoma, is a primary malignancy of the liver that is frequently linked to chronic liver disease and cirrhosis. This type of liver cancer can also arise in patients with hereditary hemochromatosis, especially in the presence of cirrhosis.</data>
      <data key="d2">82adde9844cf0b1ccd052f91b304fb6e,9610fe47fea8bd5de48277db4233764c</data>
      <data key="d3">1819</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">2214</data>
      <data key="d7">17cc1ac0c1924b41a6b2a47f4826a0c2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HEPATITIS V&#205;RICAS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Viral hepatitis refers to liver inflammation caused by viral infections, which can lead to chronic liver disease.</data>
      <data key="d2">82adde9844cf0b1ccd052f91b304fb6e</data>
      <data key="d3">1819</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2215</data>
      <data key="d7">20f77c4017ec44f7bf3503d8840e07e8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENFERMEDAD DE WILSON">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Enfermedad de Wilson, also known as Wilson's disease, is a rare genetic disorder related to copper metabolism, first described in humans in 1912. It is caused by mutations in the ATP7B gene, leading to excessive accumulation of copper in the body. This accumulation primarily affects the liver and the central nervous system, but it can also impact other organs such as the kidneys and cornea. The disorder is inherited in an autosomal recessive manner, meaning that both parents must carry the gene mutation for a child to be affected.

Individuals with Enfermedad de Wilson may experience a range of health complications, including liver disease, neurological symptoms, and systemic effects, with symptoms often appearing in individuals under 40 years of age. Dietary control of copper intake is essential for managing the condition and preventing serious health issues. The disorder is characterized by decreased biliary excretion of copper, which contributes to its harmful buildup in the body. Overall, Enfermedad de Wilson is a significant health concern that requires careful monitoring and management to mitigate its effects.</data>
      <data key="d2">071b24f95071bec88fffa3ee7cbcde41,232591ff32b33e56e95717d4582a5903,4216916ebab948061d2dca4afe7e1e84,5310010ed05fb1b56f667b44eb14106d,66bc37c9c52956b468bccf2ef89662de,71dd228326087dc7aaa0d310d49b6f42,78482a90281ee54a4aac11bb36bd6e36,82adde9844cf0b1ccd052f91b304fb6e,85df619f6d535bbe5c0485f540242903,d6bd9fbceb166872651dd2deade92849,d750228cb05d1651c8df327308bdc550,ea816809115cc37c571bd36191042123,ed4179754d9e9ed3a2971c762ec54390</data>
      <data key="d3">1792</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">41</data>
      <data key="d6">2216</data>
      <data key="d7">892f8d11d62344ca989c84e56345a3ad</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HEMOCROMATOSIS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Hemochromatosis is a medical condition characterized by excessive iron absorption and accumulation in the body, which can lead to significant health issues. This iron overload primarily affects various organs, including the liver, and can result in severe complications such as liver damage and endocrine dysfunction. The condition may also lead to dysfunction of the parathyroid glands, potentially causing hypoparathyroidism and hypocalcemia. Hemochromatosis can be caused by genetic factors and is often associated with enzymatic deficiencies that result in pathological iron deposits. To manage the condition and prevent complications, dietary control of iron intake is essential. Additionally, it has been noted that hemochromatosis may be related to elevated copper levels in some cases.</data>
      <data key="d2">18f04b0302d49a339210d54d9cc55cd3,1d2e960cc2c2980890a657d98fab39b6,232591ff32b33e56e95717d4582a5903,2eeab2238556165bbea897f24c5b1c7a,41a33a13610bbae73208f0d201781724,45921035725d6b94d451399b0dac4079,49058309baf3bb1fadf8956dd9ed0e89,5cab61b11d03ef705141159d100ae7c5,66bc37c9c52956b468bccf2ef89662de,764b89f13f6ebf12ba0d0ec4a73d2c68,82adde9844cf0b1ccd052f91b304fb6e</data>
      <data key="d3">1816</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
