    <node id="CIRROSIS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Cirrhosis is a late-stage liver disease characterized by extensive scarring (fibrosis) of the liver, resulting from various liver diseases and conditions, including hepatitis, chronic alcoholism, and hereditary hemochromatosis. This chronic condition can develop from prolonged liver damage and is often exacerbated by complications related to parenteral nutrition, particularly after hepatic steatosis. 

Cirrhosis leads to significant complications, such as hepatic encephalopathy, fluid retention, hyponatremia, and severe nutritional deficiencies, which are critical considerations in the nutritional assessment of affected patients. The disease is also associated with metabolic complications and can result from the accumulation of copper in the liver, as seen in conditions like Wilson's disease. 

Overall, cirrhosis is a severe liver condition that not only impacts liver function but also poses serious risks to the nutritional status and overall health of individuals, making it a significant factor in the prognosis of liver disease.</data>
      <data key="d2">036e8182deab774d39f06cd811738c9b,1290ddd67bed33472ff31398d8699503,1911ce26c118381473eccb438875b29c,232591ff32b33e56e95717d4582a5903,5310010ed05fb1b56f667b44eb14106d,5341a1454aa3fed0eab02f9ce7fea5d1,9480cb55d00db88cfe1decdf011cb41f,a3d834f1d854ecde12e808ae924b8eb0,b4b789f8b1484fea88d0b5c58dfde854,ea1d430dfc773b669eec18759441b31a,ecc0b96af9066f424f1c114102063682,f7b74dc42fea9d33e4675a41e15975ab,fa0165b0420f4c761829a08a3ab08508,fe69a5b07baa1ca7328e65ff39f7719a</data>
