    </node>
    <node id="NUTRICI&#211;N ORAL">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">"NUTRICI&#211;N ORAL" refers to the method of feeding that involves the intake of food and liquids through the mouth. This approach is the most common form of nutrition for hospitalized patients and is essential for improving the nutritional status of individuals, particularly after surgeries such as those for gastric cancer. While it is generally the preferred method of nutrient intake, there are circumstances where it may be challenging, such as in cases of acute pancreatitis or liver disease, where the ability to consume food orally can be compromised. Additionally, oral nutrition is recommended for patients with advanced gastric cancer when feasible, and it plays a critical role in the recovery process following liver transplantation. For patients with chronic kidney disease, oral nutrition is aimed at optimizing dietary intake to support their health. Overall, "NUTRICI&#211;N ORAL" is a vital component of patient care, facilitating recovery and maintaining nutritional health across various medical conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">3159307e5633698722f72fde1e82702f,3437d6cdf014282ec447fc7f4de92cab,70a9e916b8c55784c53828958c76eb09,88dcf64f61e33adc3702b1ec401e2d4e,a7baff5fb024c516ea6ae8c236dc945d,b86f20e3d8bb0128635881d88ba6bdfa,bf76380d2417653bab17a6a9b91d85bc,d59761afc53676cf5d1fe62616f0d7d7</data>
