    <node id="NUTRICION ENTERAL">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">NUTRICION ENTERAL refers to a form of artificial nutrition that is utilized primarily as a treatment for inflammatory bowel disease, particularly in cases of malnutrition. It is recommended to support the adaptation of the remaining intestine following extensive intestinal resection. This approach ensures that patients receive the necessary nutrients directly into the gastrointestinal tract, facilitating recovery and improving overall health outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d2">d147df5b06fa62d87b7edd3f20dcd246,d66daca6ec5ffce0fc73bce55a3c0ff0</data>
      <data key="d3">669</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">2239</data>
      <data key="d7">9c3578d450a9441093278ce81dffc460</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NUTRICION PARENTERAL">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">NUTRICION PARENTERAL, or parenteral nutrition, refers to the intravenous administration of nutrients, which allows for the delivery of essential dietary components directly into the bloodstream, bypassing the digestive system. This method is particularly utilized in clinical settings for patients whose energy requirements cannot be adequately met through oral or enteral routes. It is especially beneficial for individuals with significant intestinal resections or other conditions that impair nutrient absorption.</data>
      <data key="d2">cd2b394eb36bf2bba5fba6e1145cdda2,d147df5b06fa62d87b7edd3f20dcd246</data>
      <data key="d3">625</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">16</data>
      <data key="d6">2240</data>
      <data key="d7">73407068cd9b4abe835c4d3a8e4a066d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LOPERAMIDA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Loperamida, also known as loperamide, is a medication primarily used for treating diarrhea. It works by slowing down gut movement, which helps manage the condition effectively. Loperamida is commonly included in treatment regimens for patients with various types of diarrhea, including those with malabsorption syndrome. It is particularly effective for cases of mild to moderate diarrhea, making it a widely used antidiarrheal medication.</data>
      <data key="d2">28f6d6818b8f0ce3d2b8f483fe0dac6c,5cf8b1c5491f78fda9bca97f3d14c5a2,81d0f9e0238db446bd67439b8222ab32,d147df5b06fa62d87b7edd3f20dcd246,e0196a8a0ead0a27b38ddfb16a5c5772,e5838e52fbc5d3d18fc0533b71148201</data>
      <data key="d3">655</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">2241</data>
      <data key="d7">aa6f2aee82304c01b42dc6375edfbc6d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SULFATO DE CODE&#205;NA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Codeine sulfate is an alternative antidiarrheal medication that can be used in conjunction with loperamide.</data>
      <data key="d2">d147df5b06fa62d87b7edd3f20dcd246</data>
      <data key="d3">625</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2242</data>
      <data key="d7">a75c4a75eec742d0b0b6bd2a749f175e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FIBRA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">FIBRA, or dietary fiber, is a crucial dietary component that plays a significant role in promoting digestive health and overall well-being. It aids in digestion, improves bowel regularity, and is particularly recommended for managing constipation, especially in patients receiving enteral nutrition. The intake of dietary fiber is linked to a reduced risk of various health issues, including certain cancers, obesity, type 2 diabetes, and cardiovascular diseases. 

Fiber is essential in pediatric nutrition, often included in formulas to support digestion and health. It is also beneficial for individuals with hereditary fructose intolerance, as it is commonly found in foods that are otherwise restricted. Additionally, fiber can aid in the absorption of water and electrolytes, and it may help prevent diarrhea in enteral nutrition patients.

The recommended daily intake of dietary fiber is between 20 to 35 grams, which is important for preventing constipation in elderly patients and improving glycemic control and cholesterol levels. Furthermore, fiber has been associated with lower levels of uric acid and a reduced risk of hyperuricemia, making it a valuable component for individuals with diabetes. Overall, FIBRA is recognized for its protective effects against various diseases and its essential role in maintaining digestive health.</data>
      <data key="d2">01302d2656a8cbb49cb7505b3182cc63,09e9623b3facc5045e80541b1cd2cada,0bdc356a46149def2545e7939372c20d,0f51b0c02b1e8200f9a98b830621c55c,1ad59ffc3e39719e331a017fe64cd07d,2f548ddee569586dbe82ca124f2695e9,609140cabbcd0c7297a7029f5365d186,7796f48230d374753b744d8e5fb1e8ae,7ba4c7b49d445c147394ac626d055ef3,9df63e54761edd6ad6c4ad6fb83ae123,ada55dc10a07d6ad1d50199d5cb5f950,beef8f83c872431c6e371987b717e43b,c7af1c950a6caab8be5d180a2889a25a,c958206b5546e8b46ab48b3f50e60611,ca3fd82e042bcc480e80a1ae56b34507,d147df5b06fa62d87b7edd3f20dcd246,ede4d21db7723c6b72e8493236b217ad,f41688eecb31d8e55fc70e993e99c3e9</data>
