    <node id="DIETA BAJA EN FIBRA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">The "DIETA BAJA EN FIBRA" is a dietary recommendation aimed at managing symptoms in patients experiencing intestinal issues. It is particularly suggested for individuals with symptomatic intestinal strictures or during flare-ups of severe ulcerative colitis. This low-fiber diet helps alleviate discomfort and is tailored to support those with specific gastrointestinal conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">d66daca6ec5ffce0fc73bce55a3c0ff0,d9948a6c321263db8a8d220a4d079379</data>
      <data key="d3">672</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">2257</data>
      <data key="d7">eb6b9bb66cd4481cb2f5195ec901b50e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIETA BAJA EN GRASA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">The "DIETA BAJA EN GRASA" is a dietary approach that is recommended to optimize intestinal motility, particularly for patients experiencing digestive issues. This diet is especially beneficial for individuals with Crohn's disease who are dealing with steatorrhea, as it suggests a modification in the intake of triglycerides. By focusing on low-fat food options, this diet aims to alleviate symptoms and improve overall digestive health.</data>
      <data key="d2">d66daca6ec5ffce0fc73bce55a3c0ff0,d9948a6c321263db8a8d220a4d079379</data>
      <data key="d3">657</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">2258</data>
      <data key="d7">c57fee9442e2489489142279768c693e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CAQUEXIA TUMORAL">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">CAQUEXIA TUMORAL is a syndrome associated with advanced cancer, characterized by hypercatabolism and mediated by inflammatory cytokines. This condition is often irreversible and complicates nutritional treatment for affected patients. It is a multifactorial syndrome that leads to the progressive loss of skeletal muscle in cancer patients, significantly impacting their quality of life and survival rates. Despite its prevalence in oncological patients, there is limited evidence regarding the influence of nutrition on the progression of CAQUEXIA TUMORAL.</data>
      <data key="d2">5664277128c865feb0e93f9fd56a3d05,a6ccbda174f6599eeeb2e4cdb00db5f2,bf234f719a608fc6858d9a1749463539</data>
      <data key="d3">1139</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">2259</data>
      <data key="d7">5eae175ade3542ffa0570e283057518e</data>
    </node>
