    </node>
    <node id="ELEMENTOS TRAZA">
      <data key="d0">NUTRIENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Elementos Traza refers to trace elements that are essential for health and are commonly measured in nutritional assessments to evaluate micronutrient status. These trace elements play a crucial role, particularly in the context of aging, as they contribute to various physiological functions. Important nutrients within this category include copper, zinc, and selenium, which may require supplementation in higher doses, especially in patients who have experienced burns, due to their depletion in such conditions. Overall, Elementos Traza are vital components of a balanced diet and are significant for maintaining optimal health.</data>
      <data key="d2">12281055b260e7dc55fcaeeab7eefde6,86d733befbaaebe4eccbc45db498a0b0,b3bff0edc314389f2ac432a753fec9f7</data>
      <data key="d3">693</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">2306</data>
      <data key="d7">abec38d2ee2d441a9f2df0e9802baba3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CATECOLAMINAS">
      <data key="d0">NUTRIENT</data>
      <data key="d1">CATECOLAMINAS, also known as catecholamines, are hormones produced primarily by chromaffin cells in the adrenal medulla and the adrenal glands. They include key hormones such as adrenaline, noradrenaline, and dopamine. These hormones play a crucial role in the body's stress response, influencing various physiological processes, including lipid metabolism and the metabolic state of critically ill patients. 

Catecholamines are particularly significant in the context of certain medical conditions. Their hypersecretion is a characteristic feature of pheochromocytomas, which are tumors of the adrenal glands that can lead to symptoms such as hypertension. The measurement of catecholamines is often utilized in the diagnosis of pheochromocytoma. Additionally, catecholamines are released in response to hypovolemia and sympathetic stimulation, especially in patients who have suffered burns, further impacting their metabolism. They are also released during stress and can affect the metabolic state in patients with acute pancreatitis. Overall, catecholamines are vital hormones that mediate the body's response to stress and various pathological conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">25192e60a2c23bd74095f4602efeac4c,4433d2fdc6ba080efa0cf63e8603172a,5fd3de876d776b591d7dfd812aa2873c,7d4d879207b235ab3018772eea448b2a,8f5500c9a9e74d855b17365c80375df3,94ef3a3488643695a99523d5c9d1f606,a0b43d4a377fca32f55f83fd79e3d9e1,b3bff0edc314389f2ac432a753fec9f7,c77f2833180015d5264f465f51c441f3,e55f521d442f8ba50012b67d7051d39f,f5ac70db902f1361fd681d1d109828c0</data>
      <data key="d3">976</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">13</data>
      <data key="d6">2307</data>
      <data key="d7">aa7b282046374830a58d746580d7627f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLUCORTICOIDES">
      <data key="d0">NUTRIENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Glucocorticoides are a class of corticosteroids and steroid hormones that play a crucial role in the treatment of various inflammatory and autoimmune conditions, including Graves' ophthalmopathy. They are primarily produced by the adrenal cortex and are involved in the regulation of metabolism and immune response. These hormones can be secreted in response to stress, particularly in patients with burns, influencing their hypermetabolic response.

While glucocorticoids are effective in managing inflammatory conditions, their use can lead to several side effects, including osteoporosis and the development of Cushing's syndrome. Additionally, they may affect insulin resistance, particularly in cystic fibrosis patients, complicating diabetes management. In certain cases, glucocorticoids are utilized to reduce aldosterone production in conditions like hyperaldosteronism. Furthermore, they can be secreted by adrenal tumors, especially functional ones, and they exert an inhibitory effect on the secretion of adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH). Overall, glucocorticoids are significant in both therapeutic applications and physiological responses within the body.</data>
      <data key="d2">00de48c582cca92f1a59df5fceb07bfb,08a470f7ca590eb337a66093ccb5c198,18f04b0302d49a339210d54d9cc55cd3,1de72f0ea3a981289854857b691b52af,209bcca22090a109f52e26657bf3c999,28bca03cd2152cf2562d7aa16d7ced1d,7f3df1437755b60344fdffd2ef3b8a9e,b3bff0edc314389f2ac432a753fec9f7,cca243547034544596d9409f2643eac0,e55f521d442f8ba50012b67d7051d39f</data>
      <data key="d3">1331</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">2308</data>
      <data key="d7">eb4683cd05db4bc78fff671f3926cf0f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLUCAG&#211;N">
      <data key="d0">NUTRIENT</data>
      <data key="d1">GLUCAG&#211;N is a hormone that plays a crucial role in glucose metabolism by raising blood glucose levels. It is particularly significant in lipid metabolism as well. This hormone is often elevated in critically ill patients and is released in individuals who have suffered burns, contributing to the hypermetabolic response observed in such conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">0b3eea4ed6da979f796aeba1ff2ee8d9,25192e60a2c23bd74095f4602efeac4c,b3bff0edc314389f2ac432a753fec9f7,e55f521d442f8ba50012b67d7051d39f</data>
      <data key="d3">1364</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">4</data>
      <data key="d6">2309</data>
      <data key="d7">42892aaa7c1c43c89240ac2469825613</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FALLO MULTIORG&#193;NICO">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">FALLO MULTIORG&#193;NICO is a medical condition characterized by the failure of multiple organ systems. This condition can arise as a complication of severe acute pancreatitis, where the inflammatory process affects various organs. Additionally, FALLO MULTIORG&#193;NICO can also occur in patients who have suffered severe burns, leading to the impairment of multiple body systems. The condition highlights the critical nature of organ function and the interconnectedness of bodily systems in response to severe medical stressors.</data>
      <data key="d2">b3bff0edc314389f2ac432a753fec9f7,f5ac70db902f1361fd681d1d109828c0</data>
      <data key="d3">1090</data>
