    <node id="EICH">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">EICH, or Graft-versus-Host Disease (GVHD), is a significant complication that can arise following hematopoietic cell transplantation, including stem cell transplants. This condition manifests in both acute and chronic forms and occurs when the transplanted immune cells (the graft) attack the recipient's (the host's) tissues. EICH can lead to various health issues, affecting the patient's metabolism and overall well-being.

Patients experiencing EICH may require specific dietary considerations, particularly those undergoing treatments such as hematopoietic cell transplants. The condition can be influenced by factors such as mucositis and the administration of glutamine treatment. It is crucial for healthcare providers to monitor and manage the nutritional needs of patients with EICH to support their recovery and mitigate complications.

In summary, EICH is a serious complication of hematopoietic stem cell transplantation that necessitates careful attention to both medical and nutritional management to enhance patient outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d2">084639519c323a72fcf1322c38efd173,17fc469cf1ddbb8fa68bad4446bd65e6,546d2f3d8a308b461c44f6b0e1351ee6,61890d3cb54fb752b95a4dfad8db8bb6,81bb0be389c10331fb2a5568b6c379dc,84aebe8ee79b67a8fe16d40b6c07331d,86a4647fb1fa580f7b72aa4833965ecb,af40d7a664dbfc9c14307a26f52b9bff,c7577b8eccf83ced48f5ecf36717b4e1,db0a4fc296a2fd5ecfbc4afcda444738</data>
      <data key="d3">1128</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">15</data>
      <data key="d6">2319</data>
      <data key="d7">a628ba742b334df89278ab31665f80ca</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EVOH">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">EVOH, which stands for hepatic veno-occlusive disease, is a complication that can arise following hematopoietic stem cell transplantation. This condition, known in Spanish as "Enfermedad Vascular Obstructiva del H&#237;gado," can also affect transplant patients and necessitates appropriate nutritional management.</data>
      <data key="d2">61890d3cb54fb752b95a4dfad8db8bb6,af40d7a664dbfc9c14307a26f52b9bff</data>
      <data key="d3">1127</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">2320</data>
      <data key="d7">34ad3b3135924e75a8af13e28ca9d043</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MUCOSITIS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Mucositis is an inflammatory condition affecting the mucous membranes, particularly in the digestive tract, and is commonly induced by chemoradiotherapy in cancer patients. This condition is characterized by inflammation and ulceration of the mucous membranes, leading to significant complications such as dysphagia and difficulties with nutritional intake. It is reported that mucositis has a high incidence rate, occurring in approximately 70% of patients undergoing intensive chemotherapy treatments. 

The condition can severely impact a patient's ability to eat, making nutritional intervention necessary. Mucositis is especially prevalent among patients receiving high-dose chemotherapy and those undergoing radiotherapy, including those who have undergone hematopoietic cell transplants. 

Management strategies for mucositis may include the administration of oral glutamine, which has been shown to reduce the duration and severity of the condition. Overall, mucositis is a painful and challenging side effect of cancer treatments that requires careful attention to ensure adequate nutrition and patient comfort.</data>
      <data key="d2">08d8220e35364eb95bacafae7dd2953b,2bbe847617971cacfe15b2c970de2d21,395385fad73fd5b7ed9eda114b482624,40dcfaa6144c6424068ba46fdf288dd2,546d2f3d8a308b461c44f6b0e1351ee6,5aa4da1a8d1e0b60ec7e801c7db1701c,61890d3cb54fb752b95a4dfad8db8bb6,8c198f866d8fb49a782cb57139b30d76,9562f39f9e78644164daeff3177a2f84,b2b9da64e886eb0b5a8b332d2ba9c7fb,c7577b8eccf83ced48f5ecf36717b4e1,e474ebdcd3e5392e4b2bed8434293849,e5b3805730df59cb36030c92aa33c194,eae8ddef005974a2ffe71cf7c61b0634,f333e5a9af59dd8504607b2559ac35dd</data>
      <data key="d3">1086</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">17</data>
      <data key="d6">2321</data>
      <data key="d7">c41f7831b08d41799d3f72cf4b42e4fd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENFERMEDAD INJERTO CONTRA HU&#201;SPED">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Graft-versus-host disease (EICH), also known as "ENFERMEDAD INJERTO CONTRA HU&#201;SPED," is a condition that arises in allogeneic transplants, where donor cells attack the recipient's body. This disease primarily affects the skin, liver, and intestines, with an incidence rate ranging from 30% to 40%. It is a significant complication following hematopoietic stem cell transplantation (TPH), highlighting the challenges associated with immune responses in transplant medicine.</data>
      <data key="d2">61890d3cb54fb752b95a4dfad8db8bb6,b2b9da64e886eb0b5a8b332d2ba9c7fb</data>
      <data key="d3">1101</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2322</data>
      <data key="d7">8d8ae1980617477e969e448ade723d93</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENFERMEDAD VENOOCLOSIVA HEP&#193;TICA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Hepatic veno-occlusive disease (EVOH), a complication that can occur after hematopoietic stem cell transplantation, affecting the liver.</data>
      <data key="d2">61890d3cb54fb752b95a4dfad8db8bb6</data>
