    <node id="APLASIA MEDULAR">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Condici&#243;n que se presenta en el postrasplante inmediato, relacionada con la reducci&#243;n de c&#233;lulas sangu&#237;neas.</data>
      <data key="d2">b2b9da64e886eb0b5a8b332d2ba9c7fb</data>
      <data key="d3">1086</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2329</data>
      <data key="d7">5cfaed45a4a24298b7c3edca7b7950ec</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INFECCIONES">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">The entity "INFECCIONES" encompasses a range of complications that can significantly impact patient health across various medical contexts. Infections are particularly prevalent in patients undergoing different phases of transplantation, especially those who have received hematopoietic cell transplants, due to the immunosuppression associated with these procedures. Additionally, infections are a common concern in critically ill patients, where they can lead to increased morbidity and mortality.

Patients with specific conditions, such as neutropenia&#8212;especially in cases of type Ib glycogen storage disease&#8212;are at a heightened risk for infections. The elderly population, particularly those who are malnourished, also face significant health risks from infections. Furthermore, infections can complicate the health of patients with chronic liver diseases and are frequently associated with catheter use, leading to serious health issues.

In surgical patients, infections are a common complication that can hinder recovery and are often evaluated in studies related to nutritional support. Infections can also contribute to the formation of intestinal fistulas, complicating treatment and recovery processes. Moreover, nutritional requirements may be altered in patients with conditions like cerebrovascular disease, which can further influence their susceptibility to infections. 

Overall, infections represent a critical health challenge across various patient populations, necessitating careful management and preventive strategies to mitigate their impact on health outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d2">0f965e7f43fe2ea0144b4b16715a04ba,25192e60a2c23bd74095f4602efeac4c,4015c8a1a16c5c93f3b853d9cac57bf4,4dff41ef8e80b9381bc674195671c430,71dd228326087dc7aaa0d310d49b6f42,b2b9da64e886eb0b5a8b332d2ba9c7fb,b6180d67d6fdf45ff73aa2a63a8e3f74,bd3be91ff178508497a810f873861eca,be7eb01a6daf673db6a127f66c79c96c,e34642244b0c7f309ab1c1be1e81da31,eb8877823f796fc9a02f5103dc4650cc,fe3ebcedac0857680bcc85bb58dcb3e1</data>
      <data key="d3">1101</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">2330</data>
      <data key="d7">50e90e53ea734b189b180b4c1aed5098</data>
    </node>
    <node id="REACCIONES ADVERSAS MEDICAMENTOSAS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Efectos secundarios que pueden ocurrir en pacientes postrasplante debido a la medicaci&#243;n.</data>
      <data key="d2">b2b9da64e886eb0b5a8b332d2ba9c7fb</data>
      <data key="d3">1130</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2331</data>
      <data key="d7">09ee44ebb9704b8ead44d6fc66fdfa41</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALTERACIONES METAB&#211;LICAS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">ALTERACIONES METAB&#211;LICAS se refiere a cambios en el metabolismo que pueden ocurrir en pacientes trasplantados, as&#237; como en aquellos con enfermedad hep&#225;tica cr&#243;nica. Estas alteraciones pueden afectar la salud general de los pacientes, impactando espec&#237;ficamente en el manejo de la glucosa, prote&#237;nas y l&#237;pidos en el organismo.</data>
      <data key="d2">1290ddd67bed33472ff31398d8699503,b2b9da64e886eb0b5a8b332d2ba9c7fb</data>
      <data key="d3">1788</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">2332</data>
      <data key="d7">6c31b2f7726d44d68028aa6414736db6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EICH CR&#211;NICA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">EICH CR&#211;NICA, o enfermedad injerto contra hu&#233;sped cr&#243;nica, es una forma prolongada de la enfermedad que puede manifestarse en el postrasplante tard&#237;o. Esta condici&#243;n aparece generalmente despu&#233;s del d&#237;a 100 del trasplante y se caracteriza por la aparici&#243;n de infecciones recurrentes y afectaciones en m&#250;ltiples &#243;rganos. Debido a su naturaleza prolongada, la EICH cr&#243;nica requiere un manejo m&#233;dico continuo y cuidadoso para abordar sus complicaciones y mejorar la calidad de vida del paciente.</data>
      <data key="d2">5b26dc0fdf0ff94c225f96626b785972,b2b9da64e886eb0b5a8b332d2ba9c7fb</data>
      <data key="d3">1097</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">5</data>
      <data key="d6">2333</data>
      <data key="d7">9ef619f12d3348be963e1c40df8aced2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRASPLANTE">
      <data key="d0" />
      <data key="d1">The term "TRASPLANTE" refers to various medical procedures involving the transfer of cells or organs to treat serious health conditions. One specific type is the hematopoietic cell transplant, which involves the transfer of stem cells to address diseases such as leukemia and other hematological disorders. Additionally, transplant procedures can lead to complications that necessitate specialized nutritional support for patients. Furthermore, transplantation may also be required for individuals suffering from severe liver damage, such as that caused by Wilson's disease. Overall, "TRASPLANTE" encompasses a range of surgical interventions aimed at improving health outcomes for patients with critical medical conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">084639519c323a72fcf1322c38efd173,3898563966943dbfc8a0f6e84bc627b1,b2b9da64e886eb0b5a8b332d2ba9c7fb,beaa66c30582232126ce2c762a9c6bdb</data>
      <data key="d3">1130</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">12</data>
      <data key="d6">2334</data>
      <data key="d7">d4ee6dce56ba4804a08b5735c350ae9e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CICLOFOSFAMIDA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Ciclofosfamida is a medication that serves multiple purposes in medical treatment. It is primarily recognized as an immunosuppressive drug, which is utilized in clinical trials to address inflammatory responses in brain lesions of patients with Adrenoleukodystrophy (ALD). Additionally, ciclofosfamida is commonly used in oncological treatments. However, it is important for patients to take this medication with meals to minimize gastrointestinal discomfort. It is also noteworthy that ciclofosfamida can lead to malabsorption and malnutrition in patients, highlighting the need for careful monitoring during its use.</data>
      <data key="d2">04f886244ff7d736eb270f175d6b120e,0745ce31d3922cbba1497dbe1d2845de,af40d7a664dbfc9c14307a26f52b9bff</data>
      <data key="d3">1099</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">2335</data>
      <data key="d7">dfa90af635f748a2b2b0fc8236aa0cab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CICLOSPORINA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Ciclosporina is an immunosuppressive medication that is primarily used in the treatment of complications following organ transplants. It has been tested for its efficacy in treating alcoholic liver disease (ALD) but has not shown significant clinical benefits. The drug can lead to various side effects, including diarrhea and neurological issues, which may improve upon discontinuation of the medication. Additionally, ciclosporina can contribute to the development of hypomagnesemia in patients undergoing chemotherapy and may cause alterations in lipid metabolism. It is important to note that the levels of ciclosporina in the body can be increased by the consumption of grapefruit juice, and the medication may also interact with weight loss drugs.</data>
      <data key="d2">0745ce31d3922cbba1497dbe1d2845de,0c6c9eb4aba5c4642a75d76bd7ea08ba,44f263c31f91c1bc594cbb28bd397c89,488970234520652b063db88e8346031c,4f1c852e5d25eed4db322b75624d3e2b,84aebe8ee79b67a8fe16d40b6c07331d,86a4647fb1fa580f7b72aa4833965ecb,af40d7a664dbfc9c14307a26f52b9bff</data>
      <data key="d3">1099</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">2336</data>
      <data key="d7">2eca9ac8acbc49f1a2e885a25da96c95</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIPOMAGNESEMIA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Hypomagnesemia is a medical condition characterized by insufficient levels of magnesium in the body, specifically defined as a plasma magnesium concentration lower than 1.5 mEq/l. This condition is often associated with various factors, including alcohol abuse, severe malnutrition, and nutritional deficiencies. It has been observed in a percentage of patients, particularly those undergoing treatment with certain medications such as cisplatin and cyclosporine, which can adversely affect their overall health. Additionally, hypomagnesemia is linked to an increased risk of mortality in hospitalized patients. In some cases, patients diagnosed with hypomagnesemia have been treated with magnesium supplements to address the deficiency.</data>
      <data key="d2">0c6c9eb4aba5c4642a75d76bd7ea08ba,214cb2b8ddec52ff69849d87feb8e141,814d394c907e59b29a5f0d9e82aed423,85ee651c191c3b0c5fe786fe2e0718a4,863198ceabd6e1c11a0d0944c676737c,af40d7a664dbfc9c14307a26f52b9bff,ed9dfe1b1e334597565187840983df05</data>
      <data key="d3">612</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">21</data>
      <data key="d6">2337</data>
      <data key="d7">e620ff805bee4ce5ac95e5f48bfaf1a7</data>
    </node>
