    </node>
    <node id="PEG">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">PEG, or percutaneous endoscopic gastrostomy, is a medical procedure designed to facilitate nutrition for patients who are unable to eat adequately by mouth. This method involves placing a feeding tube directly into the stomach, allowing for long-term nutritional support. PEG is particularly beneficial for individuals requiring enteral nutrition, including those with specific medical conditions such as diabetes and MELAS syndrome, where managing nutritional status is crucial. Overall, PEG serves as an essential treatment tool for providing necessary nutrition to patients who cannot consume food orally.</data>
      <data key="d2">02d838eb7d60f4fe558e304b6b6933e8,242881af1a0439ab16c10b788cff01fd,2d50b3b46d83d6e824edc64e142cbf8a,4b0d0238a19e23ffee592c9d0817e226,9c43759c41fe226a2702de300b9e580a,a089d87928ddde83e1945eece488d3f5,beaa66c30582232126ce2c762a9c6bdb,e3370b71146ad5063d6b1e9c3d8a3bd2</data>
      <data key="d3">1178</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">5</data>
      <data key="d6">2341</data>
      <data key="d7">41845e292c5f4fccbfd3dc78302e91b7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TROMBOPENIA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">A condition that increases the risk of bleeding, affecting the placement of feeding tubes</data>
      <data key="d2">beaa66c30582232126ce2c762a9c6bdb</data>
      <data key="d3">1080</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2342</data>
      <data key="d7">33c7bbf48f8143ab883b35889bb9ab67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NA&#218;SEAS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">NA&#218;SEAS refers to the sensation of needing to vomit, which is a common symptom experienced by patients with cancer. This condition can significantly limit their intake of food, thereby impacting their overall nutritional health. Nausea is not only a distressing symptom but also complicates the administration of enteral nutrition, making it a critical concern in the management of patients undergoing treatment. As a side effect of various medications, nausea can further exacerbate the challenges faced by these patients in maintaining adequate nutrition.</data>
      <data key="d2">1ff94f3fcc6eff16c2fdae5eb9b3aefa,6c8b41c09b1ada129f0908d6e5d18185,9562f39f9e78644164daeff3177a2f84,beaa66c30582232126ce2c762a9c6bdb</data>
      <data key="d3">1092</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">2343</data>
      <data key="d7">0109581d1ee140e4931e09f39c8601f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&#205;LEO">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">&#205;LEO refers to the ileum, which is the final part of the small intestine. This section plays a crucial role in the absorption of various nutrients, including thyroid hormone. Additionally, the term "ileus" is associated with a condition that can complicate the administration of enteral nutrition, indicating that issues in the ileum can affect nutritional intake and absorption. Thus, the ileum is significant not only for its role in nutrient absorption but also for its potential complications in medical conditions such as ileus.</data>
      <data key="d2">be2de576112f22070e8ef8a5cb9e1017,beaa66c30582232126ce2c762a9c6bdb</data>
      <data key="d3">1351</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">2344</data>
      <data key="d7">adaf4012ce14498db0a4fc6f0fb83976</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HLA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">HLA, or Human Leukocyte Antigen, is a crucial system of proteins and a group of genes located on chromosome 6 that plays a significant role in the immune system. It is essential for determining compatibility between donors and recipients in organ transplants, ensuring that the body can accept the transplanted tissue. Additionally, HLA is associated with the body's immune response and has implications for susceptibility to various conditions, including autoimmune diseases such as Type 1 Diabetes and allergies, particularly food allergies. The HLA system is characterized by a high frequency of genetic recombination, which is relevant in the context of genetic inheritance, including conditions like congenital adrenal hyperplasia. Overall, HLA is integral to both transplant medicine and the understanding of immune-related health issues.</data>
      <data key="d2">095d265c06452e52ccbfbc871a19a899,39a7166ca777be46a1613c54a746e2b8,beaa66c30582232126ce2c762a9c6bdb,db0a4fc296a2fd5ecfbc4afcda444738,ed9fbb82a9162d601f8370ef97810e54</data>
      <data key="d3">1129</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">2345</data>
      <data key="d7">758fce3bf01746f595e99817bdb107b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FIBROSIS QUISTICA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Fibrosis qu&#237;stica (Cystic fibrosis) is a genetic disorder that affects the lungs and digestive system, requiring careful nutritional management.</data>
      <data key="d2">ce8f5f24dbbf8624e49a6f85c2ad0d04</data>
      <data key="d3">761</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">2346</data>
      <data key="d7">1008b944f7f24b77a29598db247c8704</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NUTRICION EN FIBROSIS QUISTICA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Nutrition in cystic fibrosis focuses on maintaining adequate nutritional status to improve disease course and quality of life.</data>
      <data key="d2">ce8f5f24dbbf8624e49a6f85c2ad0d04</data>
      <data key="d3">761</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2347</data>
      <data key="d7">dce113ecbddb480389dee9e97dd55d12</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OMEGA 3">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Omega 3 is a type of fatty acid that has been extensively studied for its nutritional benefits. It is known for its anti-inflammatory properties, which may contribute to managing symptoms associated with cancer cachexia. However, while there is some evidence supporting its benefits, the use of Omega 3 in specific contexts lacks clear conclusions, indicating that further research may be needed to fully understand its effects and applications.</data>
      <data key="d2">bb209dcfffac9c89dae0ad4ed2908d85,ce8f5f24dbbf8624e49a6f85c2ad0d04</data>
      <data key="d3">1208</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">2348</data>
      <data key="d7">b4c2f057ae584433ae608bfd7af2145d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VALORACION NUTRICIONAL">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">VALORACION NUTRICIONAL refers to the comprehensive process of assessing an individual's nutritional status, which is essential for identifying and managing various health conditions. This assessment is particularly important in specific populations, such as patients with cystic fibrosis, where it involves evaluating dietary intake, clinical status, and biochemical markers to monitor overall health. Additionally, nutritional assessment plays a crucial role in the care of frail elderly individuals, helping to evaluate their nutritional needs and status. Overall, VALORACION NUTRICIONAL is vital for addressing issues like sarcopenia and frailty, ensuring that appropriate interventions can be implemented to improve health outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d2">56da8c0bffe9c7811cc9790d767f4972,6458fe5a14762d5f8c1561f80acb844a,ce8f5f24dbbf8624e49a6f85c2ad0d04</data>
      <data key="d3">762</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">2349</data>
      <data key="d7">b7806e2c32a94104b368b9a5257d68a9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HISTORIA DIET&#201;TICA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">The "HISTORIA DIET&#201;TICA" is a comprehensive method used to assess an individual's dietary habits over time. It involves conducting extensive interviews to gather detailed information about both current and past dietary practices. This qualitative assessment is particularly important in specific populations, such as patients with cystic fibrosis, where it is conducted every three months to evaluate nutritional intake. The Historia Diet&#233;tica is often compared with other dietary assessment methods, highlighting its role in providing a thorough understanding of habitual food intake.</data>
      <data key="d2">0c83fbbd4b26fbfe03a1df6d36025796,4681d149c863ed06c99b19443f841abd,a4d8922d5e65304a84f8a61999f77933,ce8f5f24dbbf8624e49a6f85c2ad0d04,f14ec70726a24a574ae5d437903fddb8</data>
      <data key="d3">1476</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">2350</data>
      <data key="d7">7a5a6fe3ccf44d12ae200a390609e1e5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VALORACION CL&#205;NICA Y ANTROPOM&#201;TRICA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Clinical and anthropometric assessment is performed every three months to monitor growth and nutritional status in cystic fibrosis patients.</data>
      <data key="d2">ce8f5f24dbbf8624e49a6f85c2ad0d04</data>
      <data key="d3">762</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2351</data>
      <data key="d7">ce6ebc4dfef7442d84abaaf061087e45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VALORACION HEMATOL&#211;GICA Y BIOQU&#205;MICA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Hematological and biochemical assessment is conducted annually to evaluate various health markers in cystic fibrosis patients.</data>
      <data key="d2">ce8f5f24dbbf8624e49a6f85c2ad0d04</data>
      <data key="d3">762</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2352</data>
      <data key="d7">39e3c8e1cf314da4a91fed8dc1fed896</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GASTO ENERG&#201;TICO EN REPOSO">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">The "GASTO ENERG&#201;TICO EN REPOSO" refers to the amount of energy that the body utilizes while at rest. This expenditure can vary among individuals, particularly in patients with specific health conditions such as cancer. The resting energy expenditure (GER) is essential for understanding the energy requirements of the body in a resting state and can be calculated using specific formulas. This calculation is particularly important for determining the energy needs of patients, including those with conditions like cystic fibrosis, where accurate assessment of energy expenditure is crucial for effective management and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d2">148443357888793655b663333268775a,ce8f5f24dbbf8624e49a6f85c2ad0d04,d667e6abc204bbfa6aba210705db6d56</data>
      <data key="d3">761</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">2353</data>
      <data key="d7">0f5dce381358491ca8c56ae65592dc23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FUNCION PULMONAR">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Pulmonary function is assessed to determine the severity of respiratory issues in cystic fibrosis patients, influencing nutritional needs.</data>
      <data key="d2">ce8f5f24dbbf8624e49a6f85c2ad0d04</data>
      <data key="d3">761</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2354</data>
      <data key="d7">79607533afca49d6b653c99c8e66b6d8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DENSITOMETRIA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Densitometry is a method used to assess bone density and body composition, important in the nutritional evaluation of cystic fibrosis patients.</data>
      <data key="d2">ce8f5f24dbbf8624e49a6f85c2ad0d04</data>
      <data key="d3">762</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2355</data>
      <data key="d7">9cecd8c789c14af4bfb65da5b9a63171</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANTIOXIDANTS">
      <data key="d0" />
      <data key="d1">Antioxidants are compounds found in foods that may reduce the incidence of cardiovascular disease. However, the effectiveness of antioxidants when taken as supplements is a subject of debate.</data>
      <data key="d2">a826c03dcc41919d172b2e1ae8eeceb2,ce8f5f24dbbf8624e49a6f85c2ad0d04</data>
      <data key="d3">1725</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">2356</data>
      <data key="d7">2497ef21c51144a6a8d4883f29e1c877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FQ">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">FQ refers to Cystic Fibrosis, also known as Fibrosis Cystic or Fibrosis Qu&#237;stica, which is a genetic disorder that significantly impacts the lungs and digestive system. This condition is characterized by the production of thick and sticky mucus, which can lead to severe respiratory issues and nutritional challenges. Individuals with cystic fibrosis require careful monitoring and treatment of various health parameters, including detailed dietary assessments to manage their nutritional status effectively. The disorder can result in complications such as malnutrition, necessitating specific dietary management and supplementation. Additionally, there are established guidelines that provide consensus on the nutritional needs of patients with cystic fibrosis, emphasizing the importance of tailored dietary strategies to support their health.</data>
      <data key="d2">8efd8d610de6aced043032198b436f3f,9704c66c0d725d94e3dbd8f09440ed27,add3641ae46ac521317bb4c12922895a,d76cb2d50e736da37be7fe2a415a7281,dc7539b4d637d6dd09963c2564f4f084,f1f6ad756644e84b6caa8e4e3adb94da,f6135205f57ce2216ab62038ac34a2bb</data>
      <data key="d3">1038</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">38</data>
      <data key="d6">2357</data>
      <data key="d7">702e0100173148b09c0b03d0ee9150a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KREON">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Kreon is a medication that the patient is taking to assist in the digestion of food. It is specifically used to provide pancreatic enzymes to individuals with exocrine pancreatic insufficiency, helping to improve their digestive processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">5cf8b1c5491f78fda9bca97f3d14c5a2,f6135205f57ce2216ab62038ac34a2bb</data>
      <data key="d3">1043</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">2358</data>
      <data key="d7">812757a56ade488faad77c8be4e4a95d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VITAMINS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Vitamins are essential organic compounds that play a crucial role in normal growth and nutrition for both children and adults. They are vital nutrients that may require supplementation in specific circumstances, such as after bariatric surgery, where reduced fat intake can affect absorption. Certain vitamins, particularly lipid-soluble ones like vitamin K, can interact with anticoagulants, exhibiting antagonistic effects, while vitamins A, D, and E may have agonistic effects.

In clinical settings, vitamins are often included in nutritional solutions to meet the daily requirements of patients, especially those who are critically ill or have specific health conditions. For instance, vitamins B1, C, D, and E are recommended for supplementation in burned patients to support healing and reduce oxidative stress. Additionally, individuals with diabetes, particularly the elderly, may require certain vitamins to address potential deficiencies.

Vitamins are also included in dietary reference intakes for various age groups, ensuring that both children and adults receive adequate nutrition. In cases of malabsorption, such as in individuals with celiac disease, certain vitamins may need to be supplemented to prevent deficiencies. Overall, vitamins are integral to maintaining health and supporting recovery in various medical contexts.</data>
      <data key="d2">27f06efb5e9ac8bbc3f8bd31ee5a2777,33d5ce24ed9abf269f25077518d11a85,396dda5c9597828f0749f963f42142b4,3ec4c9fa90e7083cd895d95efed02f84,4f1c852e5d25eed4db322b75624d3e2b,814d394c907e59b29a5f0d9e82aed423,87da0f3b46604498629565db45667b3b,8d9c5b85d058d0d3cde4479b5589a7d1,ae99604da4e0a7da3a6e99e197205ab5,b46fefa9f55d92c74acad101fc4f812e,f5660be03d70d5dd506c197283dd79ae,f6135205f57ce2216ab62038ac34a2bb</data>
      <data key="d3">1608</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">15</data>
      <data key="d6">2359</data>
      <data key="d7">0e8935fab47347438b3fe9e273f9a19d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MCT">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">MCT, or Medium-Chain Triglycerides, are a type of fat recognized for their quick energy source and various health benefits. They are commonly used in enteral formulas and nutritional products, particularly for individuals with specific dietary needs, such as those with pancreatic issues or chronic pancreatitis. MCTs are easier to digest compared to long-chain triglycerides, making them suitable for patients who may struggle with fat absorption.

In addition to their use in enteral nutrition, MCTs are included in certain infant formulas to enhance fat absorption and provide essential fatty acids, which are crucial for infant growth and development. They have also been observed to improve metabolic conditions in patients with glycogen storage diseases and can help manage lactic acidosis and hypertriglyceridemia by promoting fatty acid oxidation and reducing glycolysis.

Overall, MCTs serve as a valuable dietary fat that not only provides extra calories for patients with pancreatitis but also supports weight maintenance and overall health in various clinical settings.</data>
      <data key="d2">08d66dc8414668a3ec627a1e164cfa6c,67106be274787fd7f400ce3023c33a3d,69bfdb98603d3d8c4733cb795f4c82f3,751947d2192006b89bb703587d56df99,803363886f385b089938bf122246dfa2,8366a6776a090c4e13b4746a1b23e96f,8f2e38ed25a683975b314e8e56721ad6,9a0227d0ea548fa46814e6403be885ce,9f5a8d86160d5d097bca174e2e2496c1,ede4d21db7723c6b72e8493236b217ad,f6135205f57ce2216ab62038ac34a2bb</data>
      <data key="d3">198</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">2360</data>
      <data key="d7">d76f5ffe56164306883ad11b35936771</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PANCREATIC ENZYMES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Pancreatic enzymes are essential for the digestive process, playing a crucial role in breaking down nutrients in the gastrointestinal tract. They are particularly important for individuals with conditions such as cystic fibrosis, where pancreatic insufficiency can hinder proper digestion. In these cases, pancreatic enzymes are often supplemented to aid in digestion. Additionally, these enzymes serve as treatments for patients suffering from exocrine pancreatic insufficiency, helping to improve nutrient absorption and overall digestive health.</data>
      <data key="d2">cb63c034c676fa9dbbd8e5a07dd8c279,f6135205f57ce2216ab62038ac34a2bb</data>
      <data key="d3">1043</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">2361</data>
      <data key="d7">bee9051aaf2d4276a11045ed208ebf08</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ESSENTIAL FATTY ACIDS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Essential fatty acids are crucial for health, and patients with cystic fibrosis often have deficiencies in these nutrients, particularly DHA and EPA.</data>
      <data key="d2">f6135205f57ce2216ab62038ac34a2bb</data>
      <data key="d3">1038</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">2362</data>
      <data key="d7">7d48177739fe46f9b0ee6aa96b610ca6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EICOSANOIDS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Eicosanoids are signaling molecules made from fatty acids, and patients with cystic fibrosis may have a tendency towards pro-inflammatory eicosanoids due to dietary deficiencies.</data>
      <data key="d2">f6135205f57ce2216ab62038ac34a2bb</data>
      <data key="d3">1038</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2363</data>
      <data key="d7">74202f3b59aa4424ad0b2c52e590a0f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CFRD">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Cystic Fibrosis Related Diabetes (CFRD) is a type of diabetes that develops in approximately 25% of patients with cystic fibrosis, characterized by insulin deficiency and resistance.</data>
      <data key="d2">00de48c582cca92f1a59df5fceb07bfb</data>
      <data key="d3">1051</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">5</data>
      <data key="d6">2364</data>
      <data key="d7">b60074f6d5a3447ea28fcbc42bb147c6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AMERICAN DIABETES ASSOCIATION">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">The American Diabetes Association (ADA) is a leading professional organization in the United States dedicated to diabetes research, education, and advocacy. Its primary mission is to prevent and cure diabetes while improving the lives of all individuals affected by the condition. The ADA provides comprehensive guidelines and recommendations for the management and treatment of diabetes, including criteria for diagnosing diabetes and prediabetes, as well as specific guidelines for various contexts such as pregnancy and cystic fibrosis-related diabetes.

The organization emphasizes evidence-based practices and offers clinical practice recommendations, nutrition principles, and standards of medical care in diabetes management. This includes guidelines for nutrition therapy, the use of bariatric surgery for patients with Type 2 diabetes, and management strategies for complications such as diabetic ketoacidosis, diabetic retinopathy, and diabetic neuropathy. The ADA also collaborates on guidelines for the classification and diagnosis of diabetes, including gestational diabetes and Maturity Onset Diabetes of the Young (MODY) types.

Through its research initiatives and educational resources, the American Diabetes Association aims to enhance diabetes care and promote better health outcomes for individuals living with diabetes.</data>
      <data key="d2">00de48c582cca92f1a59df5fceb07bfb,06dcefb711cbd9fdb958145cca571b83,06fe3811ad957d6841997b2a0e9be50f,19e153f7c5e2fcd13d3bf7a99298834e,1f2b31f5a0bba00ecee5e3a8fb2c83ea,265fcc321b2c0bbf0bf8200d0489e1a1,2e4409d82bf1cb293a2df7fc1a8cc9e7,34d62296f09da1a452febcc9ab8c18ad,3b21a011b73cb6fe2bb548f624a8c45c,4f6c697d19c8a4459b3accd91f111bfd,54bdd3faa067cdbb44671453ffaf4be5,58db9fa69ca87ec24d7117fea6f2fe88,5b487245068b8e8b87ffcc04970aaff4,5c11ed246e1a48261910b9b71b65014c,6a2d5ced0ad16871fec539845797e0ac,70715c061c27ab1993ef057601f5489d,762a80a4c9f24d778b79fdd596f343e3,784f8d7f49cdce2d0611be585eda7e2c,7855ffa74481ba85f44e9700279250e7,7b084f7afc5f3c66a528475ae4fa76b1,8886631425cf090457592f633ee8cae8,8bcf09481326ab5017cc3b45317538a3,a3e3a651e24883d279405241439b58bc,a4b144210b0a4b654a260ebc394de0af,a739523b87def5cdfa8fe97cdf423fe7,aa9279dacd764b478618ae36af93ad83,ac89fcaa7dcce835c0d5b9ed9ce94cea,afe0f4f37815c175f0faff06eb24a256,c98ed25e58194d76463e3553fb3f3c15,cb63c034c676fa9dbbd8e5a07dd8c279,cd2217af0cd2e70226661f038043911b,d52ef4e5955918a8a47b697fcdb4096f,e33729f8eea62e59507c132b7c2437df,e83d288a4f420cdc975112d3a63bd522,f1bd89f4a1fcc385a19f5c5323ee2f89,f836064d1a0fd1b13c5a6084a44deb7d,fe5f390fb67fd3b1ad222db3500574b9</data>
      <data key="d3">865</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">60</data>
      <data key="d6">2365</data>
      <data key="d7">7b21187514184f78af47c0b8db5738f1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UK CYSTIC FIBROSIS TRUST">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">The UK Cystic Fibrosis Trust is an organization that offers support and guidelines for managing cystic fibrosis, including recommendations for diabetes screening.</data>
      <data key="d2">00de48c582cca92f1a59df5fceb07bfb</data>
      <data key="d3">1051</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2366</data>
      <data key="d7">5ba4a377be374bb5a5dd7112d633905f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="A. RAMOS PROL">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">A. Ramos Prol is a co-author of the chapter discussing the relationship between nutrition and diabetes in cystic fibrosis patients.</data>
      <data key="d2">00de48c582cca92f1a59df5fceb07bfb</data>
      <data key="d3">1050</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2367</data>
      <data key="d7">22c1a21a19544dc9bb4c2193cb984ad3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TRATAMIENTO ENZIM&#193;TICO">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Enzymatic treatment that is not recommended to be combined with elemental or polymeric diets due to potential adverse effects on nutrient absorption.</data>
      <data key="d2">00de48c582cca92f1a59df5fceb07bfb</data>
      <data key="d3">1050</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2368</data>
      <data key="d7">2768850d196649f38673be69f27301c9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANTIBI&#211;TICOS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Antibiotics are medications that play a crucial role in the treatment of various infections and are particularly significant in specific patient populations. They can lead to side effects such as diarrhea, especially after their use has been discontinued. In patients with cystic fibrosis, antibiotics are often administered during respiratory exacerbations and can also impact their nutritional management. Additionally, antibiotics may be used prophylactically in patients with glucogenosis to prevent infections. In the context of patients with SIC, antibiotics like metronidazole are utilized to manage infections and related complications. However, it is important to note that long-term use of antibiotics can disrupt intestinal flora, potentially leading to vitamin K deficiency.</data>
      <data key="d2">00de48c582cca92f1a59df5fceb07bfb,0bdc356a46149def2545e7939372c20d,6b49a9a43dc167f62a772b262afe2bee,76287e9b94faab94755c56fead3541d7,8c87213151750706e4e87956bfc9cb88,bead99df88cb929a33cc2799a84da77e</data>
      <data key="d3">653</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">2369</data>
      <data key="d7">dd29e2be033c44b8bf4405086579f8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NE">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">NE, or Nutrici&#243;n Enteral, refers to a method of providing nutrition directly through the gastrointestinal tract via a tube. This approach is particularly beneficial for patients who cannot eat by mouth due to various medical conditions, including neurological impairments that affect swallowing. NE encompasses several specific applications, such as nocturnal enteral nutrition, which delivers nutrition during the night, and nutritional enteral feeding, which is often used in severe cases like pancreatitis.

The method is designed to meet the caloric requirements of patients and is commonly utilized in critical care settings. It is less associated with complications such as hyponatremia compared to parenteral nutrition, making it a preferred option when the digestive tract is functional. Nutritional formulas used in NE are especially important in oncology settings and for patients with swallowing difficulties.

Overall, NE serves as a crucial nutritional support system, ensuring that patients receive the necessary nutrients directly into their stomach or intestines when oral intake is not possible.</data>
      <data key="d2">01704aec4089b2f0f24dd0f0841b5ecf,0b3eea4ed6da979f796aeba1ff2ee8d9,178140d0d29364593546d7d21393f2c6,242881af1a0439ab16c10b788cff01fd,2cc103269ed63bac1a1b5cb76eab1916,4681f41ebd69a732f7a8be6c2ed0f51a,46bc44144e19709e2d7f9838c9c54ccf,49d3f1ca700a7974db89e684f3744a40,4ce0bfcd129ba597d70d90d2120c1996,500e56f18da6e8b6ed0e1eed4af927e9,5aa4da1a8d1e0b60ec7e801c7db1701c,67106be274787fd7f400ce3023c33a3d,7469cc60475806b519e91abe2796aead,8413d4414414c5cb139198857cce327a,94df5002f2221058a9bb77f456a50e61,bfdbf41efa08067d5ae1b1a67830affd,c07baf818737d704c290ca0f5eefd30f,dc7539b4d637d6dd09963c2564f4f084,e5a3c206a42a0c2ba4df914b54591a3b,e98937544f56006f5d327136c593c969,fa0165b0420f4c761829a08a3ab08508,fee4d9611c98c81859bccdb4ded03622</data>
      <data key="d3">1111</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">24</data>
      <data key="d6">2370</data>
      <data key="d7">e878f492f6c64e348f50095c12c86393</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NP">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">NP refers to Nutritional Parenteral, a method of providing nutrition intravenously to patients who cannot tolerate enteral feeding, effectively bypassing the gastrointestinal tract. This approach is particularly relevant for patient groups, including those who have undergone abdominal surgery related to digestive neoplasia, and may experience complications such as nausea and pain. In clinical settings, NP has been studied in relation to various factors, including its correlation with hospital stay duration in patients with hyponatremia. Nutritional practices and guidelines for NP emphasize the use of different lipid sources and amino acids to optimize patient care, especially in critical care environments. Overall, NP serves as a vital nutritional support system for patients who are unable to receive adequate nutrition through traditional means.</data>
      <data key="d2">01704aec4089b2f0f24dd0f0841b5ecf,178140d0d29364593546d7d21393f2c6,2cc103269ed63bac1a1b5cb76eab1916,3d5e0a3ef45c7ba15063791545dc4826,500e56f18da6e8b6ed0e1eed4af927e9,5cf8b1c5491f78fda9bca97f3d14c5a2,67106be274787fd7f400ce3023c33a3d,8413d4414414c5cb139198857cce327a,a687eee1e8bfd2d98ce40a14628dd855,c07baf818737d704c290ca0f5eefd30f,e5838e52fbc5d3d18fc0533b71148201,e5a3c206a42a0c2ba4df914b54591a3b,fa0165b0420f4c761829a08a3ab08508,fee4d9611c98c81859bccdb4ded03622</data>
      <data key="d3">1111</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">14</data>
      <data key="d6">2371</data>
      <data key="d7">611aa574921b46149b37e9599d15b29c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ELECTROLYTES">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Electrolytes are essential minerals in the body that carry an electric charge and play a crucial role in maintaining fluid balance and supporting various physiological functions. They are vital for hydration, muscle function, and overall bodily operations. Electrolytes, such as sodium and potassium, are particularly important and need to be monitored in patients receiving nutritional support, including those with diabetes, as their levels can vary based on health status. These minerals are often added to nutritional solutions to ensure that individuals maintain proper electrolyte levels for optimal health.</data>
      <data key="d2">4ada21f24df8df18dbc05f5619c0a368,814d394c907e59b29a5f0d9e82aed423,8413d4414414c5cb139198857cce327a,87da0f3b46604498629565db45667b3b,eeb6e04f12698ee82912bdf775d79381</data>
      <data key="d3">1260</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">5</data>
      <data key="d6">2372</data>
      <data key="d7">108958b6a37c48dfa5bc46daf96e8218</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYPERGLYCEMIA">
      <data key="d0" />
      <data key="d1">Hyperglycemia is a medical condition characterized by abnormally high blood glucose levels, often seen in individuals with diabetes when insulin is insufficient. It is marked by an excessive amount of glucose in the blood, which can lead to serious health complications if not managed properly. This condition is particularly concerning for diabetic patients, as well as for those receiving nutritional support, such as total parenteral nutrition, where hyperglycemia can arise as a complication due to excessive glucose infusion.

In hospitalized patients, hyperglycemia is associated with poor outcomes and is a significant concern in the context of nutritional management and guidelines for diabetes care. It is also relevant in pediatric intensive care, where it is linked to various outcomes in critically ill children. Additionally, hyperglycemia can occur in patients with severe pancreatitis and is discussed in relation to pregnancy by the International Association of Diabetes and Pregnancy Study Groups.

Overall, hyperglycemia requires careful dietary intervention and management to prevent potential complications, making it a critical focus in both clinical and nutritional settings.</data>
      <data key="d2">06fe3811ad957d6841997b2a0e9be50f,10bde114bd1285b3aeeec94dd4292d9e,178140d0d29364593546d7d21393f2c6,27f06efb5e9ac8bbc3f8bd31ee5a2777,46bc44144e19709e2d7f9838c9c54ccf,4c4d1882f0ac14b7c55941ec1c17b650,502bae6c907e900b95f5fe6aa963b0fc,52d93d6adba464a2397ddec01d152840,54bdd3faa067cdbb44671453ffaf4be5,64378df2be6b08b39bd652c0a2d3789c,7b084f7afc5f3c66a528475ae4fa76b1,8413d4414414c5cb139198857cce327a,a265c943c66b9cb5d09f89d40ddb995f,a66b01654a2f495559fd1a24efc0e64e,acd36e6dbcd98757c4cf33ff090764ee,e36c9b09870e14d19aa6eb7c82514499,eeb6e04f12698ee82912bdf775d79381</data>
      <data key="d3">1722</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">39</data>
      <data key="d6">2373</data>
      <data key="d7">7c851f2e0de74ef997c2f95b3dc11269</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GASTROPARESIA DIAB&#201;TICA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">A condition affecting diabetic patients that negatively impacts their tolerance to enteral nutrition, often requiring specific feeding strategies.</data>
      <data key="d2">d3dab2869ed103bbf74ecb1a4a822567</data>
      <data key="d3">1178</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2374</data>
      <data key="d7">df07ae517a384d31b02bebe8c0357ca8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="F&#211;RMULAS NUTRICIONALES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">F&#211;RMULAS NUTRICIONALES refers to specialized nutritional formulas that serve multiple purposes in dietary management. These formulas can be utilized to establish nutritional requirements in situations where indirect calorimetry is not available. Additionally, they are specifically designed for diabetic patients, incorporating modified carbohydrates and an increased fat content to better meet their dietary needs.</data>
      <data key="d2">111edb0e21c873322871e35619a1ebf5,d3dab2869ed103bbf74ecb1a4a822567</data>
      <data key="d3">1143</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
