      <data key="d6">2580</data>
      <data key="d7">ac08bfb91a484e29a1b724cd8e3e59a4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LACTANCIA MATERNA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">LACTANCIA MATERNA, or breastfeeding, is the process of feeding a baby with milk from the mother's breast. This practice is highly recommended for infants, particularly during the first six months of life, as it provides adequate nutrition and covers their iron needs, despite the milk's low iron content. Breastfeeding offers numerous health benefits for both the mother and the child, making it an essential aspect of infant care and development.</data>
      <data key="d2">2a1b909c98067bf7fc270d6f30199f96,62bb1dedc96b7530649f4c9762faab9d,658ee56030a35ceeeec05d15cfa49842,cf034f02916ec46dcba7e0f8d8e24c0c</data>
      <data key="d3">1493</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">14</data>
      <data key="d6">2581</data>
      <data key="d7">9a06bfcd92e4461aa62bf24de8af0dcf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GALACTOSEMIA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">GALACTOSEMIA is a genetic metabolic disorder that affects the body's ability to process galactose, a sugar found in milk. This condition requires special dietary considerations, particularly for infants, as it can lead to various health complications if not managed properly. Individuals with galactosemia must adhere to strict dietary restrictions and management strategies to avoid the adverse effects associated with the disorder. The inability to metabolize galactose can result in a range of clinical symptoms and complications, underscoring the importance of proper treatment and dietary management for those affected by this condition.</data>
      <data key="d2">0765b6a482d8348e9746183291e9e9bc,743649231e27012822c870ff5878263c,8f2e38ed25a683975b314e8e56721ad6,c4056d00ff7cbd7d7e3464150ea3d5aa,ce9cd0d993ab8b3995c1a78e9306bcad,cf034f02916ec46dcba7e0f8d8e24c0c,f290b280f5c5a9e2e8326fec68b6016f</data>
      <data key="d3">837</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">39</data>
      <data key="d6">2582</data>
      <data key="d7">2df4b3d353e042e98a3b69a993faa81c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIV">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">HIV, or Human Immunodeficiency Virus, is a viral infection that primarily targets the immune system, leading to a range of health complications. One of the rare complications associated with HIV is adrenal insufficiency. The virus can be transmitted from mother to child during breastfeeding, highlighting the importance of understanding its transmission routes. Additionally, individuals infected with HIV may experience increased cardiovascular risk and menopausal symptoms, particularly in women. Overall, HIV poses significant challenges to health, necessitating awareness and management of its various effects on the body.</data>
      <data key="d2">81491129b1fbfcc2e010109419cde1f0,c925b6205ea7a28ba24640c28ec558da,cf034f02916ec46dcba7e0f8d8e24c0c</data>
      <data key="d3">1493</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">2583</data>
      <data key="d7">602e2d814e804bb58a41234f69bd4a32</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRUCELLOSIS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Brucellosis is an infectious disease caused by bacteria that can be transmitted through unpasteurized milk and can affect breastfeeding.</data>
      <data key="d2">cf034f02916ec46dcba7e0f8d8e24c0c</data>
      <data key="d3">1493</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2584</data>
      <data key="d7">d4b3da01447043559f59c068f477ca06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TUBERCULOSIS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Tuberculosis is an infectious bacterial disease that primarily affects the lungs but can also lead to serious complications in other parts of the body. It is particularly concerning in low-income countries, where it can result in adrenal insufficiency due to the destruction of the adrenal glands. This condition, known as primary adrenal insufficiency, can arise as a complication of the disease. Additionally, untreated tuberculosis poses risks to breastfeeding, potentially affecting both the mother and the infant. Other complications associated with tuberculosis include ascites quilosa, which is the accumulation of lymphatic fluid in the abdominal cavity. Overall, tuberculosis is a significant health issue that can lead to various severe health outcomes if not properly managed.</data>
      <data key="d2">1ac394c08f6c4bdfbee5440e56a97dd0,c77caafffd9b5d42c51cb2359ad9cc6d,c925b6205ea7a28ba24640c28ec558da,cf034f02916ec46dcba7e0f8d8e24c0c</data>
      <data key="d3">1491</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">2585</data>
      <data key="d7">6dfa4d83ab3241539f91be860b9b3e0b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CES&#193;REA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Ces&#225;rea, or cesarean section, is a surgical procedure employed to deliver a baby through incisions made in the abdomen and uterus. This method of delivery can have implications for breastfeeding initiation, potentially affecting the early stages of breastfeeding for the newborn. Additionally, ces&#225;rea is associated with a distinct microbiota composition in newborns, which is characterized by the presence of skin-related microorganisms. This difference in microbiota may have further implications for the health and development of infants born via cesarean section.</data>
      <data key="d2">b36b8846f109ee75806c6ea03015e5d5,cf034f02916ec46dcba7e0f8d8e24c0c</data>
      <data key="d3">661</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">2586</data>
      <data key="d7">7294de0db8894d198c1e9aadd21cf53d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LECHE MATERNA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">LECHE MATERNA, or breast milk, is the milk produced by a mother to feed her infant, providing essential nutrients and antibodies crucial for the infant's health. It is recommended for feeding infants whenever possible, as it serves as the natural food for newborns. Breast milk is particularly important for premature infants, aiding in their nutritional needs and helping with intestinal adaptation. It is considered the optimal nutrition for newborns, promoting self-regulation in feeding practices. Additionally, LECHE MATERNA contains vital components such as iodine and other essential vitamins, making it a key element in infant nutrition. The composition and significance of breast milk have been extensively analyzed, underscoring its importance in supporting the health and development of infants.</data>
      <data key="d2">4a251705839547c8dcd0e9ddc5bb4066,724f16660e0077267b0836a0e1fb02c3,89855c293083756b0ff7a0081f67e9fd,aacaabd084b7839bdedf5fbebfd7dcf4,cf034f02916ec46dcba7e0f8d8e24c0c,e742c891694171ad455b038f416b7728,ea5e972294754a47333efb4c580cf15c</data>
      <data key="d3">638</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">2587</data>
      <data key="d7">0c2d2c25cb8f4c8f8902c9616a289f5a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIABETES TIPO II">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Type II diabetes is a chronic condition that affects the way the body processes blood sugar (glucose) and can be influenced by breastfeeding.</data>
      <data key="d2">cf034f02916ec46dcba7e0f8d8e24c0c</data>
      <data key="d3">1493</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2588</data>
      <data key="d7">5fd44bc99ebf4aed8fc25feab6f508b9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ARTRITIS REUMATOIDE">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Artritis Reumatoide es una enfermedad autoinmune que se caracteriza por la inflamaci&#243;n cr&#243;nica de las articulaciones. Esta condici&#243;n puede estar relacionada con niveles elevados de cobre en el organismo. Adem&#225;s, se ha observado que la artritis reumatoide puede beneficiarse de la salud proporcionada por la lactancia materna. Es importante destacar que esta enfermedad autoinmune tambi&#233;n puede coexistir con otros trastornos autoinmunes, como la diabetes mellitus tipo 1.</data>
      <data key="d2">232591ff32b33e56e95717d4582a5903,806e7b517eea737f1b254b699db60046,cf034f02916ec46dcba7e0f8d8e24c0c</data>
      <data key="d3">1493</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">2589</data>
      <data key="d7">fad94f2322674499a3ba085c74a8c5db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V&#205;NCULO AFECTIVO">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">The emotional bond established between a mother and her child, which can be enhanced through breastfeeding.</data>
      <data key="d2">cf034f02916ec46dcba7e0f8d8e24c0c</data>
      <data key="d3">1493</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2590</data>
      <data key="d7">9f65a1f2a6a84571953f7af70226807f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHUPETE">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">A pacifier, which is sometimes used by parents, can affect breastfeeding practices and is discussed in the context of breastfeeding recommendations.</data>
      <data key="d2">cf034f02916ec46dcba7e0f8d8e24c0c</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">2591</data>
