    </node>
    <node id="VITAMIN B6">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Vitamin B6, also known as pyridoxine, is a vital nutrient that plays a significant role in various metabolic processes. It serves as a cofactor for enzymes involved in amino acid metabolism, particularly in the conversion of homocysteine to cystathionine. This vitamin is essential for protein metabolism and cognitive development. Additionally, Vitamin B6 is involved in oxalate metabolism, and its deficiency can lead to increased oxalate production.

Research has explored the potential role of Vitamin B6 in managing metabolic disorders, although findings have been inconclusive for certain conditions such as Glycogen Storage Disease Type VI (GSDVI). For patients undergoing dialysis, it is recommended to take Vitamin B6 in doses of 10-20 mg per day to support their health. Vitamin B6 can be found in various supplements, including products like Hidropolivit, highlighting its importance in dietary intake.</data>
      <data key="d2">24cc40d6e3b225945cd4d8c2484632e4,550574bce077f34348bd11c78dc814a1,7754f367b453475179218cac4bc24de1,8366a6776a090c4e13b4746a1b23e96f,9da930839072b524410f2345ab1df8b1,acfde2ff93b969353912836034d74242</data>
      <data key="d3">1659</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">2664</data>
      <data key="d7">81defa6ed4d7438d8c46ff95cacc15b7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VITAMIN H">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Vitamin H, also known as biotin, is important for fatty acid synthesis and is included in nutritional supplements.</data>
      <data key="d2">8366a6776a090c4e13b4746a1b23e96f</data>
      <data key="d3">1627</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2665</data>
      <data key="d7">2a71782481064e6b9025e5d008149928</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VITAMIN C">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Vitamin C is a vital water-soluble nutrient known for its essential roles in immune function, skin health, and collagen formation. It is present in various supplements, such as Hidropolivit, and is recognized for its antioxidant properties, which may help prevent the formation of free radicals and reduce oxidative damage in cells. While Vitamin C is important for overall health, its intake should be carefully managed in certain populations. For instance, it is recommended that patients with iron overload limit their Vitamin C intake, as excessive amounts can increase iron absorption. Additionally, patients undergoing dialysis are advised to take doses of less than 150 mg per day. Furthermore, Vitamin C supplementation has been associated with increased urinary oxalate excretion due to its metabolism to oxalate. Overall, Vitamin C is an essential nutrient that supports various bodily functions, but its supplementation should be approached with caution in specific health conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">550574bce077f34348bd11c78dc814a1,65ba063e1a9e43a87dbfe9cf627674b1,75457662a196b78b5738344c4dbd89a8,7ff62e1dc423be2f4b2fe08546751d0d,8366a6776a090c4e13b4746a1b23e96f,83d2bb17e643e047bb3ad2ecc88d8271,9da930839072b524410f2345ab1df8b1,acfde2ff93b969353912836034d74242</data>
      <data key="d3">1630</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">2666</data>
      <data key="d7">f8863da7f5d14c1b9ea88049eb8aaa30</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VITAMIN E">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Vitamin E is a fat-soluble antioxidant that plays a crucial role in protecting cells from damage and supporting immune function. It is known for its ability to protect cell membranes from oxidative damage, which may contribute to a reduced incidence of Alzheimer's disease. Vitamin E is commonly found in dietary supplements, such as those containing DHA, and is recommended in specific dosages for patients with certain conditions, including those with hereditary hemochromatosis, where its levels are often decreased. Supplementation in these cases may help alleviate oxidative stress, further underscoring the importance of Vitamin E in maintaining cellular health.</data>
      <data key="d2">75457662a196b78b5738344c4dbd89a8,8366a6776a090c4e13b4746a1b23e96f,83d2bb17e643e047bb3ad2ecc88d8271,acfde2ff93b969353912836034d74242,d76cb2d50e736da37be7fe2a415a7281</data>
      <data key="d3">1628</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">5</data>
      <data key="d6">2667</data>
      <data key="d7">48a5a491f8d64c9fb83a3a11488ef906</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IRON">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Iron is a vital micronutrient essential for the production of hemoglobin, playing a crucial role in overall health, particularly in infants, toddlers, and women of childbearing age. Its dietary reference intake varies across different age groups, as outlined in dietary guidelines. Iron deficiency and resulting anemia are common, especially after bariatric surgery, necessitating supplementation to prevent these issues. This is particularly important for patients receiving erythropoietin, as iron supplementation is often required to address anemia in these cases, including during dialysis treatment.

Iron is frequently included in infant formulas and supplements, although its addition can be controversial, especially for preterm infants. It is important to note that while iron is essential for health, excessive intake can lead to oxidative stress and neurodegeneration. Additionally, certain dietary components, such as corn starch, can interfere with iron absorption, highlighting the need for careful management of iron intake to meet the recommended dietary allowance (RDA) and prevent deficiencies in vulnerable populations.</data>
      <data key="d2">565aef05382a327b4bdefb2ab3d3b4be,75457662a196b78b5738344c4dbd89a8,79afec9f55a4cb9aa31a7e4566dae279,7ff62e1dc423be2f4b2fe08546751d0d,8366a6776a090c4e13b4746a1b23e96f,9303a85464d02f1cc012aaca21edf9bb,97a728c18b63007d07b6a4a5fc22d5e4,9a0227d0ea548fa46814e6403be885ce,9da930839072b524410f2345ab1df8b1,acf7f978dbec397c60ed463f4eb2fcf6,bd26f1257d0d7f3e1de6624b3b4a3cc2,fa356397bc52582bdbbd1868639feec9</data>
      <data key="d3">1627</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">13</data>
      <data key="d6">2668</data>
      <data key="d7">3bf2076fbe844440964353cca62ccd3b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DEXTRINOMALTOSE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Dextrinomaltose is a carbohydrate used as a source of energy in nutritional supplements and infant formulas.</data>
      <data key="d2">8366a6776a090c4e13b4746a1b23e96f</data>
