    <node id="BALANCE HIDROELECTROL&#205;TICO">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">BALANCE HIDROELECTROL&#205;TICO refers to the critical process of monitoring and managing the hydroelectrolytic balance in patients, particularly those receiving parenteral nutrition. This balance is essential for maintaining fluid and electrolyte levels, which are vital for overall health. The importance of this management is especially pronounced in vulnerable populations, such as newborns, where precise control of these levels is crucial for their well-being.</data>
      <data key="d2">103ae80b955f35401aca706444999b29,2bd5549759c6a0d8393bb93fdccb747b</data>
      <data key="d3">661</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">2718</data>
      <data key="d7">bd1ea9cfbb0a43ba8e7aa44858a69544</data>
    </node>
    <node id="P&#201;RDIDAS INSENSIBLES DE AGUA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Refers to the unavoidable loss of water from the body, particularly significant in newborns due to their skin immaturity.</data>
      <data key="d2">103ae80b955f35401aca706444999b29</data>
      <data key="d3">661</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2719</data>
      <data key="d7">e40f95a8af2e48f0921c49e076aebfea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TEMPERATURA AMBIENTAL">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">The environmental temperature that can affect the health and hydration needs of newborns.</data>
      <data key="d2">103ae80b955f35401aca706444999b29</data>
      <data key="d3">661</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2720</data>
      <data key="d7">b08a6d4dcf394b0fbbc77855f41d4f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIURESIS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Diuresis is defined as the increased production of urine, a physiological process that plays a significant role in managing fluid and electrolyte balance in various patient populations. In the context of patients undergoing dialysis, diuresis can impact their overall fluid and electrolyte status, necessitating careful monitoring and management. Additionally, diuresis is a critical phase in the management of neonates, where the process of urine production is essential for maintaining proper fluid balance. Thus, diuresis is an important factor in both adult and neonatal care, highlighting its relevance in different medical scenarios.</data>
      <data key="d2">103ae80b955f35401aca706444999b29,15ffbfba2decfecb69a26bcb1e4bd898,9da930839072b524410f2345ab1df8b1</data>
      <data key="d3">627</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">4</data>
      <data key="d6">2721</data>
      <data key="d7">2e98e6ebea494754a619bf3ec26a28a1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NEONATOS">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">NEONATOS refers to newborn infants, particularly those who are premature and necessitate specialized medical care, especially in the areas of fluid and electrolyte management. Additionally, neonates may present with hyperammonemia, which can arise from urea cycle disorders, thereby requiring careful monitoring and treatment to ensure their health and well-being.</data>
      <data key="d2">0487e1a4525547575a1d284297280f9a,15ffbfba2decfecb69a26bcb1e4bd898</data>
      <data key="d3">627</data>
