    </node>
    <node id="FASE DIUR&#201;TICA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">The diuretic phase is a critical stage in the management of neonates, where fluid and electrolyte balance is closely monitored.</data>
      <data key="d2">15ffbfba2decfecb69a26bcb1e4bd898</data>
      <data key="d3">627</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2725</data>
      <data key="d7">97fb77072da04632abb3d8c99f826c65</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FASE PRE-DIUR&#201;TICA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">The pre-diuretic phase refers to the period before diuresis begins, where careful monitoring of fluid intake and output is essential.</data>
      <data key="d2">15ffbfba2decfecb69a26bcb1e4bd898</data>
      <data key="d3">627</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2726</data>
      <data key="d7">4735adc9d4d74cb1bdb0d32e60d85025</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NATREMIA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">NATREMIA refers to the concentration of sodium in the blood, which is a critical factor in diagnosing and managing conditions such as hyponatremia. It plays an essential role in various physiological functions and is influenced by a range of medical conditions and treatments. Monitoring natremia is particularly important in assessing fluid balance, especially in neonates and in patients undergoing treatment for conditions like Syndrome of Inappropriate Antidiuretic Hormone secretion (SIADH). Overall, natremia serves as a vital indicator of health and is crucial for effective medical management.</data>
      <data key="d2">05a2506becec805cd1374d73de9a9fec,15ffbfba2decfecb69a26bcb1e4bd898,22769156746e1c11980b01e7fffbcac7,85cfae7019d0efa6f0c87f5cd5b9ce8e,94481f05649e1ec83f8cc2e4a70b660b,a2bc3ecb4c946bf6d4905a8d4fe7d013,a56fad7f6dac4a75bd4748b247aa9215,c73e5e558cab6ae4736ce1619142c0a4</data>
