    </node>
    <node id="ACIDOSIS METABOLICA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Metabolic acidosis is a condition that can occur in premature newborns receiving parenteral nutrition due to bicarbonate reabsorption issues.</data>
      <data key="d2">a0b6196d53d4eb1c8ca5b1a3a78e8fc7</data>
      <data key="d3">631</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">4</data>
      <data key="d6">2731</data>
      <data key="d7">1a7805327f074557a475eccc48ecc1ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BUN">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">BUN, or Blood Urea Nitrogen, is a clinical marker that serves as an important indicator of metabolic issues, particularly in infants. It is especially relevant in the context of amino acid administration and parenteral nutrition. Monitoring BUN levels can help healthcare providers assess the metabolic status of infants receiving these treatments, ensuring that any potential complications are identified and addressed promptly.</data>
      <data key="d2">a0b6196d53d4eb1c8ca5b1a3a78e8fc7,e41a9b2827462263a11ef60ce74c160b</data>
      <data key="d3">662</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">2732</data>
      <data key="d7">83ebf3be593c4a38a3841498078267b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AMINOVEN INFANT">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Aminoven Infant is a preparation of amino acids available for nutritional support in infants, specifically designed to supplement cysteine and provide essential nutrients.</data>
      <data key="d2">e41a9b2827462263a11ef60ce74c160b</data>
      <data key="d3">662</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">2733</data>
      <data key="d7">295b7fbbf4ba4bdea6b5220b79713826</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIPOTERMIA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Hypothermia is a condition that can affect preterm infants, leading to increased energy requirements for maintaining body temperature.</data>
      <data key="d2">e41a9b2827462263a11ef60ce74c160b</data>
      <data key="d3">664</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2734</data>
      <data key="d7">fd582c81b262417ea4984120244d59b9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIPOPLUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">LIPOPLUS is a specialized fat emulsion designed for intravenous nutrition, primarily utilized in parenteral nutrition. It contains a blend of soybean oil, medium-chain triglycerides, and fish oil, which together provide essential fatty acids necessary for patient care. Additionally, LIPOPLUS is recognized as a fourth-generation lipid emulsion that incorporates omega-3 fatty acids, offering potential anti-inflammatory and hepatoprotective effects. The formulation is particularly beneficial in pediatric nutrition, where it combines fish oil with other lipid components to meet the nutritional needs of young patients. Overall, LIPOPLUS serves as a comprehensive solution for delivering vital lipids in various clinical settings.</data>
      <data key="d2">44bcef3c5453091f304bd39882ae1a07,87da0f3b46604498629565db45667b3b,a9268f98da988e7f94a3faf7bc7627c7,bb7584256e051f1c3d8cb13502ade155</data>
      <data key="d3">659</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">2735</data>
      <data key="d7">5b0fea4f7ce24702b01dd0725b0b20d3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COLESTASIS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Coleostasis is a medical condition characterized by impaired bile flow from the liver to the duodenum. This condition can occur in newborns and is often associated with parenteral nutrition, which is a method of feeding that bypasses the gastrointestinal tract. The inability of bile to flow properly can significantly impact the nutritional status of affected individuals, highlighting the importance of monitoring and managing this condition in clinical settings.</data>
      <data key="d2">036e8182deab774d39f06cd811738c9b,44bcef3c5453091f304bd39882ae1a07</data>
      <data key="d3">659</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">4</data>
      <data key="d6">2736</data>
      <data key="d7">2e9d4bd48e6346ce822cad1d52effc89</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENFERMEDADES PULMONARES">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">"ENFERMEDADES PULMONARES" refers to respiratory diseases that can significantly affect the health and nutritional needs of newborns. Additionally, these pulmonary diseases may play a role in the development of Syndrome of Inappropriate Antidiuretic Hormone secretion (SIADH), highlighting their potential impact on various health conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">44bcef3c5453091f304bd39882ae1a07,addd6db95fafa3464a6f085f8d0041ce</data>
      <data key="d3">1420</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2737</data>
      <data key="d7">f131ae39732f4b7591d5ec4a0c73eb48</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIPOCALEMIA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">HIPOCALEMIA refers to two distinct medical conditions: hypokalemia and hypocalcemia. 

Hypokalemia is characterized by low potassium levels in the blood, which can lead to various complications, including nephrogenic diabetes insipidus. It can be caused by renal disorders or excessive loss of potassium, and may result in symptoms such as muscle weakness and arrhythmias. Additionally, hypokalemia can affect urinary composition and contribute to stone formation.

On the other hand, hypocalcemia is a condition marked by low calcium levels in the blood. This condition can occur in premature newborns due to inadequate calcium reserves, as well as in patients with eating disorders due to nutritional deficiencies. Hypocalcemia can also arise from parathyroid dysfunction, magnesium metabolism issues, elevated phosphorus levels, and is a concern in patients with chronic kidney disease. It may manifest as a complication in patients with diabetic ketoacidosis. Treatment for hypocalcemia often involves vitamin D and its metabolites to help restore calcium levels.

In summary, HIPOCALEMIA encompasses both hypokalemia and hypocalcemia, each with distinct causes, symptoms, and treatment approaches related to their respective electrolyte imbalances.</data>
      <data key="d2">18f04b0302d49a339210d54d9cc55cd3,1fb4c8ba1bc908ea3a3c7717a073fa4f,44bcef3c5453091f304bd39882ae1a07,473502714ce73073f93b598ca0edcc21,64d85b2cd48849f9143370c8c04ab04c,85df619f6d535bbe5c0485f540242903,863198ceabd6e1c11a0d0944c676737c,92ae465a11b49807321be06ec9f5bc5a,afe0f4f37815c175f0faff06eb24a256,d59a9ea18d433acb64d60dbe17f5ae9b,e165a1c74230c4f9f29f883df45e4c8c</data>
      <data key="d3">621</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">15</data>
      <data key="d6">2738</data>
      <data key="d7">7f4cb74907ee495c9e8b6d66bce6be5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIPOFOSFOREMIA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Hipofosfatemia, also known as hypophosphatemia, is a medical condition characterized by low phosphorus levels in the blood. This condition can arise from various factors, including inadequate intake of phosphorus, particularly in newborns. Additionally, hypophosphatemia is often observed in patients who are on parenteral nutrition, necessitating careful management to address the deficiency.</data>
      <data key="d2">44bcef3c5453091f304bd39882ae1a07,814d394c907e59b29a5f0d9e82aed423</data>
      <data key="d3">622</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">2739</data>
      <data key="d7">0ba1bc37a5da412daf1a41a596fe9fd0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KERNICTERUS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Kernicterus is a type of brain damage that can occur in newborns with severe jaundice, which can be a risk in cases of hyperbilirubinemia.</data>
      <data key="d2">44bcef3c5453091f304bd39882ae1a07</data>
      <data key="d3">659</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2740</data>
      <data key="d7">d065fe32fe45473e9de9f203c65a06a9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DBP">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">DBP refers to two distinct concepts: a type of bariatric surgery and developmental brain injury. In the context of bariatric surgery, DBP is a malabsorptive technique that may require patients to make dietary adjustments and take nutritional supplements to address potential deficiencies, such as vitamin A deficiency. This highlights the importance of nutritional assessment for individuals undergoing this procedure. On the other hand, DBP also denotes developmental brain injury, which is a significant concern for premature newborns, as it is closely related to their nutritional needs. Thus, DBP encompasses both a surgical approach with implications for dietary management and a medical condition that underscores the critical role of nutrition in early development.</data>
      <data key="d2">44bcef3c5453091f304bd39882ae1a07,79afec9f55a4cb9aa31a7e4566dae279,91f7ece6ed9226a98b3f59f46000ebf6</data>
      <data key="d3">611</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">2741</data>
      <data key="d7">456b375747124fc3b4de1dc018a3e495</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PEDITRACE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">PEDITRACE is a pediatric multi-trace element solution used for the nutritional needs of preterm infants.</data>
      <data key="d2">fa356397bc52582bdbbd1868639feec9</data>
      <data key="d3">609</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">2742</data>
      <data key="d7">c122c1bed22a46f6a1d31c2bf3b1cb0b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OLIGO-ZINC">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Oligo-zinc is a zinc supplement designed for use in preterm infants to meet their nutritional requirements.</data>
      <data key="d2">fa356397bc52582bdbbd1868639feec9</data>
      <data key="d3">609</data>
