    </node>
    <node id="CARNITINA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Carnitina, also known as carnitine, is a vital substance that plays a crucial role in the transport of long-chain fatty acids into the mitochondria for oxidation, which is essential for energy production in the body. This process is particularly relevant for long-chain fatty acids and is important for various populations, including preterm infants. The levels of carnitina are monitored in patients undergoing dietary treatment to ensure adequate energy production. While carnitina is often recommended as a supplement, it is advised to be used only when levels are low or when symptoms of deficiency appear, as its empirical use lacks clear evidence. Overall, carnitina is an amino acid that is integral to metabolic processes and energy generation in the body.</data>
      <data key="d2">214cb2b8ddec52ff69849d87feb8e141,3c2bb3d9d6534668e58f571710c9bb02,4d84210c815fb41f2fa44f3e01aafa02,a9268f98da988e7f94a3faf7bc7627c7,fa356397bc52582bdbbd1868639feec9</data>
      <data key="d3">585</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">5</data>
      <data key="d6">2745</data>
      <data key="d7">10e95e63a5d847b99f4cf14cabf65bb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COPPER">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Copper, also known as "cobre," is an essential trace mineral that plays a vital role in various bodily functions. It is necessary for health in low concentrations but can be toxic at high levels. The dietary reference intake for copper varies across different age groups, and specific intake recommendations are outlined in dietary guidelines. 

Deficiency of copper is common in certain medical conditions, such as after biliopancreatic diversion and in patients undergoing hematopoietic cell transplantation, which can lead to various health issues. In infants receiving nutritional supplementation, copper levels are closely monitored to prevent toxicity. Excessive copper can lead to serious health conditions, including Wilson's disease, and may contribute to oxidative stress, potentially impacting neurodegenerative diseases.

Copper is also studied for its potential role in various diseases, including cancer. In the context of parenteral nutrition, copper is considered a contaminant, and its levels should be carefully managed to avoid toxicity. Overall, copper is a crucial micronutrient that must be maintained within a balanced range to support health effectively.</data>
      <data key="d2">1dbfa8fa6421e3dd5e585baa8a10c25c,2bd5549759c6a0d8393bb93fdccb747b,4216916ebab948061d2dca4afe7e1e84,565aef05382a327b4bdefb2ab3d3b4be,5cab61b11d03ef705141159d100ae7c5,74a9c62d0f932e27419151241f7db445,75457662a196b78b5738344c4dbd89a8,86a4647fb1fa580f7b72aa4833965ecb,91f7ece6ed9226a98b3f59f46000ebf6,acf7f978dbec397c60ed463f4eb2fcf6,add3641ae46ac521317bb4c12922895a,bd26f1257d0d7f3e1de6624b3b4a3cc2,d6bd9fbceb166872651dd2deade92849,d750228cb05d1651c8df327308bdc550,ea920d08266b329db73fb7b804bbb9ce,fa356397bc52582bdbbd1868639feec9</data>
      <data key="d3">1792</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">16</data>
      <data key="d6">2746</data>
      <data key="d7">40c39925eb6442948d7fce55d3ea35cf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SELENIUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Selenium is a trace mineral that plays a critical role in various bodily functions and is essential for overall health. It is recognized for its antioxidant properties and is involved in metabolism. The dietary reference intake for selenium varies by age group, with recommended daily intake levels typically ranging from 50 to 70 &#956;g/day in certain cases. While selenium is not routinely required, it may be recommended for individuals with specific health conditions, including those undergoing dietary treatment or suffering from chronic diseases.

Selenium is particularly important for infant nutrition and is included in multi-trace element solutions. Its deficiency has been reported in patients with cirrhosis and other chronic health issues, which can adversely affect their overall health. Additionally, selenium is being studied for its potential health benefits, including its role in cancer prevention and cardiovascular health, as well as its significance in managing liver disease. Overall, selenium is a vital micronutrient that contributes to various aspects of health and well-being.</data>
      <data key="d2">214cb2b8ddec52ff69849d87feb8e141,565aef05382a327b4bdefb2ab3d3b4be,5aaadc19de8fb96a4951c0ba38192991,9da930839072b524410f2345ab1df8b1,acf7f978dbec397c60ed463f4eb2fcf6,add3641ae46ac521317bb4c12922895a,ea1d430dfc773b669eec18759441b31a,ea920d08266b329db73fb7b804bbb9ce,fa356397bc52582bdbbd1868639feec9</data>
      <data key="d3">585</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">12</data>
      <data key="d6">2747</data>
      <data key="d7">e47aacbe33d343ed8ef486cf04b7123d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MANGANESE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Manganese is a trace element that plays a significant role in nutritional supplementation, particularly for infants. It is important to monitor manganese levels in such contexts to ensure adequate intake. However, manganese should be minimized in parenteral nutrition due to the potential risk of toxicity. The dietary reference intake for manganese is specified for different age groups in nutritional guidelines, highlighting its importance as a mineral. These guidelines outline recommended daily intake levels for manganese, ensuring that individuals receive appropriate amounts based on their age and nutritional needs.</data>
      <data key="d2">2bd5549759c6a0d8393bb93fdccb747b,565aef05382a327b4bdefb2ab3d3b4be,acf7f978dbec397c60ed463f4eb2fcf6,fa356397bc52582bdbbd1868639feec9</data>
