    </node>
    <node id="C. GUTI&#201;RREZ SAN ROMAN">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">C. Guti&#233;rrez San Roman is one of the authors discussing complications related to long-term central venous catheters in pediatrics.</data>
      <data key="d2">7c60735b2451e2d314fe6c5377b7a79d</data>
      <data key="d3">751</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2807</data>
      <data key="d7">e4bbc3649de94945ac070d949498678b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CENTRAL VENOUS CATHETERS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Central venous catheters are medical devices designed to be inserted into a large vein, providing long-term access to the bloodstream for various medical treatments. They are particularly useful for long-term intravenous access, allowing for the administration of medications, fluids, and nutrients. However, it is important to note that the use of central venous catheters can lead to complications, especially in pediatric patients.</data>
      <data key="d2">3d5e0a3ef45c7ba15063791545dc4826,7c60735b2451e2d314fe6c5377b7a79d</data>
      <data key="d3">771</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">2808</data>
      <data key="d7">f8a4a4c4d3a4456dbda5eb12a9defe5c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OBSTRUCTION OF CENTRAL VENOUS CATHETERS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Obstruction of central venous catheters is a complication that can occur in 14-36% of cases, affecting their functionality.</data>
      <data key="d2">7c60735b2451e2d314fe6c5377b7a79d</data>
      <data key="d3">771</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2809</data>
      <data key="d7">008eb16f6db74ad2b38b2bd942510ada</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GASTROINTESTINAL PRIMING">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Gastrointestinal priming is a feeding strategy aimed at preparing the digestive system of preterm infants for enteral feeding.</data>
      <data key="d2">7c60735b2451e2d314fe6c5377b7a79d</data>
      <data key="d3">585</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2810</data>
      <data key="d7">d9ffd414ed514da9bcc709c5c8cbf92a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="THROMBOSIS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Thrombosis refers to the formation of a blood clot inside a blood vessel, which can complicate the use of central venous catheters in pediatric patients.</data>
      <data key="d2">7c60735b2451e2d314fe6c5377b7a79d</data>
      <data key="d3">771</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2811</data>
      <data key="d7">a5a4eb5601304d3b925bef78a66542fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CATHETER OCLUSION">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Catheter occlusion is a blockage that prevents the normal flow of fluids through a catheter, often requiring medical intervention.</data>
      <data key="d2">7c60735b2451e2d314fe6c5377b7a79d</data>
      <data key="d3">771</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2812</data>
      <data key="d7">c50a4f33ea014ce59980ba905912cb8a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NUTRITIONAL STRATEGIES">
      <data key="d0" />
      <data key="d1">Nutritional strategies are planned approaches designed to enhance dietary intake and improve the nutritional status of patients, particularly those with specific medical conditions such as liver disease and acute pancreatitis. These strategies aim to provide adequate nutrition tailored to the unique needs of individuals facing these health challenges.</data>
      <data key="d2">6935432f69970131a6485f38e3f06003,7c60735b2451e2d314fe6c5377b7a79d,c45c1688135653a80812cdaf426ec9a3</data>
      <data key="d3">585</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2813</data>
      <data key="d7">21e73c176f314b39b83e5cf2d8025898</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UROKINASA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Urokinase is a medication used as a thrombolytic agent to dissolve blood clots.</data>
      <data key="d2">1c3eeb30a49c70f750b79e7bc1dfef52</data>
      <data key="d3">766</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2814</data>
      <data key="d7">3beadcf9ef0646b4a90e51c132648f7e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALTEPLASA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Alteplase is a medication utilized primarily for fibrinolysis, particularly in patients experiencing ischemic stroke, where it aids in dissolving blood clots. It serves as a fibrinolytic agent and is also indicated for treating catheter occlusions. In the context of acute ischemic stroke, alteplase is employed for thrombolysis, although its administration may sometimes need to be interrupted shortly after initiation. Overall, alteplase is recognized for its effectiveness in managing blood clots across various medical conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">1c3eeb30a49c70f750b79e7bc1dfef52,2f91f92c99da813a8d1be2f6ac0fa552,ea7d8ba0550547d4cff0b5d18c3f6ae5,fe3ebcedac0857680bcc85bb58dcb3e1</data>
      <data key="d3">766</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">2815</data>
      <data key="d7">45b6dfb84cbd4bdfa11b0734f168882a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TROMBOSIS INTRALUMINAL">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Trombosis intraluminal refers to the formation of a thrombus within the lumen of a catheter, leading to partial or complete obstruction.</data>
      <data key="d2">1c3eeb30a49c70f750b79e7bc1dfef52</data>
      <data key="d3">766</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2816</data>
      <data key="d7">be43fa4f4b0f4589952cb7e86442da1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TROMBOSIS EXTRALUMINAL">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Trombosis extraluminal refers to the formation of a thrombus outside the catheter, which can also lead to obstruction.</data>
      <data key="d2">1c3eeb30a49c70f750b79e7bc1dfef52</data>
      <data key="d3">766</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2817</data>
      <data key="d7">b28516ca76ac490d91c840d3a335d33c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="M&#201;DICO RESPONSABLE">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">The "M&#201;DICO RESPONSABLE" is the professional responsible for ensuring that patients receive the necessary information and uphold their right to informed consent. Additionally, this physician oversees the treatment and management of patients dealing with catheter-related issues, ensuring comprehensive care and support throughout the patient's medical journey.</data>
      <data key="d2">1c3eeb30a49c70f750b79e7bc1dfef52,e475fe60f9aab6773eddfe8bad25eef6</data>
      <data key="d3">1080</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">2818</data>
      <data key="d7">4b5894fcadf64676838d4b15fb833c1f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CATETEROGRAF&#205;A">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Cateterograf&#237;a is a diagnostic procedure that involves the administration of contrast through a catheter. This technique is utilized to identify and evaluate mechanical or thrombotic issues within the body. By using a catheter to deliver the contrast agent, healthcare professionals can obtain detailed images and insights into the vascular system, aiding in the diagnosis and management of various medical conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">1c3eeb30a49c70f750b79e7bc1dfef52,20a97c2641138578710a84f5040c86ef</data>
      <data key="d3">766</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">2819</data>
      <data key="d7">3ec86637818e4891a9f002f894943ba5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ECOGRAF&#205;A">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">ECOGRAF&#205;A, also known as ultrasound, is a diagnostic imaging technique utilized for various medical evaluations. It is primarily employed to assess liver and pancreatic lesions in patients. Additionally, ECOGRAF&#205;A is effective in detecting obstructions and the presence of thrombi in catheters, making it a valuable tool in diagnosing vascular issues. This imaging procedure plays a crucial role in providing insights into both organ health and potential complications related to catheter use.</data>
      <data key="d2">1c3eeb30a49c70f750b79e7bc1dfef52,20a97c2641138578710a84f5040c86ef,b00c312f46e790b32fe99743480f9e47</data>
      <data key="d3">766</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">2820</data>
      <data key="d7">30dea2a39833406aa3cdd2d5e3b1501a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="S&#205;NDROME PINCH-OFF">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">S&#237;ndrome pinch-off occurs when a portion of the catheter is compressed between the clavicle and the first rib, potentially leading to complications.</data>
      <data key="d2">1c3eeb30a49c70f750b79e7bc1dfef52</data>
      <data key="d3">766</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2821</data>
      <data key="d7">7ff846d5bd1e4aa7ac7a1849d6a52108</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TROMBO MURAL">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Trombo mural refers to a thrombus that forms on the wall of the catheter, which can lead to obstruction.</data>
      <data key="d2">1c3eeb30a49c70f750b79e7bc1dfef52</data>
      <data key="d3">766</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">2822</data>
      <data key="d7">498bb2edf04a4b5094a3b23f7e3cbc18</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TROMBOSIS VENOSA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Trombosis venosa, or venous thrombosis, is a serious complication that can arise during the maintenance of central venous catheters (CVCs). This condition is characterized by the formation of blood clots in the veins, which can occur in patients who have long-term CVCs. The presence of these clots can lead to significant clinical manifestations, including the potential loss of venous access. In severe cases, this complication may necessitate intestinal transplantation. Overall, thrombosis venosa is a critical concern associated with catheter use, highlighting the importance of monitoring and managing patients with central venous catheters to prevent such adverse outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d2">1c3eeb30a49c70f750b79e7bc1dfef52,20a97c2641138578710a84f5040c86ef,21bd0c4198fa2be0aa931387bd631728,379909adba66c6e316f560669cd30472,6b49a9a43dc167f62a772b262afe2bee,c031cb3bc7513b4a3f8e4f264c00a675,ff844169c2b512733f5bde26e6f1ed32</data>
      <data key="d3">768</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">12</data>
      <data key="d6">2823</data>
      <data key="d7">f4d709c9f76e4789835ce73ac73bfaff</data>
    </node>
