      <data key="d6">3080</data>
      <data key="d7">075ccb567da84c29b7c65c1ff829641a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="D&#205;MERO D">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">D&#205;MERO D is a biomarker known as D-dimer, which is a fibrin degradation product. It is often elevated in conditions associated with increased clotting and inflammation. Notably, D-dimer has been linked to the risk of developing frailty in elderly individuals. This connection highlights its significance in assessing health risks related to aging and the potential for increased vulnerability in older populations.</data>
      <data key="d2">bdb8258b310cb9ed8a0e4354fac5a33c,caab2e12a2bce3c947d663cf8b71cb67</data>
      <data key="d3">469</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">3081</data>
      <data key="d7">f6ab012be4fb4ad2a2a107673a11e28b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IGF-I">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Insulin-like growth factor 1 (IGF-I) is a hormone that plays a crucial role in growth and development, particularly in muscle tissue. It is known to influence muscle growth and development positively. Research indicates that a decrease in IGF-I levels is associated with increased frailty in the elderly, highlighting its importance in maintaining physical health and strength as individuals age. Thus, IGF-I is significant not only for growth during earlier life stages but also for the prevention of frailty in older adults.</data>
      <data key="d2">4b3664b368fad59fd55189a65e19b000,caab2e12a2bce3c947d663cf8b71cb67</data>
      <data key="d3">469</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">3082</data>
      <data key="d7">667f35c684e5496e8edcc19542c44137</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANDR&#211;GENOS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">ANDR&#211;GENOS are a group of hormones that include testosterone and DHEA (dehydroepiandrosterone). These hormones are primarily known for their role in male physiology, particularly in contributing to muscle mass and strength. The decline of androgens with age is associated with increased frailty in individuals. Additionally, an increase in androgen levels is a characteristic feature of polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS), a condition that affects women's hormonal balance. Thus, androgens play significant roles in both male and female health, influencing various physiological processes and conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">401a6af689562ae0b9d7fe627eb56d0f,caab2e12a2bce3c947d663cf8b71cb67</data>
      <data key="d3">469</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">3083</data>
      <data key="d7">b30f8f14ac7443d4b491bde0df11a0e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HORMONA DEL CRECIMIENTO">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">The "HORMONA DEL CRECIMIENTO," or growth hormone (GH), is a crucial hormone produced by the pituitary gland that plays a significant role in stimulating growth, cell reproduction, and regeneration in humans and other animals. It is essential for muscle and tissue growth, and its decline with age can lead to frailty and a loss of muscle mass. In specific medical contexts, such as in patients with Turner syndrome, GH is secreted to enhance adult height, although its effectiveness may vary based on multiple factors.

Additionally, growth hormone regulates both growth and metabolism, and an excess of this hormone can result in conditions such as acromegaly. GH is also utilized in the treatment of patients with severe burns, as it aids in reducing healing time and associated costs. During pregnancy, the growth hormone secreted by the placenta increases levels of insulin-like growth factor 1 (IGF-1), further highlighting its importance in various physiological processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">2e73034a100b1adb0a00561c08416bda,2ec23f85b858abc42196ef48260e8cb0,2f30de565a9a9acf7ac0930483276bbb,a2ccbbd76866c4f88a82e64c95764b04,b18ef5c018c100e1582224fe82f03626,caab2e12a2bce3c947d663cf8b71cb67,ebfccc35cef320c7240cf36dd52e847a</data>
      <data key="d3">1142</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">3084</data>
      <data key="d7">b4ffbd17e4ee4f2cba9477dae4911d41</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IL-6">
      <data key="d0" />
      <data key="d1">Interleukin 6 (IL-6) is a pro-inflammatory cytokine that plays a significant role in the inflammatory response, particularly in critically ill patients. It is involved in the immune response and is relevant in the context of various diseases, including those associated with frailty and a pro-inflammatory state in the elderly. IL-6 has been identified as a key factor in the inflammatory processes that can affect health outcomes in vulnerable populations.</data>
      <data key="d2">25192e60a2c23bd74095f4602efeac4c,4b3664b368fad59fd55189a65e19b000,caab2e12a2bce3c947d663cf8b71cb67,d76cb2d50e736da37be7fe2a415a7281</data>
