      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">27</data>
      <data key="d6">3187</data>
      <data key="d7">b2eaa93ebeb9460ab2825a4685b2e0a5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIET">
      <data key="d0" />
      <data key="d1">The term "diet" encompasses the types of food that a person, animal, or community habitually consumes, which can have a significant impact on health outcomes. Diet plays a crucial role in managing various health conditions, such as nefrolitiasis, gout, and cardiovascular disease. It involves modifications to an individual's usual food intake to promote optimal health and manage issues like obesity. 

Additionally, dietary modifications may be necessary prior to medical tests, typically requiring restrictions on certain food groups for 24-72 hours to ensure accurate results. For individuals with hereditary fructose intolerance, a strict diet that excludes fructose, sucrose, and sorbitol is essential to prevent metabolic complications. Diets can also include specific eating plans, such as low-fat and low-carbohydrate diets, which are designed for weight loss and overall health improvement.</data>
      <data key="d2">108b158c33fead285203903273758748,20863ac06779722abc2cbc2dd023777e,5b7201af7da852101713ee9f11864f50,7855ffa74481ba85f44e9700279250e7,9a0227d0ea548fa46814e6403be885ce,a0162312192878417ef2c4ef8f9cffba,a4b144210b0a4b654a260ebc394de0af,c4f12f866dc714cdbb2323a920f524c2,cdc634d994ef64b248220c990c844160</data>
      <data key="d3">1646</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">18</data>
      <data key="d6">3188</data>
      <data key="d7">62785e6c37894de087c5f0a68f0c8814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EPA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Eicosapentaenoic acid (EPA) is an essential omega-3 fatty acid recognized for its significant health benefits, particularly in cardiovascular health. It plays a crucial role in reducing inflammation and supporting overall cardiovascular function. Additionally, EPA is associated with cognitive health, contributing to the maintenance of cognitive functions and overall brain health, especially when considered alongside another omega-3 fatty acid, DHA.

EPA is derived from alpha-linolenic acid (ALA) and has been studied for its potential benefits in various health contexts, including its role in managing conditions such as cystic fibrosis. In clinical settings, EPA is utilized as a treatment for patients undergoing hematopoietic cell transplantation, where it helps to reduce inflammation and complications like graft-versus-host disease (EICH).

Furthermore, EPA is often discussed in dietary recommendations and is known for its anti-inflammatory properties, which also support immune function. The research surrounding EPA includes randomized clinical trials, which are considered the gold standard in nutritional research, although they may not always be the most suitable for evaluating the effects of dietary modifications on health. Overall, EPA is a vital nutrient with diverse applications in health and disease management.</data>
      <data key="d2">0a43b350a248173236a96a64e266b744,2ab0f03a87bdf418c83a614b2183332f,3e6ae05671fed21e332ee155332df21e,680ff2c9e67ec29c5952df66bd83c984,bfe7b32b596449644b274ed7486a507a,c2211f0deb7328a77cb632140690da03,c7577b8eccf83ced48f5ecf36717b4e1,d2f16314e7098030a6780411d2e03023,dd8c7ee9ef48992cebb11e84fbb0fe34,dea64d5eb807430bc52e8bed6e30c393,e1766b4a9f6cff776650a29195cee9fc</data>
      <data key="d3">1614</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">18</data>
      <data key="d6">3189</data>
      <data key="d7">03153ca76edf4d6daf953bcf9d8b4216</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALZHEIMER'S DISEASE">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Alzheimer's disease is a progressive neurodegenerative condition characterized by cognitive decline, memory loss, and impaired functioning, which significantly impacts daily living and nutrition for affected individuals. It is often associated with aging and is linked to oxidative stress and the accumulation of beta-amyloid peptides. Various dietary interventions are currently being researched to address the challenges posed by this disorder. Overall, Alzheimer's disease presents substantial challenges in cognitive function and memory, necessitating ongoing exploration of potential nutritional factors and interventions.</data>
      <data key="d2">65143afbc494705be9ccc4e48bee296b,680ff2c9e67ec29c5952df66bd83c984,75457662a196b78b5738344c4dbd89a8,91b470ee527a45d8e81a821eeb9ce259,dd8c7ee9ef48992cebb11e84fbb0fe34,f7b4cba3bbdeec64513c036fd4166835</data>
      <data key="d3">1626</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">23</data>
      <data key="d6">3190</data>
      <data key="d7">a705cfe8911140b2b8f852051d244165</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FATTY ACIDS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Fatty acids are essential components of fats found in both the human body and dietary sources. They play a significant role in influencing inflammation and health outcomes, particularly in relation to respiratory diseases. Additionally, fatty acids are vital components of lipids that contribute to brain health. Omega-3 fatty acids, such as DHA (docosahexaenoic acid) and EPA (eicosapentaenoic acid), are especially beneficial for cognitive function, highlighting the importance of fatty acids in both physical and mental health.</data>
      <data key="d2">680ff2c9e67ec29c5952df66bd83c984,ea8be3e3b3d7195ddc85512be460b6f8</data>
      <data key="d3">1053</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">3191</data>
      <data key="d7">fde7705d30454b39881df238e7afbc53</data>
    </node>
