    <node id="ADENOHIP&#211;FISIS">
      <data key="d0">GEO</data>
      <data key="d1">The adenohip&#243;fisis, also known as the anterior pituitary, is the front part of the pituitary gland responsible for the production of several hormones. Among these hormones are prolactin and gonadotropins, which play crucial roles in various physiological processes within the body.</data>
      <data key="d2">78b093da6669fb9ed3258ed62a73260e,9ea2863d43f4c93ae3681a0faf1a0a3d</data>
      <data key="d3">1294</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">15</data>
      <data key="d6">3676</data>
      <data key="d7">c20a7d3adc5e4fdb97ca2c31c130997c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NEUROHIP&#211;FISIS">
      <data key="d0">GEO</data>
      <data key="d1">The neurohip&#243;fisis, or neurohypophysis, is the posterior part of the pituitary gland. It plays a crucial role in the endocrine system by storing and releasing hormones that are produced in the hypothalamus. Among the key hormones released by the neurohip&#243;fisis are oxytocin and vasopressin, also known as antidiuretic hormone (ADH). These hormones are essential for various physiological functions, including regulating water balance and facilitating childbirth.</data>
      <data key="d2">4f92e69f74f9c3ceabd5aa23ddc2b591,78b093da6669fb9ed3258ed62a73260e,c6994d09848639470e57b0ab0b7166cf</data>
      <data key="d3">1324</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">3677</data>
      <data key="d7">35cfddae3955423fbbbd6c6a5827353b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BOLSA DE RATHKE">
      <data key="d0">GEO</data>
      <data key="d1">La bolsa de Rathke es una estructura embrionaria que da origen a la adenohip&#243;fisis durante el desarrollo fetal.</data>
      <data key="d2">78b093da6669fb9ed3258ed62a73260e</data>
      <data key="d3">1294</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">3678</data>
      <data key="d7">9e8668280aee4eabb8de8fa2fd55b721</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIPOTALAMO">
      <data key="d0">GEO</data>
      <data key="d1">El hipot&#225;lamo es una regi&#243;n del cerebro que regula la secreci&#243;n de hormonas hipofisarias a trav&#233;s de hormonas adenohipofisotropas.</data>
      <data key="d2">78b093da6669fb9ed3258ed62a73260e</data>
      <data key="d3">1294</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">3679</data>
      <data key="d7">18912d19e39b4437b9842200c0c22af4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROLACTINA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Prolactina es una hormona producida por la adenohip&#243;fisis, que desempe&#241;a un papel crucial en la regulaci&#243;n de la lactancia. Su producci&#243;n est&#225; bajo el control inhibidor de la dopamina, lo que significa que la dopamina act&#250;a para limitar la secreci&#243;n de prolactina en el organismo.</data>
      <data key="d2">08a470f7ca590eb337a66093ccb5c198,78b093da6669fb9ed3258ed62a73260e</data>
      <data key="d3">1294</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">3680</data>
      <data key="d7">6948b46be8b149be83d72112e55fe7f5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TSH">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">TSH, or Thyroid-Stimulating Hormone, is a crucial hormone produced by the adenohypophysis (anterior pituitary gland) that plays a significant role in regulating thyroid function. It stimulates the thyroid gland to produce thyroid hormones, which are essential for metabolism, growth, and overall bodily functions. TSH levels are often measured in blood tests to evaluate thyroid function and can indicate various health conditions.

In patients with conditions such as hypophysitis, TSH may be deficient, leading to alterations in thyroid hormone synthesis. Additionally, low TSH levels can be observed in individuals with Anorexia Nerviosa, reflecting changes in the thyroid axis due to malnutrition. The regulation of TSH itself is influenced by Thyrotropin-Releasing Hormone (TRH), and its release follows a circadian pattern.

TSH is also significant in the context of diagnosing and treating hypothyroidism, particularly in patients with pituitary deficiencies. It serves as a threshold for confirmatory studies in congenital hypothyroidism and is monitored in patients with thyroid disorders to assess their thyroid function. Overall, TSH is vital for maintaining normal thyroid activity and metabolic health.</data>
      <data key="d2">08a470f7ca590eb337a66093ccb5c198,14299e85574ef5c76c7164e2c4d20a53,1f9d0f4850dde2dbd335969b016fc359,49d3f1ca700a7974db89e684f3744a40,4c65e91aa4dbec2d2ad859d07fbcb2cd,55a9e6e8ff365dac34555c938dd3c244,640676d4cf19e18530a974c0bac4cfb1,68be432b2dd3e64676989cd544f23d8c,78b093da6669fb9ed3258ed62a73260e,8ddb42b0859e58bd2f937125505ed9d5,92e235fe76b5046c18c1cb2c782e1ac2,9d100ca2bc5b797561fe3b3a4bb61586,a2303199b7587142ad661841870790ec,b18ef5c018c100e1582224fe82f03626,b972e75c5d62cc5cd76d9fc308265621,bbe700b5b290ea119e511c94a6f0c67f,be2de576112f22070e8ef8a5cb9e1017,c4f0f024f16be34d17dbbbae57042b2b,d4fb31e0ff471ed415f98bc11ca26d1d,e5bb316d81c54ddbb3d8c7d9c2f9fc14</data>
      <data key="d3">1299</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
