    </node>
    <node id="PROP-1">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">PROP-1 is a transcription factor that is the most common cause of combined pituitary deficits, leading to various hormonal deficiencies.</data>
      <data key="d2">9ea2863d43f4c93ae3681a0faf1a0a3d</data>
      <data key="d3">1345</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">3719</data>
      <data key="d7">4fc55838cc6949a695a52a49b7605466</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PIT-1">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">PIT-1, also known as POU1F1, is a crucial transcription factor that plays a significant role in the differentiation of somatotropic, lactotropic, and, to a lesser extent, thyrotropic precursor cells. This protein is essential for the proper development and function of these hormone-producing cells in the pituitary gland.</data>
      <data key="d2">9d100ca2bc5b797561fe3b3a4bb61586,9ea2863d43f4c93ae3681a0faf1a0a3d</data>
      <data key="d3">1323</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">3720</data>
      <data key="d7">358c71c7eb0b43a0b15e3e05f7c3789a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T-PIT">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">T-PIT is a transcription factor that is essential for the specific differentiation of corticotropic cells. It plays a crucial role in the development of pituitary cells and is involved in the regulation of growth hormone production.</data>
      <data key="d2">9d100ca2bc5b797561fe3b3a4bb61586,9ea2863d43f4c93ae3681a0faf1a0a3d,e717441e0bce4da41df774553a5ddcaf</data>
      <data key="d3">1323</data>
