      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">3721</data>
      <data key="d7">4f2eed9b23174af0b131b2b4480870b1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DISPLASIA SEPT&#211;PTICA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Septo-optic dysplasia is a condition characterized by optic nerve hypoplasia, pituitary hormone alterations, and midline brain defects.</data>
      <data key="d2">9ea2863d43f4c93ae3681a0faf1a0a3d</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">3722</data>
      <data key="d7">fc398f55052a4fa7b9fd719428735484</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIPOPITUITARISMO">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">HIPOPITUITARISMO, or hypopituitarism, is a medical condition characterized by the insufficient production of one or more hormones by the pituitary gland. This condition can lead to a variety of health issues, including hormonal deficiencies and clinical symptoms such as amenorrhea primary. The pituitary gland may fail to produce adequate amounts of hormones, including growth hormone, which is crucial for various bodily functions. Hypopituitarism can manifest as either partial or total loss of pituitary hormonal function and is often discussed within the field of endocrinology, particularly concerning the treatment and monitoring of growth hormone levels. Additionally, it may require management in patients with pituitary tumors and can occur as an adverse effect of certain treatments, such as ipilimumab. Overall, hypopituitarism represents a significant clinical syndrome that necessitates careful evaluation and management.</data>
      <data key="d2">40253f8b49d61452dbc56454cd1a07e0,404f3831a7bac02ee23cb4d4f4f2b0e9,4faa9b46acd7540904fc5b0772bb7c51,55a9e6e8ff365dac34555c938dd3c244,68c1e1d1d60cf84e8ba37450cd6dc7d3,742d2991389effe376f335dad6fbafa1,9d100ca2bc5b797561fe3b3a4bb61586,9ea2863d43f4c93ae3681a0faf1a0a3d,b972e75c5d62cc5cd76d9fc308265621,fa557ef3cae0faa594a71dc7e2d84cdc</data>
      <data key="d3">1345</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">34</data>
      <data key="d6">3723</data>
      <data key="d7">58f143074edf48f58dae2b65d612229e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HORMONAS HIPOFISARIAS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Hormonas hipofisarias are hormones produced by the pituitary gland that play a vital role in regulating various endocrine functions within the body. These hormones are essential for numerous bodily processes, including growth, metabolism, and reproduction. They are particularly significant in the context of hormone replacement therapy, especially for individuals experiencing deficiencies due to conditions such as hypopituitarism. Hormonas hipofisarias include hormones like ACTH and prolactin, which can be influenced by various factors, including mutations in transcription factors and conditions such as hypophysitis. Overall, the proper functioning of these hormones is crucial for maintaining overall health and well-being.</data>
      <data key="d2">1de72f0ea3a981289854857b691b52af,55a9e6e8ff365dac34555c938dd3c244,6083387b51909347a13ed5aa1c92bb26,9ea2863d43f4c93ae3681a0faf1a0a3d,a1403e657780088123382f4725789e67,df1d5ee8cb182840589e630b67691942,fa557ef3cae0faa594a71dc7e2d84cdc</data>
      <data key="d3">1362</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">3724</data>
      <data key="d7">d73a70cefaee494fb4020e114c8c0f41</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GEN&#201;TICAS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">GEN&#201;TICAS refers to the genetic factors that play a significant role in various medical conditions. One of the primary etiologies of hypopituitarism, a condition characterized by hormonal deficiencies, is linked to genetic influences. Additionally, germline mutations in the prolactin receptor gene have been associated with hyperprolactinemia, a condition marked by elevated levels of prolactin in the blood. This highlights the importance of genetic factors in both hormonal regulation and related disorders.</data>
      <data key="d2">2b6e6d589cd4e83086e44c77205b3740,9ea2863d43f4c93ae3681a0faf1a0a3d</data>
      <data key="d3">1345</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">3725</data>
      <data key="d7">8fad01b3304245c98d0305a982fd7f61</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ADULTOS">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">"ADULTOS" refers to adults, who are individuals that have reached maturity and possess different nutritional requirements compared to children and infants. In the context of dietary guidelines for managing diabetes, adults are included as they generally tolerate starch and glucose polymers; however, they should avoid these if symptoms persist on a sucrose-free diet. Adults over the age of 19 are specifically referenced in energy requirement calculations. Additionally, adults can experience health issues such as adrenal insufficiency due to hypopituitarism and may face complications like osteomalacia or osteoporosis, particularly those with inflammatory bowel disease (EII), which can arise from malnutrition and other related factors. To maintain metabolic health, adults are advised to follow a balanced diet that includes corn starch and other essential nutrients. Furthermore, while adults may benefit from a ketogenic diet, its effectiveness may diminish over time due to low adherence to the dietary regimen.</data>
      <data key="d2">0a9b42ff082f2d0bb036ec3365d9a6e9,658ee56030a35ceeeec05d15cfa49842,9ea2863d43f4c93ae3681a0faf1a0a3d,a079902e52676a05df746098c930a0e7,bd31c6face847551241359e37e4baa2e,c958206b5546e8b46ab48b3f50e60611,df73c75d84a055e0a67bc7f14d35e9d5,eaa2ba81b06f325e284b5dbdc45b5c9f</data>
      <data key="d3">619</data>
