    </node>
    <node id="CRANEORAFINGIOMAS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Craneofaringiomas, also known as craniopharyngiomas, are tumors that can impact the pituitary gland, potentially resulting in hormonal deficiencies such as hypopituitarism. These tumors originate from remnants of Rathke's pouch and account for approximately 1-3% of all brain tumors. They exhibit a bimodal distribution, primarily affecting both children and adults.</data>
      <data key="d2">39838811d935b8d580e419ac87ef32ab,fa557ef3cae0faa594a71dc7e2d84cdc</data>
      <data key="d3">1346</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">4</data>
      <data key="d6">3734</data>
      <data key="d7">6413727aeee54dafbc888871043f2dee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SARCOIDOSIS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Sarcoidosis is an inflammatory disease that can impact various organs, including the pituitary gland. This condition may lead to several complications, such as diabetes insipidus and hypopituitarism, both of which are related to the dysfunction of the pituitary gland. Additionally, sarcoidosis can cause hypercalcemia, which results from the increased endogenous production of calcitriol. Overall, sarcoidosis presents a range of potential health issues, particularly concerning endocrine function and calcium metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d2">4faa9b46acd7540904fc5b0772bb7c51,ae9d1fec31d651598e2db7436382c906,fa557ef3cae0faa594a71dc7e2d84cdc</data>
