    <node id="PRL">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Prolactin (PRL) is a hormone produced by the pituitary gland that plays a crucial role in lactation, promoting milk production in mammals. In addition to its primary function in lactation, PRL is involved in the regulation of the menstrual cycle and has various roles in metabolism, immune system regulation, and the development of the reproductive system. Elevated levels of prolactin can occur in certain medical conditions, such as pituitary adenomas, particularly in patients with Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia type 1 (MEN1), and in individuals with acromegaly. Overall, PRL is essential for reproductive health and has significant implications in various physiological processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">2b6e6d589cd4e83086e44c77205b3740,2e73034a100b1adb0a00561c08416bda,4c65e91aa4dbec2d2ad859d07fbcb2cd,9d100ca2bc5b797561fe3b3a4bb61586,fbba0b43850ff5e50b710069959c2416</data>
