    </node>
    <node id="DIABETES INS&#205;PIDA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Diabetes ins&#237;pida is a medical condition characterized by excessive thirst (polydipsia) and the excretion of large amounts of severely diluted urine (polyuria), leading to an imbalance in body fluids. This disorder arises from the body's inability to concentrate urine, often due to dysfunctions in the hypothalamus or pituitary gland. It can be associated with neurogenic causes, such as necrosis of the neurohypophysis, and may also occur as a result of untreated adrenal insufficiency. In some cases, it is linked to the presence of pituitary adenomas or hypothalamic damage due to pineal tumors, which disrupt the regulation of water in the body. The condition is discussed in the context of treatment options, including glucocorticoid replacement therapy, as outlined in the Manual de Endocrinolog&#237;a y Nutrici&#243;n.</data>
      <data key="d2">2b390802e6a6df561cafb9593e97d6bd,309cfad754049a78b0459a3e71f496ef,55a9e6e8ff365dac34555c938dd3c244,67fc85f14d95a9a24054c2a3ec125e37,755475a08c9b594dacee6386b782065f,773a1daa539dd85e562a59e09f434af1,9b9d487a35d8197bfe9d1aa0fce19c32,c6994d09848639470e57b0ab0b7166cf,e5bb316d81c54ddbb3d8c7d9c2f9fc14</data>
      <data key="d3">1324</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">17</data>
      <data key="d6">3753</data>
      <data key="d7">fd1c3dc0f2504cffa8ec196e64920335</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLUCOCORTICOIDES">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Glucocorticoides, or glucocorticoids, are a class of steroid hormones that play a significant role in regulating metabolism and the body's response to stress. They are commonly used in the treatment of various medical conditions, including adrenal insufficiency and adrenal disorders. These hormones can affect sex hormone-binding globulin (SHBG) levels and overall hormonal balance. 

In clinical practice, glucocorticoids are utilized to manage conditions such as congenital adrenal hyperplasia, where they help control hormonal levels. Additionally, they can reduce aldosterone production in specific situations. However, the use of glucocorticoids is associated with certain risks, including an increased likelihood of fractures and the potential development of Cushing's syndrome due to their effects on the body.

Overall, glucocorticoids are important medications in the management of various endocrine and metabolic disorders, but their administration requires careful consideration of dosage adjustments and monitoring for adverse effects.</data>
      <data key="d2">1400aceda067bb7180ca90f0a5335bfe,15cc53e1fcf2a1afd24bf25a2b059f98,4beab004731d7baff10fd2796a523b55,55a9e6e8ff365dac34555c938dd3c244,62abcb4e755766817e948341a56b196b,8eb2b7021402eb440fe88889b78c2785,b30c94def1763c77fe1fc302cc896d6c,dcbf695abeb609e2cdbff56c1bde675c</data>
      <data key="d3">1290</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">11</data>
      <data key="d6">3754</data>
      <data key="d7">cc53d79a14bc45528fb21b5c0e037cba</data>
    </node>
