    <node id="SWEDEN">
      <data key="d0">GEO</data>
      <data key="d1">Sweden is a country known for its significant contributions to medical research, particularly in the fields of cardiovascular disease and bariatric surgery. It has been the site of various studies examining cardiovascular disease and mortality rates following bariatric surgery, including a nationwide observational cohort study that provided valuable insights into these health issues. Additionally, Sweden has conducted research on the incidence and prevalence of TSH-secreting pituitary adenomas, further highlighting its role in advancing medical knowledge. Furthermore, Sweden is one of the five northern European countries that played a crucial role in the establishment of the Enhanced Recovery After Surgery (ERAS) group, which focuses on improving surgical recovery protocols.</data>
      <data key="d2">172da539e648a8f72cc1576d16e35f46,980d6217f6ec073bf1673f14542ebbf0,b0a60df651e774a77b1969babdf3b21a,ecd0e2cc6ed4a480591f4ac32cd7f691</data>
      <data key="d3">520</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">3756</data>
      <data key="d7">506943a7ae40492996842053b4c19093</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CUSHING'S SYNDROME">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Cushing's syndrome is a hormonal disorder characterized by excessive levels of cortisol in the body, often resulting from prolonged exposure to this hormone. The condition can arise due to various causes, including pituitary adenomas, which are tumors in the pituitary gland that produce adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH), leading to increased cortisol production. Symptoms of Cushing's syndrome may include weight gain, skin changes, muscle weakness, elevated cortisol levels in urine, and disordered gonadal function. The disorder is associated with various endocrine disorders and can lead to significant health issues if left untreated.

Diagnosis of Cushing's syndrome requires specific methods to confirm the presence of high cortisol levels and to identify the underlying cause. Treatment options typically involve medications that inhibit steroidogenesis or cortisol synthesis, aiming to reduce cortisol levels and alleviate symptoms. Overall, Cushing's syndrome necessitates a comprehensive approach to diagnosis and management due to its complex nature and the range of clinical manifestations it presents.</data>
      <data key="d2">05a6995cef9c8d0279b9f026da1ffed1,172da539e648a8f72cc1576d16e35f46,4c034422eb6302c32e0d5fdce5b3046d,7082a5d31b18dd81d4c4a0e8ed02623b,7f3df1437755b60344fdffd2ef3b8a9e,85eef7ed9c9b92d86939a6687ea2acfd,88a8ebb15abeea8eaded56ef3d2df73f,9ff14a3c49bd56b2fb12b3fd8ed246de,b2ca509109e853875e707e29a98391e0,bcbca87f5ebf2c5d260810cf83a70257,dbe034ccd694ae06cf72d347d7dbdacb</data>
      <data key="d3">1332</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">44</data>
      <data key="d6">3757</data>
      <data key="d7">ba87908d9b5b49aca0d8e3dda17eb621</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYPOTHALAMUS">
      <data key="d0">GEO</data>
      <data key="d1">The hypothalamus is a critical region of the brain that plays a vital role in controlling the pituitary gland and regulating various hormonal functions within the body. It is integral to the endocrine system, influencing the secretion of adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) from the pituitary gland. Additionally, the hypothalamus is involved in the dysregulation that can lead to hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, particularly in patients suffering from Anorexia Nervosa. This highlights its importance not only in general hormone regulation but also in specific medical conditions related to hormonal imbalances.</data>
      <data key="d2">172da539e648a8f72cc1576d16e35f46,1f9d0f4850dde2dbd335969b016fc359,b2ca509109e853875e707e29a98391e0</data>
      <data key="d3">1596</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">3758</data>
      <data key="d7">40536e6aa4cd49c2af8d894b97b6d1c7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYPOTHALAMIC-PITUITARY AXIS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">The hypothalamic-pituitary axis is a complex and critical regulatory system in the body that encompasses a set of interactions between the hypothalamus and the pituitary gland. This axis plays a vital role in regulating numerous bodily functions by influencing the function of the pituitary gland and the hormones it produces.</data>
      <data key="d2">172da539e648a8f72cc1576d16e35f46</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">3759</data>
      <data key="d7">2295f2e88fd64e3b9e3dc6eaa858829c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CORTICOTROPINOMA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Corticotropinoma is a type of pituitary adenoma that produces ACTH and can lead to Cushing's syndrome.</data>
      <data key="d2">172da539e648a8f72cc1576d16e35f46</data>
      <data key="d3">1332</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">3760</data>
      <data key="d7">88d3d5469d3f4e518c174c5ecbaa8b1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MACROADENOMA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">MACROADENOMA refers to a type of pituitary adenoma that is characterized by its size, measuring larger than 1 cm. This condition is more prevalent in women and can lead to various symptoms due to the tumor's size and location. One of the significant effects of a macroadenoma is the production of excess prolactin, which can result in hyperprolactinemia. Additionally, macroadenomas are commonly associated with Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia type 1 (MEN1). The presence of a macroadenoma can also lead to hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, further complicating the clinical picture. Overall, macroadenomas are notable for their potential to cause a range of hormonal and physical symptoms due to their impact on the pituitary gland.</data>
      <data key="d2">172da539e648a8f72cc1576d16e35f46,2261671ccad439da1ed9ea5a27857dc8,2b6e6d589cd4e83086e44c77205b3740,e5bb316d81c54ddbb3d8c7d9c2f9fc14,fbba0b43850ff5e50b710069959c2416</data>
      <data key="d3">1315</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">5</data>
      <data key="d6">3761</data>
      <data key="d7">8845c0f010ef4544a71f6f0bee540abd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYPOTHYROIDISM">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Hypothyroidism is a common endocrine disorder characterized by the insufficient production of thyroid hormones by the thyroid gland. This condition can be associated with various factors, including Klinefelter syndrome, which is a genetic condition that can lead to hormonal imbalances. Additionally, hypothyroidism can be induced by the medication amiodarone, which affects thyroid function and may result in decreased hormone production. The condition can also be related to pituitary function, indicating that issues with the pituitary gland may contribute to the development of hypothyroidism. Overall, hypothyroidism is a multifaceted disorder that can arise from genetic, medication-related, and endocrine system factors.</data>
      <data key="d2">0d78e460e4fb4a82bb6e11496f7d5aed,172da539e648a8f72cc1576d16e35f46,45213238a7d4c834b6db62ef71dcda43,5509bb6959e4faa5989289c1ddbcc96b</data>
      <data key="d3">173</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">3762</data>
      <data key="d7">448d9b190efa488e870c1044cc118413</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYPOPITUITARISM">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Hypopituitarism is a medical condition characterized by the insufficient production of certain hormones by the pituitary gland. This condition can arise in various contexts, including in patients with hereditary hemochromatosis, a genetic disorder that can lead to decreased hormone production from the pituitary gland.</data>
      <data key="d2">172da539e648a8f72cc1576d16e35f46,83d2bb17e643e047bb3ad2ecc88d8271</data>
      <data key="d3">173</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">4</data>
      <data key="d6">3763</data>
      <data key="d7">64b0ba86d1b241cf851535cc14e2dba2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROLACTINOMAS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Prolactinomas are a specific type of pituitary adenoma characterized by the overproduction of prolactin, a hormone that can lead to a range of health complications. These tumors are particularly prevalent among patients with Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia type 1 (MEN1), a genetic condition that affects the endocrine glands. The excess prolactin produced by prolactinomas can result in various symptoms and health issues, making them significant in the context of endocrine disorders.</data>
      <data key="d2">172da539e648a8f72cc1576d16e35f46,fbba0b43850ff5e50b710069959c2416</data>
      <data key="d3">1315</data>
