    <node id="GL&#193;NDULA PINEAL">
      <data key="d0">GEO</data>
      <data key="d1">La gl&#225;ndula pineal es una peque&#241;a gl&#225;ndula endocrina en el cerebro que produce melatonina y est&#225; relacionada con varios tumores, como los germinomas.</data>
      <data key="d2">309cfad754049a78b0459a3e71f496ef</data>
      <data key="d3">1343</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">3781</data>
      <data key="d7">d8e0cc3eb59e457ea890a2d04a93d07b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GERMINOMAS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Los germinomas son un tipo de tumor que se origina en las c&#233;lulas germinales y son los m&#225;s frecuentes en la gl&#225;ndula pineal.</data>
      <data key="d2">309cfad754049a78b0459a3e71f496ef</data>
      <data key="d3">1343</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">3782</data>
      <data key="d7">db1767c50a204a2dab623c4d98bf1834</data>
    </node>
    <node id="S&#205;NDROME DE PARINAUD">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">El s&#237;ndrome de Parinaud es un conjunto de s&#237;ntomas neurol&#243;gicos que puede presentarse en pacientes con tumores en la regi&#243;n pineal, as&#237; como en aquellos con tumores en la regi&#243;n selar, como los adenomas hipofisarios. Uno de los s&#237;ntomas caracter&#237;sticos del s&#237;ndrome de Parinaud es la par&#225;lisis de la mirada superior, que puede afectar la capacidad del paciente para mover los ojos hacia arriba. Este s&#237;ndrome es relevante en el contexto de tumores que afectan estas &#225;reas del cerebro, indicando la necesidad de una evaluaci&#243;n neurol&#243;gica en pacientes con tales condiciones.</data>
      <data key="d2">309cfad754049a78b0459a3e71f496ef,755475a08c9b594dacee6386b782065f</data>
      <data key="d3">1343</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">3783</data>
      <data key="d7">53f63d08756a405498477a37594b6404</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HCG">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Human Chorionic Gonadotropin (HCG) is a hormone produced during pregnancy that plays a crucial role in stimulating thyroid hormone production and influencing TSH (Thyroid Stimulating Hormone) levels. In addition to its role in pregnancy, HCG is utilized in medical treatments to stimulate testosterone production in males. It is important to note that HCG can also be produced by tumors of the pineal gland, which may lead to effects on the production of both testosterone and estrogens. Furthermore, HCG can cause secondary hyperthyroidism by binding to the TSH receptor. Overall, HCG is a multifaceted hormone with significant implications in both reproductive health and endocrine function.</data>
      <data key="d2">0cf04c95a8e6bbdf9201bf3fff9103f6,1776df2694e83680f59e035be5ffb00b,309cfad754049a78b0459a3e71f496ef,4c18507e3d98242cdbcddd1a1997b92f,92e235fe76b5046c18c1cb2c782e1ac2</data>
      <data key="d3">1299</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">3784</data>
      <data key="d7">4590549754c84ee3b758e4928c30bef9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AIP">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">AIP is a tumor suppressor gene that plays a significant role in the development of pituitary adenomas. It is particularly associated with isolated familial pituitary adenomas (FIPA). Additionally, the AIP gene may also be linked to the formation of craniopharyngiomas.</data>
      <data key="d2">309cfad754049a78b0459a3e71f496ef,39838811d935b8d580e419ac87ef32ab,640676d4cf19e18530a974c0bac4cfb1</data>
      <data key="d3">1346</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">4</data>
      <data key="d6">3785</data>
      <data key="d7">7371ef2cfac6498787670a31ba140ad0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEN1">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">MEN1, or Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia Type 1, is a hereditary genetic syndrome that significantly increases the risk of developing multiple endocrine tumors. This condition is characterized by the formation of tumors in various endocrine glands, including the parathyroid glands, anterior pituitary, and pancreatic islet cells. Among the tumors associated with MEN1 are gastrinomas, which produce the gastrin hormone, and insulinomas, which are linked to a prevalence of 6-10% in cases of insulinoma. MEN1 is recognized as a tumor suppressor gene and is a focus of clinical guidelines and treatment strategies aimed at managing the associated risks of both endocrine and non-endocrine tumors. Overall, MEN1 is a complex syndrome that necessitates careful monitoring and management due to its multifaceted impact on endocrine health.</data>
      <data key="d2">02b8a693303f9f8603c740fefc53d526,11fcae49d6b540a5ad5cdfe74ed269a9,2a73a65aa862d055c1e417cc50a42166,2ad120e12741c91b54f0eee4e16a3022,309cfad754049a78b0459a3e71f496ef,7e4e3e944e1b58b8d92e93ee4a9f33cc,8f51c67fbf44368dcfc2d7c767e2cef6,9851218c3244d6f4279055a04d219044,fbba0b43850ff5e50b710069959c2416</data>
      <data key="d3">1310</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">29</data>
      <data key="d6">3786</data>
      <data key="d7">a5f5a83253d04fc6877cb569d02f743f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PRKARIA">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">PRKARIA es un gen que act&#250;a como supresor tumoral y est&#225; implicado en la patogenia de los adenomas hipofisarios.</data>
      <data key="d2">309cfad754049a78b0459a3e71f496ef</data>
      <data key="d3">1323</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">3787</data>
      <data key="d7">588728d43f044e2388366d651e6f9cd9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RB">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">RB es un gen supresor tumoral que se ha asociado con la aparici&#243;n de adenomas hipofisarios.</data>
      <data key="d2">309cfad754049a78b0459a3e71f496ef</data>
      <data key="d3">1323</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">3788</data>
      <data key="d7">ed409d3e6b664f4ea031475361f253a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GNAS">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">GNAS is a gene that encodes the alpha subunit of the stimulatory G protein, playing a crucial role in the regulation of hormone signaling pathways, particularly those associated with growth hormone. This gene can be mutated in pituitary adenomas, which are tumors that can affect hormone production. GNAS is recognized as an oncogene and is particularly implicated in the formation of pituitary adenomas, especially somatotroph adenomas, which are characterized by excessive growth hormone secretion.</data>
      <data key="d2">2e73034a100b1adb0a00561c08416bda,309cfad754049a78b0459a3e71f496ef,755475a08c9b594dacee6386b782065f</data>
      <data key="d3">1322</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">3789</data>
      <data key="d7">ebea37213a76488d831dbdfbd9986e96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="H-RAS">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">H-RAS es un oncog&#233;n que se ha implicado en la aparici&#243;n de adenomas hipofisarios.</data>
      <data key="d2">309cfad754049a78b0459a3e71f496ef</data>
      <data key="d3">1323</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">3790</data>
      <data key="d7">8edd38d7b4894304ac1f490d6cc8c6eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CCND1">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">CCND1 es un oncog&#233;n relacionado con la proliferaci&#243;n celular y se ha implicado en adenomas hipofisarios.</data>
      <data key="d2">309cfad754049a78b0459a3e71f496ef</data>
      <data key="d3">1323</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
