      <data key="d6">3906</data>
      <data key="d7">115d5bd8611b4e8f840585b07b2f6a81</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BUPROPION">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Bupropion is a medication that has been approved for the treatment of depression and for aiding smoking cessation. It functions as a norepinephrine and dopamine reuptake inhibitor, which means it helps to increase the levels of these neurotransmitters in the brain. Additionally, bupropion is known to influence appetite regulation and reward mechanisms. Notably, it does not appear to affect prolactin levels, making it a unique option among antidepressants.</data>
      <data key="d2">3eddab8e3e55bbd7e6cbcd3776fecb29,488970234520652b063db88e8346031c,b344a34a623d4ade6b3ed6e1adae0016</data>
      <data key="d3">1432</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">11</data>
      <data key="d6">3907</data>
      <data key="d7">d0665070d804434c996ab2e9f38b6e3c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANTIPSICOTICOS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Antipsychotics, known as "antipsic&#243;ticos" in Spanish, are medications primarily used to treat psychiatric disorders. They are particularly noted for being the most common pharmacological cause of hyperprolactinemia, especially among first-generation antipsychotics. Additionally, some antipsychotics are associated with the development of the Syndrome of Inappropriate Antidiuretic Hormone secretion (SIADH).</data>
      <data key="d2">b344a34a623d4ade6b3ed6e1adae0016,c5ebf416dfb2be58a226e15fb9771c86</data>
      <data key="d3">1318</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">3908</data>
      <data key="d7">1203af12f5604ed7a405eed812687a41</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AMISULPIRIDA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Amisulpride is another second-generation antipsychotic linked to frequent and marked increases in prolactin levels.</data>
      <data key="d2">b344a34a623d4ade6b3ed6e1adae0016</data>
      <data key="d3">1318</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">3909</data>
      <data key="d7">bbd7f03fd0cf46a4ba51ca42bb014769</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SINDROME DE CUSHING">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Cushing's syndrome is a condition caused by chronic exposure to high levels of cortisol, often due to a pituitary tumor producing ACTH.</data>
      <data key="d2">b344a34a623d4ade6b3ed6e1adae0016</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">3910</data>
      <data key="d7">f124a3ead112400c88a509ba1feb3274</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIPOFISIS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">The pituitary gland, also known as the hipofisis, is a small gland situated at the base of the brain. It plays a crucial role in the endocrine system by producing various hormones, one of which is adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH). The presence of tumors in the pituitary gland can lead to the overproduction of ACTH, potentially resulting in Cushing's syndrome, a condition characterized by excessive cortisol levels in the body.</data>
      <data key="d2">b344a34a623d4ade6b3ed6e1adae0016,c5ebf416dfb2be58a226e15fb9771c86</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">3911</data>
      <data key="d7">4c70043757d646e4981e8abcc5519385</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TAYLOR D">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Taylor D is an author of a publication discussing antidepressant-induced hyperprolactinemia.</data>
      <data key="d2">b344a34a623d4ade6b3ed6e1adae0016</data>
