    </node>
    <node id="HYPERINSULINISM">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Hyperinsulinism is a metabolic condition characterized by excessive levels of insulin in the body. This condition often leads to hypoglycemia, which is a dangerously low level of glucose in the blood. Hyperinsulinism can be associated with insulinoma, a type of tumor that produces excess insulin. Additionally, it can arise due to the effects of excess growth hormone, further complicating the metabolic balance.</data>
      <data key="d2">2261671ccad439da1ed9ea5a27857dc8,2a73a65aa862d055c1e417cc50a42166</data>
      <data key="d3">886</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">3984</data>
      <data key="d7">aea13a9115df4878a7bb121b89c73292</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYPERTIGLYCERIDEMIA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Hypertriglyceridemia is a metabolic condition characterized by elevated levels of triglycerides in the blood. This condition often necessitates management, particularly in patients receiving nutritional support. Additionally, hypertriglyceridemia can be associated with acromegaly, indicating a potential link between these two health issues.</data>
      <data key="d2">2261671ccad439da1ed9ea5a27857dc8,814d394c907e59b29a5f0d9e82aed423</data>
      <data key="d3">886</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">3985</data>
      <data key="d7">7a45456db50145808958bcfe78326967</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYPERCALCIURIA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Hypercalciuria is a medical condition characterized by the presence of excessive calcium in the urine. This condition is often studied in relation to the formation of kidney stones, as the elevated levels of calcium can contribute to their development. Additionally, hypercalciuria may occur in patients with acromegaly, a disorder resulting from excess growth hormone, which can lead to various metabolic changes affecting calcium levels.</data>
      <data key="d2">2261671ccad439da1ed9ea5a27857dc8,550574bce077f34348bd11c78dc814a1,f757219e84dba985f7c8d66ac951b9f2</data>
      <data key="d3">1583</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">3986</data>
      <data key="d7">561d57b5b4ce461196740f6472c59913</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYPERPHOSPHATEMIA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Hyperphosphatemia is a medical condition characterized by elevated levels of phosphate in the blood. This condition can occur in various populations, including preterm infants, and is often discussed in the context of dietary phosphorus management, particularly in patients with chronic kidney disease. Additionally, hyperphosphatemia can be associated with metabolic imbalances linked to excess growth hormone levels. Overall, it is important to monitor and manage phosphate levels to prevent potential complications related to this condition.</data>
      <data key="d2">08bc4a83d18482b48f5bbef4fbf316f3,2261671ccad439da1ed9ea5a27857dc8,9ef354c0ee5ac877ce8fba79fcc7e2b3</data>
