    <node id="GLUCAGON">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">GLUCAGON is a hormone produced by the alpha cells in the pancreatic islets of the pancreas. Its primary function is to elevate blood glucose levels, which it achieves by promoting gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis, processes that convert glycogen into glucose. This hormone plays a crucial role in glucose metabolism and acts as a counter-regulatory hormone to insulin, particularly important in the context of hypoglycemia. Additionally, glucagon's release can be suppressed during hypoglycemic episodes. It is also noted for its potential role in regulating metabolism during parenteral nutrition and is particularly relevant in the management of Type 1 Diabetes and glycogen storage diseases.</data>
      <data key="d2">0cdbb308e3359fd3ddbb1d828d12dc04,46466da830644a88f74108645c668fd5,50fafa000d5f2830cbf66f3201657396,5b487245068b8e8b87ffcc04970aaff4,704cfd179507a977ecc0691a5bd2861e,7b084f7afc5f3c66a528475ae4fa76b1,bcded135629c889cb0a10ae968743582,d4fb31e0ff471ed415f98bc11ca26d1d,ed9fbb82a9162d601f8370ef97810e54</data>
      <data key="d3">1713</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">5</data>
      <data key="d6">4080</data>
      <data key="d7">02667b880edc4221a36d754c7d696c96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AUMENTO DE LA HIGIENE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Increased hygiene has been associated with a higher incidence of Type 1 Diabetes, suggesting a link between environmental factors and the disease.</data>
      <data key="d2">ed9fbb82a9162d601f8370ef97810e54</data>
