    </node>
    <node id="DIABETES MELLITUS TIPO 1">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Diabetes Mellitus Tipo 1 (DM1) is a chronic autoimmune condition characterized by the destruction of insulin-producing beta cells in the pancreas, leading to little or no insulin production. This condition requires continuous and careful management of insulin and dietary intake to maintain healthy blood glucose levels. Patients with DM1 often face challenges related to poor glycemic control, which can complicate their overall health management. 

Additionally, Diabetes Mellitus Tipo 1 is associated with a higher risk of developing eating disorders, particularly in young women. It frequently coexists with other autoimmune conditions, such as thyroid diseases and celiac disease. The patient has been managing this condition for 6 years, highlighting the long-term commitment required to effectively control the disease and mitigate its associated risks.</data>
      <data key="d2">1033f1626ca6ac2b3bab298805a17aec,4885c129837f58f091d24d2cbc8aae66,48f1fe343d54cd0f353be34b736a352c,71a67838e4fbd705b0fb3b14b20664ee,77fb6e19a2882617993d1d283afcb86a,7949b821c1ca2e4adc524e7775626396,806e7b517eea737f1b254b699db60046,84ce554b4fe9832d90b74a225fe956b9,a960ccbf15a28151d1461c4203408408,abf638322cfb1c522bfbee1852e28a19,d0231ac4e41b0e826d80763b5b8c61e0</data>
      <data key="d3">1073</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">19</data>
      <data key="d6">4092</data>
      <data key="d7">e22a43a9fddd4bd7be7c90c02cdf91db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CROMOSOMA 6P21">
      <data key="d0">GEO</data>
      <data key="d1">Chromosome 6p21 is the location of the major susceptibility locus for type 1 diabetes, containing genes related to the immune response.</data>
      <data key="d2">d0231ac4e41b0e826d80763b5b8c61e0</data>
      <data key="d3">1073</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">4093</data>
      <data key="d7">3ac11218625e4715ae2895e9e8462c99</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIABETES MELLITUS TYPE 2">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Diabetes Mellitus Type 2 (DM2) is a chronic condition characterized by insulin resistance and high blood sugar levels. It primarily affects the way the body processes blood sugar (glucose) and is often associated with obesity and other risk factors. This form of diabetes typically develops in adults and is marked by relative insulin deficiency. Management of Diabetes Mellitus Type 2 often involves lifestyle changes, including exercise and diet, and may also require medication. The condition is commonly linked to obesity, highlighting the importance of weight management in its prevention and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d2">043ed163c427b880fe39884076cae7ea,06fe3811ad957d6841997b2a0e9be50f,34d62296f09da1a452febcc9ab8c18ad,3defb74705676a025b15398e300eb230,64821313d6eb9af473ab515aff2ba3c7,6a2d5ced0ad16871fec539845797e0ac,784f8d7f49cdce2d0611be585eda7e2c,a3e3a651e24883d279405241439b58bc,aa9279dacd764b478618ae36af93ad83,fe5f390fb67fd3b1ad222db3500574b9</data>
      <data key="d3">1602</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">34</data>
      <data key="d6">4094</data>
      <data key="d7">32cba02386c84144b6582acabb35f341</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLUCEMIA PLASM&#193;TICA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">GLUCEMIA PLASM&#193;TICA refers to the measurement of glucose levels in the blood, specifically in the plasma. This measurement is crucial for diagnosing conditions such as diabetes and prediabetes. Additionally, glucemia plasm&#225;tica is utilized to evaluate carbohydrate metabolism, providing important insights into an individual's metabolic health.</data>
      <data key="d2">34d62296f09da1a452febcc9ab8c18ad,48e690b80c196e397d9ab9527b38c3d4</data>
      <data key="d3">884</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">4095</data>
      <data key="d7">c6656b0d8a854553abc197021796cca9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PREDIABETES">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Prediabetes is a condition characterized by blood sugar levels that are elevated beyond the normal range but not sufficiently high to be classified as diabetes. This condition signifies an increased risk for developing type 2 diabetes (DM2). In the case of Mar&#237;a, her glucose levels indicated that she may have prediabetes, suggesting that she is at a heightened risk for progressing to diabetes if lifestyle changes are not implemented.</data>
      <data key="d2">34d62296f09da1a452febcc9ab8c18ad,6a2d5ced0ad16871fec539845797e0ac,f1bd89f4a1fcc385a19f5c5323ee2f89</data>
      <data key="d3">865</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">5</data>
      <data key="d6">4096</data>
      <data key="d7">7d84bc794a8b47b6ac8576450b3a282c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HBA1C">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">HbA1c is a blood test that measures average blood sugar levels over the past 2 to 3 months, making it a crucial tool for managing and monitoring diabetes. It provides insights into a patient's glycemic control, with values consistently above 8.5% indicating poor management of blood glucose levels. Throughout treatment, HbA1c levels are monitored to assess the effectiveness of diabetes management strategies. In the context of the BLOOM-DM study, HbA1c serves as an important measure for evaluating diabetes control. The latest analytical results for a patient indicated an HbA1c level of 6.2%, suggesting good control of their diabetes. Overall, HbA1c is essential for both the diagnosis and ongoing management of diabetes, helping healthcare providers tailor treatment plans effectively.</data>
      <data key="d2">1033f1626ca6ac2b3bab298805a17aec,34d62296f09da1a452febcc9ab8c18ad,3f685a05a9682db6dcde958d1918cc77,5cf8b1c5491f78fda9bca97f3d14c5a2,623ef060e116bc05259d5e6d3d8c0996,6edff59a3b151e946e7664f13bcb0931,8d2d57471630a504ffa4a39c259d9b18,bfdbf41efa08067d5ae1b1a67830affd,c3975fe03d2af26b95b09795c7769e8b</data>
      <data key="d3">1080</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">5</data>
      <data key="d6">4097</data>
      <data key="d7">afa12a79c27b4739ab2d50ea0c059a02</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LATINOS">
      <data key="d0">GEO</data>
      <data key="d1">Latinos are an ethnic group identified as having a higher risk for developing Diabetes Mellitus Type 2.</data>
