      <data key="d6">4152</data>
      <data key="d7">90fd463e10d54c48b1608e8d747025ca</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INSULIN RESISTANCE">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Insulin resistance is a condition characterized by the body's cells becoming less responsive to insulin, which can lead to elevated blood glucose levels. This condition is often associated with various metabolic disorders, including type 2 diabetes and metabolic syndrome. Insulin resistance is frequently observed in individuals with obesity and is linked to cardiovascular disease. Additionally, it is commonly seen in patients with Klinefelter syndrome and Polycystic Ovary Syndrome, both of which contribute to the development of metabolic syndrome. Insulin resistance can also occur in critically ill patients, highlighting its relevance across different health contexts. Overall, insulin resistance plays a significant role in the pathophysiology of several metabolic conditions and is influenced by dietary factors and other health-related issues.</data>
      <data key="d2">4885c129837f58f091d24d2cbc8aae66,5509bb6959e4faa5989289c1ddbcc96b,67d484d5c0c84fff32782bd773c0fd7a,723cbca2d5cfbf8cf05a923c52569641,dda58e50ce4bb9fa40e3c61e40e1b9c7,ea920d08266b329db73fb7b804bbb9ce</data>
      <data key="d3">1372</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">4153</data>
      <data key="d7">7d56980486344cc5a0d70484d3f643dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLUCONEOGENESIS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Gluconeogenesis is a vital metabolic pathway that generates glucose from non-carbohydrate substrates. This process primarily occurs in the liver, with additional activity in the kidneys and intestines. It plays a crucial role in maintaining blood sugar levels, especially during fasting or periods of low carbohydrate intake. Gluconeogenesis is particularly significant in the context of glycogen storage diseases, such as Glycogen Storage Disease Type I (GSDI), and is also affected by specific deficiencies, such as fructose-1,6-bisphosphate deficiency. Furthermore, the process is heightened in critically ill patients, underscoring its importance in metabolic regulation during stress. Overall, gluconeogenesis is an anabolic pathway essential for synthesizing glucose from non-carbohydrate precursors, ensuring energy availability in various physiological states.</data>
      <data key="d2">25192e60a2c23bd74095f4602efeac4c,449046c83c1432d2b8cc6cacb5eadf51,4885c129837f58f091d24d2cbc8aae66,704cfd179507a977ecc0691a5bd2861e,b5e899a3def3da339630eb0d2bfe4af8,bcded135629c889cb0a10ae968743582,fb8d31ef68f83e6b01f6fc8548f36d0c</data>
      <data key="d3">1734</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">15</data>
      <data key="d6">4154</data>
      <data key="d7">dfdf900d78104f28b77d5c6916f8bdc1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIP&#211;LISIS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">LIP&#211;LISIS es el proceso metab&#243;lico mediante el cual se descomponen las grasas en el cuerpo, transformando los triglic&#233;ridos en &#225;cidos grasos libres y glicerol, que pueden ser utilizados como fuente de energ&#237;a. Este proceso puede verse alterado en diversas condiciones de salud, como en pacientes con c&#225;ncer, donde contribuye a la p&#233;rdida de peso, o en trastornos como la hiperlipidemia familiar combinada. Adem&#225;s, la lip&#243;lisis tiende a aumentar en pacientes cr&#237;ticamente enfermos, lo que indica su importancia en situaciones de estr&#233;s metab&#243;lico. La regulaci&#243;n de la lip&#243;lisis tambi&#233;n est&#225; influenciada por los niveles de insulina, lo que resalta su papel en el metabolismo energ&#233;tico. En el contexto del embarazo, la lip&#243;lisis se convierte en una fuente de energ&#237;a crucial para la madre.</data>
      <data key="d2">25192e60a2c23bd74095f4602efeac4c,4885c129837f58f091d24d2cbc8aae66,6c8b41c09b1ada129f0908d6e5d18185,e57266facd7498d191a9107e090446e2,ebfccc35cef320c7240cf36dd52e847a</data>
      <data key="d3">621</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">4</data>
      <data key="d6">4155</data>
      <data key="d7">bb96181f5321479690934cb470a5fad8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="S&#205;NTESIS PROTEICA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Protein synthesis is the process by which cells build proteins, essential for growth and repair, and is regulated by insulin among other factors.</data>
      <data key="d2">4885c129837f58f091d24d2cbc8aae66</data>
      <data key="d3">1372</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">4156</data>
      <data key="d7">3d00d49add9945d28c960d470c21e0e5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLUT2">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">GLUT2 is a glucose transporter protein that plays a crucial role in the transport of glucose into cells. It is particularly important in the entry of glucose into &#946;-cells, which is essential for the secretion of insulin. Additionally, GLUT2 is involved in the absorption of glucose and fructose in the intestines, highlighting its significance in both glucose metabolism and dietary sugar absorption.</data>
      <data key="d2">638e8fa7af82ec833d23c7df72ee9b1f,9baa05f21f51764ac1d0537294bdc7db,c7c764ddf88b5379a4bb35819cd25b82</data>
      <data key="d3">663</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">4157</data>
      <data key="d7">1e15950f1af041b68ce6b53a8056ed00</data>
    </node>
    <node id="M&#218;SCULO">
      <data key="d0">GEO</data>
      <data key="d1">M&#218;SCULO refers to muscle tissue, a specialized type of tissue in the body that plays a crucial role in movement and strength. It utilizes glycogen as a primary energy source for muscle contraction and is involved in the storage and utilization of glycogen during physical activity. Muscle tissue contains approximately 20% of the total body magnesium, which is essential for various physiological functions. Additionally, it plays a significant role in glucose uptake and metabolism; however, its function can be impaired in cases of insulin resistance, leading to an increase in lipid content.

The development and performance of muscle tissue are influenced by factors such as exercise and protein intake, with muscle being created through protein synthesis. During stress situations, muscle tissue also releases glutamine, which serves as a key source of this important amino acid. Overall, proper nutrition and training are vital for enhancing the performance of muscle tissue, ensuring its optimal function in the body.</data>
      <data key="d2">194ecedd60f53d4157bd44eac52cbcd8,39da0855b83941d97766254aa36ed2fd,439efa4d64a3de0b12975ea727c939f4,863198ceabd6e1c11a0d0944c676737c,c7c764ddf88b5379a4bb35819cd25b82,dcb97f7ffdb0aea3051bdf53e04bcc29,f0bfe278aa9f43f4bc9d056de67a5b96</data>
      <data key="d3">1756</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">4158</data>
      <data key="d7">489d5152086e43e9b6d35c78de627343</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CORAZ&#211;N">
      <data key="d0">GEO</data>
      <data key="d1">The entity "CORAZ&#211;N," which translates to "heart," is a vital organ that plays a crucial role in the cardiovascular system. It can be significantly impacted by iron overload, which may lead to various cardiovascular issues. Additionally, the heart is also affected by insulin resistance, a condition that can result in alterations in lipid metabolism. These factors highlight the heart's susceptibility to metabolic disturbances and the importance of maintaining overall health to prevent cardiovascular complications.</data>
      <data key="d2">45921035725d6b94d451399b0dac4079,c7c764ddf88b5379a4bb35819cd25b82</data>
      <data key="d3">987</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">4159</data>
      <data key="d7">b8b6637cf78348f79057a993e0e21c8e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RI&#209;&#211;N">
      <data key="d0">GEO</data>
      <data key="d1">Ri&#241;&#243;n, also known as the kidney, is a vital organ that plays several important roles in the body. It serves as a donor of glutamine during periods of stress, thereby enhancing the availability of this amino acid. Additionally, the kidney is implicated in various metabolic disorders, and in certain cases of glucogenosis, renal transplantation may be necessary. Furthermore, the kidney is involved in glucose metabolism and can be affected by insulin resistance, which contributes to the development of metabolic syndrome. Overall, Ri&#241;&#243;n is essential for maintaining metabolic balance and responding to physiological challenges.</data>
      <data key="d2">194ecedd60f53d4157bd44eac52cbcd8,76287e9b94faab94755c56fead3541d7,c7c764ddf88b5379a4bb35819cd25b82</data>
      <data key="d3">644</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">4160</data>
      <data key="d7">caf9ee5b1f6b40468788522f34a48147</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C&#193;LCIO">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Calcium is a chemical element that plays a crucial role in various cellular processes, including muscle contraction and neurotransmitter release.</data>
      <data key="d2">9baa05f21f51764ac1d0537294bdc7db</data>
      <data key="d3">1141</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">4161</data>
      <data key="d7">2b944b89095840789641ef4998431fcb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C&#201;LULAS &#914;">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">C&#201;LULAS &#914;, or beta cells, are insulin-producing cells located in the pancreas that play a crucial role in glucose metabolism. These cells are responsible for the secretion of insulin, a hormone that regulates blood sugar levels. The secretion of insulin from beta cells is influenced by various factors, including the presence of GLP-1 (glucagon-like peptide-1), which enhances their function.</data>
      <data key="d2">50fafa000d5f2830cbf66f3201657396,9baa05f21f51764ac1d0537294bdc7db</data>
      <data key="d3">998</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">4</data>
      <data key="d6">4162</data>
      <data key="d7">cdb8311a8f2144d8bcca174857a0ee93</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NADH">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">NADH is a coenzyme that plays a key role in the production of ATP during cellular respiration.</data>
      <data key="d2">9baa05f21f51764ac1d0537294bdc7db</data>
      <data key="d3">1733</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">4163</data>
      <data key="d7">ed111432930842a2a5e4fb8bea286b62</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FADH">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">FADH is another coenzyme involved in the electron transport chain, contributing to ATP production.</data>
      <data key="d2">9baa05f21f51764ac1d0537294bdc7db</data>
      <data key="d3">1733</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">4164</data>
      <data key="d7">2ccfbb67fe054c629545dc12f8fd6aa8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CADENCIAS DE INSULINA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Insulin pulses refer to the rhythmic release of insulin from beta cells in response to glucose levels.</data>
      <data key="d2">9baa05f21f51764ac1d0537294bdc7db</data>
      <data key="d3">1141</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">4165</data>
      <data key="d7">3fb0ecf63ed545c58a6e72b8ecd34aa6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROINSULINA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Proinsulin is a precursor to insulin, which is converted into insulin and C-peptide in the pancreas.</data>
      <data key="d2">9baa05f21f51764ac1d0537294bdc7db</data>
      <data key="d3">998</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">4166</data>
      <data key="d7">15b61f428da14e7b95585bba5a49f27e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KREBS CYCLE">
      <data key="d0" />
      <data key="d1">The Krebs cycle, also known as the citric acid cycle or tricarboxylic acid (TCA) cycle, is a series of chemical reactions utilized by all aerobic organisms to generate energy. This cycle involves the oxidation of acetate, which is derived from carbohydrates, fats, and proteins, ultimately contributing to the production of adenosine triphosphate (ATP), the energy currency of the cell.</data>
      <data key="d2">9baa05f21f51764ac1d0537294bdc7db,fb8d31ef68f83e6b01f6fc8548f36d0c</data>
      <data key="d3">1733</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">4</data>
      <data key="d6">4167</data>
      <data key="d7">90692fb34a3041538f9cac94089e9b2d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACCORD">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">The ACCORD study was a clinical trial that investigated the effects of intensive glucose lowering in type 2 diabetes and its impact on mortality and cardiovascular events.</data>
      <data key="d2">7b084f7afc5f3c66a528475ae4fa76b1</data>
      <data key="d3">1617</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">4168</data>
      <data key="d7">68a630a62c4440b59c5de355386b6f41</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLUT4">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">GLUT4 is a glucose transporter protein that plays a crucial role in insulin-regulated glucose uptake in muscle and fat tissues, significantly affecting glucose metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d2">7b084f7afc5f3c66a528475ae4fa76b1</data>
      <data key="d3">1617</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">4169</data>
      <data key="d7">3643f1c937ae40a4aef51d9343023b71</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIABETES TYPE 2">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Diabetes Type 2 is a chronic condition that significantly impacts the body's ability to process blood sugar (glucose). It is often linked to various cardiovascular risks and is commonly associated with high sugar consumption. Management of Diabetes Type 2 typically involves lifestyle changes, such as diet and exercise, as well as medication to help control blood sugar levels.</data>
      <data key="d2">018644ab78da6f804c57546f08ff6a65,06dcefb711cbd9fdb958145cca571b83,7b084f7afc5f3c66a528475ae4fa76b1</data>
      <data key="d3">1617</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">4170</data>
      <data key="d7">00f845c2a4654fe7a6c10cdb2ea2e20b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYPOGLYCEMIA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Hypoglycemia is a condition characterized by abnormally low blood sugar levels, which poses significant health concerns for various patient populations. It is particularly prevalent in individuals with glycogen storage diseases (GSD), especially in hepatic glucogenosis type I, where managing hypoglycemia is a primary treatment goal. Patients with GSDI often experience hypoglycemia, and it can also occur in GSDVI patients, particularly after fasting.

Beyond glycogen storage diseases, hypoglycemia can arise from multiple factors, including tumors that produce insulin-like growth factor 2 (IGF-2). It is commonly observed in individuals with diabetes, especially those undergoing insulin therapy or taking other glucose-lowering medications. In diabetic patients, hypoglycemia may occur during dietary changes or as a result of medication administration.

Additionally, hypoglycemia can affect patients post-bariatric surgery, often linked to dumping syndrome, which leads to symptoms such as sweating and weakness. It can also manifest in individuals with acute adrenal insufficiency, contributing to low blood sugar levels. Overall, hypoglycemia is a multifaceted condition that requires careful management across various medical contexts.</data>
      <data key="d2">06fe3811ad957d6841997b2a0e9be50f,24cc40d6e3b225945cd4d8c2484632e4,27f06efb5e9ac8bbc3f8bd31ee5a2777,46466da830644a88f74108645c668fd5,5b487245068b8e8b87ffcc04970aaff4,704cfd179507a977ecc0691a5bd2861e,79afec9f55a4cb9aa31a7e4566dae279,7b084f7afc5f3c66a528475ae4fa76b1,9f5a8d86160d5d097bca174e2e2496c1,a4b144210b0a4b654a260ebc394de0af,d1b2835d6e86f09adefd22378bb6da15,dda50550d881b4487154c85b2f279034,fec130428af4ee62996a9b72494212dc</data>
      <data key="d3">1711</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">25</data>
      <data key="d6">4171</data>
      <data key="d7">63c33bcac7a34911a88c8e39f17d495e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FETOPLACENTAL UNIT">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">The fetoplacental unit refers to the combined functions of the fetus and placenta, which influence maternal metabolism and insulin sensitivity during pregnancy.</data>
      <data key="d2">7b084f7afc5f3c66a528475ae4fa76b1</data>
      <data key="d3">1713</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">4172</data>
      <data key="d7">3a8dd76efca34139aabd864e643e2ff8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LEPTIN">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Leptin is a hormone produced by adipose (fat) tissue that plays a crucial role in regulating energy balance and metabolism. It influences insulin sensitivity and appetite, particularly during pregnancy. Research has shown that leptin levels are significantly altered in patients with anorexia nervosa, where low levels of the hormone correlate with reduced body weight. This indicates that leptin is not only important for general energy regulation but also has specific implications for individuals with eating disorders, such as anorexia nervosa.</data>
      <data key="d2">1f9d0f4850dde2dbd335969b016fc359,203ff7a2671a44d48a1a326f469c5543,7b084f7afc5f3c66a528475ae4fa76b1</data>
      <data key="d3">1596</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">4173</data>
      <data key="d7">94b51f66bf254500b96a97393ea30fba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SPANISH SOCIETY OF ENDOCRINOLOGY AND NUTRITION">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">The Spanish Society of Endocrinology and Nutrition (SEEN) is a professional organization dedicated to the study and management of endocrine and nutritional issues. This includes a focus on endocrine disorders such as diabetes mellitus and metabolic control. SEEN plays a crucial role in providing guidelines and conducting research related to endocrine and nutritional health, particularly in the area of diabetes management. Through its efforts, the organization aims to enhance understanding and improve the management of these critical health issues.</data>
      <data key="d2">5b487245068b8e8b87ffcc04970aaff4,8bcf09481326ab5017cc3b45317538a3,e36c9b09870e14d19aa6eb7c82514499</data>
      <data key="d3">1507</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">4174</data>
      <data key="d7">4fcc47b0657c4bf7a558a0e63544a903</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEZQUITA-RAYA P">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Mezquita-Raya P is an author affiliated with the Diabetes Mellitus Working Group of the Spanish Society of Endocrinology and Nutrition. They have made significant contributions to the development of guidelines focused on the management of hypoglycemia, particularly in the context of diabetes patients. Mezquita-Raya P is also recognized for their involvement in the creation of a document that addresses the evaluation and management of hypoglycemia in individuals with diabetes.</data>
      <data key="d2">5b487245068b8e8b87ffcc04970aaff4,8bcf09481326ab5017cc3b45317538a3</data>
