      <data key="d7">a9dc491e89f44471ac4a4a02def84faf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NORADRENALINE">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Noradrenaline is a hormone that plays a role in the body's response to low blood sugar levels, stimulating glucose production.</data>
      <data key="d2">5b487245068b8e8b87ffcc04970aaff4</data>
      <data key="d3">1711</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">4181</data>
      <data key="d7">cd8c2854355f44958ad017a9b052b1bc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PANCREATIC TUMORS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Pancreatic tumors can lead to hyperinsulinism, causing episodes of hypoglycemia in affected individuals.</data>
      <data key="d2">5b487245068b8e8b87ffcc04970aaff4</data>
      <data key="d3">1711</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">4182</data>
      <data key="d7">965ea0a0f4914e2da323d06510f34fde</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AUTOIMMUNE DISEASES">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Autoimmune diseases can lead to the production of antibodies against insulin, contributing to hypoglycemia.</data>
      <data key="d2">5b487245068b8e8b87ffcc04970aaff4</data>
      <data key="d3">1711</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">4183</data>
      <data key="d7">e7785194440e46eaabc7e24ee5562ec1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CETOACIDOSIS DIAB&#201;TICA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Cetoacidosis diab&#233;tica, also known as diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA), is a severe metabolic complication of diabetes mellitus. It is characterized by hyperglycemia, hyperketonemia, and metabolic acidosis, which can lead to a dangerous buildup of acids in the bloodstream. This condition often requires urgent medical intervention due to its serious nature. The body produces high levels of ketones during this process, which can also increase the production of uric acid due to metabolic changes. Symptoms of diabetic ketoacidosis include high blood sugar levels, the presence of ketones in the urine, and acidosis, making it a critical condition that necessitates immediate treatment.</data>
      <data key="d2">8bcf09481326ab5017cc3b45317538a3,ab93481c5db482b6da2462b4687e4313,ac2955cad85e541f3b61de2f4df0a3a9,afe0f4f37815c175f0faff06eb24a256,d77ee7b5259ebdf92e8375c5f2d322e6</data>
      <data key="d3">876</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">12</data>
      <data key="d6">4184</data>
      <data key="d7">af074d603d6d4b198f8150802b751192</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SEANQUISIT ER">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Seanquist ER is an author involved in the workgroup of the American Diabetes Association and the Endocrine Society, focusing on hypoglycemia and diabetes management.</data>
      <data key="d2">8bcf09481326ab5017cc3b45317538a3</data>
      <data key="d3">865</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">4185</data>
      <data key="d7">88f1d3cb615443188af8bd1e6cd934e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CAD">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">CAD, or Coronary Artery Disease, is a condition that can complicate diabetes management and requires specific dietary considerations. However, the term CAD is also used to refer to diabetic ketoacidosis, a serious complication of diabetes characterized by high levels of ketones in the blood due to insufficient insulin. This condition leads to metabolic acidosis and is a critical health concern for individuals with diabetes. It is important to differentiate between these two meanings of CAD, as one pertains to heart health and the other to a severe metabolic state resulting from diabetes.</data>
      <data key="d2">8bcf09481326ab5017cc3b45317538a3,bcded135629c889cb0a10ae968743582,eeb6e04f12698ee82912bdf775d79381</data>
      <data key="d3">624</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">4186</data>
      <data key="d7">c0bcd9d59a914874a5ecee9576d6f3f4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UMPIERREZ G">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">G. Umpierrez is a researcher who has contributed to the literature on diabetic emergencies, including diabetic ketoacidosis and hyperglycemic hyperosmolar state.</data>
      <data key="d2">afe0f4f37815c175f0faff06eb24a256</data>
      <data key="d3">876</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">4187</data>
      <data key="d7">619a8fc34e5a4ba4b6e7ec2518f2b630</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WOLFSDORF J">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">J. Wolfsdorf is a researcher and author of consensus statements regarding diabetic ketoacidosis in infants, children, and adolescents.</data>
      <data key="d2">afe0f4f37815c175f0faff06eb24a256</data>
      <data key="d3">876</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">4188</data>
      <data key="d7">8765b64538c6460697d2c0d32553b094</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACIDOSIS METAB&#211;LICA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">ACIDOSIS METAB&#211;LICA, or metabolic acidosis, is a medical condition characterized by an excess of acid in the body fluids. This condition can arise from various underlying issues, including kidney dysfunction, where the kidneys fail to adequately remove acid from the body. It is often associated with diabetic ketoacidosis, a severe complication of diabetes, and can also occur in patients with conditions such as VIPoma, which leads to severe diarrhea and electrolyte imbalances.

In addition to these causes, metabolic acidosis can lead to the redistribution of phosphate, which is particularly relevant in the context of hypophosphatemia. It is also recognized as a complication in patients with renal insufficiency, where it can adversely affect their nutritional status. Furthermore, metabolic acidosis may manifest in infants suffering from glucose-galactose malabsorption, typically resulting from severe dehydration and diarrhea.

Overall, metabolic acidosis is a complex condition that can arise from multiple factors, including metabolic disturbances and renal impairment, and it requires careful management to address the underlying causes and prevent further complications.</data>
      <data key="d2">473502714ce73073f93b598ca0edcc21,5bb504bf68288cc0ba6a8f0dd9e4c245,92d73407a11da797ae160c4ac13a6efe,a87e8e113488b153c5b4b2d2fca712c0,afe0f4f37815c175f0faff06eb24a256,cda0904fd9d29455ee008c43bc9f7b41,d77ee7b5259ebdf92e8375c5f2d322e6,e329b5d4c64709889680df357bd1cc09,fee4d9611c98c81859bccdb4ded03622</data>
      <data key="d3">846</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">5</data>
      <data key="d6">4189</data>
      <data key="d7">74f721ac102d4b2b832f5b0cf207fa9e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CETOACIDOSIS">
      <data key="d0" />
      <data key="d1">Cetoacidosis is a serious metabolic condition primarily associated with diabetes, particularly diabetic ketoacidosis, which occurs when the body produces high levels of ketones due to insufficient insulin. This condition can lead to elevated blood sugar levels and is a significant complication for individuals with diabetes. Additionally, ketoacidosis can also occur in adults with Maple Syrup Urine Disease, often triggered by intercurrent illnesses. Overall, cetoacidosis is characterized by the body's excessive production of ketones, making it a critical health concern that requires prompt medical attention.</data>
      <data key="d2">06b374fc6a6ebf2091ac0a00329bd027,21339c051dc2f47471026673b3ac6436,48f1fe343d54cd0f353be34b736a352c,638e8fa7af82ec833d23c7df72ee9b1f,afe0f4f37815c175f0faff06eb24a256</data>
      <data key="d3">1141</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">11</data>
      <data key="d6">4190</data>
      <data key="d7">c0541e8c53c443a8a8ce7773a473cf7a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SHH">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">SHH, or Hyperglycemic Hyperosmolar State, is a severe complication of diabetes that results in high blood sugar levels without significant ketoacidosis.</data>
      <data key="d2">bcded135629c889cb0a10ae968743582</data>
      <data key="d3">899</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">4191</data>
      <data key="d7">d1f720d529fe4c3a99d8166b7c1c022c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CATECHOLAMINES">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Catecholamines are hormones produced by the adrenal glands, which include epinephrine and norepinephrine. These hormones play a crucial role in increasing blood glucose levels and counteracting insulin. Additionally, the excess production of catecholamines is a significant indicator in diagnosing pheochromocytomas, a type of tumor that can affect adrenal gland function.</data>
      <data key="d2">bcded135629c889cb0a10ae968743582,c3d752ff301355241e1420e398f0e6a4</data>
      <data key="d3">977</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">4192</data>
      <data key="d7">7d42e4413a8c4ae5bc2b2c38b7a1b7d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GROWTH HORMONE">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Growth Hormone (GH) is a crucial hormone that plays a significant role in growth, cell reproduction, and regeneration, as well as influencing glucose metabolism. In patients with Anorexia Nervosa, GH levels are found to be elevated; however, this is accompanied by a decrease in Insulin-like Growth Factor 1 (IGF-1) due to resistance to GH. Additionally, GH levels may be diminished in individuals with liver disease, which can negatively impact metabolism and muscle mass. Furthermore, GH is being explored as a potential anti-aging treatment for older adults, although it is not an approved indication and carries various effects and risks. Overall, Growth Hormone is integral to several physiological processes, but its levels and effects can vary significantly depending on health conditions and age.</data>
      <data key="d2">1f9d0f4850dde2dbd335969b016fc359,5341a1454aa3fed0eab02f9ce7fea5d1,bcded135629c889cb0a10ae968743582,f979a07da5d7c814ccf957629ab91cc5</data>
      <data key="d3">398</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">4193</data>
      <data key="d7">994d0ad481c8457cbf632298cf55940c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLYCOGENOLYSIS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Glycogenolysis is the biochemical process of breaking down glycogen into glucose, playing a crucial role in maintaining blood sugar levels, particularly during periods of fasting or intense physical activity. This process involves the degradation of glycogen and can be influenced by various conditions, including glucogenosis, which may alter the normal functioning of glycogenolysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">bcded135629c889cb0a10ae968743582,d1b2835d6e86f09adefd22378bb6da15</data>
      <data key="d3">898</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">4194</data>
      <data key="d7">197aa92472e74196b17476350c38d60f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HORMONES">
      <data key="d0" />
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">bcded135629c889cb0a10ae968743582</data>
      <data key="d3">1713</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">4195</data>
      <data key="d7">9bbcbc83099c4c548c914cb784f4435c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYPERGLYCEMIC HYPEROSMOLAR STATE">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">HYPERGLYCEMIC HYPEROSMOLAR STATE is a serious medical condition primarily affecting individuals with diabetes. It is characterized by extremely high blood sugar levels and increased osmolality, which can lead to neurological symptoms and, in severe cases, potential coma. The condition is often associated with significant dehydration, making it a critical health issue that requires prompt medical attention.</data>
      <data key="d2">cda0904fd9d29455ee008c43bc9f7b41,ed9e6da7ef23d47467822d2a1e461195</data>
      <data key="d3">1852</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">4196</data>
      <data key="d7">d5c27c11086247bca046e9cf3724871b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIABETIC KETOACIDOSIS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Diabetic ketoacidosis is a serious complication of diabetes that arises when the body produces high levels of ketones due to insufficient insulin. This condition leads to metabolic acidosis, characterized by a dangerous buildup of acids in the blood. Key indicators of diabetic ketoacidosis include high blood sugar levels, the presence of ketones in the urine, and acidosis. It is a critical medical emergency that requires prompt treatment to prevent severe health consequences.</data>
      <data key="d2">08f89bb92750950761f8f59bffa402c1,cda0904fd9d29455ee008c43bc9f7b41,ed9e6da7ef23d47467822d2a1e461195</data>
      <data key="d3">1852</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">4197</data>
      <data key="d7">818c476ed941415dbb1e63b297fe4c89</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KITABCHI">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">A researcher who has contributed to the understanding of hyperglycemic crises in adult patients with diabetes.</data>
      <data key="d2">cda0904fd9d29455ee008c43bc9f7b41</data>
      <data key="d3">1852</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">4198</data>
      <data key="d7">e53ce3a60c374d19a5501f6551293208</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UMPIRERZ">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">A researcher involved in the study of hyperglycemic crises and diabetes management.</data>
      <data key="d2">cda0904fd9d29455ee008c43bc9f7b41</data>
