    <node id="DIABETES MELLITUS TYPE 1">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Diabetes Mellitus Type 1 (DM1) is a chronic autoimmune disease characterized by the destruction of insulin-producing beta cells in the pancreas, leading to little or no insulin production. This condition typically develops in children and young adults, although it can be diagnosed at any age, as evidenced by cases where individuals are diagnosed in their late twenties. The lack of insulin results in high blood sugar levels, necessitating careful management of diet, physical activity, and insulin levels to maintain blood glucose control. Additionally, individuals with Diabetes Mellitus Type 1 may be predisposed to other autoimmune conditions, such as Klinefelter syndrome. Overall, effective management of DM1 is crucial to prevent various complications associated with the disease.</data>
      <data key="d2">043ed163c427b880fe39884076cae7ea,06fe3811ad957d6841997b2a0e9be50f,5509bb6959e4faa5989289c1ddbcc96b,784f8d7f49cdce2d0611be585eda7e2c,aa9279dacd764b478618ae36af93ad83,acd36e6dbcd98757c4cf33ff090764ee,bbb0b98043e40738bce354afe3de265c,fe5f390fb67fd3b1ad222db3500574b9</data>
      <data key="d3">1594</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">28</data>
      <data key="d6">4234</data>
      <data key="d7">5f235b9ffd8249bfacae1da5b006cd6d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EDEMA MACULAR">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Edema macular is a condition that occurs when fluid accumulates in the macula, leading to vision impairment, often associated with diabetic retinopathy.</data>
      <data key="d2">784f8d7f49cdce2d0611be585eda7e2c</data>
      <data key="d3">1603</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">4235</data>
      <data key="d7">f49906ed97874f86ad6ddc816717c8ff</data>
    </node>
