    </node>
    <node id="LITIO">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">LITIO, or lithium, is a medication primarily used as a mood stabilizer in the treatment of mood disorders. It is known to affect various physiological processes in the body. One significant effect of lithium is its potential to alter calcium levels, leading to conditions such as hypercalcemia. Additionally, the excretion of lithium is influenced by sodium intake, which can impact its effectiveness and safety.

Lithium is also associated with several side effects, including the development of primary hypothyroidism in up to 20% of patients undergoing treatment. Another notable side effect is nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, which can occur in some individuals taking the medication. Overall, while lithium is an effective treatment for mood disorders, it requires careful monitoring due to its various effects on the body and potential side effects.</data>
      <data key="d2">1fb4c8ba1bc908ea3a3c7717a073fa4f,773a1daa539dd85e562a59e09f434af1,838d1ef59dfea4cab2a724bc85b02632,8824d8c01c7332dd9fd487d1ece0f7e5,9de04e5aa72249acf96899ca74c17e0b</data>
      <data key="d3">1574</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">5</data>
      <data key="d6">4430</data>
      <data key="d7">c86cfca53d5144dcb5cebc22c7900ff0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AMIODARONA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Amiodarona is an antiarrhythmic medication that is recommended to be taken with meals to minimize gastrointestinal discomfort. This drug is notable for its high iodine content, which can lead to thyroid dysfunction in approximately 15-20% of patients. Specifically, amiodarona can induce both hyperthyroidism and hypothyroidism, particularly in individuals with pre-existing thyroiditis. The risk of hypothyroidism is especially pronounced in regions where there is a low intake of iodine. Overall, amiodarona is a significant medication in the management of arrhythmias, but it carries the potential for thyroid-related side effects that require monitoring.</data>
      <data key="d2">04f886244ff7d736eb270f175d6b120e,838d1ef59dfea4cab2a724bc85b02632,be2de576112f22070e8ef8a5cb9e1017,cca243547034544596d9409f2643eac0,f701d832b0dc8b95c2cb09997d543897</data>
      <data key="d3">1350</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">4</data>
      <data key="d6">4431</data>
      <data key="d7">955428e652874780b0c51776c5e88149</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INTERFER&#211;N &#913;">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Interfer&#243;n &#945; is a medication known for its antiviral and antineoplastic properties. It is utilized in the treatment of various conditions, including hepatitis C, leukemias, mastocytosis, carcinoid syndrome, and Beh&#231;et's disease. However, it can also lead to thyroid dysfunction, manifesting as both hyperthyroidism and hypothyroidism. Additionally, Interfer&#243;n &#945; may induce autoimmune thyroid diseases, such as Hashimoto's thyroiditis.</data>
      <data key="d2">838d1ef59dfea4cab2a724bc85b02632,f701d832b0dc8b95c2cb09997d543897</data>
      <data key="d3">1675</data>
