    <node id="T3">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">T3, or triiodothyronine, is a crucial thyroid hormone that plays a significant role in regulating metabolism and energy levels in the body. It is the more active form of thyroid hormone, derived from T4 (thyroxine) in target cells. T3 is particularly important for metabolic processes and is typically found at low levels in patients with Anorexia Nervosa, indicating its relevance in energy regulation and overall metabolic health.</data>
      <data key="d2">1f9d0f4850dde2dbd335969b016fc359,4138cb85d4afff56555b4a536340ca90,be2de576112f22070e8ef8a5cb9e1017,d4fb31e0ff471ed415f98bc11ca26d1d</data>
      <data key="d3">1597</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">4444</data>
      <data key="d7">0c37139dabc2444099cb0f8fb4beb76f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T4">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">T4, also known as thyroxine, is a crucial thyroid hormone produced by the thyroid gland. It plays a significant role in regulating metabolism and is essential for normal growth and metabolic processes. T4 levels are often measured in thyroid function tests to assess thyroid health. In certain conditions, such as Anorexia Nervosa, T4 can be found at low-normal levels due to malnutrition, indicating a potential disruption in metabolic regulation. Additionally, T4 is converted into its active form, T3, in target cells, which is necessary for exerting its metabolic effects.</data>
      <data key="d2">1f9d0f4850dde2dbd335969b016fc359,4138cb85d4afff56555b4a536340ca90,be2de576112f22070e8ef8a5cb9e1017,d4fb31e0ff471ed415f98bc11ca26d1d</data>
      <data key="d3">1597</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">4445</data>
      <data key="d7">b37d92ad5320421485e98e193a5418e2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T3 REVERSA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Reverse T3 (rT3) is an inactive form of T3 that can increase in certain conditions, such as severe illness or hypothyroidism.</data>
      <data key="d2">4138cb85d4afff56555b4a536340ca90</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">4446</data>
      <data key="d7">37754c422a1c4c3abed63d0c60507e07</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ADRENALINA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">ADRENALINA, also known as adrenaline, is a hormone produced by the adrenal glands that plays a crucial role in the body's response to stress. It increases heart rate and enhances energy availability, which can be particularly important in emergency situations. The response to adrenaline can be affected by conditions such as hypothyroidism. Additionally, adrenaline is utilized as an emergency treatment for severe anaphylactic reactions, especially in patients with food allergies. It is essential for patients at risk of severe allergic reactions to have this medication readily available for self-administration in case of an emergency.</data>
      <data key="d2">0f0303399f29734dede9db544e1dda9e,4138cb85d4afff56555b4a536340ca90,ac74fa24a5f29d72ff023c010162fea5</data>
      <data key="d3">1131</data>
