    <node id="WHO">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">The World Health Organization (WHO) is a specialized agency of the United Nations dedicated to international public health. It plays a crucial role in providing guidelines and recommendations on various health-related topics, including alcohol consumption, nutrition, and health. The WHO is also involved in specific health management practices, such as providing formulas for calculating resting energy expenditure in patients, particularly in the context of managing cystic fibrosis. Additionally, the organization has made significant contributions to medical classifications, exemplified by its publication of a new classification of thyroid tumors in 2017, which introduced modifications to the previous classification. Furthermore, the WHO estimates the minimum amount of digestible carbohydrates necessary to prevent ketosis, highlighting its commitment to evidence-based health recommendations. Overall, the WHO's multifaceted approach underscores its vital role in shaping global health policies and practices.</data>
      <data key="d2">1ad5b4d7f49ca2d3d44adc99227f07d8,68816641022021ef30afd4a52368fbf1,70e4538100be270d06e7a3cdc254ba28,767d564db2dbf0182707caaf1b0a52d2,7ff62e1dc423be2f4b2fe08546751d0d,8778654a17a48b8ea7b1cec378d52346,9303a85464d02f1cc012aaca21edf9bb,c2211f0deb7328a77cb632140690da03,e9bb1d3d1af940dd655e0cca9bbb5c02</data>
      <data key="d3">1550</data>
