      <data key="d6">4722</data>
      <data key="d7">0bbef11124524c4582a8788ab406a8eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEDULLARY THYROID CANCER">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Medullary thyroid cancer is a type of thyroid cancer that can be hereditary and is associated with multiple endocrine neoplasia syndromes.</data>
      <data key="d2">e775708e66a97968834f7476479a21b2</data>
      <data key="d3">427</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">4723</data>
      <data key="d7">3f40229aa8114131b172331030a0bd2e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEN2">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">MEN2, or Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia type 2, is a genetic syndrome characterized by a clear association with specific activating mutations in the RET proto-oncogene. This condition is inherited in an autosomal dominant manner and has significant implications for the diagnosis and treatment of associated tumors. MEN2 primarily increases the risk of developing medullary thyroid carcinoma and pheochromocytoma, distinguishing it from MEN1, which involves different types of tumors. The syndrome is considered rare and is marked by the onset and aggressiveness of medullary thyroid carcinoma, along with the potential for other endocrine tumors.</data>
      <data key="d2">02b8a693303f9f8603c740fefc53d526,2415ccd829f700cbe1975f4bca87245f,92d73407a11da797ae160c4ac13a6efe,e775708e66a97968834f7476479a21b2,fbba0b43850ff5e50b710069959c2416</data>
      <data key="d3">1312</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">4724</data>
      <data key="d7">41f4ebb141c940cb83d5c43410011e83</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RET ONCOGENE">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">The RET oncogene is a gene that, when mutated, is associated with multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2 and medullary thyroid carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d2">e775708e66a97968834f7476479a21b2</data>
      <data key="d3">1312</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">4725</data>
      <data key="d7">e566575da4aa4e9fbf56ccb83753c577</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHAMBON G">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Chambon G is an author of a study related to the measurement of serum thyrocalcitonin in patients with thyroid disorders.</data>
      <data key="d2">e775708e66a97968834f7476479a21b2</data>
