      <data key="d6">4751</data>
      <data key="d7">c81b482de84e4f5d8dda760039d35ee9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RISK OF RECURRENCE">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">The risk of recurrence refers to the likelihood that the thyroid cancer will return after treatment, as assessed by the ATA guidelines.</data>
      <data key="d2">b1ce0d940909277d321ee55bffcc69b3</data>
      <data key="d3">1257</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">4752</data>
      <data key="d7">a08035bd66314588a4af9cdbf0ed2229</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HISTOLOGY">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Histology refers to the microscopic examination of the tumor's cells, which is crucial for determining the aggressiveness of the cancer.</data>
      <data key="d2">b1ce0d940909277d321ee55bffcc69b3</data>
      <data key="d3">1316</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">4753</data>
      <data key="d7">d17b98dd226c48df9af0def722afe8e9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METASTASIS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Metastasis is the spread of cancer cells from the original tumor site in the thyroid to other parts of the body, which affects staging and treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d2">b1ce0d940909277d321ee55bffcc69b3</data>
      <data key="d3">1316</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">4754</data>
      <data key="d7">07776a562fa14da4b889d8293cc0886d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CALCITONIN">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Calcitonin is a hormone produced by the thyroid gland that plays a crucial role in regulating calcium levels in the blood. It is particularly significant in the context of medullary thyroid carcinoma, where it serves as a tumor marker. Calcitonin's levels are monitored post-surgery to predict the evolution of patients with this type of cancer, and it is also used to assess the presence and extent of the disease. Additionally, calcitonin is utilized as a biomarker in the diagnosis and management of medullary thyroid cancer, highlighting its importance in both clinical and diagnostic settings.</data>
      <data key="d2">02db15b9ad7bc5655e1907ad3fa4e8ad,08bc93c6dcf6df5ebf660f30b1f1ef2d,53eefebd95b547fa56967d97b31beec1,72b937574e8e90d19c9549a10b75057c,931560e7015f30adee5741819cdd0fab,a2303199b7587142ad661841870790ec,d4fb31e0ff471ed415f98bc11ca26d1d,de6e1d8ac9f422acb17fd26b77b485da</data>
      <data key="d3">967</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">4</data>
      <data key="d6">4755</data>
      <data key="d7">770a271a169f4bdca09c64f91f087ecf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MACHENS A">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Machens A is a researcher known for their contributions to studies focused on biomarker-based risk stratification for medullary thyroid cancer.</data>
