    </node>
    <node id="HIPO- HIPERNATREMIA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Hypo- and hypernatremia refer to low and high sodium levels in the blood, respectively, which can occur in patients receiving parenteral nutrition.</data>
      <data key="d2">d936cb199b788cfcddbfb6e51b1e6e48</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">4916</data>
      <data key="d7">b63eb6b5aede438b8b7fd63344e91feb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIPO- HIPERPOTASEMIA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Hypo- and hyperkalemia refer to low and high potassium levels in the blood, respectively, which can occur in patients receiving parenteral nutrition.</data>
      <data key="d2">d936cb199b788cfcddbfb6e51b1e6e48</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">4917</data>
      <data key="d7">9250c6f5269f4fd899a11e4b31af5c9c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TABLA 5">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Table 5 presents a comprehensive overview of several key aspects related to patient care and nutrition. It includes the results of a study aimed at assessing the patient's hyperglycemic syndrome, providing valuable insights into this condition. Additionally, Table 5 summarizes the hydroelectrolytic content of various secretions, which is crucial for ensuring proper supplementation in parenteral nutrition. Furthermore, it outlines the mandatory and voluntary formats for presenting nutritional information, highlighting the importance of clear communication in dietary practices. Overall, Table 5 serves as an essential resource for understanding and managing nutritional needs in clinical settings.</data>
      <data key="d2">3dbf07634cee8d4d443f59ed50f98b54,b7a2c1e4dd77f47605fdb33f4a2c2bdf,d936cb199b788cfcddbfb6e51b1e6e48</data>
