    <node id="DHEA-S">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">DHEA-S (Dehydroepiandrosterone sulfate) is a hormone produced by the adrenal glands, playing a significant role in various physiological processes. It is often measured to assess adrenal function, particularly in the context of hyperandrogenism and conditions such as Polycystic Ovary Syndrome. Normal levels of DHEA-S can be crucial for evaluating the malignancy of adrenal lesions, as it serves as a controversial marker in the study of adrenal incidentalomas, indicating malignancy with greater specificity than sensitivity.

In addition to its role in adrenal function, DHEA-S levels can be altered in individuals with eating disorders, highlighting its importance in metabolic health. The hormone is also noted to decrease with age, and lower levels have been associated with fragility and loss of muscle mass. Furthermore, DHEA-S is measured in relation to early puberty, underscoring its relevance in developmental processes. Overall, DHEA-S is a multifaceted hormone that provides valuable insights into adrenal health and various clinical conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">05a6995cef9c8d0279b9f026da1ffed1,1436fed20a28649406ac34dcfb553734,151237aa6e4e4aaa2d1115a10ab09be0,28bca03cd2152cf2562d7aa16d7ced1d,4b3664b368fad59fd55189a65e19b000,70e6c476cce381861896b5a6be2f39ad,97afc23d7c06c09383611da00b084017,a9f15831bf090502bcde79d58544ea8b,b7b162b783ea688c5fe9b7f531b46aaf,dda58e50ce4bb9fa40e3c61e40e1b9c7</data>
      <data key="d3">1336</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">4955</data>
      <data key="d7">e27ecc9c57da4601b3b36c72f143ba01</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIPERCORTISOLISMO">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">HIPERCORTISOLISMO is a condition characterized by excessive cortisol production in the body. This condition is often evaluated in patients with adrenal incidentalomas, which are unexpected findings of adrenal masses during imaging studies. Additionally, hypercortisolism can occur in individuals with eating disorders, highlighting its potential association with various health issues. The diagnosis and assessment of hypercortisolism typically involve hormonal testing to measure cortisol levels, providing a comprehensive evaluation of the patient's hormonal status.</data>
      <data key="d2">151237aa6e4e4aaa2d1115a10ab09be0,6ebb5385c8eda09a187e6d0595812bbf,70e6c476cce381861896b5a6be2f39ad</data>
      <data key="d3">1327</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">4956</data>
      <data key="d7">8dfc3e2548204b61b589d67562154bd7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AUTOAGRESI&#211;N">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Autoagresi&#243;n refers to self-harming behaviors that can occur in individuals with eating disorders, often as a coping mechanism for emotional distress.</data>
      <data key="d2">70e6c476cce381861896b5a6be2f39ad</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">4957</data>
      <data key="d7">86974f5b3fb2402188ef0feef1ba8b48</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LESIONES ORODENTALES">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Lesiones orodentales are oral and dental injuries that can occur in individuals with eating disorders, often due to behaviors such as vomiting.</data>
      <data key="d2">70e6c476cce381861896b5a6be2f39ad</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">4958</data>
      <data key="d7">1c0a38627a694f9aaddb1a85ef0cc056</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EROSIONES DENTALES">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Erosiones dentales are dental erosions that can occur in individuals with eating disorders, particularly those who engage in purging behaviors.</data>
      <data key="d2">70e6c476cce381861896b5a6be2f39ad</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">4959</data>
      <data key="d7">4eecf06c29bc4333b57be8b6839856a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DESMINERALIZACIONES DEL ESMALTE">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Desmineralizaciones del esmalte refers to the loss of minerals from tooth enamel, which can occur in individuals with eating disorders due to poor nutrition and acid exposure from vomiting.</data>
      <data key="d2">70e6c476cce381861896b5a6be2f39ad</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">4960</data>
      <data key="d7">295073189004461b897b308a10a68b05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CORTES">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Cortes are cuts that may be self-inflicted by individuals with eating disorders as a form of self-harm.</data>
      <data key="d2">70e6c476cce381861896b5a6be2f39ad</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">4961</data>
      <data key="d7">04eacc757c3c4245b1f70ff531221a9c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ARA&#209;AZOS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Ara&#241;azos are scratches that may be self-inflicted by individuals with eating disorders as a form of self-harm.</data>
      <data key="d2">70e6c476cce381861896b5a6be2f39ad</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">4962</data>
      <data key="d7">e20fd679190b44ed97bbd30299d36f7b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIPOGLUCEMIA INSUL&#205;NICA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Hipoglucemia insul&#237;nica is a condition that can occur when insulin levels are high relative to glucose levels, often discussed in the context of metabolic responses in eating disorders.</data>
      <data key="d2">70e6c476cce381861896b5a6be2f39ad</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">4963</data>
      <data key="d7">fa4f7ffff771459fae9a97fdb255c8db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIABETES INS&#205;PIDA PARCIAL">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Diabetes ins&#237;pida parcial is a condition that can affect water balance in the body, potentially relevant in the context of eating disorders.)&lt;|COMPLETE|&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">70e6c476cce381861896b5a6be2f39ad</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">4964</data>
      <data key="d7">556b631285c3479d99fa3bbafd89bb02</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DSM-IV">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">The DSM-IV is a manual published by the American Psychiatric Association that outlines the criteria for diagnosing mental disorders, including anorexia nerviosa.</data>
      <data key="d2">8029c950c540c731bb9334bd90348239</data>
      <data key="d3">1596</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">4965</data>
      <data key="d7">cd8e4f5d7ee94353a5572787e04e38a4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DSM-5">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">The DSM-5, or the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, Fifth Edition, is a comprehensive manual that provides standardized criteria for the classification and diagnosis of mental health conditions. It serves as an essential resource for mental health professionals, offering detailed criteria for diagnosing various disorders, including eating disorders such as anorexia nervosa and bulimia nerviosa. The DSM-5 is the updated version of the previous edition, DSM-IV, and includes revised criteria that reflect the latest research and understanding of mental health conditions. Overall, the DSM-5 plays a crucial role in the field of mental health by standardizing the diagnostic process and improving the accuracy of mental health assessments.</data>
      <data key="d2">3bc554a0031c436483a977c2b9e57b48,8029c950c540c731bb9334bd90348239,9c65bcd67b717641c8d9d94ef9cfb210,ecf27310ce7cbfc335117dafa5d4eefb</data>
      <data key="d3">1599</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">4</data>
      <data key="d6">4966</data>
      <data key="d7">fd637967c15e4c04bc1d71e5f8d0fdfd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GRUPO DE TRABAJO DE LA GU&#205;A DE PR&#193;CTICA CL&#205;NICA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">This group is responsible for developing clinical practice guidelines on eating disorders in Spain, including anorexia nerviosa.</data>
      <data key="d2">8029c950c540c731bb9334bd90348239</data>
      <data key="d3">75</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">4967</data>
