    <node id="EFSA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">The European Food Safety Authority (EFSA) is an agency dedicated to providing scientific advice on food safety and nutrition within the European Union. It plays a crucial role in assessing risks associated with the food chain, including specific recommendations on the consumption of substances such as methylmercury. EFSA also offers scientific guidance on dietary reference values for fats and fatty acids, ensuring that nutritional standards are based on sound research. Additionally, the agency evaluates health claims related to very low calorie diets, contributing to informed decision-making regarding dietary supplements and overall food safety.</data>
      <data key="d2">018644ab78da6f804c57546f08ff6a65,1e60716fb6d7eb11fdf78dc47bd9a5d9,34f5f2681b28d568aea92e2633ebae56,dbdb3cd3b9065e8846fd2eb15b9d5879</data>
      <data key="d3">718</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">10</data>
      <data key="d6">4982</data>
      <data key="d7">e235f3800fa74cc984e9639eca232e2d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VLCD">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">The Very Low Calorie Diet (VLCD) is a dietary regimen characterized by a caloric intake of less than 12 kcal/kg of ideal body weight per day, typically ranging between 450 and 800 kcal daily. This approach is primarily utilized for significant weight loss, particularly in obese patients, and is often implemented as a preparatory measure before bariatric surgery. VLCDs involve substituting one or more meals with low-calorie formulas, which usually provide around 200 kcal per serving. The primary goal of VLCDs is to facilitate short-term weight loss, reduce liver size, and improve health conditions associated with obesity.</data>
      <data key="d2">032fc2148da74e586c8fd91e073c2be6,1e60716fb6d7eb11fdf78dc47bd9a5d9,3926ed628d3d5919b8a65e44fffbc3b9,5a0d6adfbef59dd174ade59071dd9aa5,64347a9be918a67f786cbdf9f9db9c8a</data>
      <data key="d3">719</data>
