    <node id="ADRENOLEUCODISTROFIA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Adrenoleucodistrofia, also known as adrenoleukodystrophy, is a hereditary genetic disorder that affects lipid metabolism, specifically the metabolism of very long-chain fatty acids. This condition is characterized by the abnormal accumulation of these fatty acids in the body, which primarily impacts the myelin sheath of the nervous system and the adrenal cortex. As a result, individuals with adrenoleucodistrofia experience a range of neurological decline and various metabolic issues, including problems related to the adrenal glands.

The disorder is notable for its variable phenotypic expression, meaning that symptoms can differ significantly among affected individuals. It is relevant in the differential diagnosis of mitochondrial diseases due to its overlapping symptoms. Management of adrenoleucodistrofia often requires dietary interventions to help mitigate the effects of the disorder. Overall, adrenoleucodistrofia represents a complex interplay of genetic and metabolic factors that necessitate careful clinical attention.</data>
      <data key="d2">0745ce31d3922cbba1497dbe1d2845de,1ac394c08f6c4bdfbee5440e56a97dd0,34299d74df25d13e56cd62aab766e618,4eb5a5cd6147917dd1380d49c613a78b,8cdedff25a6a366c17615073d05a5aa2,9ceafecc91648750126099fd54024495,b8db8cc3349b8ac0cce45497da6244fc,b92f47a5ea6a962826cedb992b832f70</data>
      <data key="d3">1500</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">52</data>
      <data key="d6">5038</data>
      <data key="d7">75ebf992e7d64ad283a318cd07d31076</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CYP21A2">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">CYP21A2 is an enzyme that plays a crucial role in the production of 21-hydroxylase, and its deficiency can lead to primary adrenal insufficiency. This condition is particularly significant in young males who exhibit symptoms of adrenal insufficiency. The diagnosis of adrenal insufficiency often involves the detection of antibodies related to CYP21A2, highlighting its importance in clinical assessments. Additionally, the gene CYP21A2 is responsible for encoding the enzyme, and its deficiency is a known cause of congenital adrenal hyperplasia.</data>
      <data key="d2">095d265c06452e52ccbfbc871a19a899,1ac394c08f6c4bdfbee5440e56a97dd0,4eb5a5cd6147917dd1380d49c613a78b</data>
      <data key="d3">1501</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">5039</data>
      <data key="d7">34a9871b96604753acf48d59cb185c48</data>
    </node>
