    </node>
    <node id="MITOTANO">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Mitotano is a medication primarily used for the symptomatic treatment of advanced adrenal carcinoma, specifically in cases that are inoperable, metastatic, or recurrent. It functions by inhibiting several enzymes involved in steroidogenesis and exhibits adrenolytic effects. Additionally, Mitotano can induce the enzyme CYP3A4, which may influence the metabolism of other drugs. This multifaceted approach makes Mitotano a critical option in managing advanced adrenocortical carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d2">209bcca22090a109f52e26657bf3c999,28bca03cd2152cf2562d7aa16d7ced1d,40253f8b49d61452dbc56454cd1a07e0,8eb2b7021402eb440fe88889b78c2785,dbe034ccd694ae06cf72d347d7dbdacb</data>
      <data key="d3">1331</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">11</data>
      <data key="d6">5083</data>
      <data key="d7">e62e8c269d1b486fbd65106c48156595</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LOSCAZO J">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Loscazo J is a notable author and editor in the field of internal medicine. He is mentioned in references concerning disorders of the adrenal cortex, highlighting his contributions to the understanding of these medical conditions. Loscazo J has played a significant role in the book "Principles of Internal Medicine," where he addresses topics such as congenital adrenal hyperplasia. Additionally, he serves as an editor for the esteemed medical textbook "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine," where he has contributed to the sections on pheochromocytoma and has been actively involved in the broader medical literature and research related to internal medicine.</data>
      <data key="d2">1400aceda067bb7180ca90f0a5335bfe,8fe4d0101ea7a6ddaefd71c963d9ecc7,973f9100fbfeb9ef31038f51312620a9,b7b162b783ea688c5fe9b7f531b46aaf,dbe034ccd694ae06cf72d347d7dbdacb</data>
      <data key="d3">1283</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">5084</data>
      <data key="d7">8cfe4f9e481647b5a1fa659f50e01456</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HORMONAS ESTEROIDES">
      <data key="d0" />
      <data key="d1">HORMONAS ESTEROIDES son un grupo de hormonas que incluyen la aldosterona y el cortisol. Estas hormonas desempe&#241;an un papel crucial en la regulaci&#243;n de diversas funciones en el cuerpo.</data>
      <data key="d2">473502714ce73073f93b598ca0edcc21,dbe034ccd694ae06cf72d347d7dbdacb</data>
      <data key="d3">1328</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5085</data>
      <data key="d7">dabd496ed89a4688a389756d31c4c486</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENFERMEDAD DE MCCUNE-ALBRIGHT">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">La enfermedad de McCune-Albright es causada por mutaciones som&#225;ticas en el GNAS, que afectan la regulaci&#243;n de la prote&#237;na Gs&#945; y el AMPc.</data>
      <data key="d2">62abcb4e755766817e948341a56b196b</data>
      <data key="d3">285</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">5086</data>
      <data key="d7">a4aacfecff6f4fba8025ce2a15b5bff8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GL&#193;NDULAS SUPRARRENALES">
      <data key="d0">GEO</data>
      <data key="d1">The "GL&#193;NDULAS SUPRARRENALES," or adrenal glands, are vital organs responsible for the production of various hormones, including androgens such as dehydroepiandrosterone (DHEA) and cortisol. These glands play a significant role in the endocrine system and are involved in several physiological processes. In particular, the production of androgens by the adrenal glands can contribute to hyperandrogenism, a condition often observed in polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS). Additionally, the bilateral growth of the adrenal glands can serve as a diagnostic indicator for Cushing's syndrome, a disorder characterized by excessive cortisol production. Furthermore, the adrenal glands are also the site where pheochromocytomas, tumors that can affect hormone production, may occur, which are associated with mutations in the RET gene. Overall, the adrenal glands are crucial for hormone regulation and can be implicated in various medical conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">401a6af689562ae0b9d7fe627eb56d0f,62abcb4e755766817e948341a56b196b,92d73407a11da797ae160c4ac13a6efe</data>
      <data key="d3">1329</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">5087</data>
      <data key="d7">6713b86bc8594d06b3bf2d720c4f6cb3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TUMORES NEUROENDOCRINOS PANCRE&#193;TICOS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Los tumores neuroendocrinos pancre&#225;ticos son una causa de secreci&#243;n ect&#243;pica de ACTH, que puede contribuir al s&#237;ndrome de Cushing.</data>
      <data key="d2">62abcb4e755766817e948341a56b196b</data>
      <data key="d3">1329</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5088</data>
      <data key="d7">4f29f355d6ae458997c121da3250e893</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARCINOMAS DE C&#201;LULAS PEQUE&#209;AS PULMONARES">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Los carcinomas de c&#233;lulas peque&#241;as pulmonares pueden causar s&#237;ndrome de Cushing por producci&#243;n ect&#243;pica de ACTH.</data>
      <data key="d2">62abcb4e755766817e948341a56b196b</data>
      <data key="d3">1329</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5089</data>
      <data key="d7">5ad3cc32596b412fa802b59882a6c407</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROTE&#205;NA GS&#913;">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">La prote&#237;na Gs&#945; es una prote&#237;na reguladora que juega un papel crucial en la se&#241;alizaci&#243;n celular y est&#225; implicada en la enfermedad de McCune-Albright.</data>
      <data key="d2">62abcb4e755766817e948341a56b196b</data>
      <data key="d3">285</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">5090</data>
      <data key="d7">e504e826b1da46169c8683dbcb236b3c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LITERATURA REVISADA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">La literatura revisada se refiere a la informaci&#243;n actualizada sobre el s&#237;ndrome de Cushing, que se encuentra en fuentes como UpToDate.</data>
      <data key="d2">62abcb4e755766817e948341a56b196b</data>
      <data key="d3">1329</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5091</data>
      <data key="d7">a4086d9ac04944d1b2dad21a05f8bc4b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLANDULAS SUPRARRENALES">
      <data key="d0">GEO</data>
      <data key="d1">Las gl&#225;ndulas suprarrenales son &#243;rganos que producen hormonas, incluyendo cortisol, y su crecimiento bilateral puede ser diagn&#243;stico del s&#237;ndrome de Cushing.</data>
      <data key="d2">62abcb4e755766817e948341a56b196b</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">5092</data>
      <data key="d7">d2970b4b48d34ecc8a104dc3dfe8fc5e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MUTACIONES SOM&#193;TICAS">
      <data key="d0" />
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">62abcb4e755766817e948341a56b196b</data>
      <data key="d3">285</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">5093</data>
      <data key="d7">ad809faf8d3e4a91bd74fc8f4554654b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLUCOCORTICOIDS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Glucocorticoids are a class of steroid hormones that play a significant role in the regulation of metabolism and immune response. However, when used long-term or excessively, glucocorticoids can lead to osteoporosis, a condition characterized by weakened bones. This adverse effect occurs due to their impact on calcium absorption and bone formation, making individuals more susceptible to bone density loss. Additionally, resistance to glucocorticoids can result in various clinical conditions, highlighting the importance of careful management of these medications.</data>
      <data key="d2">08bc93c6dcf6df5ebf660f30b1f1ef2d,65ba063e1a9e43a87dbfe9cf627674b1,bcbca87f5ebf2c5d260810cf83a70257</data>
      <data key="d3">604</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">5094</data>
      <data key="d7">abc4a51c949942c0a9bd60c0b5e64505</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DEXAMETHASONE">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Dexamethasone is a synthetic glucocorticoid and corticosteroid medication utilized in the diagnosis and treatment of various medical conditions. It is commonly employed in tests to suppress cortisol production, aiding in the diagnosis of conditions such as Cushing syndrome. Additionally, Dexamethasone is used to differentiate between functional and tumor-related causes of hyperandrogenism. Its therapeutic applications include the treatment of Cushing's syndrome, where it works to suppress adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) levels. Overall, Dexamethasone plays a significant role in both diagnostic and therapeutic settings related to hormonal disorders.</data>
      <data key="d2">05a6995cef9c8d0279b9f026da1ffed1,8fe4d0101ea7a6ddaefd71c963d9ecc7,bcbca87f5ebf2c5d260810cf83a70257</data>
      <data key="d3">1332</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">5095</data>
      <data key="d7">b3f9595dc4b241eaa38bc3169e4abf4b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RESISTENCIA TISULAR A GLUCOCORTICOIDES">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Tissue resistance to glucocorticoids is a condition where the body's tissues do not respond effectively to glucocorticoid hormones, leading to clinical symptoms of excess hormones.</data>
      <data key="d2">bcbca87f5ebf2c5d260810cf83a70257</data>
      <data key="d3">1327</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">5096</data>
      <data key="d7">4e22939ad8ef41fdb096129a14f6a97f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MUTACIONES EN EL DOMINIO DE FIJACI&#211;N A LOS ESTEROIDES">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Mutations in the steroid-binding domain can affect the function of glucocorticoid receptors, leading to resistance to glucocorticoids.</data>
      <data key="d2">bcbca87f5ebf2c5d260810cf83a70257</data>
      <data key="d3">1327</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5097</data>
      <data key="d7">78abfefa3ee64c7ca3331b335bcd96db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="S&#205;NDROME DE BARTTER">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">El s&#237;ndrome de Bartter es un defecto gen&#233;tico raro que se manifiesta como un trastorno autos&#243;mico recesivo, afectando espec&#237;ficamente a la porci&#243;n gruesa del asa ascendente de Henle en los ri&#241;ones. Este s&#237;ndrome se caracteriza por la presencia de hipopotasemia (bajos niveles de potasio en sangre) y alcalosis metab&#243;lica, junto con niveles elevados de renina y aldosterona. A pesar de estas alteraciones, no se presenta hipertensi&#243;n. La condici&#243;n resulta en un hiperaldosteronismo hiperrenin&#233;mico, que es consecuencia de la p&#233;rdida de sodio y cloro a nivel renal.</data>
      <data key="d2">473502714ce73073f93b598ca0edcc21,e5103e718912067f1a2766544cf1b4db</data>
      <data key="d3">11</data>
      <data key="d4">0</data>
      <data key="d5">5</data>
      <data key="d6">5098</data>
      <data key="d7">34bd507a135e4c959f25009f0e7fecf4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIABETES MAL CONTROLADA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">La diabetes mal controlada puede estar asociada con hipercortisolismo fisiol&#243;gico y caracter&#237;sticas del s&#237;ndrome de Cushing.</data>
      <data key="d2">e5103e718912067f1a2766544cf1b4db</data>
      <data key="d3">1327</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5099</data>
      <data key="d7">72313d9de13a454cb3ed3d19f0493405</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHILADELPHIA">
      <data key="d0">GEO</data>
      <data key="d1">Philadelphia is a city in Pennsylvania, USA, renowned for its historical significance and its prominent medical institutions. It serves as the location for Elsevier, the publisher of the Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, which provides essential guidelines and information on endocrine disorders. Additionally, Philadelphia is noted for its role in medical publishing, being home to other notable publishers such as Lippincott-Raven and WB Saunders Company, which published works like "Enteral and Tube Feeding." Overall, Philadelphia is recognized not only for its rich history but also as a key hub for medical literature and publishing in the United States.</data>
      <data key="d2">209bcca22090a109f52e26657bf3c999,2415ccd829f700cbe1975f4bca87245f,2a73a65aa862d055c1e417cc50a42166,313611d83ceda2a812387fbcffbedad6,41b6e4025d59564d7881f459e230639d,46466da830644a88f74108645c668fd5,47ba8105b81c59f81a9038446898d200,6aa8928e063b4107007dc14cc1b301bd,7ea112a550fd775a80c3eda15663fba5,7f3df1437755b60344fdffd2ef3b8a9e,85df619f6d535bbe5c0485f540242903,85ee651c191c3b0c5fe786fe2e0718a4,8f5500c9a9e74d855b17365c80375df3,97afc23d7c06c09383611da00b084017,9944c8e8d3d7769c5297f8dfb9b6f664</data>
      <data key="d3">1301</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">12</data>
      <data key="d6">5100</data>
      <data key="d7">f0e51f9f0b8e4a23864788928db7ed7d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ADENOMA SUPRARRENAL">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Adenoma suprarrenal is a benign tumor of the adrenal gland that can produce aldosterone, leading to conditions such as Cushing's syndrome and hyperaldosteronism.</data>
      <data key="d2">7f3df1437755b60344fdffd2ef3b8a9e</data>
      <data key="d3">1338</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5101</data>
      <data key="d7">13377bbf8a37405fa76d42c33b332f2d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYPERALDOSTERONISM FAMILIAR TIPO 1">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Familial hyperaldosteronism type 1 is an autosomal dominant hereditary syndrome that causes excessive aldosterone production due to a genetic mutation.</data>
      <data key="d2">7f3df1437755b60344fdffd2ef3b8a9e</data>
      <data key="d3">1338</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">5102</data>
      <data key="d7">f953e7000f1549fc98a2e303fe0a7f74</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYPERALDOSTERONISM FAMILIAR TIPO 2">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Familial hyperaldosteronism type 2 is a hereditary condition characterized by hyperaldosteronism without suppression by dexamethasone, often indistinguishable from primary hyperaldosteronism.</data>
      <data key="d2">7f3df1437755b60344fdffd2ef3b8a9e</data>
      <data key="d3">1338</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5103</data>
      <data key="d7">1719e1e1c1b34c89b884a0cfccd6c96c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYPERALDOSTERONISM">
      <data key="d0" />
      <data key="d1">Hyperaldosteronism is a medical condition characterized by the excessive production of aldosterone, a hormone that plays a crucial role in regulating blood pressure and electrolyte balance. This condition often arises due to an adrenal adenoma or hyperplasia, which are abnormalities in the adrenal glands. As a result of the elevated aldosterone levels, individuals with hyperaldosteronism typically experience hypertension (high blood pressure) and may also have electrolyte imbalances, particularly low renin levels in the blood.</data>
      <data key="d2">05edb31ec01c621a00d71bd5868784e0,1cdfccda20ac47427fc4c103ddd87cf3,41b6e4025d59564d7881f459e230639d,7f3df1437755b60344fdffd2ef3b8a9e</data>
      <data key="d3">1338</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">13</data>
      <data key="d6">5104</data>
      <data key="d7">e20d1e6a534043f2a658befe7e3b344d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIPERALDOSTERONISMO FAMILIAR TIPO 3">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">HIPERALDOSTERONISMO FAMILIAR TIPO 3 es un trastorno autos&#243;mico dominante caracterizado por un fenotipo agresivo que se manifiesta a trav&#233;s de hipertensi&#243;n grave y resistente al tratamiento desde la infancia. Esta condici&#243;n es causada por mutaciones en el gen KCNJ5, el cual codifica el canal de potasio Kir 3.4.</data>
      <data key="d2">261db9bb173f461ca4f26fd087d3da2a,bb2f48277736ce24d1ff0310a682376d</data>
      <data key="d3">183</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">4</data>
      <data key="d6">5105</data>
      <data key="d7">82ed03a4d85b4d2fbabec3e03fdc6fd5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KCNJ5">
      <data key="d0">GENE</data>
      <data key="d1">El gen KCNJ5 codifica el canal de potasio Kir 3.4, cuya mutaci&#243;n est&#225; relacionada con el hiperaldosteronismo familiar tipo 3.</data>
      <data key="d2">261db9bb173f461ca4f26fd087d3da2a,bb2f48277736ce24d1ff0310a682376d</data>
      <data key="d3">183</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5106</data>
      <data key="d7">241bfc3614e24623bc613077aea15593</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SEUDOHIPOALDOSTERONISMO">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">SEUDOHIPOALDOSTERONISMO, or pseudohypoaldosteronism, is a condition defined by the body's resistance to the action of mineralocorticoids, particularly aldosterone. This resistance results in a syndrome characterized by salt-wasting, which manifests as hyponatremia (low sodium levels) and hyperkalemia (high potassium levels), despite the presence of elevated levels of both aldosterone and renin in the bloodstream. The symptoms associated with SEUDOHIPOALDOSTERONISMO are similar to those observed in hypoaldosteronism, highlighting the condition's impact on electrolyte balance and fluid regulation in the body.</data>
      <data key="d2">05edb31ec01c621a00d71bd5868784e0,261db9bb173f461ca4f26fd087d3da2a</data>
      <data key="d3">183</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">5107</data>
      <data key="d7">bf8f8af3d9424e5da6ced0584bed92f0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NR3C2">
      <data key="d0">GENE</data>
      <data key="d1">El gen NR3C2 codifica el receptor de los mineralocorticoides, y su mutaci&#243;n est&#225; asociada con el seudohipoaldosteronismo renal tipo 1.</data>
      <data key="d2">261db9bb173f461ca4f26fd087d3da2a</data>
      <data key="d3">183</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5108</data>
      <data key="d7">dc0263aa71874e48a3ed58ae013bcc8a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SUPRARRENALES">
      <data key="d0">GEO</data>
      <data key="d1">Las suprarrenales son gl&#225;ndulas que producen hormonas, y su disfunci&#243;n puede estar relacionada con trastornos como el hiperaldosteronismo y el seudohipoaldosteronismo.</data>
      <data key="d2">261db9bb173f461ca4f26fd087d3da2a</data>
      <data key="d3">183</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">5109</data>
      <data key="d7">c77379ca1db145bb96d18c2c8debb331</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RENINA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">RENINA is an enzyme produced by the kidneys that plays a crucial role in the regulation of blood pressure and fluid balance. It is significant in the evaluation of adrenal disorders, as its secretion is influenced by various factors, including the levels of deoxycorticosterone acetate (DOCA), with higher DOCA levels leading to decreased renin secretion. Overall, RENINA is essential for maintaining cardiovascular health and homeostasis in the body.</data>
      <data key="d2">261db9bb173f461ca4f26fd087d3da2a,6ebb5385c8eda09a187e6d0595812bbf,dcbf695abeb609e2cdbff56c1bde675c</data>
      <data key="d3">1330</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">5110</data>
      <data key="d7">7b509ad2952744c2a0508efddf3f1db8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENAC">
      <data key="d0">GENE</data>
      <data key="d1">El canal de sodio sensible a amilorida (ENaC) es un canal i&#243;nico que regula la reabsorci&#243;n de sodio en los ri&#241;ones y est&#225; implicado en trastornos como el seudohipoaldosteronismo.</data>
      <data key="d2">261db9bb173f461ca4f26fd087d3da2a</data>
      <data key="d3">183</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5111</data>
      <data key="d7">0e8dc21f850e471fb1f5eb439b49892c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STEWART PM">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Stewart PM is a distinguished medical professional and researcher known for his contributions to the field of endocrinology, particularly regarding the adrenal cortex and its disorders. He is an author of the Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, where he has notably contributed a chapter focused on the adrenal cortex. Through his research and writings, Stewart PM has established himself as a significant figure in the study of adrenal health and related conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">05edb31ec01c621a00d71bd5868784e0,b9bf0b94c01d3dfa9ef4cbce09dd7ec3,fec130428af4ee62996a9b72494212dc</data>
      <data key="d3">1300</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">5112</data>
      <data key="d7">fde169b18e5f4f9682a2b4118a28220e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NEWELL-PRICE JDC">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Newell-Price JDC is a distinguished medical professional and researcher known for their contributions to the field of endocrinology. They co-authored the chapter on the adrenal cortex in the Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, showcasing their expertise and involvement in academic literature. Additionally, Newell-Price JDC has made significant contributions to the study of the adrenal cortex, further establishing their reputation as an authority in this area of research.</data>
      <data key="d2">05edb31ec01c621a00d71bd5868784e0,fec130428af4ee62996a9b72494212dc</data>
