    <node id="DIAGN&#211;STICO DE HIPOALDOSTERONISMO">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Diagnosis of hypoaldosteronism involves identifying insufficient aldosterone production, which can lead to electrolyte imbalances and low blood pressure.</data>
      <data key="d2">05edb31ec01c621a00d71bd5868784e0</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">5122</data>
      <data key="d7">7f21a785c9994d7381ef9af04ababb65</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIPOALDOSTERONISMO">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Hipoaldosteronismo is a medical condition characterized by the insufficient production of aldosterone, a hormone crucial for regulating sodium and potassium levels in the body. This deficiency can lead to significant imbalances in these electrolytes, often resulting in conditions such as hyponatremia (low sodium levels) and hyperkalemia (high potassium levels). The condition affects the body's ability to maintain proper electrolyte balance, which is essential for various physiological functions.</data>
      <data key="d2">05edb31ec01c621a00d71bd5868784e0,1cdfccda20ac47427fc4c103ddd87cf3,3733abb85045220c8e373e316f858791,473502714ce73073f93b598ca0edcc21,a56fad7f6dac4a75bd4748b247aa9215,fee4d9611c98c81859bccdb4ded03622</data>
      <data key="d3">1429</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">4</data>
      <data key="d6">5123</data>
      <data key="d7">eaaa1ba838544d9c913b9467d331b914</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIPOALDOSTERONISMO HIPORRENIN&#201;MICO">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Hyporeninemic hypoaldosteronism is a specific type of hypoaldosteronism where there is low renin activity alongside low aldosterone levels.</data>
      <data key="d2">05edb31ec01c621a00d71bd5868784e0</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">5124</data>
      <data key="d7">25a143ad4dec40b7abc73b4c3ee82dd1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIPOALDOSTERONISMO SECUNDARIO">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">HIPOALDOSTERONISMO SECUNDARIO, or secondary hypoaldosteronism, is a condition characterized by reduced production of aldosterone due to factors that originate outside the adrenal glands. This condition arises from external influences that disrupt the normal functioning of the adrenal glands, leading to decreased aldosterone levels. Common causes include adrenal gland damage and various hormonal imbalances that interfere with the regulation of aldosterone production.</data>
      <data key="d2">05edb31ec01c621a00d71bd5868784e0</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">5125</data>
      <data key="d7">7837f184e7a44dfc85f3fbf2b3ded529</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIPOALDOSTERONISMO PRIMARIO">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Primary hypoaldosteronism is a condition where the adrenal glands fail to produce sufficient aldosterone, often leading to electrolyte imbalances.</data>
      <data key="d2">05edb31ec01c621a00d71bd5868784e0</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">5126</data>
      <data key="d7">dfcfe055a1774538af37f6f3f8b9b07b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BORNSTEIN SR">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Bornstein SR is an author recognized for contributing to the Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline concerning the diagnosis and treatment of primary adrenal insufficiency. This work has been published in the Journal of Clinical Endocrinology and Metabolism, where Bornstein SR provided essential guidelines aimed at improving the understanding and management of this condition.</data>
      <data key="d2">152aeb8f10c2f4548114403c05af6b55,b9bf0b94c01d3dfa9ef4cbce09dd7ec3</data>
