    </node>
    <node id="11-DESOXICORTICOSTERONA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">11-Deoxycorticosterone is a steroid hormone that can influence blood pressure and electrolyte balance, often associated with conditions affecting aldosterone levels.</data>
      <data key="d2">41b6e4025d59564d7881f459e230639d</data>
      <data key="d3">1281</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5136</data>
      <data key="d7">5f5f0695942241ae910e04e98a29da04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DOCA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">DOCA, or Deoxycorticosterone, is a steroid hormone that plays a significant role in the regulation of blood pressure and electrolyte balance. When present in excess, DOCA can lead to conditions such as hypertension and hypokalemia. Additionally, Deoxycorticosterone acetate (DOCA) is a synthetic form of this mineralocorticoid, which is known to cause hypertension and is associated with the secretion of aldosterone. Overall, both forms of DOCA are linked to increased blood pressure and electrolyte imbalances, highlighting their importance in endocrine and cardiovascular health.</data>
      <data key="d2">41b6e4025d59564d7881f459e230639d,dcbf695abeb609e2cdbff56c1bde675c</data>
      <data key="d3">1635</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">4</data>
      <data key="d6">5137</data>
      <data key="d7">cab87847d3dd41af98a35983f98b50ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIPERALDOSTERONISMO PRIMARIO">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Hiperaldosteronismo primario, tambi&#233;n conocido como primary hyperaldosteronism, es un trastorno endocrino caracterizado por la producci&#243;n excesiva de aldosterona. Esta condici&#243;n puede resultar en hipertensi&#243;n y hipokalemia, as&#237; como en otros problemas de salud. Adem&#225;s, el hiperaldosteronismo primario es un &#225;rea de investigaci&#243;n importante para Mart&#237;n Cuesta Hern&#225;ndez, quien se enfoca en los trastornos endocrinos relacionados con esta condici&#243;n.</data>
      <data key="d2">41b6e4025d59564d7881f459e230639d,75a4b2d504de480eaef247aa682bfd05,bb2f48277736ce24d1ff0310a682376d,dcbf695abeb609e2cdbff56c1bde675c</data>
      <data key="d3">1281</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">20</data>
      <data key="d6">5138</data>
      <data key="d7">f830e23a850d4da196c9f08e465900fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CYP11B2">
      <data key="d0" />
      <data key="d1">CYP11B2 is a gene that plays a crucial role in the production of aldosterone, a hormone that regulates sodium and potassium levels in the body. Its genetic variations are significant in the context of familial hyperaldosteronism type 1, a condition characterized by excessive production of aldosterone.</data>
      <data key="d2">41b6e4025d59564d7881f459e230639d,bb2f48277736ce24d1ff0310a682376d</data>
      <data key="d3">1282</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">5139</data>
      <data key="d7">4304d2e1563448b09025279e773c12b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CYP11B">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">CYP11B refers to the gene coding for 11-&#946;-hydroxylase, which is involved in steroid hormone biosynthesis.</data>
      <data key="d2">dcbf695abeb609e2cdbff56c1bde675c</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5140</data>
      <data key="d7">ddf3a668be1348c4a780806104c68e94</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CYP17">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">CYP17 refers to the gene coding for 17-&#945;-hydroxylase, which plays a role in the production of steroid hormones.</data>
      <data key="d2">dcbf695abeb609e2cdbff56c1bde675c</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5141</data>
      <data key="d7">7c413bd3f28f4f99958bccb1f16a777c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYPOKALEMIA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Hypokalemia is a medical condition characterized by low potassium levels in the blood. This condition can significantly impact various populations, including preterm infants, who may be particularly vulnerable to its effects. Additionally, hypokalemia is often associated with excess levels of deoxycorticosterone acetate (DOCA), which can contribute to the development of this electrolyte imbalance.</data>
      <data key="d2">9ef354c0ee5ac877ce8fba79fcc7e2b3,dcbf695abeb609e2cdbff56c1bde675c</data>
      <data key="d3">1635</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">5142</data>
      <data key="d7">51572f4f32ba4795a22359d7c2a1a332</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GEN QUIM&#201;RICO">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">A chimeric gene resulting from the crossover of sequences between CYP11B1 and CYP11B2, affecting aldosterone production.</data>
      <data key="d2">dcbf695abeb609e2cdbff56c1bde675c</data>
      <data key="d3">1281</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5143</data>
      <data key="d7">76cfb6d78ff84126a09f22b29513d04c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HISTORIA FAMILIAR">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">The term "HISTORIA FAMILIAR" refers to the medical history of family members, which can play a crucial role in understanding various health conditions and their genetic predispositions. Family history is particularly significant in diagnosing primary hyperaldosteronism, especially among young patients presenting with hypertension. Additionally, it can indicate a genetic predisposition to conditions such as nephrolithiasis. Furthermore, a family history of type 1 diabetes, growth retardation, iron deficiency anemia, and steatorrhea may serve as risk factors for celiac disease. Moreover, the history of atopic diseases within a family can influence the likelihood of developing food allergies. Overall, "HISTORIA FAMILIAR" encompasses a wide range of hereditary health issues that can impact individual health outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d2">41babae4bb4b80271c9bfd9da1528392,a29197a0d36ee2c688c9c959435e999f,bc394d70461777b6300d5449185a7859,dcbf695abeb609e2cdbff56c1bde675c</data>
      <data key="d3">1281</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">4</data>
      <data key="d6">5144</data>
      <data key="d7">2f39bc7d65844fbda101d145cd00d09e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RESISTENCIA PRIMARIA AL CORTISOL">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Primary cortisol resistance is a rare familial syndrome characterized by increased cortisol secretion without clinical signs of hypercortisolism.</data>
      <data key="d2">dcbf695abeb609e2cdbff56c1bde675c</data>
      <data key="d3">1327</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5145</data>
      <data key="d7">916b8ed5a25a4ecfa483adc41403361d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="S&#205;NDROME FAMILIAR">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">A hereditary syndrome that can lead to primary hyperaldosteronism, often presenting with hypertension and a family history of similar conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">dcbf695abeb609e2cdbff56c1bde675c</data>
      <data key="d3">1281</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5146</data>
      <data key="d7">97e8b5f260d3442887ca5af613ac174a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HISTORIA FAMILIAR DE HIPERTENSI&#211;N">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Family history of hypertension can indicate a genetic predisposition to conditions like primary hyperaldosteronism.</data>
      <data key="d2">dcbf695abeb609e2cdbff56c1bde675c</data>
      <data key="d3">1281</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5147</data>
      <data key="d7">8cbd397c66e143149f8620af4300cb2e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PRUEBAS CL&#193;SICAS INDIRECTAS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Classic indirect tests, such as the dexamethasone suppression test, are used for diagnosing conditions like primary hyperaldosteronism.</data>
      <data key="d2">dcbf695abeb609e2cdbff56c1bde675c</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5148</data>
      <data key="d7">371e0345897c408dbcebd5a8886e4272</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ESTUDIO GEN&#201;TICO DIRECTO">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Direct genetic testing is recommended for diagnosing familial hyperaldosteronism and should replace classic indirect tests.</data>
      <data key="d2">dcbf695abeb609e2cdbff56c1bde675c</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5149</data>
      <data key="d7">2238b91d26024956aa181d97b228f1f1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ICTUS A UNA EDAD JOVEN">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Stroke at a young age can be associated with familial hyperaldosteronism and may indicate a genetic risk factor.</data>
      <data key="d2">dcbf695abeb609e2cdbff56c1bde675c</data>
      <data key="d3">1281</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5150</data>
      <data key="d7">3355407c706a4a0da090f34318fb5c96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIPERTENSI&#211;N RESISTENTE">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Resistant hypertension is a condition where blood pressure remains high despite treatment, often linked to primary hyperaldosteronism.</data>
      <data key="d2">dcbf695abeb609e2cdbff56c1bde675c</data>
      <data key="d3">1281</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5151</data>
      <data key="d7">ac1e87f22de343b094be1f698ed4716f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TUMORES SUPRARRENALES">
      <data key="d0" />
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">dcbf695abeb609e2cdbff56c1bde675c</data>
      <data key="d3">1635</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5152</data>
      <data key="d7">cbc37ff343bb42828f4fc81998a0ab7a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIPERALDOSTERONISMO FAMILIAR TIPO 2">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">El hiperaldosteronismo familiar de tipo 2 es un trastorno autos&#243;mico dominante que no presenta supresi&#243;n de aldosterona con dexametasona y se relaciona con la regi&#243;n 7p22.</data>
      <data key="d2">bb2f48277736ce24d1ff0310a682376d</data>
      <data key="d3">1281</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5153</data>
      <data key="d7">67e341e6e9d04a85b07a60694ad513f4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CYP11B1">
      <data key="d0">GENE</data>
      <data key="d1">El gen CYP11B1 tambi&#233;n est&#225; relacionado con la producci&#243;n de hormonas esteroides y su intercambio con CYP11B2 es significativo en el hiperaldosteronismo familiar tipo 1.</data>
      <data key="d2">bb2f48277736ce24d1ff0310a682376d</data>
      <data key="d3">1281</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">5154</data>
      <data key="d7">b6e4a20612874c8aa38945e5bafb7672</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ADENOMA PRODUCTOR DE ALDOSTERONA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Aldosterone-producing adenoma is a type of tumor in the adrenal gland that secretes excess aldosterone, causing primary hyperaldosteronism.</data>
      <data key="d2">1cdfccda20ac47427fc4c103ddd87cf3</data>
      <data key="d3">1338</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5155</data>
      <data key="d7">477b0838c2f840569828e54d090a7767</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ADRENALCTOMY">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Adrenalectomy is a surgical procedure to remove one or both adrenal glands, often performed to treat conditions like hyperaldosteronism.</data>
      <data key="d2">1cdfccda20ac47427fc4c103ddd87cf3</data>
      <data key="d3">1338</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5156</data>
      <data key="d7">5c17bb26c7a54d30b331c98b142e3a58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RENIN">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Renin is an enzyme secreted by the kidneys that plays a crucial role in blood pressure regulation and fluid balance.</data>
      <data key="d2">1cdfccda20ac47427fc4c103ddd87cf3</data>
      <data key="d3">1330</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5157</data>
      <data key="d7">c521fd7a436f40daa7b72098c2d65fae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MANEJO DE HIPERALDOSTERONISMO">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Management of hyperaldosteronism involves diagnosis, treatment options including surgery, and monitoring of aldosterone and renin levels.</data>
      <data key="d2">1cdfccda20ac47427fc4c103ddd87cf3</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">5158</data>
      <data key="d7">87d23f2077f04d11a8795d1740f5e9bd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NIVEL DE RENINA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Renin levels are measured to assess the activity of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, which is crucial in diagnosing conditions like hyperaldosteronism.</data>
      <data key="d2">1cdfccda20ac47427fc4c103ddd87cf3</data>
      <data key="d3">1338</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5159</data>
      <data key="d7">3e5bb28dc8fd4091a57d85e3a2fd58f4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIETA RICA EN SODIO">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">A sodium-rich diet may be recommended to prevent hyperkalemia in patients with hypoaldosteronism following adrenal surgery.</data>
      <data key="d2">1cdfccda20ac47427fc4c103ddd87cf3</data>
      <data key="d3">1429</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5160</data>
      <data key="d7">95be476258a4488b9f33dbfcf344cc6f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METANEFRINAS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">METANEFRINAS are biochemical markers utilized in the diagnosis of adrenal tumors and related conditions, particularly pheochromocytoma and paragangliomas. They are metabolites of catecholamines, which are substances produced by the adrenal glands. These metabolites are typically measured in urine tests, serving as a diagnostic marker for pheochromocytoma, a type of tumor that can affect adrenal function. Additionally, metanephrines are assessed in laboratory tests to evaluate patients with adrenal incidentalomas, helping to identify potential underlying tumors. Overall, the measurement of metanephrines plays a crucial role in the diagnostic process for various adrenal-related conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">6ebb5385c8eda09a187e6d0595812bbf,7d4d879207b235ab3018772eea448b2a,8f5500c9a9e74d855b17365c80375df3,8fe4d0101ea7a6ddaefd71c963d9ecc7,94ef3a3488643695a99523d5c9d1f606,a0b43d4a377fca32f55f83fd79e3d9e1</data>
      <data key="d3">975</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">4</data>
      <data key="d6">5161</data>
      <data key="d7">7fe749586a3345d4992e351be5202660</data>
    </node>
    <node id="YODO-COLESTEROL">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">A treatment option for malignant pheochromocytoma involving the administration of radioactive iodine-labeled cholesterol.</data>
      <data key="d2">8f5500c9a9e74d855b17365c80375df3</data>
      <data key="d3">975</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5162</data>
      <data key="d7">b8cdf2a4f67a462c8d6835c61a390c7a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FENOXIBENZAMIDA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Fenoxibenzamida is a medication specifically utilized for the preoperative management of pheochromocytoma, a type of tumor that can cause hypertensive crises. It is recognized as the drug of choice for achieving alpha-blockade in this context, effectively helping to control blood pressure and mitigate the risks associated with surgery for patients suffering from this condition.</data>
      <data key="d2">4433d2fdc6ba080efa0cf63e8603172a,8f5500c9a9e74d855b17365c80375df3</data>
      <data key="d3">975</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5163</data>
      <data key="d7">9f6229f2549046979690f9bac98f0a80</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TUMORES SECRETORES DE CATECOLAMINAS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Tumors that secrete catecholamines, including pheochromocytomas and paragangliomas, affecting the body's stress response.</data>
      <data key="d2">8f5500c9a9e74d855b17365c80375df3</data>
      <data key="d3">978</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">5164</data>
      <data key="d7">bc30501a1cc3417eab657eb7bc27eb0a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CROMAFINES">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Cells in the adrenal medulla and sympathetic ganglia that produce catecholamines and are involved in the development of pheochromocytomas.</data>
      <data key="d2">8f5500c9a9e74d855b17365c80375df3</data>
      <data key="d3">978</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5165</data>
      <data key="d7">492917bef39d4087865d4cba9012b9ce</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MUTACIONES EN L&#205;NEA GERMINAL">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Genetic mutations that can be identified in some cases of pheochromocytomas, contributing to familial forms of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d2">8f5500c9a9e74d855b17365c80375df3</data>
      <data key="d3">978</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5166</data>
      <data key="d7">912d4576b1b645ca823f73f39e0b8e8e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NEFROANGIOESCLEROSIS">
      <data key="d0" />
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">8f5500c9a9e74d855b17365c80375df3</data>
      <data key="d3">979</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5167</data>
      <data key="d7">87f00df9e466450c9b24d6ba7180e3e4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MIBG">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">MIBG (metaiodobenzylguanidine) is a radiopharmaceutical utilized in both the diagnosis and treatment of tumors that secrete catecholamines, with a particular focus on pheochromocytoma. It serves as an important tool in imaging, allowing for the detection of pheochromocytomas and paragangliomas. MIBG provides valuable anatomical and functional information about these tumors, enhancing the understanding and management of conditions associated with catecholamine-secreting tumors.</data>
      <data key="d2">a0b43d4a377fca32f55f83fd79e3d9e1,c3d752ff301355241e1420e398f0e6a4</data>
      <data key="d3">977</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">4</data>
      <data key="d6">5168</data>
      <data key="d7">da0035c19f754eb6a80bf5d69a949966</data>
    </node>
    <node id="D&#205;AZ P&#201;REZ JA">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">D&#237;az P&#233;rez JA is an author recognized for his contributions to the medical literature on pheochromocytomas and paragangliomas. He has specifically written about these topics in the "Manual de Endocrinolog&#237;a y Nutrici&#243;n," highlighting his expertise in the field. His work is frequently referenced in discussions surrounding these endocrine tumors, underscoring his significance in advancing knowledge related to these conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">4433d2fdc6ba080efa0cf63e8603172a,973f9100fbfeb9ef31038f51312620a9,a0b43d4a377fca32f55f83fd79e3d9e1</data>
      <data key="d3">975</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">5169</data>
      <data key="d7">7cb4078577034099b85a77a6516d0362</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRESPO HERN&#193;NDEZ I">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Crespo Hern&#225;ndez I is an author recognized for contributions to the medical literature concerning pheochromocytomas and paragangliomas. This individual has been referenced in various contexts related to these conditions, particularly noted for their work in the "Manual de Endocrinolog&#237;a y Nutrici&#243;n." Through these contributions, Crespo Hern&#225;ndez I has played a significant role in advancing the understanding of these endocrine tumors.</data>
      <data key="d2">4433d2fdc6ba080efa0cf63e8603172a,973f9100fbfeb9ef31038f51312620a9,a0b43d4a377fca32f55f83fd79e3d9e1</data>
      <data key="d3">975</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">5170</data>
      <data key="d7">d2599216593348efb5dfdb8e49cc8143</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CALCIOANTAGONISTAS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Calcioantagonistas are medications that can influence catecholamine levels, which may result in false positives in diagnostic tests for pheochromocytomas. Additionally, these medications can affect vascular resistance and may interfere with the diagnosis of pheochromocytoma, particularly when administered prior to MIBG testing.</data>
      <data key="d2">a0b43d4a377fca32f55f83fd79e3d9e1,c3d752ff301355241e1420e398f0e6a4</data>
      <data key="d3">975</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5171</data>
      <data key="d7">adc1241f4adf464e8cc6e8dbfa81fc0d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANTIDEPRESIVOS TRIC&#205;CLICOS">
      <data key="d0" />
      <data key="d1">Antidepresivos tric&#237;clicos, or tricyclic antidepressants, are a class of medications known for their various effects on the body. They can interfere with the detection of catecholamines, which may result in false positives during testing. Additionally, these medications can inhibit gastric motility and affect the measurement of catecholamines and metanephrines in urine tests. Overall, antidepresivos tric&#237;clicos have significant implications for both diagnostic testing and gastrointestinal function.</data>
      <data key="d2">4433d2fdc6ba080efa0cf63e8603172a,5eb14e2cbec104d89ad73e2ac968b7fd,94ef3a3488643695a99523d5c9d1f606,a0b43d4a377fca32f55f83fd79e3d9e1,c3d752ff301355241e1420e398f0e6a4</data>
      <data key="d3">976</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">5172</data>
      <data key="d7">f13a1712fb2c496cb09d5e38353ccd5b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PARAGANGLIOMA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Paraganglioma is a rare tumor that arises from the paraganglia, which are neuroendocrine tissues associated with the autonomic nervous system. This type of tumor is often linked to genetic mutations and can occur in various locations throughout the body, although it is frequently found in the head and neck region. Paragangliomas are related to pheochromocytomas and can exhibit malignant behavior, with the potential to metastasize. Additionally, these tumors may produce catecholamines, further highlighting their connection to the nervous system and their association with certain genetic syndromes.</data>
      <data key="d2">0ec21b45b9a154289761f46105b364f2,4433d2fdc6ba080efa0cf63e8603172a,5fd3de876d776b591d7dfd812aa2873c,94ef3a3488643695a99523d5c9d1f606,973f9100fbfeb9ef31038f51312620a9,c3d752ff301355241e1420e398f0e6a4</data>
      <data key="d3">984</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">5173</data>
      <data key="d7">e956091e7c7044ce805e6e6d03c9fe11</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SDHB">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">SDHB is a gene that plays a significant role in familial paraganglioma syndrome type 4, which is characterized by the development of paragangliomas and other tumors. It is associated with hereditary pheochromocytomas and paragangliomas, particularly affecting the adrenal glands. Mutations in the SDHB gene can lead to an increased risk of tumor development, including the potential for metastatic disease in pheochromocytomas and paragangliomas. Overall, SDHB is crucial in understanding the genetic susceptibility to these types of tumors.</data>
      <data key="d2">02b8a693303f9f8603c740fefc53d526,4433d2fdc6ba080efa0cf63e8603172a,94ef3a3488643695a99523d5c9d1f606,c3d752ff301355241e1420e398f0e6a4,c77f2833180015d5264f465f51c441f3</data>
      <data key="d3">984</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">4</data>
      <data key="d6">5174</data>
      <data key="d7">e72737c8f5b840ff88092cbc3eed7148</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRISIS HIPERTENSIVAS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Crisis hipertensivas are episodes of high blood pressure that can occur in patients with feocromocitomas and require careful management.</data>
      <data key="d2">4433d2fdc6ba080efa0cf63e8603172a</data>
      <data key="d3">975</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5175</data>
      <data key="d7">1cbd4dd4245a493fa724e1a2915f7457</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VON HIPPEL-LINDAU">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Von Hippel-Lindau is an autosomal dominant disorder that affects approximately 1 in 35,000 people. It is characterized by the presence of various tumors, including pheochromocytomas and paragangliomas, among others. This genetic condition leads to the development of multiple tumor types, highlighting its significant impact on affected individuals.</data>
      <data key="d2">973f9100fbfeb9ef31038f51312620a9,c3d752ff301355241e1420e398f0e6a4</data>
      <data key="d3">986</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">5176</data>
      <data key="d7">ecdc08aa5ff94aedb9b07b8a9e1462ab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SUCCINATE DEHYDROGENASE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Succinato deshidrogenasa (SDH) is a mitochondrial enzyme complex involved in the tricarboxylic acid cycle and is associated with tumor suppression.</data>
      <data key="d2">973f9100fbfeb9ef31038f51312620a9</data>
      <data key="d3">986</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">5177</data>
      <data key="d7">024ff10a67ef44db91e20a6146b5d302</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PHEOCHROMOCYTOMA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Pheochromocytoma is a tumor of the adrenal gland that produces catecholamines, leading to various clinical symptoms.</data>
      <data key="d2">973f9100fbfeb9ef31038f51312620a9</data>
      <data key="d3">986</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">5178</data>
      <data key="d7">6e516ad12c1d4451a7b862f9bc908660</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VHL GENE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">The VHL gene is a tumor suppressor gene associated with Von Hippel-Lindau syndrome and is involved in the regulation of hypoxia-inducible factors.</data>
      <data key="d2">973f9100fbfeb9ef31038f51312620a9</data>
      <data key="d3">986</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">5179</data>
      <data key="d7">e09e5c16dc51415ea94c3d03e7e91758</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HARRISON'S PRINCIPLES OF INTERNAL MEDICINE">
      <data key="d0" />
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">973f9100fbfeb9ef31038f51312620a9</data>
      <data key="d3">1283</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">5180</data>
      <data key="d7">a7829f0280204757842a3735956c5c60</data>
