      <data key="d7">32f250011326477981e6506e80942697</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ADRENAL INCIDENTALOMA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">ADRENAL INCIDENTALOMA is a medical condition characterized by the discovery of an adrenal mass that is not associated with symptoms of adrenal dysfunction. This condition typically arises incidentally during imaging studies conducted for unrelated reasons, and it does not present any clinical symptoms related to adrenal hormone overproduction or deficiency.</data>
      <data key="d2">1bfdc8a3b2272265ee6fd132fcf3a8ce,b7b162b783ea688c5fe9b7f531b46aaf</data>
      <data key="d3">1336</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">5203</data>
      <data key="d7">d4881761762f4549b5166ca137e79709</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BANKOS I">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Bankos I is a contributor to the European Society of Endocrinology guidelines on adrenal incidentalomas.</data>
      <data key="d2">b7b162b783ea688c5fe9b7f531b46aaf</data>
      <data key="d3">1334</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5204</data>
      <data key="d7">e6a3a8280c5544a7a6b8b58997ab48cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TAC ABDOMINAL">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">TAC abdominal, also known as a computed tomography (CT) scan of the abdomen, is a diagnostic imaging procedure utilized primarily to identify incidentalomas in the adrenal glands and to detect masses or abnormalities within the abdominal region. This imaging technique has been instrumental in confirming diagnoses, such as pancreatic adenocarcinoma, by providing detailed images that help in the assessment of various conditions affecting the abdominal organs.</data>
      <data key="d2">1bfdc8a3b2272265ee6fd132fcf3a8ce,94ef3a3488643695a99523d5c9d1f606,acd36e6dbcd98757c4cf33ff090764ee</data>
      <data key="d3">1335</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">5205</data>
      <data key="d7">0469ee1c9574427492f99f27946da704</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DHEA">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Dehydroepiandrosterone (DHEA) is a weak androgen hormone produced by the adrenal glands. It plays a significant role in the body's hormonal balance and is often measured in the evaluation of adrenal function. DHEA can activate certain steroid receptors, albeit with lower affinity. In cases of aromatase deficiency, DHEA levels may be elevated, which can contribute to virilization symptoms. Additionally, DHEA is relevant in the study of adrenal incidentalomas, as it is an important marker in assessing adrenal health. It is noteworthy that DHEA can be converted into more potent androgens, such as testosterone, further emphasizing its role in the endocrine system.</data>
      <data key="d2">1bfdc8a3b2272265ee6fd132fcf3a8ce,401a6af689562ae0b9d7fe627eb56d0f,6ebb5385c8eda09a187e6d0595812bbf,88e88db2c25e4b16fdf9464354512152,ebfccc35cef320c7240cf36dd52e847a</data>
      <data key="d3">1336</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">5206</data>
      <data key="d7">7bd3e823d44e46b3a2cb792e4b918b3c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="POTASSIUM">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Potassium is an essential mineral and electrolyte that plays a crucial role in various bodily functions. It is commonly measured in blood tests to assess adrenal function and other health conditions. Found abundantly in fruits, vegetables, and other foods, potassium is vital for heart health and may aid in controlling blood pressure, particularly in individuals with high sodium intake. 

In addition to its cardiovascular benefits, potassium influences kidney health and the formation of kidney stones. It helps regulate fluid balance in the body, making it important for overall health. However, potassium intake may need to be restricted in the diets of certain patients, especially those with chronic kidney disease, to prevent complications such as hyperkalemia. For patients without diuresis undergoing treatment with ACE inhibitors, potassium intake should not exceed 1 mEq/kg/day. Consequently, potassium levels are closely monitored and managed in dietary recommendations for individuals with kidney issues to ensure their health and well-being.</data>
      <data key="d2">1bfdc8a3b2272265ee6fd132fcf3a8ce,2f1fed346bb25032751634adf513337a,5236affa95aaf73b0f2d08e839e4db54,550574bce077f34348bd11c78dc814a1,60d4458582de1ce500542bb9bfb34a86,66b7c4d336056f832579dc20ffb92992,97a728c18b63007d07b6a4a5fc22d5e4,9da930839072b524410f2345ab1df8b1,da58daf0bc31441182ccfd5807a91127,e40d5e26456fbd223dc887d7d6d0eeb7</data>
      <data key="d3">1650</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">13</data>
      <data key="d6">5207</data>
      <data key="d7">410492423fa24ce2b2442481c3eaaf55</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLUCOSE">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">GLUCOSE is a simple sugar that serves as a primary energy source for the body and is critical for cellular function. It is often measured in blood tests for metabolic assessment and plays a significant role in diabetes management. As a non-essential nutrient, glucose can be obtained through various metabolic pathways, making it vital for energy, especially in critically ill patients. Additionally, glucose is important in the context of PET scans, where it is utilized to assess metabolic activity. In dietary management, glucose may be administered intravenously to patients undergoing surgical procedures related to metabolic disorders. Overall, glucose is a key component of many carbohydrates and is essential for the energy needs of living organisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">108b158c33fead285203903273758748,1bfdc8a3b2272265ee6fd132fcf3a8ce,4351677d6166c9c88ab941dc8dcecb82,46bc44144e19709e2d7f9838c9c54ccf,502bae6c907e900b95f5fe6aa963b0fc,a66b01654a2f495559fd1a24efc0e64e,fb0fe813a0b63d3b7f4a1bccaf15d291,fb8d31ef68f83e6b01f6fc8548f36d0c</data>
      <data key="d3">1717</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">10</data>
      <data key="d6">5208</data>
      <data key="d7">049a9905eb624463a395f6ff1e831de3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PANCREATITIS LITI&#193;SICA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">A medical condition that led to the admission of the patient, which is characterized by inflammation of the pancreas due to gallstones.</data>
      <data key="d2">1bfdc8a3b2272265ee6fd132fcf3a8ce</data>
      <data key="d3">1080</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5209</data>
      <data key="d7">5cbd23104ec14ca68db49967eb874a84</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HORMONAL STUDIES">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Tests conducted to evaluate adrenal function and assess the presence of conditions like Cushing's syndrome or hyperaldosteronism in patients with incidentalomas.</data>
      <data key="d2">1bfdc8a3b2272265ee6fd132fcf3a8ce</data>
      <data key="d3">1080</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">4</data>
      <data key="d6">5210</data>
      <data key="d7">9435e6cb3f204cd88076d6ca87a0af6a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYPERALDOSTERONISM PRIMARIO">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">A condition that may be screened for in patients with adrenal incidentalomas, characterized by excessive production of aldosterone.</data>
      <data key="d2">1bfdc8a3b2272265ee6fd132fcf3a8ce</data>
      <data key="d3">1080</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5211</data>
      <data key="d7">2288eb6a1339442ea2fcedf23218496f</data>
    </node>
