    <node id="TURNER SYNDROME">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Turner Syndrome is a chromosomal disorder that primarily affects females. It is characterized by the absence of all or part of a second sex chromosome. This genetic disorder can lead to a range of physical and developmental issues, impacting various aspects of health and growth in affected individuals.</data>
      <data key="d2">766fec9f74738cf0af53aee77d58dc78,bc241ef500af250c0f0e0253bf5ae0f2</data>
      <data key="d3">1610</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">5363</data>
      <data key="d7">6910a8a4d23942928f691912b23d2aaf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANDROGEN INSENSITIVITY SYNDROME">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome (AIS) is a condition characterized by the body's inability to respond to androgens, which are male hormones. Individuals with this syndrome possess a male (XY) karyotype but develop female physical traits as a result of their resistance to these hormones. Despite having male genetic makeup, the lack of response to androgens leads to the manifestation of female characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d2">766fec9f74738cf0af53aee77d58dc78,bc241ef500af250c0f0e0253bf5ae0f2</data>
      <data key="d3">1609</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">5</data>
      <data key="d6">5364</data>
      <data key="d7">89cd72531db3412c8e3a54b5b8a4a9f9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HORMONE ANTIM&#220;LLERIAN">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Hormone antim&#252;llerian is produced by Sertoli cells in the testes and plays a crucial role in male sexual differentiation by inhibiting the development of female reproductive structures.</data>
      <data key="d2">766fec9f74738cf0af53aee77d58dc78</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5365</data>
      <data key="d7">6d03153ef62f482589378fa87bee4ef2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TESTOSTERONE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Testosterone is a steroid hormone that plays a crucial role in the development of male reproductive tissues and secondary sexual characteristics. It is commonly measured in hormonal studies, with elevated levels often noted in patient analyses. In women, testosterone is studied for its effects on conditions such as anorexia nervosa, particularly concerning bone density, and is also assessed to diagnose hyperandrogenism, especially in the context of polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS).

In medical treatments, testosterone is administered to adult male transsexuals to facilitate virilization and amenorrhea, using various methods including injections and gels. Additionally, it is utilized in the treatment of conditions like Klinefelter syndrome to promote the development of male secondary sexual characteristics. However, testosterone levels may decrease in patients with liver disease, which can lead to metabolic and muscular issues.

For men with hypogonadism, testosterone therapy is a common treatment, but it necessitates careful evaluation of prostate size, prostate-specific antigen (PSA) levels, and hematocrit before initiation to ensure safety and efficacy.</data>
      <data key="d2">203ff7a2671a44d48a1a326f469c5543,5341a1454aa3fed0eab02f9ce7fea5d1,592d424edaa447a54ab4489d82495db6,70bc9625e649f5e478fdef15e27dda58,766fec9f74738cf0af53aee77d58dc78,81491129b1fbfcc2e010109419cde1f0,84ebd4f784ceacfe0744f6bb9cd9a5a3,d4fb31e0ff471ed415f98bc11ca26d1d,dda58e50ce4bb9fa40e3c61e40e1b9c7</data>
      <data key="d3">1307</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">19</data>
      <data key="d6">5366</data>
      <data key="d7">885f91968fb04ce693dfbe27d7da6694</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIHYDROTESTOSTERONE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Dihydrotestosterone (DHT) is a potent androgen that plays a critical role in the development of male characteristics. It is essential for the formation of male external genitalia and the emergence of secondary sexual characteristics. DHT's influence is significant in the overall development of male physical traits, underscoring its importance in male sexual differentiation and maturation.</data>
      <data key="d2">766fec9f74738cf0af53aee77d58dc78,d4fb31e0ff471ed415f98bc11ca26d1d</data>
      <data key="d3">1307</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">5367</data>
      <data key="d7">e0ab6064e9ca4942a31a6544c413f04d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="5&#913;-REDUCTASE TYPE 2">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">5&#945;-reductase type 2 is an enzyme that converts testosterone into dihydrotestosterone, essential for male genital development.</data>
      <data key="d2">766fec9f74738cf0af53aee77d58dc78</data>
      <data key="d3">1307</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5368</data>
      <data key="d7">059a83ce5423412c9b424d43e7227eba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CELLS OF LEYDIG">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Cells of Leydig are located in the testes and are responsible for producing testosterone, which is vital for male sexual differentiation.</data>
      <data key="d2">766fec9f74738cf0af53aee77d58dc78</data>
      <data key="d3">1307</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5369</data>
      <data key="d7">9879b064278340c8b308fc41f455cce7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CELLS OF SERTOLI">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Cells of Sertoli are found in the testes and support the development of sperm cells and produce hormone antim&#252;llerian.</data>
      <data key="d2">766fec9f74738cf0af53aee77d58dc78</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5370</data>
      <data key="d7">75a75e887bb042449f1e87788cb78105</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GENETIC DISORDERS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Genetic disorders encompass a range of conditions caused by abnormalities in genes or chromosomes, affecting physical and developmental traits.</data>
      <data key="d2">766fec9f74738cf0af53aee77d58dc78</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5371</data>
      <data key="d7">f756f9be696f413e95de7b277a1d3f04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANOMALIES OF SEXUAL DIFFERENTIATION">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Anomalies of sexual differentiation refer to a spectrum of congenital conditions that result in discordance between chromosomal, gonadal, and phenotypic sex.</data>
      <data key="d2">766fec9f74738cf0af53aee77d58dc78</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5372</data>
      <data key="d7">3fe2fb8acbcf4b1287dca2a5a9eac357</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARIOTYPE 46,XY">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">A karyotype of 46,XY indicates a typical male chromosomal pattern, but can be associated with various disorders affecting sexual development.</data>
      <data key="d2">766fec9f74738cf0af53aee77d58dc78</data>
      <data key="d3">1609</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5373</data>
      <data key="d7">e9f50f3a6ebb432490f5aad5cd7495e9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="XY INDIVIDUALS">
      <data key="d0" />
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">766fec9f74738cf0af53aee77d58dc78</data>
      <data key="d3">1609</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5374</data>
      <data key="d7">d9193e8d3ce648fca61abbf43d158381</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GONADAL DYSGENESIS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Gonadal Dysgenesis is a condition characterized by the underdevelopment of gonads, which can lead to various disorders of sexual development.</data>
      <data key="d2">bc241ef500af250c0f0e0253bf5ae0f2</data>
      <data key="d3">1609</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5375</data>
      <data key="d7">8582228e07a3411193df6be77e7fcae5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="5&#913;-REDUCTASE DEFICIENCY">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">5&#945;-Reductase Deficiency is a condition that affects male sexual development, where the body cannot convert testosterone into dihydrotestosterone.</data>
      <data key="d2">bc241ef500af250c0f0e0253bf5ae0f2</data>
      <data key="d3">1609</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5376</data>
      <data key="d7">4e9eec86e721432eb96f98e091740522</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PTEN HAMARTOMA SYNDROME">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">PTEN Hamartoma Syndrome is a rare genetic disorder associated with mutations in the PTEN gene, leading to the development of hamartomas and increased cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d2">bc241ef500af250c0f0e0253bf5ae0f2</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">5377</data>
      <data key="d7">341fb217948e4aef95d200cbcb989837</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DSD">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Developmental Sexual Disorder (DSD) refers to a group of conditions characterized by atypical development of physical sex characteristics. This term encompasses a range of congenital conditions known as Differences of Sex Development (DSD), which involve atypical chromosomal, gonadal, or anatomical sex development. These conditions can manifest in various ways, affecting an individual's physical sex characteristics and overall sexual development.</data>
      <data key="d2">0cf04c95a8e6bbdf9201bf3fff9103f6,bc241ef500af250c0f0e0253bf5ae0f2</data>
      <data key="d3">995</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">5378</data>
      <data key="d7">8530470b4d6e4d58abe44904323829ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CANCER RISK">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Cancer risk encompasses the increased likelihood of developing certain types of cancer due to various factors. In individuals with Disorders of Sex Development (DSD), particularly those with XY karyotypes and specific gonadal conditions, the risk of cancer is notably heightened. Additionally, obesity is recognized as a significant factor that elevates cancer risk. Furthermore, numerous studies have explored how dietary factors, including fish consumption and omega-3 fatty acids, can influence cancer risk, indicating that lifestyle choices may also play a critical role in the likelihood of developing cancer.</data>
      <data key="d2">20863ac06779722abc2cbc2dd023777e,97c27548ceb554d425303875efc5f9b4,bc241ef500af250c0f0e0253bf5ae0f2</data>
      <data key="d3">995</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">5379</data>
      <data key="d7">51e0c187ce6040cba4dddc0e35d702c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GONADAL MALIGNANCY">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Gonadal malignancy refers to cancer that originates in the gonads, with increased risk associated with certain genetic conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">bc241ef500af250c0f0e0253bf5ae0f2</data>
      <data key="d3">995</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5380</data>
      <data key="d7">e6a94e1f11d04cd5a6ec271ecb0da13a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYPERPLASIA SUPRARRENAL CONG&#201;NITA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia is a genetic disorder affecting adrenal gland function, leading to hormone imbalances and potential sexual development issues.</data>
      <data key="d2">bc241ef500af250c0f0e0253bf5ae0f2</data>
      <data key="d3">995</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5381</data>
      <data key="d7">5990f98b19aa45b19ca960f417670c39</data>
    </node>
