    </node>
    <node id="INMUNIDAD">
      <data key="d0">GEO</data>
      <data key="d1">INMUNIDAD refers to the body's ability to resist infections and diseases through various biological mechanisms. This concept encompasses the various ways in which the immune system protects the body from harmful pathogens and maintains overall health.</data>
      <data key="d2">0f0303399f29734dede9db544e1dda9e,af95ffa2f3eaddc28a86d59cd2d89415</data>
      <data key="d3">52</data>
      <data key="d4">0</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">5597</data>
      <data key="d7">9b670feb53114a3782f878200ab2f612</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&#193;CIDO &#218;RICO">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">&#193;cido &#250;rico is a waste product formed from the breakdown of purines, primarily produced in the liver and to a lesser extent in the small intestine. It serves as the final product of purine metabolism. While &#225;cido &#250;rico acts as a potent extracellular antioxidant, it can also induce oxidative stress in certain cells, which is associated with an increased risk of cardiovascular diseases. Elevated levels of &#225;cido &#250;rico can lead to hyperuricemia, a condition that may result in various health issues, including gout and the formation of uric acid stones in the kidneys. Therefore, maintaining balanced levels of &#225;cido &#250;rico is crucial for overall health.</data>
      <data key="d2">12c1878563254283012703d4f20ff4a9,1ad59ffc3e39719e331a017fe64cd07d,3ea911f4fe93a5403638aacc9085b483,634b74df7b9403e4c578f3fc84fe3335,869a4bbc356ac84d0b4ed76c5f7a64b6,9ea70909c43b9b7923a0fa217c0e7290,ab93481c5db482b6da2462b4687e4313</data>
      <data key="d3">1388</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">14</data>
      <data key="d6">5598</data>
      <data key="d7">a5201c3db03d40349ba8d0afb7a9fd93</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIPERURICEMIA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Hiperuricemia is a medical condition characterized by elevated levels of uric acid in the blood. This condition is often associated with various metabolic and vascular issues, including obesity, hypertension, and metabolic syndrome. Hiperuricemia can lead to several health complications, such as the formation of uric acid stones, gout, and an increased risk of cardiovascular disease. Additionally, it may contribute to other health problems, including renal disease and arthritis, particularly gouty arthritis. Overall, hiperuricemia represents a significant health concern due to its association with multiple serious conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">12c1878563254283012703d4f20ff4a9,1ad59ffc3e39719e331a017fe64cd07d,3ea911f4fe93a5403638aacc9085b483,9ea70909c43b9b7923a0fa217c0e7290,a81765e6d24ccef0c86801f71673de98,ab93481c5db482b6da2462b4687e4313,b703f173982cf68d36653cc0d9cc1fcb</data>
      <data key="d3">1386</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">31</data>
      <data key="d6">5599</data>
      <data key="d7">c8b360e632a847d69c936959a76a2e1d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ARTRITIS GOTOSA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">La artritis gotosa es una enfermedad inflamatoria que se produce por la acumulaci&#243;n de cristales de urato en las articulaciones, a menudo asociada con niveles elevados de &#225;cido &#250;rico.</data>
      <data key="d2">3ea911f4fe93a5403638aacc9085b483</data>
      <data key="d3">1386</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
