      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5797</data>
      <data key="d7">2d5c34e0089640b088f745228b4cf9ba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RET PROTO-ONCOGENE">
      <data key="d0">GENE</data>
      <data key="d1">The RET proto-oncogene is crucial for the development of MEN2 syndromes, with different mutations associated with varying risks of tumor development.</data>
      <data key="d2">2415ccd829f700cbe1975f4bca87245f</data>
      <data key="d3">1312</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5798</data>
      <data key="d7">83888c1df5e341c4b44a8a3bf20b2198</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WELLS ET AL.">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Wells and colleagues revised the American Thyroid Association guidelines for the management of medullary thyroid carcinoma, contributing to the understanding of thyroid cancer management.</data>
      <data key="d2">2415ccd829f700cbe1975f4bca87245f</data>
      <data key="d3">1301</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5799</data>
      <data key="d7">12a15bbf491f4c6890aeb0e4562cfec4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VALD&#201;S GALLEGO">
      <data key="d0" />
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">2415ccd829f700cbe1975f4bca87245f</data>
      <data key="d3">1479</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5800</data>
      <data key="d7">e99a0d81a08d4f7ea68131403903ba39</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HYPERPARATHYROIDISM PRIMARIO">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Primary hyperparathyroidism is a condition often associated with MEN1 and MEN2 syndromes, characterized by excessive secretion of parathyroid hormone.</data>
      <data key="d2">2415ccd829f700cbe1975f4bca87245f</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">5801</data>
      <data key="d7">1d0369e7d9dc46de8502f848eb98805d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROADENOMA HIPOFISARIO">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Microadenoma hipofisario is a small tumor located on the pituitary gland. It is noteworthy that the patient's mother had a microadenoma hipofisario, which was successfully resolved with treatment. This type of tumor can produce hormones, including prolactin, and is frequently examined in relation to multiple endocrine neoplasia (MEN) syndromes.</data>
      <data key="d2">2415ccd829f700cbe1975f4bca87245f,e279d3a0da085b47528c08f486afe0a5</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">5802</data>
      <data key="d7">9be55c2121ae4287a9237fdf470e5fde</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SINDROME MEN1">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">MEN1 syndrome is characterized by the triad of primary hyperparathyroidism, pituitary tumors, and pancreatic endocrine tumors.</data>
      <data key="d2">2415ccd829f700cbe1975f4bca87245f</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">5803</data>
      <data key="d7">f4a68b8970944e09981b76e9887956ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SINDROME MEN3">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">MEN3 is another name for MEN2B syndrome, which is characterized by aggressive medullary thyroid carcinoma and other specific features.</data>
      <data key="d2">2415ccd829f700cbe1975f4bca87245f</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">5804</data>
      <data key="d7">e081a6e2eb374aacafa4a15267b7290c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FENOTIPO MARFANOIDE">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Marfanoid phenotype refers to a set of physical characteristics that may be associated with MEN2B syndrome, including skeletal deformities and laxity of joints.</data>
      <data key="d2">2415ccd829f700cbe1975f4bca87245f</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">5805</data>
      <data key="d7">0150c52558de4e15a75c8be63f354d84</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NEUROMAS MUCOSOS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Mucosal neuromas are a characteristic feature of MEN2B syndrome, often found on the lips and tongue.</data>
      <data key="d2">2415ccd829f700cbe1975f4bca87245f</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">5806</data>
      <data key="d7">e99f61fa38ad40b28311a17a96ec6295</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GANGLIONEUROMATOSIS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Ganglioneuromatosis is a condition associated with MEN2B syndrome, characterized by the presence of ganglioneuromas in various tissues.)&lt;|COMPLETE|&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">2415ccd829f700cbe1975f4bca87245f</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">5807</data>
      <data key="d7">9055a97343a345c5971c7958d99368c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEN4">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">MEN4 is a newly identified clinical entity similar to MEN1, characterized by mutations in the CDKN1B gene, affecting a small percentage of patients.</data>
      <data key="d2">02b8a693303f9f8603c740fefc53d526</data>
      <data key="d3">1310</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5808</data>
      <data key="d7">167883c6ff0f460d95b687f6b5e5f980</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AIRE">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">AIRE is a gene linked to autoimmune polyglandular syndrome type 1, which involves multiple autoimmune disorders.</data>
      <data key="d2">02b8a693303f9f8603c740fefc53d526</data>
      <data key="d3">1310</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5809</data>
      <data key="d7">1db8e39af05f4d8d9f654501d949641a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ARNOLD A.">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Arnold A. is an author who has contributed to the understanding of multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 and its genetics.</data>
      <data key="d2">02b8a693303f9f8603c740fefc53d526</data>
      <data key="d3">1310</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5810</data>
      <data key="d7">1b6fac1973224d359dfcdabb4196dc7d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VALD&#201;S GALLEGO N.">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Vald&#233;s Gallego N. is an author known for his contributions to the field of endocrinology and nutrition. He has authored a manual that focuses on endocrine neoplasia, with a specific emphasis on multiple endocrine neoplasia. His work in the "Manual de Endocrinolog&#237;a y Nutrici&#243;n" highlights his expertise and dedication to advancing knowledge in these important areas of health.</data>
      <data key="d2">02b8a693303f9f8603c740fefc53d526,7e4e3e944e1b58b8d92e93ee4a9f33cc</data>
      <data key="d3">1457</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">5811</data>
      <data key="d7">78c409d0233b44649c89b6e5e5555bfa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LITIASIS RENAL">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">LITIASIS RENAL, also known as kidney stones or renal lithiasis, is a medical condition characterized by the formation of solid mineral and salt deposits in the kidneys. This condition affects approximately 5-15% of the population and can lead to significant morbidity and healthcare costs. The formation of kidney stones can be influenced by various factors, including the concentration of solutes in the urine, dietary habits, hydration levels, and metabolic disorders. High calcium levels in the urine, hyperuricemia, and metabolic syndrome are also associated with the development of this condition. Litiasis renal can cause considerable pain and complications in health, making it a significant urological pathology that requires attention and management.</data>
      <data key="d2">02b8a693303f9f8603c740fefc53d526,06adf563f18feaa5629f0e368a72d227,12c1878563254283012703d4f20ff4a9,1ad59ffc3e39719e331a017fe64cd07d,451bbcaee8b1a025277b84488ea7db61,57d928013f61475d356dcec181cc0c6c,634b74df7b9403e4c578f3fc84fe3335,9ea70909c43b9b7923a0fa217c0e7290,b703f173982cf68d36653cc0d9cc1fcb</data>
      <data key="d3">1381</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">48</data>
      <data key="d6">5812</data>
      <data key="d7">0b0bf8fd5ef04ae5949e6e05e79bb0f8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIPERPARATIROIDISMO">
      <data key="d0" />
      <data key="d1">Hiperparatiroidismo es una alteraci&#243;n hormonal que puede afectar a pacientes con insuficiencia renal, influyendo en su nutrici&#243;n. Esta condici&#243;n, tambi&#233;n conocida como hyperparathyroidism, es un trastorno que puede presentarse en individuos con enfermedad renal cr&#243;nica, lo que requiere intervenciones nutricionales espec&#237;ficas para manejar sus efectos.</data>
      <data key="d2">02b8a693303f9f8603c740fefc53d526,a87e8e113488b153c5b4b2d2fca712c0,bf76380d2417653bab17a6a9b91d85bc</data>
      <data key="d3">1341</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">5813</data>
      <data key="d7">805f538442e94e71a5e94a59fbb9edba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="D">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">D is a hypothetical patient who presents with symptoms and a medical history suggestive of Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia type 1 (MEN1). In addition to being a clinical case study, D also serves as a response option in the context of the questions presented in the modules. This dual role highlights D's significance in both medical education and the understanding of MEN1.</data>
      <data key="d2">545db57c3c81825310618d52b413a5a5,fbba0b43850ff5e50b710069959c2416</data>
      <data key="d3">1315</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">17</data>
      <data key="d6">5814</data>
      <data key="d7">2354cc54ddc641b8a0863a88104c9e02</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TNE">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">TNE refers to neuroendocrine tumors, which can be non-functioning and are often associated with MEN1.</data>
      <data key="d2">fbba0b43850ff5e50b710069959c2416</data>
      <data key="d3">1315</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5815</data>
      <data key="d7">128720a0dc1644b39c90440ddad848d8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GASTRINOMAS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Gastrinomas are a type of neuroendocrine tumor characterized by the secretion of gastrin, a hormone that stimulates gastric acid production. These tumors are often located in the duodenum and are commonly associated with Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, a condition marked by excessive gastric acid secretion leading to recurrent peptic ulcers. Additionally, gastrinomas can be linked to Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia type 1 (MEN1), a genetic disorder that affects the endocrine glands.</data>
      <data key="d2">9851218c3244d6f4279055a04d219044,fbba0b43850ff5e50b710069959c2416</data>
      <data key="d3">1314</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">4</data>
      <data key="d6">5816</data>
      <data key="d7">a4636d9975fb411790f977ce69f67270</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NEUROENDOCRINE TUMOR">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Neuroendocrine tumors (NETs) are a diverse group of tumors that originate from neuroendocrine cells, which are specialized cells that produce hormones. One specific type of neuroendocrine tumor is found in the pancreas, where it affects the hormone-producing cells, leading to significant health complications. These tumors can be associated with various syndromes, including Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia type 1 (MEN1), highlighting their complex nature and potential for systemic effects.</data>
      <data key="d2">6edff59a3b151e946e7664f13bcb0931,fbba0b43850ff5e50b710069959c2416</data>
      <data key="d3">1257</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">5817</data>
      <data key="d7">31b785c1c8364279a077078eb618baee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANALYTICS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Analytics refers to the medical tests and evaluations performed to assess the patient's condition, including hormone levels.</data>
      <data key="d2">fbba0b43850ff5e50b710069959c2416</data>
      <data key="d3">1315</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5818</data>
      <data key="d7">708e49bc072044c3a665c12b11bee3e6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MACROADENOMA HIPOFISARIO">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Macroadenoma hipofisario refers to a large pituitary adenoma that produces prolactin, commonly seen in patients with MEN1.</data>
      <data key="d2">fbba0b43850ff5e50b710069959c2416</data>
      <data key="d3">1315</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5819</data>
      <data key="d7">10462cae77014c5ab9132f84095d3a77</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROTOONCOGEN RET">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Protooncogen RET is a gene that, when mutated, can lead to MEN2, and its study is important in the context of MEN syndromes.</data>
      <data key="d2">fbba0b43850ff5e50b710069959c2416</data>
      <data key="d3">1310</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5820</data>
      <data key="d7">636b1d17636943c5a9a275869a0008bc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENETS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">The European Neuroendocrine Tumor Society (ENETS) is an organization that provides guidelines and recommendations for the management of neuroendocrine tumors.</data>
      <data key="d2">7e4e3e944e1b58b8d92e93ee4a9f33cc</data>
      <data key="d3">1310</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5821</data>
      <data key="d7">665e68a37d434de99ae2973674d2d252</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CARCINOMA DE COLON">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Colon carcinoma, also known as colon cancer, is a type of cancer that affects the colon and can lead to gastrointestinal bleeding, which may contribute to iron deficiency anemia. It is important to note that patients with Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia type 1 (MEN1) may develop colon carcinoma; however, there is currently no established link between the MEN1 gene and the tumorigenesis of colon carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d2">6595cec039fe5a2c290eea9ab7e3b2fc,7e4e3e944e1b58b8d92e93ee4a9f33cc</data>
      <data key="d3">1808</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">5822</data>
      <data key="d7">e11bf2d3a584437dbbef700d02daecfc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GU&#205;A CL&#205;NICA DE 2012">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">The "GU&#205;A CL&#205;NICA DE 2012" encompasses comprehensive recommendations for the treatment and management of various medical conditions. Specifically, it provides guidelines for the treatment of patients with Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and pancreatic tumors. Additionally, the guidelines address the management of patients with Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia type 1 (MEN1), detailing when surgical interventions should be considered. Overall, the 2012 Clinical Guidelines serve as a crucial resource for healthcare professionals in the effective treatment of these complex conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">11fcae49d6b540a5ad5cdfe74ed269a9,7e4e3e944e1b58b8d92e93ee4a9f33cc</data>
      <data key="d3">1310</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5823</data>
      <data key="d7">80482308d60c4f9cb922aab977ff3d27</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ZOLLINGER-ELLISON SYNDROME">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Zollinger-Ellison syndrome is a medical condition characterized by the presence of gastrin-secreting tumors, which result in excessive production of gastric acid. This overproduction can lead to various symptoms, including diarrhea and the development of peptic ulcers. The management of Zollinger-Ellison syndrome often involves surgical intervention, as highlighted in clinical guidelines, emphasizing the importance of addressing the underlying tumors to alleviate the associated symptoms and complications.</data>
      <data key="d2">11fcae49d6b540a5ad5cdfe74ed269a9,9851218c3244d6f4279055a04d219044</data>
      <data key="d3">1314</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">4</data>
      <data key="d6">5824</data>
      <data key="d7">7246fc489b084daa80e953ab0474426a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DREIJERINK KM">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Dreijerink KM is an author of a referenced study on breast-cancer predisposition in multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1.</data>
      <data key="d2">9851218c3244d6f4279055a04d219044</data>
      <data key="d3">1310</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5825</data>
      <data key="d7">a09b684b118a4929aad188587866ac7b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VALD&#201;S GALLEGO N">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Vald&#233;s Gallego N is an author of a referenced manual on endocrinology and nutrition.</data>
      <data key="d2">9851218c3244d6f4279055a04d219044</data>
      <data key="d3">1457</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5826</data>
      <data key="d7">6e1f9ee33838401a88f04419f706e3d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GASTRINA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Gastrina is a hormone produced by G cells in the stomach, primarily responsible for stimulating the secretion of gastric acid. It plays a crucial role in the digestive process and is often found to be elevated in patients with gastrinomas, which are tumors that secrete excessive amounts of this hormone. Additionally, gastrin may influence intestinal motility, further contributing to its importance in gastrointestinal function.</data>
      <data key="d2">0cdbb308e3359fd3ddbb1d828d12dc04,9851218c3244d6f4279055a04d219044</data>
      <data key="d3">1314</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">5827</data>
      <data key="d7">6df95552a0d541f3acce9af220198a22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TEST DE SECRETINA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">The secretin test is a functional test used to diagnose gastrinomas by measuring the level of gastrin in response to secretin administration.</data>
      <data key="d2">9851218c3244d6f4279055a04d219044</data>
      <data key="d3">1314</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5828</data>
      <data key="d7">584bc793f8a94bb68219746295a52752</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HORMONAS GASTROINTESTINALES">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">HORMONAS GASTROINTESTINALES, or gastrointestinal hormones, are a group of hormones that play a crucial role in regulating various functions within the digestive system. One notable hormone in this category is gastrin, which is particularly significant in the context of gastrinomas, a type of tumor that secretes excessive amounts of gastrin. These hormones are integral to the digestive process, and their levels and functions were evaluated in patients to identify any abnormalities that may affect digestion.</data>
      <data key="d2">9851218c3244d6f4279055a04d219044,acd36e6dbcd98757c4cf33ff090764ee</data>
      <data key="d3">1080</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5829</data>
      <data key="d7">03ae29f6af344fbe8104eec55ba6071d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INHIBIDORES DE LA BOMBA DE PROTONES">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Los inhibidores de la bomba de protones son medicamentos que reducen la producci&#243;n de &#225;cido en el est&#243;mago y se utilizan en el tratamiento de diversas condiciones, como el s&#237;ndrome de Zollinger-Ellison. Estos medicamentos tambi&#233;n se han probado en pacientes para tratar la regurgitaci&#243;n. Sin embargo, es importante tener en cuenta que su uso puede estar relacionado con la producci&#243;n de diarrea.</data>
      <data key="d2">44f263c31f91c1bc594cbb28bd397c89,9851218c3244d6f4279055a04d219044,ce4c057e0a625bc9e64301a0cca1e206</data>
      <data key="d3">1314</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">5830</data>
      <data key="d7">a3c4fa62a53245c3b600b0656d718a80</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GASTRINOMA PANCRE&#193;TICO">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Pancreatic gastrinomas are gastrin-secreting tumors that can occur in the pancreas, contributing to the symptoms of Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.</data>
      <data key="d2">9851218c3244d6f4279055a04d219044</data>
      <data key="d3">1314</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5831</data>
      <data key="d7">24f86d00b31d4b8487593befdd04c627</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MIGUEL A. RUBIO HERRERA">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Miguel A. Rubio Herrera is a licensed physician and a specialist in Endocrinology and Nutrition. He currently holds the position of Head of the Clinical Nutrition and Dietetics Section at Hospital Cl&#237;nico San Carlos in Madrid. As a medical professional, he focuses on the fields of endocrinology and nutrition, contributing to the healthcare services at the San Carlos Clinical University Hospital in Madrid.</data>
      <data key="d2">11fcae49d6b540a5ad5cdfe74ed269a9,89ca65c4bdc30027c9c9ed1e1b442939</data>
      <data key="d3">1463</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">10</data>
      <data key="d6">5832</data>
      <data key="d7">7178cd7e65f04c17b74d847da318f2f7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UNIVERSIDAD COMPLUTENSE DE MADRID">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Universidad Complutense de Madrid is the university where Miguel A. Rubio Herrera completed his doctorate in 1989 and is currently an associate professor.</data>
      <data key="d2">11fcae49d6b540a5ad5cdfe74ed269a9</data>
      <data key="d3">1463</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5833</data>
      <data key="d7">b4fcf129ac044dc0b982d8f92fe8920b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SEEDO">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">SEEDO, or Sociedad Espa&#241;ola para el Estudio de la Obesidad, is a prominent organization dedicated to addressing obesity in Spain. It plays a crucial role in establishing therapeutic intervention criteria for obesity and is actively involved in developing consensus guidelines regarding obesity treatment. SEEDO supports the implementation of hypocaloric diets, particularly those based on the Mediterranean diet, recognizing their health benefits in the management of obesity. Through its initiatives, SEEDO aims to promote effective strategies for obesity prevention and treatment, contributing to the overall health of the population.</data>
      <data key="d2">11fcae49d6b540a5ad5cdfe74ed269a9,7855ffa74481ba85f44e9700279250e7,89b0bde0358432506a0e03d4691dd2ab</data>
      <data key="d3">1646</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">4</data>
      <data key="d6">5834</data>
      <data key="d7">8418aeed97884c68b99c7f333aab95c8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NAOS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">NAOS is a strategy developed for addressing obesity in Spain, in which Miguel A. Rubio Herrera has participated.</data>
      <data key="d2">11fcae49d6b540a5ad5cdfe74ed269a9</data>
      <data key="d3">1463</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5835</data>
      <data key="d7">2518b012327f4be3b3917c4170aa3995</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DRECE">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">DRECE is a study focused on the epidemiology of cardiovascular risk in Spain, in which Miguel A. Rubio Herrera has been involved.</data>
      <data key="d2">11fcae49d6b540a5ad5cdfe74ed269a9</data>
      <data key="d3">1463</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">5836</data>
      <data key="d7">33e5347361fd4f7f9e0df639bdb89016</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MANUAL DE OBESIDAD M&#211;RBIDA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Manual de Obesidad m&#243;rbida is a book published by Miguel A. Rubio Herrera, focusing on morbid obesity.</data>
      <data key="d2">11fcae49d6b540a5ad5cdfe74ed269a9</data>
      <data key="d3">1463</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
