    <node id="C&#193;NCER COLORRECTAL">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">C&#225;ncer colorrectal, or colorectal cancer, is a type of cancer that affects the colon and rectum. Its incidence has been linked to various dietary factors and lifestyle choices. Research indicates that the consumption of legumes may reduce the risk of developing adenomas and carcinomas associated with this cancer. Additionally, a higher intake of dietary fiber and whole grains has been shown to lower the risk of colorectal cancer, suggesting that fiber plays a protective role through various proposed mechanisms.

Obesity is identified as a contributing risk factor for colorectal cancer, highlighting the importance of maintaining a healthy weight. Furthermore, the consumption of omega-3 fatty acids has also been associated with a reduced risk of this disease. Conversely, a high intake of red and processed meats has been linked to an increased risk of colorectal adenomas, emphasizing the negative impact of certain dietary choices.

The relationship between colorectal cancer and the consumption of fruits and vegetables is noted to be inversely related, although the evidence supporting this connection is considered modest. Overall, dietary habits, including the intake of fiber, legumes, and omega-3 fatty acids, alongside lifestyle factors such as obesity, play significant roles in the risk of developing c&#225;ncer colorrectal.</data>
      <data key="d2">11c3cb37b1a2c111ceb227aefa8d9e1a,240ded3c69bd7ae82bed4089517ffb6b,3dd309c324107af87b7988bd0284892e,8f4f0fffa52482a1b5a520849d10c692,a6ba6423064bccccefee095de7435164,b110392fc91bc3d7875124ec3fa07c46,d667e6abc204bbfa6aba210705db6d56,fa42f07df0762860c72a58c869627aec</data>
