    <node id="PANCREATICODUODENECTOMY">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Pancreaticoduodenectomy, commonly referred to as the Whipple procedure, is a complex surgical operation that involves the removal of the head of the pancreas, part of the small intestine (duodenum), and other nearby structures. This procedure is primarily performed on patients diagnosed with pancreatic cancer. Due to the extensive nature of the surgery, patients often require specialized nutritional support during their recovery.</data>
      <data key="d2">6216a2d06144ebde48b78330f488c603,9f0dc667db1447b9e51420cc6ba091c9,e36c9b09870e14d19aa6eb7c82514499</data>
      <data key="d3">1239</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">5</data>
      <data key="d6">6740</data>
      <data key="d7">365afbdf6a524d28817d7217620330bd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NUTRITION SUPPORT">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Nutrition support encompasses a range of methods designed to provide nutritional intake to patients who are unable to eat normally due to various medical conditions. This includes enteral feeding, which delivers nutrients directly to the gastrointestinal tract, and parenteral feeding, which provides nutrients intravenously. It is particularly crucial for managing conditions such as acute pancreatitis, where patients may struggle to absorb food effectively. 

Nutrition support is essential in critical care settings and is especially important for hospitalized pediatric patients, including those with obesity or other health issues. The primary goal of nutrition support is to ensure that patients receive adequate nutrients to meet their needs when they cannot do so through regular eating. Overall, nutrition support plays a vital role in the recovery and management of patients with significant health challenges.</data>
      <data key="d2">04b13f0ccbff81f5f7e92e7bd239cd92,3d5af63a33abe5094b25a96394f69969,52d93d6adba464a2397ddec01d152840,6935432f69970131a6485f38e3f06003,c84372864fd2ee7b7ed425b2d597efdb,e36c9b09870e14d19aa6eb7c82514499</data>
      <data key="d3">1239</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">6741</data>
      <data key="d7">298bad939f404126b0153e277408e07c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TAPIA MJ">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Tapia MJ is a researcher and author known for their contributions to the field of diabetes management, particularly in relation to parenteral nutrition. They have co-authored studies that examine the prevalence of diabetes and its management within the context of parenteral nutrition. Additionally, Tapia MJ played a significant role in a study focused on parenteral nutrition-associated hyperglycemia, which was published in the journal Diabetes Care. Their work highlights the critical intersection of diabetes care and nutritional support in clinical settings.</data>
      <data key="d2">152aeb8f10c2f4548114403c05af6b55,e36c9b09870e14d19aa6eb7c82514499</data>
