    <node id="MADRE">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">MADRE refers to the concept of a mother, who plays a crucial role in the development of a newborn's microbiome through close contact during birth and early feeding. This relationship is essential for establishing a healthy microbial environment for the infant. Additionally, in a specific case, the mother of a patient has a history of thyroid crises and is a smoker, which may influence the patient's overall health. This highlights the multifaceted impact a mother can have, not only in nurturing and supporting the newborn's development but also in potentially affecting the health outcomes of older children or patients through her own health behaviors and medical history.</data>
      <data key="d2">410fab643c125e0f8944ebae5dba5ec0,b36b8846f109ee75806c6ea03015e5d5</data>
      <data key="d3">661</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">6792</data>
      <data key="d7">b1a6d18b4b034f27bbb3f0b72a3d8727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ABUELA">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">The grandmother of the patient who has a diagnosis of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus (DM2), indicating a family history of diabetes.</data>
      <data key="d2">410fab643c125e0f8944ebae5dba5ec0</data>
      <data key="d3">1586</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">6793</data>
      <data key="d7">0cbeba4474b243ada7f29fcbb07cdb5b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TCA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">TCA, or Trastornos de la Conducta Alimentaria, refers to a group of psychiatric disorders that encompass conditions such as anorexia nerviosa and other eating disorders characterized by abnormal eating behaviors and attitudes towards food and weight control. Patients struggling with TCA often exhibit restrictive eating and purging behaviors, which can significantly impact their nutritional intake and overall health. 

These eating disorders are particularly prevalent among individuals with diabetes mellitus type 1 (DM1), especially young women, and can exacerbate the complications associated with diabetes. The presence of TCA may necessitate dietary treatment and nutritional education to address the specific needs of affected patients. In some cases, the severity of the disorder may influence the need for involuntary treatment to ensure the patient's safety and well-being.</data>
      <data key="d2">04221ccceb5e95933ff349645c328457,3f685a05a9682db6dcde958d1918cc77,410fab643c125e0f8944ebae5dba5ec0,48f1fe343d54cd0f353be34b736a352c,770e8796aac9c96d967ef99d1f307e06,eaa675a346becd53a4fcfacbb99def88,ecf27310ce7cbfc335117dafa5d4eefb,f5660be03d70d5dd506c197283dd79ae,fb23af50b771da28e73b9545fa4f09b0</data>
      <data key="d3">1587</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">16</data>
      <data key="d6">6794</data>
      <data key="d7">c3c4c3e255414f7c9a186b25d6757a17</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRISIS TIROT&#211;XICAS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Thyroid crises experienced by the patient's mother, which may have health implications for the patient.</data>
