    </node>
    <node id="MAL CONTROL GLUC&#201;MICO">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Mal control gluc&#233;mico refers to the poor management of blood glucose levels in patients, which can lead to complications in diabetes.</data>
      <data key="d2">dc7539b4d637d6dd09963c2564f4f084</data>
      <data key="d3">1109</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">7014</data>
      <data key="d7">b08aea9e24bd4d3bb656d99aae1497a5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VITAMINAS LIPOSOLUBLES">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">VITAMINAS LIPOSOLUBLES, or fat-soluble vitamins, are essential nutrients that play a crucial role in various bodily functions. These vitamins, which include vitamins A, D, E, and K, are soluble in fats and oils, allowing them to be absorbed in the dietary fat consumed. In patients with cystic fibrosis (Fibrosis Qu&#237;stica), monitoring the intake of fat-soluble vitamins is particularly important due to malabsorption issues commonly associated with the condition. Additionally, the absorption of these vitamins may be further impacted by weight loss medications. Ensuring adequate levels of vitamins A, D, and E is vital for cystic fibrosis patients to prevent deficiencies and support their overall health.</data>
      <data key="d2">3f295222427803a7fc92b83386e5ec56,488970234520652b063db88e8346031c,71dd228326087dc7aaa0d310d49b6f42,f3d21fef234a5c6fed6ef3fb2c8da1f4</data>
      <data key="d3">1080</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">7015</data>
      <data key="d7">5de21cec191d4fbaabcfe97cd705ba32</data>
    </node>
    <node id="F&#211;RMULA POLIM&#201;RICA HIPERCAL&#211;RICA HIPERPROTEICA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">A type of enteral nutrition formula that is hypercaloric and hyperproteic, specifically designed for diabetic patients.</data>
      <data key="d2">3f295222427803a7fc92b83386e5ec56</data>
      <data key="d3">1080</data>
