      <data key="d6">7142</data>
      <data key="d7">80468c6a3b74493c9d727cebf11bafdc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALBUMIN">
      <data key="d0" />
      <data key="d1">Albumin is a vital protein found in the blood, playing a significant role as a prognostic indicator in various medical contexts, particularly in cancer patients. It is synthesized in the liver, and its levels are closely associated with liver function and nutritional status, especially in patients with cirrhosis. Albumin levels are routinely measured to assess nutritional status and liver function in patients, and they serve as a marker in nutritional assessments to evaluate overall health. In patients with conditions such as ELA (likely referring to a specific medical condition), monitoring albumin levels is crucial, as higher levels are linked to better survival rates. Additionally, albumin levels can indicate nutritional status and are influenced by refeeding in malnourished individuals. Overall, albumin is recognized as a key serum protein that can predict morbidity and mortality, particularly in relation to a patient's nutritional condition.</data>
      <data key="d2">214cb2b8ddec52ff69849d87feb8e141,3b361c0a9b5d08df1a67476a3ae3b968,58ae94a34f90df368b032f1618c7bcec,6edff59a3b151e946e7664f13bcb0931,add3641ae46ac521317bb4c12922895a,cea5f90ebf49c4157cab16ee26b9d97c,f5660be03d70d5dd506c197283dd79ae,f7b74dc42fea9d33e4675a41e15975ab</data>
