      <data key="d7">0afabfd8351b49ac8500f69efb414e68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ADA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">The American Diabetes Association (ADA) is a prominent organization dedicated to diabetes research, education, and advocacy. It plays a crucial role in providing comprehensive information and establishing criteria related to diabetes, including diagnostic blood glucose ranges. The ADA focuses on developing evidence-based recommendations for diabetes care and management, which encompass guidelines for prevention and dietary advice tailored for individuals living with diabetes. Through its efforts, the ADA aims to enhance the understanding and management of diabetes, ultimately improving the quality of life for those affected by the condition.</data>
      <data key="d2">48e690b80c196e397d9ab9527b38c3d4,67d484d5c0c84fff32782bd773c0fd7a,b1698ec972215d493ab6114914dde2a4,c98ed25e58194d76463e3553fb3f3c15,f558c372d16630f14b614e232fc151de</data>
      <data key="d3">871</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">7290</data>
      <data key="d7">40773b48213c46eca17a6a71ceae4793</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AHA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">The AHA, or American Heart Association, is an organization committed to combating heart disease and stroke through extensive research and educational initiatives. It has played a significant role in public health by providing dietary guidelines aimed at promoting heart health. These guidelines were initially issued from 2000 to 2006 and received an update in 2013, reflecting the AHA's ongoing dedication to improving cardiovascular health through informed dietary practices.</data>
      <data key="d2">67d484d5c0c84fff32782bd773c0fd7a,c2a8dd9d28d1992a73de2834131b69c9</data>
      <data key="d3">1618</data>
