      <data key="d7">808ccc3cb6dd4a48b51e7413e6045631</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AGCML N-3">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">AGCML n-3 refers to high supplements of omega-3 fatty acids that may increase lipid peroxidation and reduce cytokine production.</data>
      <data key="d2">dea64d5eb807430bc52e8bed6e30c393</data>
      <data key="d3">607</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">7823</data>
      <data key="d7">0a9092d9d84842baa64aa6780eae12e5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CITOQUINAS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Citoquinas, also known as cytokines, are crucial signaling molecules that mediate and regulate various biological processes, including immunity, inflammation, and hematopoiesis. They play a significant role in the immune response, acting as inflammatory mediators that can be influenced by dietary intake. In patients with renal issues, dietary adjustments can help reduce the levels of cytokines, highlighting their relevance in managing inflammation and immune responses.

Citoquinas are released by cells and are involved in metabolic inflammation, making them important in various health conditions, including chronic pancreatitis. During sepsis, inflammatory cytokines can be released, potentially affecting liver function and leading to hepatobiliary complications. Additionally, elevated levels of citoquinas have been observed in patients with chronic liver disease, where they are associated with anorexia and malnutrition. These proteins can also interfere with protein synthesis, which may impact the effectiveness of artificial nutrition in advanced patients.

Overall, citoquinas are essential proteins in cell signaling, particularly in the context of immune responses and inflammation, and their regulation is vital for maintaining health and managing various medical conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">036e8182deab774d39f06cd811738c9b,0a78cb1d502baf0116e4326e7fbcae41,803363886f385b089938bf122246dfa2,a6ccbda174f6599eeeb2e4cdb00db5f2,b4b789f8b1484fea88d0b5c58dfde854,c947e3441a061fe1807f7aaf29becdce,d2f16314e7098030a6780411d2e03023,dea64d5eb807430bc52e8bed6e30c393,e4002acd67016cc1f7428e0a3ab42977,ec69668515f0a014f1a602bcdc46c47f</data>
      <data key="d3">303</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">7824</data>
      <data key="d7">4f61d867e87d4fc4b5a50f3c13f04052</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INGESTA DE TOCOFEROL">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Tocopherol intake is related to the dietary consumption of vitamin E, which can influence lipid peroxidation.</data>
