    <node id="YOGUR">
      <data key="d0">FOOD</data>
      <data key="d1">Yogur is a dairy product that is recognized for its numerous health benefits and dietary versatility. It is a food item that is often included in a moderate low-sodium diet and serves as a dessert option in various meal plans. Yogur undergoes fermentation, a process that involves bacteria that help to partially hydrolyze lactose, making it more tolerable for individuals who are lactose intolerant. This fermentation process not only improves lactose digestion but also enhances the overall nutritional profile of the yogurt.

Yogur contains 2.2 mg of iron per 100 grams and has a glycemic index of 51, indicating its moderate impact on blood sugar levels. It is also a good source of calcium, which is essential for bone health, and is recommended for daily intake. Additionally, yogur is beneficial for gut health, particularly during episodes of diarrhea, as it contains probiotics that support digestive function.

Furthermore, yogur is a soft food option suitable for patients with dysphagia, although caution is advised when mixing it with fruits due to potential risks. Overall, yogur is a nutritious and versatile food that can be enjoyed by a wide range of individuals, contributing positively to their dietary needs.</data>
      <data key="d2">1d959525e9a5fe22094271d67d0b9108,5116416429465894c102d9fb93d6a8fc,527c3c962d22018377b32c52e984d4e1,5700f71dc6a101296f5bfec99b1e796f,6ff2742793c37a38db1c11c14d2a6629,94afd4bf40b661992e66cf7963ef236c,ae2bf939fd143e4d8d998722ad86ba66,bd22bf95ac5fc6dc95539efb99c5b11a,e318ae466c5fed49e6aeb9c9a23d2d38,e742c891694171ad455b038f416b7728</data>
      <data key="d3">844</data>
