    <node id="PROYECTO DEL MICROBIOMA HUMANO">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">El Proyecto del Microbioma Humano es una iniciativa de investigaci&#243;n a gran escala que estudia la diversidad y funcionalidad de la microbiota intestinal humana.</data>
      <data key="d2">90ec2b7e4fe7ed5c84bafaaba8834021</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">8976</data>
      <data key="d7">9b5d1b0bc2cb4da8830cb2f596aa1a3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="META HIT">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">MetaHIT es un proyecto de investigaci&#243;n que se centra en la caracterizaci&#243;n de la microbiota intestinal y su relaci&#243;n con la salud humana.</data>
      <data key="d2">90ec2b7e4fe7ed5c84bafaaba8834021</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">8977</data>
      <data key="d7">84633d8aae5946c7ad25c009ef8adf1b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LACTOBACILLUS">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Lactobacillus is a genus of bacteria that resides in the gastrointestinal tract and is widely recognized for its numerous health benefits, particularly in relation to digestive health. This genus is commonly utilized as probiotics, which are live microorganisms that confer health advantages when consumed in adequate amounts. Lactobacillus is known for its ability to ferment lactose and produce lactic acid, contributing to gut health and the maintenance of a positive metabolic profile.

Research has indicated that Lactobacillus may have beneficial effects on obesity and metabolic health, making it a subject of interest in studies related to these areas. Additionally, Lactobacillus can ferment undigested carbohydrates into D-lactic acid, which may lead to complications in certain patients, such as those with short bowel syndrome (SIC). The growth and activity of Lactobacillus can be enhanced by prebiotic fibers, further supporting its role in promoting gut health.

Overall, Lactobacillus is a significant type of beneficial bacteria within the phylum Firmicutes, playing a crucial role in fermentation processes and contributing positively to digestive and metabolic health.</data>
      <data key="d2">05360f0d7332f748b436b98d64e4eeb7,113f5fda648e7322f4771e2ade72481f,2689c33de87eab465f004fff5f0530b8,5a1ea1a4dfb1b591756a8dc60f504da2,6b49a9a43dc167f62a772b262afe2bee,8d9c5b85d058d0d3cde4479b5589a7d1,90ec2b7e4fe7ed5c84bafaaba8834021,b36b8846f109ee75806c6ea03015e5d5</data>
      <data key="d3">1005</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">14</data>
      <data key="d6">8978</data>
      <data key="d7">21fb5acac1a44dc98dd24332606b25eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STREPTOCOCCUS">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Streptococcus is a genus of bacteria belonging to the phylum Firmicutes. These bacteria are commonly found in the gastrointestinal tract and play a significant role in human health and microbiota. Streptococcus can have both beneficial and pathogenic effects, highlighting its dual nature in the context of human health.</data>
      <data key="d2">90ec2b7e4fe7ed5c84bafaaba8834021,b36b8846f109ee75806c6ea03015e5d5</data>
      <data key="d3">1005</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">8979</data>
      <data key="d7">4b1267e1437a452f87dedff7a16d980c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACTEROIDES">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacteroides is a genus of bacteria that plays a crucial role in the human gut, particularly in the digestion of complex carbohydrates. It is predominantly found in the intestines and is part of the phylum Bacteroidetes. Research has shown that Bacteroides is negatively associated with obesity, suggesting that its presence may be linked to healthier weight management. Interestingly, this genus has also been found to increase in individuals who successfully lose weight. Additionally, Bacteroides is more prevalent in the intestinal microbiota of African children, which is associated with diets rich in plant fiber. Overall, Bacteroides is essential for digestive health and may influence body weight regulation.</data>
      <data key="d2">05360f0d7332f748b436b98d64e4eeb7,113f5fda648e7322f4771e2ade72481f,1ea9b98ae148853da00e395690177314,90ec2b7e4fe7ed5c84bafaaba8834021,959173b0e9d9c5ea2edabff579240462,b36b8846f109ee75806c6ea03015e5d5</data>
      <data key="d3">1003</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">8980</data>
      <data key="d7">4614595acb2943fe84eba122950cee59</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACILLUS">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacillus is a genus of bacteria that encompasses species significant for their roles in fermentation and intestinal health. These bacteria are recognized for their resilience and are utilized as probiotics, contributing to various health benefits.</data>
      <data key="d2">5a1ea1a4dfb1b591756a8dc60f504da2,90ec2b7e4fe7ed5c84bafaaba8834021</data>
      <data key="d3">1025</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">8981</data>
      <data key="d7">da2b205530a748ae86deb11d1790c695</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CLOSTRIDIUM">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Clostridium is a genus of bacteria within the phylum Firmicutes, encompassing species that can be either beneficial or pathogenic in the human intestine. This genus has been associated with various health implications, including its role in gut health and disease. Notably, certain species of Clostridium have been linked to obesity and metabolic alterations, highlighting their impact on metabolic health. Overall, Clostridium plays a complex role in the human gut, contributing to both beneficial functions and potential pathogenic effects.</data>
      <data key="d2">05360f0d7332f748b436b98d64e4eeb7,113f5fda648e7322f4771e2ade72481f,90ec2b7e4fe7ed5c84bafaaba8834021,b36b8846f109ee75806c6ea03015e5d5</data>
      <data key="d3">1003</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">4</data>
      <data key="d6">8982</data>
      <data key="d7">9acb5c2fa47f48c49bb929081274b5bc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FIRMICUTES">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Firmicutes is a phylum of bacteria that plays a significant role in the human intestinal microbiota, particularly noted for its high prevalence in the gut microbiota of European children, where it is associated with diets rich in fats and animal proteins. This phylum is crucial for the fermentation of nutrients and has been linked to increased energy absorption, especially in obese individuals, where it tends to be found in higher proportions compared to the Bacteroidetes phylum. Research has indicated that the abundance of Firmicutes can change following antibiotic treatment and that it shows a proportional decrease in individuals after undergoing Roux-en-Y gastric bypass surgery. Overall, Firmicutes represents a substantial component of the gut microbiome, influencing metabolic processes and potentially contributing to obesity.</data>
      <data key="d2">15ac197b57d904b3bf72905c4d946c0d,1ea9b98ae148853da00e395690177314,90ec2b7e4fe7ed5c84bafaaba8834021,959173b0e9d9c5ea2edabff579240462,e189588f51b4bb84b3621a1f846d07bc,ffdc08ec09899ab7dfa255af4cecac1c</data>
      <data key="d3">1023</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">8983</data>
      <data key="d7">0e36e3e27c6c4ae48bbb48c20f29995b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACTEROIDETES">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacteroidetes is a phylum of bacteria that plays a crucial role in digestion and intestinal health. It is a significant component of the gut microbiota and is associated with lean body mass. Research indicates that individuals with obesity tend to have lower ratios of Bacteroidetes compared to Firmicutes, suggesting that this phylum is linked to reduced energy absorption. Additionally, Bacteroidetes is influenced by antibiotic treatment, as demonstrated in various studies. Overall, Bacteroidetes is essential for maintaining a healthy gut environment and may have implications for body weight regulation.</data>
      <data key="d2">15ac197b57d904b3bf72905c4d946c0d,90ec2b7e4fe7ed5c84bafaaba8834021,e189588f51b4bb84b3621a1f846d07bc,ffdc08ec09899ab7dfa255af4cecac1c</data>
      <data key="d3">1023</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">5</data>
      <data key="d6">8984</data>
      <data key="d7">45380f07a15b4d0b8017e495902fe712</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACTINOBACTERIAS">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Actinobacterias es un filo de bacterias que incluye especies que son parte de la microbiota intestinal y tienen funciones metab&#243;licas importantes.</data>
      <data key="d2">90ec2b7e4fe7ed5c84bafaaba8834021</data>
