    </node>
    <node id="ENTEROCOCCUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Enterococcus is a genus of bacteria belonging to the phylum Firmicutes, commonly found in the intestines and playing a significant role in gut health. Notably, Enterococcus species, such as E. faecalis and E. faecium, are known to be involved in catheter-related infections, highlighting their clinical relevance in healthcare settings.</data>
      <data key="d2">b36b8846f109ee75806c6ea03015e5d5,fe3ebcedac0857680bcc85bb58dcb3e1</data>
      <data key="d3">766</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">8997</data>
      <data key="d7">c775219f1aa74784a8db8a81102186b1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VEILLONELLA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Veillonella is a genus of bacteria within the phylum Firmicutes, known for its role in the human microbiome.</data>
      <data key="d2">b36b8846f109ee75806c6ea03015e5d5</data>
      <data key="d3">1002</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">8998</data>
      <data key="d7">92816a97f80f401c8c65382cf0ab8325</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EUBACTERIUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Eubacterium is a genus of bacteria that plays a significant role in metabolic health and obesity. It is part of the normal gut flora and is involved in various metabolic processes essential for maintaining gut health. Eubacterium belongs to the phylum Firmicutes, highlighting its importance in the overall functioning of the gastrointestinal system.</data>
      <data key="d2">05360f0d7332f748b436b98d64e4eeb7,113f5fda648e7322f4771e2ade72481f,b36b8846f109ee75806c6ea03015e5d5</data>
      <data key="d3">1003</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">8999</data>
      <data key="d7">26401f5490bf420c912cdf995f5ca842</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FUSOBACTERIUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Fusobacterium is a genus of bacteria that belongs to the phylum Fusobacteria. It is commonly found as part of the normal flora in the human mouth and gut. While many species of Fusobacterium coexist harmlessly within these environments, some are associated with various diseases. This dual role highlights the complexity of Fusobacterium, as it can contribute to both health and disease states in humans.</data>
      <data key="d2">113f5fda648e7322f4771e2ade72481f,b36b8846f109ee75806c6ea03015e5d5</data>
      <data key="d3">1003</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">9000</data>
      <data key="d7">4fe0d3fbb72846ee93dda211c902fa46</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PEPTOSTREPTOCOCCUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Peptostreptococcus is a genus of bacteria within the phylum Firmicutes, significant in the context of human health.</data>
      <data key="d2">b36b8846f109ee75806c6ea03015e5d5</data>
      <data key="d3">1002</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9001</data>
      <data key="d7">c84ca47636044bd889dadea665b47aaf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RUMINOCOCCUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Ruminococcus is a genus of bacteria belonging to the phylum Firmicutes. This genus is recognized for its significant role in the fermentation of dietary fibers, contributing to the digestive processes in various organisms. Additionally, Ruminococcus has been associated with decreased amounts in obese individuals, suggesting a potential link between its presence and body weight regulation.</data>
      <data key="d2">b36b8846f109ee75806c6ea03015e5d5,ffdc08ec09899ab7dfa255af4cecac1c</data>
      <data key="d3">1002</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9002</data>
      <data key="d7">85ff7b7e2e554d35a07cbf79ab9c3cef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIFIDOBACTERIUM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Bifidobacterium is a genus of bacteria that plays a crucial role in promoting gut health. It is commonly found in the intestines and is known for its beneficial effects, including the modulation of inflammation and metabolic processes. Research has shown that Bifidobacterium is negatively correlated with obesity and diabetes, with lower quantities of this bacterium observed in obese individuals. This genus is frequently utilized in probiotic treatments, highlighting its importance in maintaining a healthy gut microbiome. Bifidobacterium belongs to the phylum Actinobacteria and is recognized for its contributions to overall gut health, making it a significant focus of studies related to obesity and metabolic health.</data>
      <data key="d2">05360f0d7332f748b436b98d64e4eeb7,113f5fda648e7322f4771e2ade72481f,2689c33de87eab465f004fff5f0530b8,379909adba66c6e316f560669cd30472,5a1ea1a4dfb1b591756a8dc60f504da2,b36b8846f109ee75806c6ea03015e5d5,e189588f51b4bb84b3621a1f846d07bc,ffdc08ec09899ab7dfa255af4cecac1c</data>
      <data key="d3">1025</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">12</data>
      <data key="d6">9003</data>
      <data key="d7">9b37fddf64c9415699ef3b84da23871a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROTEOBACTERIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Proteobacteria is a major phylum of bacteria that encompasses a diverse range of pathogens and plays a significant role in the context of gut microbiota. Research studies have indicated that Proteobacteria can exhibit changes in response to antibiotic administration, highlighting its dynamic nature and importance in microbial ecology and health.</data>
      <data key="d2">15ac197b57d904b3bf72905c4d946c0d,b36b8846f109ee75806c6ea03015e5d5</data>
      <data key="d3">1023</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">9004</data>
      <data key="d7">eb10ed60e1c042a999012b4b882e7913</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FILO: FIRMICUTES">
      <data key="d0" />
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">b36b8846f109ee75806c6ea03015e5d5</data>
      <data key="d3">1002</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9005</data>
      <data key="d7">c24a301d7ed74f1b8f15bbccf1045eb1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NI&#209;OS AFRICANOS">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Grupo de ni&#241;os que presentan una microbiota intestinal rica en Bacteroidetes y con una mayor riqueza microbiana en comparaci&#243;n con los ni&#241;os europeos, debido a su dieta alta en fibra y baja en grasas animales.</data>
      <data key="d2">1ea9b98ae148853da00e395690177314</data>
      <data key="d3">1003</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">9006</data>
      <data key="d7">0eff3b0adfda49e78bb6c09e89685dbd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NI&#209;OS EUROPEOS">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Grupo de ni&#241;os que presentan una microbiota intestinal caracterizada por una mayor proporci&#243;n de Firmicutes y una menor riqueza microbiana, atribuida a su dieta alta en grasas y prote&#237;nas de origen animal y baja en fibra.</data>
      <data key="d2">1ea9b98ae148853da00e395690177314</data>
      <data key="d3">1002</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">9007</data>
      <data key="d7">dc03c5d4a3f044a4a5cf2594ef530a26</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIETA ALTA EN FIBRA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Patr&#243;n diet&#233;tico que se asocia con un aumento en la proporci&#243;n de Bacteroidetes y una disminuci&#243;n en Firmicutes en la microbiota intestinal.</data>
      <data key="d2">1ea9b98ae148853da00e395690177314</data>
      <data key="d3">1002</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9008</data>
      <data key="d7">a7830c8a007945c8917c8366eb172df8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALTERACIONES DE LA ENERG&#205;A">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Bloque que describe c&#243;mo los cambios en la dieta afectan la microbiota intestinal y la energ&#237;a del cuerpo.</data>
      <data key="d2">1ea9b98ae148853da00e395690177314</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">9009</data>
      <data key="d7">a374a50690654886b18b010b2ecdeb13</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PATRONES DIET&#201;TICOS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Factores que influyen en la composici&#243;n de la microbiota intestinal, mostrando diferencias significativas entre grupos con h&#225;bitos diet&#233;ticos diferentes.</data>
      <data key="d2">1ea9b98ae148853da00e395690177314</data>
      <data key="d3">1002</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9010</data>
      <data key="d7">d03f0920c7f44ae5b2179a975683464d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIOTA DE COMUNIDADES OCCIDENTALES">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Descripci&#243;n de la microbiota intestinal de comunidades occidentales, caracterizada por una ingesta alta en grasa y prote&#237;nas de origen animal y un bajo contenido en fibra.</data>
      <data key="d2">1ea9b98ae148853da00e395690177314</data>
      <data key="d3">1002</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9011</data>
      <data key="d7">45d61f9f589346408a3628d758692f81</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIOTA DE COMUNIDADES ORIENTALES">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Descripci&#243;n de la microbiota intestinal de comunidades orientales, que muestra una mayor riqueza microbiana y una mayor proporci&#243;n de Bacteroidetes.</data>
      <data key="d2">1ea9b98ae148853da00e395690177314</data>
      <data key="d3">1002</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9012</data>
      <data key="d7">51f871897c1445dbb5e7fe3c41b15a07</data>
    </node>
