      <data key="d6">9013</data>
      <data key="d7">53b9247255fe4a2bb59364fc13c63475</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BACTERIANA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacteriana is a research entity focused on the study of intestinal microbiota and its relation to obesity and metabolic diseases.</data>
      <data key="d2">2689c33de87eab465f004fff5f0530b8</data>
      <data key="d3">1009</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">9014</data>
      <data key="d7">365235b1760c4d72842f7b86b7e93791</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AKKERMANSIA MUCINIPHILA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Akkermansia muciniphila is a beneficial bacterium that plays a significant role in gut health and metabolic processes. This species of bacteria is known for its ability to degrade mucin, a component of the mucus layer in the gut, and is linked to improved metabolic profiles, particularly in relation to obesity and diabetes. Research indicates that higher levels of Akkermansia muciniphila correlate with better metabolic health outcomes in rodents, especially when its abundance is normalized through dietary changes and prebiotic intake.

Interestingly, Akkermansia muciniphila can also increase in abundance following broad-spectrum antibiotic treatment, which may have implications for reducing the incidence of diabetes. Overall, this bacterium is associated with the gut microbiota and is considered important for metabolic health, with potential applications in obesity prevention and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d2">15ac197b57d904b3bf72905c4d946c0d,2689c33de87eab465f004fff5f0530b8,959173b0e9d9c5ea2edabff579240462,b2549791b62afc5e1244c2c610af55e6</data>
      <data key="d3">1009</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">9015</data>
      <data key="d7">53cc7224d6884322b64a2cb0ff110e2e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENDOTOXEMIA METAB&#211;LICA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Endotoxemia metab&#243;lica refers to the presence of endotoxins in the bloodstream, often linked to inflammation and metabolic disorders.</data>
      <data key="d2">2689c33de87eab465f004fff5f0530b8</data>
      <data key="d3">996</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9016</data>
      <data key="d7">e1f9d452e2cb4422989cd2dadfc64de7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="S&#205;NTESIS DE &#193;CIDOS GRASOS DE CADENA CORTA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Synthesis of short-chain fatty acids (SCFA) is a metabolic process that occurs in the gut, associated with various health benefits including improved gut health and reduced inflammation.</data>
      <data key="d2">2689c33de87eab465f004fff5f0530b8</data>
      <data key="d3">996</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9017</data>
      <data key="d7">15fb36c67bd540d49d5565caea26f9cb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MICROBIOTA">
      <data key="d0" />
      <data key="d1">Microbiota refers to the community of microorganisms, including bacteria, that inhabit various environments within the human body, with a significant concentration residing in the gut. This community plays a vital role in several aspects of health, including digestion, metabolism, and the development of allergic diseases. The composition of the microbiota can be influenced by various factors, such as antibiotic use, which can alter its balance. Additionally, the microbiota is notably affected in conditions like obesity, where it can impact metabolic functions and contribute to the overall health of an individual.</data>
      <data key="d2">05360f0d7332f748b436b98d64e4eeb7,113f5fda648e7322f4771e2ade72481f,15ac197b57d904b3bf72905c4d946c0d,2582e3a8c599c599624ee99e7754a7e0,2689c33de87eab465f004fff5f0530b8,959173b0e9d9c5ea2edabff579240462,fa4851ce095f27e98350dcb826e5b09c</data>
      <data key="d3">1007</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">27</data>
      <data key="d6">9018</data>
      <data key="d7">f530166c64c748938ebfea02c0b2a648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NEYRINCK">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Neyrinck is a researcher who studied the effects of broad-spectrum antibiotics on obese rodents and their metabolic alterations.</data>
      <data key="d2">15ac197b57d904b3bf72905c4d946c0d</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9019</data>
      <data key="d7">49ab6be92eab4ee88d5aeba36cb0ca92</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DEKZENNE">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Dekzenne is a researcher who collaborated with Neyrinck in studying the effects of antibiotics on the microbiota of obese rodents.</data>
      <data key="d2">15ac197b57d904b3bf72905c4d946c0d</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9020</data>
      <data key="d7">4ab6b5c5db4b42f981814771c4b9e58d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CANI">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Cani is a researcher who supports the idea that broad-spectrum antibiotic therapy significantly reduces plasma levels of lipopolysaccharides in animal models.</data>
      <data key="d2">15ac197b57d904b3bf72905c4d946c0d</data>
      <data key="d3">1007</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9021</data>
      <data key="d7">a44e7586495e40bdb2e6d8492e42ea5e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MEMBREZ">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Membrez is a researcher who studied the effects of antibiotic treatment on genetically obese rodents with insulin resistance.</data>
      <data key="d2">15ac197b57d904b3bf72905c4d946c0d</data>
      <data key="d3">1811</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">9022</data>
      <data key="d7">2a00f172cf4e436abaf4866ba25ebb38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="H. PYLORI">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">H. pylori, or Helicobacter pylori, is a bacterium that can infect humans, particularly the stomach. It is often associated with various gastrointestinal diseases, including ulcers, and has implications for iron absorption. Additionally, H. pylori has been mentioned in the context of antibiotic exposure, which may influence weight gain. This bacterium plays a significant role in gastrointestinal health and disease, highlighting its importance in medical research and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d2">15ac197b57d904b3bf72905c4d946c0d,79435b6ab45648695f4a36f31b7fb458</data>
      <data key="d3">1811</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">9023</data>
      <data key="d7">206cbe8927a0465594b95f4c4aec4d2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANTIBIOTIC THERAPY">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Antibiotic therapy is a treatment method employed to combat bacterial infections, frequently initiated empirically in severe cases. This approach involves the use of antibiotics to address infections and has been the subject of research regarding its effects on microbiota and potential weight gain.</data>
      <data key="d2">15ac197b57d904b3bf72905c4d946c0d,be901905a9eef428a784ea179931028a</data>
      <data key="d3">1390</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">9024</data>
      <data key="d7">31d68602f90047b9a3917026e447cd90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INFECCIONES BACTERIANAS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Infecciones bacterianas refer to bacterial infections that antibiotics are used to treat, which can impact microbiota diversity.</data>
      <data key="d2">15ac197b57d904b3bf72905c4d946c0d</data>
      <data key="d3">841</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9025</data>
      <data key="d7">45ad1a91a8634dc2957b587dfcf340f5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALERGIAS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Alergias refer to allergies, which are immune system reactions to specific substances. These reactions can be influenced by various factors, including genetic predispositions and environmental conditions. Additionally, changes in microbiota due to antibiotic use may also play a role in the development of allergies. Among the different types of allergies, food allergies are notable as they represent adverse immune responses to certain foods. These food allergies can manifest in early childhood and may vary in duration and severity over time.</data>
      <data key="d2">15ac197b57d904b3bf72905c4d946c0d,39a7166ca777be46a1613c54a746e2b8,9d1d9a50a4a29ae7660c32b749e46dbd</data>
      <data key="d3">841</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">11</data>
      <data key="d6">9026</data>
      <data key="d7">fbec50ff479d49ef8e35fab522e5062f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIVERSIDAD FILOGEN&#201;TICA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Diversidad filogen&#233;tica refers to the genetic diversity of microbial communities, which can be altered by antibiotic treatment.</data>
      <data key="d2">15ac197b57d904b3bf72905c4d946c0d</data>
      <data key="d3">841</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9027</data>
      <data key="d7">b298517c175a4b7a935b893e7647d75c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SISTEMA INMUNE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">El sistema inmune es el conjunto de c&#233;lulas y mecanismos que protegen al organismo contra infecciones y enfermedades, incluyendo reacciones al&#233;rgicas. Este sistema es fundamental para la defensa del cuerpo y su desarrollo en ni&#241;os puede influir en la prevalencia de alergias. Adem&#225;s, el sistema inmune es una red compleja de c&#233;lulas y prote&#237;nas que defiende al organismo contra infecciones, y su desarrollo y mantenimiento est&#225;n influenciados por la microbiota intestinal. Cambios en esta microbiota, como los provocados por el uso de antibi&#243;ticos, pueden afectar el funcionamiento del sistema inmune.</data>
      <data key="d2">15ac197b57d904b3bf72905c4d946c0d,235520bdfb411a2997762c99ac421bf3,39a7166ca777be46a1613c54a746e2b8,4b3664b368fad59fd55189a65e19b000,d10e6c00bbb982bda449b9cb815691a3</data>
      <data key="d3">841</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">6</data>
      <data key="d6">9028</data>
      <data key="d7">bcadfdeb1ac341fdac129423fd7952cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ROEDORES">
      <data key="d0" />
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">15ac197b57d904b3bf72905c4d946c0d</data>
      <data key="d3">1023</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">9029</data>
      <data key="d7">aeb6d992fe6c4bcfbfa5ef567ef280fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GENES CANDIDATOS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Los genes candidatos son aquellos identificados en estudios de asociaci&#243;n que ayudan a entender la sensibilidad y resistencia a la diabetes y obesidad.</data>
      <data key="d2">01eb4b96cd8e6a886ebc313171922a77</data>
      <data key="d3">987</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9030</data>
      <data key="d7">5f0b97e26dbc488b9a42d9d80edf18fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIVERSIDAD GEN&#201;TICA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">La diversidad gen&#233;tica se refiere a las variaciones en los genes entre individuos, lo que puede influir en la susceptibilidad a enfermedades metab&#243;licas.</data>
      <data key="d2">01eb4b96cd8e6a886ebc313171922a77</data>
      <data key="d3">1391</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9031</data>
      <data key="d7">71ed422582ce47ba8daf17b63ef60b70</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FACTORES AMBIENTALES">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Los factores ambientales son condiciones externas que pueden afectar la salud, incluyendo la dieta y el estilo de vida, y est&#225;n relacionados con el aumento de la obesidad.</data>
      <data key="d2">01eb4b96cd8e6a886ebc313171922a77</data>
      <data key="d3">1390</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9032</data>
      <data key="d7">44be03a5b30942a9a21d518399da0f78</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BUTIRATO">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Butirato, also known as butyrate, is a short-chain fatty acid produced by gut bacteria during the fermentation of dietary fiber. It plays a crucial role in energy metabolism by providing energy to colon epithelial cells and reducing intestinal permeability. Additionally, butirato is involved in the apoptosis of inflammatory cells and enhances the function of the mucosal barrier, contributing to inflammation reduction and overall gut health.</data>
      <data key="d2">0a78cb1d502baf0116e4326e7fbcae41,9538d3f95062fd16213d9c718bb42872,df0e6695be23c07606ca5b9accc62df2</data>
      <data key="d3">1006</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">5</data>
      <data key="d6">9033</data>
      <data key="d7">edf6675c517542489604ef162aab1b89</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LIPOPOLISAC&#193;RIDOS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Lipopolysaccharides (LPS) are pro-inflammatory components found in the cell wall of gram-negative bacteria. They are associated with increased intestinal permeability and are linked to chronic inflammation, which is often related to obesity.</data>
      <data key="d2">0a78cb1d502baf0116e4326e7fbcae41,9538d3f95062fd16213d9c718bb42872</data>
      <data key="d3">1006</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">4</data>
      <data key="d6">9034</data>
      <data key="d7">79fe28523c434b9ea7bddcc6751e84e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GHRLINA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Ghrelin is a hormone that stimulates appetite, its levels are influenced by gut microbiota composition and dietary intake.</data>
      <data key="d2">0a78cb1d502baf0116e4326e7fbcae41</data>
      <data key="d3">1000</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">9035</data>
      <data key="d7">61e34481dcb742599c8ae66e4dcc97cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIFIDOBACTERIAS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Bifidobacteria are beneficial gut bacteria that play a crucial role in digestion and maintaining gut health. They are associated with lower levels of ghrelin, a hormone that regulates appetite. These bacteria are typically found in the intestines and their populations can be increased by diets rich in prebiotics, which support their growth and activity. Overall, Bifidobacteria contribute positively to digestive health and metabolic regulation.</data>
      <data key="d2">0a78cb1d502baf0116e4326e7fbcae41,6c98e1347f14c72617198a910cf8c246</data>
      <data key="d3">1000</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">9036</data>
      <data key="d7">5c22062f77f7436fb7029f1af588c38d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LACTOBACILUS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Lactobacillus is a genus of bacteria that is part of the gut microbiota, known for its role in fermenting carbohydrates and producing beneficial metabolites.</data>
      <data key="d2">0a78cb1d502baf0116e4326e7fbcae41</data>
      <data key="d3">1000</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9037</data>
      <data key="d7">b6532848bb4f45b1801d4ac35ad7ed6a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BLOQUE: ALTERACIONES DE LA ENERG&#205;A">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">This section discusses the alterations in energy balance related to dietary interventions and their effects on host metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d2">0a78cb1d502baf0116e4326e7fbcae41</data>
      <data key="d3">1006</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9038</data>
      <data key="d7">d0f5613a509e4839a632a22eb1f779b8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NFKB">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">NFKB, also known as nuclear factor kappa-light-chain-enhancer of activated B cells, is a protein complex that plays a crucial role in regulating immune response and inflammation. It is essential for controlling the transcription of DNA, thereby influencing the immune response to infections. Through its regulatory functions, NFKB is integral to the body's ability to respond to various immune challenges.</data>
      <data key="d2">0a78cb1d502baf0116e4326e7fbcae41,d2f16314e7098030a6780411d2e03023</data>
      <data key="d3">303</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">9039</data>
      <data key="d7">8b8461b173f44a7a9a6de159cb1c8f1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TLR4">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Toll-like receptor 4 (TLR4) is a protein that plays a crucial role in the immune system by recognizing pathogens and activating inflammatory responses.</data>
      <data key="d2">0a78cb1d502baf0116e4326e7fbcae41</data>
      <data key="d3">1006</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9040</data>
      <data key="d7">8655e94853f142b68a540f5b6644e4da</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MACR&#211;FAGO">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Macrophages are immune cells that engulf and digest cellular debris and pathogens, playing a vital role in the immune response and inflammation.</data>
      <data key="d2">0a78cb1d502baf0116e4326e7fbcae41</data>
      <data key="d3">1006</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9041</data>
      <data key="d7">66918a6f48864d18a999175e9d1d4721</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FIAF">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">FIAF, or fat-induced adipocyte factor, is a gene expressed in the intestine that plays a crucial role in regulating fat metabolism. It inhibits lipoprotein lipase activity, which in turn affects fat storage in both the liver and adipose tissue. Additionally, FIAF is a protein that is influenced by the gut microbiota, highlighting its connection to the body's metabolic processes and the impact of intestinal health on fat regulation.</data>
      <data key="d2">2582e3a8c599c599624ee99e7754a7e0,9538d3f95062fd16213d9c718bb42872</data>
      <data key="d3">996</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">9042</data>
      <data key="d7">8666cacd22cb4d7c87490d24ec9f657e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ACIDOS BILIARES">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Bile acids are produced in the liver and play a role in fat digestion and metabolism, with primary and secondary forms affecting energy expenditure.</data>
      <data key="d2">9538d3f95062fd16213d9c718bb42872</data>
      <data key="d3">1002</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9043</data>
      <data key="d7">db968b95e7354eb98f1dded4deae0436</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RECEPTOR TOLL-LIKE 4">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">TLR4 is a receptor that recognizes lipopolysaccharides from gram-negative bacteria, activating inflammatory pathways.</data>
      <data key="d2">9538d3f95062fd16213d9c718bb42872</data>
      <data key="d3">1006</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">9044</data>
      <data key="d7">38f7830806d24f9d80bfa92c8d8cdc23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROBIOTICOS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Probiotics are beneficial bacteria that can modulate gut microbiota composition and enhance butyrate production.</data>
      <data key="d2">9538d3f95062fd16213d9c718bb42872</data>
      <data key="d3">1002</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9045</data>
      <data key="d7">fd2f8458499640bd979391c741fd7966</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLP-2">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">GLP-2, or glucagon-like peptide-2, is a hormone peptide that plays a crucial role in promoting intestinal growth and protecting against intestinal atrophy. This peptide hormone is integral to the regulation of gut function and energy homeostasis, working in conjunction with GLP-1 to support these processes. Overall, GLP-2 is essential for maintaining intestinal health and functionality.</data>
      <data key="d2">9538d3f95062fd16213d9c718bb42872,e0196a8a0ead0a27b38ddfb16a5c5772,e189588f51b4bb84b3621a1f846d07bc</data>
      <data key="d3">1001</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">4</data>
      <data key="d6">9046</data>
      <data key="d7">889a88fd32c6479faf846781ac76a378</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PYY">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">PYY (peptide YY) is an intestinal hormone that helps regulate appetite and energy balance.</data>
      <data key="d2">9538d3f95062fd16213d9c718bb42872</data>
      <data key="d3">999</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9047</data>
      <data key="d7">a65f3bef4a934d9c94f0b3e536fbf578</data>
    </node>
    <node id="YADAV ET AL">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Yadav et al are researchers who demonstrated the modulation of intestinal microbiota composition through probiotics, stimulating butyrate production and improving inflammatory status and insulin resistance.</data>
      <data key="d2">e189588f51b4bb84b3621a1f846d07bc</data>
      <data key="d3">1001</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">9048</data>
      <data key="d7">8e49fc3c6b4949b9890ccef51a35afe1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PEPTIDO INTESTINAL YY">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Peptido intestinal YY is a hormone involved in reducing appetite and regulating energy balance.</data>
      <data key="d2">e189588f51b4bb84b3621a1f846d07bc</data>
      <data key="d3">1001</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">9049</data>
      <data key="d7">f793c0f9e93a4e09baf9ff183df9ca58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SCFA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Short-chain fatty acids (SCFA) are fatty acids characterized by having fewer than six carbon atoms. They are produced during the fermentation of dietary fibers in the gut by gut bacteria. SCFAs play a crucial role in maintaining gut health, contributing to various physiological processes and overall well-being.</data>
      <data key="d2">2582e3a8c599c599624ee99e7754a7e0,e189588f51b4bb84b3621a1f846d07bc</data>
      <data key="d3">1001</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">9050</data>
      <data key="d7">316f5f9ed2c9409fa83e7faa35198f38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GPR43">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">GPR43 is a G protein-coupled receptor that mediates the effects of SCFA on glucose metabolism and appetite regulation.</data>
      <data key="d2">e189588f51b4bb84b3621a1f846d07bc</data>
      <data key="d3">1001</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9051</data>
      <data key="d7">e79d8a769e324ca4b65e8d41b2b91757</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GPR41">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">GPR41 is a G protein-coupled receptor that is involved in the signaling pathways activated by SCFA.</data>
      <data key="d2">e189588f51b4bb84b3621a1f846d07bc</data>
      <data key="d3">1001</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9052</data>
      <data key="d7">591c43e10e3b430fa13c9d7f035cfddd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GORDON ET AL">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Gordon et al are researchers who studied the effects of microbial colonization on body weight and fat in germ-free mice.</data>
