    <node id="UNIDAD DE HOSPITALIZACI&#211;N DE MEDICINA INTERNA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">The Unidad de Hospitalizaci&#243;n de Medicina Interna is a unit within the Hospital Universitario Insular de Gran Canaria that specializes in internal medicine.</data>
      <data key="d2">4c4d1882f0ac14b7c55941ec1c17b650</data>
      <data key="d3">1836</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9418</data>
      <data key="d7">cf4fb4c30ae04f1085a69a780104b653</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GRUPO AULA M&#201;DICA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Grupo Aula M&#233;dica is a prominent organization engaged in medical education and publishing, with a particular emphasis on nutrition and dietetics. The group is known for producing a variety of educational materials aimed at healthcare professionals, which include guidelines and literature in the medical field. They hold copyright for several significant texts, including those focused on clinical nutrition, pediatric nutrition, and clinical nutrition and metabolism. Additionally, Grupo Aula M&#233;dica is involved in publishing works related to evidence-based medicine and oncology, further contributing to the advancement of medical knowledge and practices. Through their efforts, they aim to enhance the education and resources available to healthcare providers.</data>
      <data key="d2">0ea889003f06c1b62aea79aae0901061,4c4d1882f0ac14b7c55941ec1c17b650,4e86ca77481672995a03989d321652bc,57a5157c4312ac09073e72ad7789ad7c,6906ee13020e48a5d50fc97dc22928fa,ce62d279f86b3364fed4e121ad2d5e1f,da9a9fd67ac55e9180d1609d1df2a255,dce680808e039e852dc0f2639835db32</data>
      <data key="d3">1485</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">4</data>
      <data key="d6">9419</data>
      <data key="d7">67bc6982958c4b38a43d8828291a1a18</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EVIDENCIAS CL&#205;NICAS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Evidencias cl&#237;nicas sobre el soporte nutricional en el paciente diab&#233;tico is a systematic review that provides clinical evidence regarding nutritional support for diabetic patients.</data>
      <data key="d2">4c4d1882f0ac14b7c55941ec1c17b650</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">9420</data>
      <data key="d7">f5417712d4b445b0afd25131ed47ee03</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLUCOSE METABOLISM DISORDERS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Glucose metabolism disorders are conditions that are addressed in clinical nutrition literature, particularly in relation to dietary management.)&lt;|COMPLETE|&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">4c4d1882f0ac14b7c55941ec1c17b650</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">9421</data>
      <data key="d7">e09bb844033d4a68893e8cce0f4a2eef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLUC&#211;GENO">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">GLUC&#211;GENO is a polysaccharide that serves as a crucial form of energy storage in animals and fungi. It primarily exists in the liver and muscle tissues, where it acts as a reserve of glucose, which is essential for various bodily functions. The metabolism of gluc&#243;geno is particularly important for muscle activity, influencing physical performance and exercise requirements. Additionally, gluc&#243;geno can be synthesized from fructose metabolism when necessary, highlighting its role in carbohydrate storage and energy management within the body. Overall, gluc&#243;geno plays a significant role in maintaining energy levels and supporting physical exertion.</data>
      <data key="d2">39da0855b83941d97766254aa36ed2fd,439efa4d64a3de0b12975ea727c939f4,4464b0da291e94ba7b7f5b51134b4a75,470e6bb2e8b8ca543addb0f07464f38c,490f5e3c6dbf3754eee50c52154da09a,bb9a5c32408b601f9ecedc2a2b82d6b6,f0bfe278aa9f43f4bc9d056de67a5b96,f290b280f5c5a9e2e8326fec68b6016f</data>
      <data key="d3">1757</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">16</data>
      <data key="d6">9422</data>
      <data key="d7">a0306779d587461387b2a5d3a29df61b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLUCONEOG&#201;NESIS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">GLUCONEOG&#201;NESIS es un proceso biol&#243;gico que se refiere a la s&#237;ntesis de gluc&#243;geno a partir de glucosa, que ocurre principalmente en el h&#237;gado y los m&#250;sculos. Este proceso es fundamental para el almacenamiento de energ&#237;a en el cuerpo, permitiendo que la glucosa se convierta en gluc&#243;geno, que puede ser utilizado posteriormente cuando el organismo necesita energ&#237;a.</data>
      <data key="d2">439efa4d64a3de0b12975ea727c939f4,4464b0da291e94ba7b7f5b51134b4a75</data>
      <data key="d3">1759</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">9423</data>
      <data key="d7">5988b1ed7d03406e8c20381675bde55d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLUCOLISIS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">La glucogen&#243;lisis es el proceso de degradaci&#243;n del gluc&#243;geno para liberar glucosa cuando es necesario.</data>
      <data key="d2">439efa4d64a3de0b12975ea727c939f4</data>
      <data key="d3">1757</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">9424</data>
      <data key="d7">c7086cc0f33448d7ae8629ee0f3fa9b9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HEXOKINASA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">La hexokinasa es una enzima que fosforila la glucosa para mantenerla dentro de las c&#233;lulas durante la glucogenog&#233;nesis.</data>
      <data key="d2">439efa4d64a3de0b12975ea727c939f4</data>
      <data key="d3">1759</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9425</data>
      <data key="d7">5989614a98de446e848b20568d0d9104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLUC&#211;GENO FOSFORILASA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">GLUC&#211;GENO FOSFORILASA is an enzyme that plays a crucial role in the process of glycogenolysis, which is the breakdown of glycogen into glucose-1-phosphate. This enzyme catalyzes the conversion of glycogen, facilitating the release of glucose-1-phosphate, an important molecule for energy production in the body.</data>
      <data key="d2">439efa4d64a3de0b12975ea727c939f4,f0bfe278aa9f43f4bc9d056de67a5b96</data>
      <data key="d3">1757</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">9426</data>
      <data key="d7">e3927f34a15b42f9972c523028325ea7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLUCOSA-6-FOSFATO">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">GLUCOSA-6-FOSFATO es un metabolito clave en el metabolismo de la glucosa y la galactosa. Se trata de una forma fosforilada de la glucosa que desempe&#241;a un papel fundamental en la gluc&#243;lisis y el metabolismo del gluc&#243;geno. Adem&#225;s, la glucosa-6-fosfato act&#250;a como un intermediario en el metabolismo de la glucosa y es crucial en el proceso de glucogenog&#233;nesis.</data>
      <data key="d2">439efa4d64a3de0b12975ea727c939f4,f0bfe278aa9f43f4bc9d056de67a5b96,f2f8594bddb0ab77444cdcd2a7799d59</data>
      <data key="d3">1824</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">9427</data>
      <data key="d7">73d795a2ef7b4181b8efb7ad562f8717</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLUCOSA-1-FOSFATO">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">GLUCOSA-1-FOSFATO is a glucose molecule that has been phosphorylated at the first carbon, making it an important intermediate in glycogen metabolism. It is formed during the conversion of glucose into glycogen, playing a crucial role in the storage and regulation of energy within the body.</data>
      <data key="d2">439efa4d64a3de0b12975ea727c939f4,f0bfe278aa9f43f4bc9d056de67a5b96</data>
      <data key="d3">1759</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">9428</data>
      <data key="d7">1fac551a800e4b6d9f934ba851426c47</data>
    </node>
    <node id="URIDIN-DIFOSFATO-GLUCOSA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">El uridin-difosfato-glucosa es un intermediario en la s&#237;ntesis de gluc&#243;geno que se forma a partir de glucosa-1-fosfato.</data>
      <data key="d2">439efa4d64a3de0b12975ea727c939f4</data>
      <data key="d3">1759</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9429</data>
      <data key="d7">fa9158310a014110866dd4fa61ad5113</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENZIMA RAMIFICANTE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Enzima ramificante is an enzyme that plays a crucial role in the synthesis of glycogen by introducing &#945;-1,6 linkages, which are essential for creating the branched structure of glycogen. This enzyme is responsible for forming the branches in the glycogen structure, facilitating its complex architecture that is vital for energy storage in organisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">439efa4d64a3de0b12975ea727c939f4,f0bfe278aa9f43f4bc9d056de67a5b96</data>
      <data key="d3">1757</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">9430</data>
      <data key="d7">6bafd7b7cba34d839dba15061ba1edd4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLUC&#211;GENO SINTASA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Gluc&#243;geno sintasa is an enzyme responsible for the synthesis of glycogen from glucose, playing a key role in glycogenogenesis.</data>
      <data key="d2">f0bfe278aa9f43f4bc9d056de67a5b96</data>
      <data key="d3">1757</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">9431</data>
      <data key="d7">ac47237b76aa40e1a5d426617be2af5d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLUCOSA-6-FOSFATASA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Glucosa-6-fosfatasa is an essential enzyme involved in glucose metabolism. It plays a critical role in converting glucose-6-phosphate into glucose, facilitating the release of free glucose into the bloodstream. This enzymatic activity is vital for maintaining normal blood glucose levels. A deficiency in glucosa-6-fosfatasa is central to the diagnosis of glucogenosis de tipo I, also known as enfermedad de Von Gierke, a genetic disorder characterized by impaired glucose production and storage.</data>
      <data key="d2">eb8877823f796fc9a02f5103dc4650cc,f0bfe278aa9f43f4bc9d056de67a5b96</data>
      <data key="d3">1824</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">4</data>
      <data key="d6">9432</data>
      <data key="d7">d6e986a3c971442e8b894a1d98afe811</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLUC&#211;GENO STORAGE DISEASE">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Glycogen Storage Disease (GSD) refers to a group of inherited metabolic disorders that result in abnormal storage of glycogen due to enzyme deficiencies.</data>
      <data key="d2">f0bfe278aa9f43f4bc9d056de67a5b96</data>
      <data key="d3">1757</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">4</data>
      <data key="d6">9433</data>
      <data key="d7">96a7e659ccbf403cb42fd592e06e2365</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOSFORILASA A">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Fosforilasa a is the active form of gluc&#243;geno fosforilasa, responsible for breaking down glycogen into glucose-1-phosphate.</data>
      <data key="d2">f0bfe278aa9f43f4bc9d056de67a5b96</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9434</data>
      <data key="d7">2fb0c12e80ce435eb9b8cb20ea00f291</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOSFORILASA B">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Fosforilasa b is the inactive form of gluc&#243;geno fosforilasa, which must be activated to participate in glycogen breakdown.</data>
      <data key="d2">f0bfe278aa9f43f4bc9d056de67a5b96</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9435</data>
      <data key="d7">c008331d092d476087f42e1bd5c74ab0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UDP-GLUCOSA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">UDP-glucosa is a nucleotide sugar that plays a crucial role in metabolism, particularly in the metabolism of galactose. It is essential for the synthesis of glycogen and other macromolecules, functioning as a glucose donor in these biochemical processes.</data>
      <data key="d2">f0bfe278aa9f43f4bc9d056de67a5b96,f2f8594bddb0ab77444cdcd2a7799d59</data>
      <data key="d3">1757</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9436</data>
      <data key="d7">6ff2db4a28c34d378ce550091e71dc62</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UDP-GLUCOSA PIROFOSFORIILASA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">UDP-glucosa pirofosforilasa is an enzyme that catalyzes the formation of UDP-glucose from glucose-1-phosphate and UTP.</data>
      <data key="d2">f0bfe278aa9f43f4bc9d056de67a5b96</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">9437</data>
      <data key="d7">6366b19fd5b1438a820c864bcc7e33d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOSFOGLUCOMUTASA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Fosfoglucomutasa is an enzyme that plays a crucial role in carbohydrate metabolism by catalyzing the conversion of glucosa-1-fosfato (glucose-1-phosphate) to glucosa-6-fosfato (glucose-6-phosphate). This conversion is essential for facilitating the entry of glucose into various metabolic pathways, thereby contributing to the overall regulation of glucose metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d2">f0bfe278aa9f43f4bc9d056de67a5b96,f2f8594bddb0ab77444cdcd2a7799d59</data>
      <data key="d3">1759</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9438</data>
      <data key="d7">dc0d9fea031c4ba5802995c060008970</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TORRENTE SANGU&#205;NEO">
      <data key="d0">GEO</data>
      <data key="d1">Torrente sangu&#237;neo refers to the bloodstream, the circulatory system that transports glucose and other nutrients throughout the body.</data>
      <data key="d2">f0bfe278aa9f43f4bc9d056de67a5b96</data>
      <data key="d3">663</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9439</data>
      <data key="d7">913c178af00249bcb0109a83e710f269</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLUCOGEN&#211;LISIS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Gluconeogenesis is the metabolic pathway that results in the generation of glucose from non-carbohydrate substrates, primarily occurring in the liver.</data>
      <data key="d2">f0bfe278aa9f43f4bc9d056de67a5b96</data>
      <data key="d3">663</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9440</data>
      <data key="d7">5e66ae602bac41c6992c4d29aeb2b003</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLUCOGENOG&#201;NESIS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Glycogenesis is the process of synthesizing glycogen from glucose, primarily occurring in the liver and muscle tissues.</data>
      <data key="d2">f0bfe278aa9f43f4bc9d056de67a5b96</data>
      <data key="d3">1757</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9441</data>
      <data key="d7">d09788b81d4a4edd8f98cbffb47e0286</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOSFORILASA KINASA">
      <data key="d0" />
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">f0bfe278aa9f43f4bc9d056de67a5b96</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">9442</data>
      <data key="d7">1455bf1aca5a4251bd4928e1cd8d3d66</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLYCOGEN STORAGE DISEASE TYPE I">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Glycogen Storage Disease Type I (GSDI) is a genetic disorder characterized by the accumulation of glycogen in the liver and kidneys due to enzyme deficiencies.</data>
      <data key="d2">704cfd179507a977ecc0691a5bd2861e</data>
      <data key="d3">1731</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">10</data>
      <data key="d6">9443</data>
      <data key="d7">4f767ec65da54fc2ad343822a112777b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HEPATOMEGALY">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Hepatomegaly is a clinical condition characterized by the mild enlargement of the liver. It is commonly associated with various metabolic disorders, particularly glycogen storage diseases such as type I glucogenosis (GSDI) and type VI glucogenosis (GSDVI). This condition can also occur in patients with fructose-1,6-bisphosphate deficiency, where fatty acid accumulation contributes to liver enlargement. Additionally, hepatomegaly may be observed in individuals with Wilson's disease, further highlighting its association with a range of hepatic and metabolic disorders. Overall, hepatomegaly serves as an important clinical manifestation indicative of underlying liver-related issues.</data>
      <data key="d2">24cc40d6e3b225945cd4d8c2484632e4,3898563966943dbfc8a0f6e84bc627b1,449046c83c1432d2b8cc6cacb5eadf51,704cfd179507a977ecc0691a5bd2861e,a81765e6d24ccef0c86801f71673de98,d1b2835d6e86f09adefd22378bb6da15</data>
      <data key="d3">1732</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">9444</data>
      <data key="d7">3e92aff759cd4eaca21fcc8750086b14</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLUCOSE-6-PHOSPHATASE">
      <data key="d0">ENZYME</data>
      <data key="d1">Glucose-6-phosphatase is an enzyme critical for glucose metabolism, deficiency of which leads to GSDI.</data>
      <data key="d2">704cfd179507a977ecc0691a5bd2861e</data>
      <data key="d3">1731</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">9445</data>
      <data key="d7">9eeaeab41f2042f491ff7ab9fe2213b5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLUCOSE-6-PHOSPHATE TRANSPORTER">
      <data key="d0">ENZYME</data>
      <data key="d1">Glucose-6-phosphate transporter is an enzyme involved in the transport of glucose-6-phosphate within cells, deficiency of which is associated with GSDI.</data>
      <data key="d2">704cfd179507a977ecc0691a5bd2861e</data>
      <data key="d3">1731</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9446</data>
      <data key="d7">0bf9c9286b524fec8a7a03fd5094cc61</data>
    </node>
    <node id="VON GIERKE DISEASE">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Von Gierke disease is another name for Glycogen Storage Disease Type I, specifically related to glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency.</data>
      <data key="d2">704cfd179507a977ecc0691a5bd2861e</data>
      <data key="d3">1731</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9447</data>
      <data key="d7">5122ceba77874acd8cd016791e8025af</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLYCOGEN">
      <data key="d0">BIOCHEMICAL COMPOUND</data>
      <data key="d1">Glycogen is a multi-branched polysaccharide composed of glucose that functions as a crucial form of energy storage in animals and fungi. It serves as a stored form of glucose within the body, playing a vital role in energy metabolism. In certain conditions, such as Glycogen Storage Disease Type I, glycogen can accumulate excessively, indicating a disruption in normal glycogen metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d2">704cfd179507a977ecc0691a5bd2861e,fb8d31ef68f83e6b01f6fc8548f36d0c</data>
      <data key="d3">1731</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">9448</data>
      <data key="d7">310e13a699a247e299792aae7edc0bf5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HEPATOCYTES">
      <data key="d0">CELL TYPE</data>
      <data key="d1">Hepatocytes are the main functional cells of the liver, where glucose-6-phosphatase is located and plays a crucial role in glucose metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d2">704cfd179507a977ecc0691a5bd2861e</data>
      <data key="d3">1731</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9449</data>
      <data key="d7">dcc171cb95514cf1a77888da741ac6ba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RENAL TUBULE CELLS">
      <data key="d0">CELL TYPE</data>
      <data key="d1">Renal tubule cells are involved in the kidney's function and are affected in Glycogen Storage Disease Type I due to the accumulation of glycogen.</data>
      <data key="d2">704cfd179507a977ecc0691a5bd2861e</data>
      <data key="d3">1731</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9450</data>
      <data key="d7">22cbd012c1f542079d3797d88745ce9c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENTEROCYTES">
      <data key="d0">CELL TYPE</data>
      <data key="d1">Enterocytes are intestinal cells that also express glucose-6-phosphatase, contributing to glucose metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d2">704cfd179507a977ecc0691a5bd2861e</data>
      <data key="d3">1731</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9451</data>
      <data key="d7">cdb0f9bf53c94b91b0b8686160405927</data>
    </node>
    <node id="G6PC">
      <data key="d0">GENE</data>
      <data key="d1">G6PC is a gene associated with glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency, which can lead to various metabolic disorders.</data>
      <data key="d2">a81765e6d24ccef0c86801f71673de98</data>
      <data key="d3">1624</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9452</data>
      <data key="d7">b0ba593718504caeb3808d687fb0fc7c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SLC37A4">
      <data key="d0">GENE</data>
      <data key="d1">SLC37A4 is a gene that encodes a glucose-6-phosphate transporter, involved in glucose metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d2">a81765e6d24ccef0c86801f71673de98</data>
      <data key="d3">1624</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9453</data>
      <data key="d7">a3e9429f0eca4a9bb5e06a2bbebb7687</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GSDIB">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Glycogen Storage Disease Type Ib (GSDIb) is a metabolic disorder characterized by the inability to properly metabolize glycogen, leading to various health complications.</data>
      <data key="d2">a81765e6d24ccef0c86801f71673de98</data>
      <data key="d3">1624</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">24</data>
      <data key="d6">9454</data>
      <data key="d7">841cf62141ce4b469a56f4a08282d877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HEMODIALYSIS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Hemodialysis is a medical procedure utilized to remove waste products and excess fluid from the blood when the kidneys are not functioning properly. It is primarily used to treat patients with kidney failure, including those with chronic kidney disease and severe cases of metabolic disorders. The process involves the filtration of blood through a machine, which effectively cleanses the blood of harmful substances. Additionally, hemodialysis often necessitates specific nutritional considerations for patients, particularly in monitoring phosphorus intake to ensure optimal health outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d2">1f8cd198519be7b535a3f5be8181931c,5705dc62240c8f823dd1bc15770d5ab2,97a728c18b63007d07b6a4a5fc22d5e4,9da930839072b524410f2345ab1df8b1,a81765e6d24ccef0c86801f71673de98,ae1766e8ae855b3c79ad6d5fdefab1b0,bf76380d2417653bab17a6a9b91d85bc</data>
      <data key="d3">1623</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">20</data>
      <data key="d6">9455</data>
      <data key="d7">33a857586d21493c952d2501201e170a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ADENOMAS HEP&#193;TICOS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Hepatic adenomas are benign tumors of the liver that can develop in patients with metabolic disorders, particularly during the second to third decade of life. These tumors are notably associated with glycogen storage disease type III (GSDIII), indicating a specific metabolic condition that may contribute to their formation.</data>
      <data key="d2">a3fd8bf0a5229c36813682803cd15e35,a81765e6d24ccef0c86801f71673de98</data>
      <data key="d3">1624</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">9456</data>
      <data key="d7">44f7ecc968c749adab6bd000abad78e6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INFANTILE HYPOGLYCEMIA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Infantile hypoglycemia refers to low blood sugar levels in infants, which can lead to cognitive deterioration if severe and frequent.</data>
      <data key="d2">a81765e6d24ccef0c86801f71673de98</data>
      <data key="d3">1624</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9457</data>
      <data key="d7">1d2146405e144d08a9c96bf58dcc4d37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GOUT">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Gout is a form of inflammatory arthritis that is characterized by sudden and severe pain, redness, and tenderness in the joints. This condition often occurs due to hyperuricemia, which is an elevated level of uric acid in the blood, commonly associated with metabolic disorders. Gout can lead to significant discomfort and swelling, making it a painful experience for those affected.</data>
      <data key="d2">6533cbcb76873caf6fd68eb95d313d24,a0162312192878417ef2c4ef8f9cffba,a81765e6d24ccef0c86801f71673de98</data>
      <data key="d3">1384</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">9458</data>
      <data key="d7">290c0e34df6f48eabdbe77920068246d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COGNITIVE DETERIORATION">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Cognitive deterioration can occur in patients with frequent severe hypoglycemia during childhood, affecting their development.</data>
      <data key="d2">a81765e6d24ccef0c86801f71673de98</data>
      <data key="d3">1624</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9459</data>
      <data key="d7">f53b901a52674fc89f0fd4cc61f5fcfd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KIDNEY FAILURE">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Kidney failure is a serious condition that may require hemodialysis or kidney transplant, often seen in patients with metabolic disorders.</data>
      <data key="d2">a81765e6d24ccef0c86801f71673de98</data>
      <data key="d3">1624</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9460</data>
      <data key="d7">7dcdea9421f5446d87791ba501d8f378</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DISLIPEMIA MIXTA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Mixed dyslipidemia refers to abnormal levels of lipids in the blood, which can increase cardiovascular risk.</data>
      <data key="d2">a81765e6d24ccef0c86801f71673de98</data>
      <data key="d3">1624</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9461</data>
      <data key="d7">c0df8f24460a44c2be3194497c77a494</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FENOTIPO PECULIAR">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">A peculiar phenotype is a distinct physical appearance or set of characteristics that can be associated with specific metabolic disorders.</data>
      <data key="d2">a81765e6d24ccef0c86801f71673de98</data>
      <data key="d3">1624</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9462</data>
      <data key="d7">604629dd58474a379dcd882cc0bdf04d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NEFROMEGALIA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Nephromegaly is the enlargement of the kidneys, which can occur due to glycogen accumulation in metabolic disorders.</data>
      <data key="d2">a81765e6d24ccef0c86801f71673de98</data>
      <data key="d3">1624</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9463</data>
      <data key="d7">26867487e39240938ca23babeadf21d4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COLLABORATIVE EUROPEAN STUDY ON GLYCOGEN STORAGE DISEASE I">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">A study group that publishes guidelines for the treatment of Glycogen Storage Disease I, focusing on nutritional objectives and recommendations for patients.</data>
      <data key="d2">948f7e888e5bb06b347a80a3fbf87ae7</data>
      <data key="d3">403</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9464</data>
      <data key="d7">a99569771e2c43d68df59db6483ea650</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLYCOGEN STORAGE DISEASE I">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">A metabolic disorder that affects the body's ability to store and use glycogen, requiring specific dietary management to maintain normal blood glucose levels.</data>
      <data key="d2">948f7e888e5bb06b347a80a3fbf87ae7</data>
      <data key="d3">403</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">9465</data>
      <data key="d7">52ca320d8237423bb8106d0e89dd4f6a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIAZOXIDE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">A medication used to inhibit endogenous insulin production in patients who cannot maintain adequate blood glucose levels through nutritional treatment.</data>
      <data key="d2">948f7e888e5bb06b347a80a3fbf87ae7</data>
      <data key="d3">403</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9466</data>
      <data key="d7">e6691d88d0ef4d7a8509925d52993653</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALMID&#211;N DE MA&#205;Z">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Almid&#243;n de ma&#237;z, or corn starch, is a carbohydrate derived from corn that serves multiple purposes in nutrition and cooking. It is commonly utilized in enteral nutrition and dietary plans for various age groups, including infants and adults, to help maintain stable blood glucose levels. In addition to its role as a carbohydrate source in nutritional formulas, corn starch is frequently employed as a thickening agent in culinary applications. It is particularly beneficial in dietary treatments for metabolic disorders, where it aids in managing blood glucose levels effectively. Furthermore, corn starch is recommended as a dietary component for patients, especially before sleep, to support the maintenance of healthy blood glucose levels.</data>
      <data key="d2">28633689bf0b9bc990e61fb26722ce16,658ee56030a35ceeeec05d15cfa49842,948f7e888e5bb06b347a80a3fbf87ae7,d028f23d86d7ed32eebe35c5d90765f2,f713611b23199b8a9eb9c28377b9f128</data>
      <data key="d3">1179</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">9</data>
      <data key="d6">9467</data>
      <data key="d7">9b1360d184c146b286be8d097a12055e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NUTRICI&#211;N ADECUADA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">The provision of appropriate nutrition to ensure proper growth and development in patients with Glycogen Storage Disease I.</data>
      <data key="d2">948f7e888e5bb06b347a80a3fbf87ae7</data>
      <data key="d3">403</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">9468</data>
      <data key="d7">e697be21e81840f5b4402665ec16b980</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PERIODOS DE AYUNO">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Periods of fasting that patients with Glycogen Storage Disease I must avoid to maintain stable blood glucose levels.</data>
      <data key="d2">948f7e888e5bb06b347a80a3fbf87ae7</data>
      <data key="d3">1131</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9469</data>
      <data key="d7">b845e452b61948c2af07ff4013920dce</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALMID&#211;N CRUDO DE MA&#205;Z">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Almid&#243;n crudo de ma&#237;z, or raw corn starch, is a dietary supplement specifically recommended for patients with glycogen storage diseases (GSD), including conditions such as GSDI and GSDIX. It plays a crucial role in managing hypoglycemia by providing a steady release of glucose, which is essential for maintaining normal blood sugar levels. This supplement is particularly beneficial for individuals with McArdle disease, as it helps sustain glucose levels before sleeping, and for those with fructose-1,6-bisphosphate deficiency, aiding in their dietary management and prevention of hypoglycemia. Overall, almid&#243;n crudo de ma&#237;z is an important dietary component that supports patients in maintaining stable blood glucose levels, especially between meals and during periods of fasting.</data>
      <data key="d2">160d4259ba08a1bfcdc877b63a4c799d,4464b0da291e94ba7b7f5b51134b4a75,449046c83c1432d2b8cc6cacb5eadf51,948f7e888e5bb06b347a80a3fbf87ae7,9f5a8d86160d5d097bca174e2e2496c1,a3fd8bf0a5229c36813682803cd15e35,ae68887594ec21900691a1100f61b43e,eb1f89320202c337496dbad5ca168356</data>
      <data key="d3">1715</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">9470</data>
      <data key="d7">56758576871b45d98b47dc5d59a8c3f2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GSDI">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Glycogen Storage Disease I (GSDI) is a metabolic disorder that significantly impairs the body's ability to store and utilize glycogen, resulting in a range of health complications for those affected. This condition exhibits symptoms that are reminiscent of fructose-1,6-bisphosphate deficiency; however, individuals with GSDI maintain normal glycogenolysis. GSDI is characterized by more severe hypoglycemia when compared to Glycogen Storage Disease Type III (GSDIII). Additionally, GSDI has distinct dietary restrictions that differ from those associated with GSDIII, although it shares some similarities in dietary recommendations with this related metabolic disorder. Overall, GSDI presents a unique set of challenges and management strategies for affected individuals.</data>
      <data key="d2">449046c83c1432d2b8cc6cacb5eadf51,9f5a8d86160d5d097bca174e2e2496c1,a3fd8bf0a5229c36813682803cd15e35,ae68887594ec21900691a1100f61b43e,b5e899a3def3da339630eb0d2bfe4af8</data>
      <data key="d3">1716</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">15</data>
      <data key="d6">9471</data>
      <data key="d7">7440d4ca3dee4ae3aa2b1576063c6963</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COLLABORATIVE EUROPEAN STUDY">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">The Collaborative European Study on Glycogen Storage Disease I is a research initiative focused on understanding and providing dietary recommendations for GSDI patients.</data>
      <data key="d2">ae68887594ec21900691a1100f61b43e</data>
      <data key="d3">1716</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9472</data>
      <data key="d7">ec9d2dea1cee43bebc833eeefb389681</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MAICENA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Maicena is a brand of cornstarch that serves as a dietary treatment for metabolic disorders, particularly in the management of blood glucose levels. It is recognized for its properties as a polysaccharide, which makes it suitable for patients with Glycogen Storage Disease Type I (GSDI). Maicena is recommended for these patients due to its slow absorption rate, which helps in maintaining stable blood glucose levels.</data>
      <data key="d2">28633689bf0b9bc990e61fb26722ce16,ae68887594ec21900691a1100f61b43e</data>
      <data key="d3">1179</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">9473</data>
      <data key="d7">063eb00411d34431a6a66ffc8c53b2b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LACOSTA">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Lacosta is a hypothetical individual referenced in dietary recommendations for managing GSDI.</data>
      <data key="d2">ae68887594ec21900691a1100f61b43e</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">9474</data>
      <data key="d7">6656ded8e6074ab784dbf292a6c2e357</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SORBITOL">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">SORBITOL is a sugar alcohol that serves as an artificial sweetener and is commonly used in various food products. It is naturally found in fruits and is known for its high sweetness while containing low calories. Sorbitol is absorbed passively in the intestine; however, it can cause osmotic diarrhea when consumed in large quantities. Additionally, individuals with hereditary fructose intolerance should avoid sorbitol, as it is a product of fructose metabolism. Furthermore, it is recommended that children with Glycogen Storage Disease Type I (GSDI) also avoid sorbitol due to its potential impact on glucose levels.</data>
      <data key="d2">7dcfce8c5d823846c83fa7ff807da54f,ae68887594ec21900691a1100f61b43e,cdc634d994ef64b248220c990c844160,fb0fe813a0b63d3b7f4a1bccaf15d291,fd661ab8e042d0fed76b34b7ceff6631</data>
      <data key="d3">850</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">10</data>
      <data key="d6">9475</data>
      <data key="d7">60c6390d51344bc7998f8554f0d326f8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="JARABE DE MA&#205;Z RICO EN FRUCTOSA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">"Jarabe de ma&#237;z rico en fructosa" is a sweetener derived from corn, commonly utilized in various processed foods and beverages. However, it is important to note that high fructose corn syrup, which is synonymous with jarabe de ma&#237;z rico en fructosa, should be avoided in children with Glycogen Storage Disease Type I (GSDI) due to its potential negative impact on glucose metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d2">5f60e39bc965632761d15273062b91fe,ae68887594ec21900691a1100f61b43e</data>
      <data key="d3">1716</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9476</data>
      <data key="d7">630009ebcadd4fa4aa69fe61f59ae941</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LACTOSA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">LACTOSA is a disaccharide sugar composed of glucose and galactose, primarily found in milk and dairy products. It plays a significant role in nutrition but can lead to lactose intolerance in some individuals. Lactose intolerance is a condition where the body is unable to properly digest lactose, resulting in various gastrointestinal symptoms. This condition can affect individuals with malabsorption issues, including those with specific gastrointestinal diseases such as Crohn's disease, particularly during flare-ups.

In addition to its presence in milk, lactose is also found in various packaged foods, where it may be used as a preservative. For individuals with dietary restrictions, such as those with fructose malabsorption or certain metabolic disorders like Glycogen Storage Disease Type I (GSDI), it is important to limit lactose intake. Overall, while lactose is a common component of many foods, its impact on digestion varies among individuals, necessitating careful dietary considerations for those affected by lactose intolerance.</data>
      <data key="d2">033a22e7215395479d0775d6a1a2563e,160d4259ba08a1bfcdc877b63a4c799d,2b4d246337cb56a96ab80fec81181838,39a7166ca777be46a1613c54a746e2b8,3b7850cb85e0e94111aee2530619ffc5,45ae0837b0632b026fac1053b7d9d4e8,527c3c962d22018377b32c52e984d4e1,94afd4bf40b661992e66cf7963ef236c,9d1d9a50a4a29ae7660c32b749e46dbd,a562e90891c11da0293bc7de511acc8f,ae68887594ec21900691a1100f61b43e,e0a2b5f5d5b3682c870f787f54f6b5ef,f713611b23199b8a9eb9c28377b9f128,fd661ab8e042d0fed76b34b7ceff6631</data>
      <data key="d3">844</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">18</data>
      <data key="d6">9477</data>
      <data key="d7">782237e5cf6b4108a2bef6e4de52e5b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GALACTOSA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Galactosa is a monosaccharide that plays a crucial role in the diet of mammals, primarily found in milk and its derivatives. It is a component of lactose and is significant in various metabolic processes. However, galactosa is a simple sugar that can be metabolized in the body, and in certain conditions, it may accumulate, leading to toxicity. This accumulation can pose risks, particularly in individuals with specific metabolic disorders. For instance, when administered, galactosa does not improve hypoglycemia in patients with type I glycogen storage disease (GSDI) and may even exacerbate acidosis. Additionally, it is advised that children with GSDI limit their intake of galactosa. Furthermore, ingestion of galactosa can lead to complications in patients with classic galactosemia, a genetic disorder that impairs the metabolism of this sugar. Overall, while galactosa is an important dietary component, its management is critical in certain medical conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">160d4259ba08a1bfcdc877b63a4c799d,2b4d246337cb56a96ab80fec81181838,ae68887594ec21900691a1100f61b43e,eb8877823f796fc9a02f5103dc4650cc,f2f8594bddb0ab77444cdcd2a7799d59,fd661ab8e042d0fed76b34b7ceff6631</data>
      <data key="d3">845</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">12</data>
      <data key="d6">9478</data>
      <data key="d7">d17e262f8cd34d96a0151a1958ec399a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIAREAS TRANSITORIAS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Las diarreas transitorias son efectos secundarios leves que pueden ocurrir al introducir almid&#243;n de ma&#237;z en la dieta, pero que generalmente se resuelven espont&#225;neamente.</data>
      <data key="d2">658ee56030a35ceeeec05d15cfa49842</data>
      <data key="d3">1179</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9479</data>
      <data key="d7">d82cb75335cc4dbd9e2880bbc3040da4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NI&#209;OS MAYORES">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Los ni&#241;os mayores de un a&#241;o deben consumir al menos tres comidas al d&#237;a con almid&#243;n de ma&#237;z y snacks frecuentes para mantener niveles de glucosa adecuados.</data>
      <data key="d2">658ee56030a35ceeeec05d15cfa49842</data>
      <data key="d3">1179</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9480</data>
      <data key="d7">fdc406c887fa4064954c97eea9497f5c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CITRATE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Citrate is a supplement used to alkalinize urine and prevent nephrolithiasis and nephrocalcinosis.</data>
      <data key="d2">9a0227d0ea548fa46814e6403be885ce</data>
      <data key="d3">1583</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">9481</data>
      <data key="d7">7a5b84ae2b1d4a1cb21cae4d46b8bfa8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BICARBONATE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Bicarbonate is a supplement known for its ability to alkalinize urine, particularly in patients suffering from lactic acidosis. It serves as an alkalizing substance that functions as a buffer in acidic environments. This property makes bicarbonate particularly useful in anaerobic situations, where it helps to neutralize lactic acid and delay the onset of fatigue.</data>
      <data key="d2">06a75d9ed2fc1cdca5b08759d3df04d4,9a0227d0ea548fa46814e6403be885ce</data>
      <data key="d3">1583</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">9482</data>
      <data key="d7">e0bdbe3401534ee494c4c7ca4fbc5d0e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NEPHROLITHIASIS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Nephrolithiasis refers to the condition of having kidney stones, which can be influenced by dietary factors. This condition can also be prevented by alkalinizing urine.</data>
      <data key="d2">550574bce077f34348bd11c78dc814a1,9a0227d0ea548fa46814e6403be885ce,f757219e84dba985f7c8d66ac951b9f2</data>
      <data key="d3">1583</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">9483</data>
      <data key="d7">ba7a8d45cd3c4e54a6a5d8f20ee8afff</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NEFROCALCINOSIS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Nefrocalcinosis is a condition characterized by calcium deposits in the kidneys, which can be managed with citrate or bicarbonate.</data>
      <data key="d2">9a0227d0ea548fa46814e6403be885ce</data>
      <data key="d3">1583</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">9484</data>
      <data key="d7">36118ce9bd5d4f6fa470cb10a6a66d57</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GSDIII">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Glycogen Storage Disease Type III (GSDIII), also known as Cori disease, is a metabolic disorder characterized by a deficiency of the enzyme amilo-1,6-glucosidase. This deficiency disrupts normal glycogen metabolism, resulting in various complications that primarily affect glucose processing. Individuals with GSDIII may experience muscular and hepatic issues as a result of this disorder. To manage symptoms and complications associated with GSDIII, dietary interventions are recommended, specifically hyperproteic and low carbohydrate diets.</data>
      <data key="d2">9f5a8d86160d5d097bca174e2e2496c1,a3fd8bf0a5229c36813682803cd15e35,b5e899a3def3da339630eb0d2bfe4af8</data>
      <data key="d3">1714</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">23</data>
      <data key="d6">9485</data>
      <data key="d7">e23a8f9003fd4ad19b1cd26852bb4f8a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AMILO-1,6-GLUCOSIDASA">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">Amilo-1,6-glucosidasa is an enzyme responsible for glycogen breakdown, and its deficiency leads to GSDIII.</data>
      <data key="d2">b5e899a3def3da339630eb0d2bfe4af8</data>
      <data key="d3">1714</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9486</data>
      <data key="d7">8c767f50df1a455daa1d242a211594d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GEN AGL">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">The AGL gene located on chromosome 1p21.2 encodes the enzyme amilo-1,6-glucosidasa, and mutations in this gene are responsible for GSDIII.</data>
      <data key="d2">b5e899a3def3da339630eb0d2bfe4af8</data>
      <data key="d3">1714</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9487</data>
      <data key="d7">64ea46e91c4b4b408e50376b8305c151</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FIBROSIS HEP&#193;TICA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Fibrosis hep&#225;tica is characterized by the formation of excess fibrous connective tissue in the liver, often resulting from chronic liver diseases, such as hereditary hemochromatosis. This condition involves the scarring of liver tissue, which can occur in various patients, including those with Glycogen Storage Disease type III (GSDIII), although it is uncommon for these patients to progress to cirrhosis.</data>
      <data key="d2">a3d834f1d854ecde12e808ae924b8eb0,a3fd8bf0a5229c36813682803cd15e35,b5e899a3def3da339630eb0d2bfe4af8</data>
      <data key="d3">1714</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">2</data>
      <data key="d6">9488</data>
      <data key="d7">ed7148ee780840a99b03acab5120cfcc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIPERTON&#205;A">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">A condition characterized by increased muscle tone, often associated with GSDIII.</data>
      <data key="d2">a3fd8bf0a5229c36813682803cd15e35</data>
      <data key="d3">1714</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9489</data>
      <data key="d7">e13fde27ca1c48bf9c5c09eac8919850</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MIKARDIOPAT&#205;A HIPERTR&#211;FICA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, a condition affecting the heart muscle, prevalent in GSDIII patients.</data>
      <data key="d2">a3fd8bf0a5229c36813682803cd15e35</data>
      <data key="d3">1714</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9490</data>
      <data key="d7">27e49fa496d64dd7a12cf630d88a94d4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NUTRICI&#211;N ENTERAL NOCTURNA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">NUTRICI&#211;N ENTERAL NOCTURNA refers to nighttime enteral nutrition, a dietary approach primarily utilized to manage glucose levels in patients with Glycogen Storage Disease Type III (GSDIII). This method is particularly beneficial for individuals with this condition, as it helps maintain stable glucose levels during the night. However, it is important to note that nocturnal enteral nutrition is generally not necessary for most patients with McArdle disease, as they are able to effectively manage their glucose levels through their dietary intake without the need for additional nighttime nutritional support.</data>
      <data key="d2">4464b0da291e94ba7b7f5b51134b4a75,638e8fa7af82ec833d23c7df72ee9b1f,a3fd8bf0a5229c36813682803cd15e35</data>
      <data key="d3">663</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">9491</data>
      <data key="d7">0f0c39ca37204265a1545d2652c14f01</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIPOURICEMIA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">A condition of elevated uric acid levels in the blood, which can be infrequent in GSDIII patients.</data>
      <data key="d2">a3fd8bf0a5229c36813682803cd15e35</data>
      <data key="d3">1714</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9492</data>
      <data key="d7">a05b97112d2f4d2b87f6b53d44c998af</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AFECTACI&#211;N RENAL">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Renal involvement, a potential complication in patients with GSDIII, though it is also infrequent.</data>
      <data key="d2">a3fd8bf0a5229c36813682803cd15e35</data>
      <data key="d3">1714</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9493</data>
      <data key="d7">28bf542c84284b6494cc070651a79b9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GSD0">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Glycogen Storage Disease Type 0, a condition characterized by a deficiency in hepatic glycogen synthase, leading to hypoglycemia and other metabolic issues.</data>
      <data key="d2">9f5a8d86160d5d097bca174e2e2496c1</data>
      <data key="d3">1712</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">10</data>
      <data key="d6">9494</data>
      <data key="d7">cde4ee829e0e467d81cee62ea8a486a4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLYCOGEN SYNTHASE">
      <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
      <data key="d1">An enzyme critical for glycogen synthesis, with its deficiency leading to various glycogen storage diseases.</data>
      <data key="d2">9f5a8d86160d5d097bca174e2e2496c1</data>
      <data key="d3">1712</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9495</data>
      <data key="d7">7739dec7d2d543bb873220a1cca1cbf6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FRUCTOSE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Fructose is a simple sugar, specifically a monosaccharide, that is naturally found in many fruits. It plays a significant role in various metabolic pathways, including energy production. Fructose is permitted in the diets of patients with certain glycogen storage diseases, indicating its potential benefits in specific medical contexts. However, it is also important to note that excessive consumption of fructose can lead to dietary intolerances, highlighting the need for moderation in its intake. Overall, fructose is a versatile sugar with both beneficial and potentially adverse effects depending on the amount consumed.</data>
      <data key="d2">9f5a8d86160d5d097bca174e2e2496c1,f290b280f5c5a9e2e8326fec68b6016f,fb0fe813a0b63d3b7f4a1bccaf15d291</data>
      <data key="d3">849</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">12</data>
      <data key="d6">9496</data>
      <data key="d7">062e41a4a23e4b929d51116127ecda45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GALACTOSE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">Galactose is a monosaccharide sugar that plays a significant role in various metabolic processes. It is a component of lactose, the sugar found in milk, and is also part of galactolipids, which are important for cellular structures. In the context of dietary considerations, galactose is permitted in the diets of patients with certain glycogen storage diseases, indicating its relevance in specific health conditions. Overall, galactose is metabolized by the body and is involved in multiple metabolic pathways, highlighting its importance in human physiology.</data>
      <data key="d2">0765b6a482d8348e9746183291e9e9bc,9f5a8d86160d5d097bca174e2e2496c1,c4056d00ff7cbd7d7e3464150ea3d5aa,ce9cd0d993ab8b3995c1a78e9306bcad,f290b280f5c5a9e2e8326fec68b6016f</data>
      <data key="d3">837</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">5</data>
      <data key="d6">9497</data>
      <data key="d7">30e9a407d5b64f8499e0a8835084d79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOPSIA HEP&#193;TICA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">BIOPSIA HEP&#193;TICA, or liver biopsy, is a medical procedure that involves the extraction of a small sample of liver tissue for analysis. This diagnostic procedure is utilized to confirm various liver conditions, including Glycogen Storage Disease Type 0 (GSD0), by studying the liver tissue. Additionally, it plays a crucial role in assessing liver damage due to iron overload in hereditary hemochromatosis and can reveal copper deposits in the liver, which is important for confirming Wilson's disease. Overall, the biopsia hep&#225;tica is an essential tool in the diagnosis and management of various liver-related health issues.</data>
      <data key="d2">68c1e1d1d60cf84e8ba37450cd6dc7d3,9f5a8d86160d5d097bca174e2e2496c1,a3d834f1d854ecde12e808ae924b8eb0,ea816809115cc37c571bd36191042123</data>
      <data key="d3">1818</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">4</data>
      <data key="d6">9498</data>
      <data key="d7">cd329b57dab24e10878aba52f6758360</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ESTUDIO GEN&#201;TICO">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">A genetic study used to confirm the diagnosis of GSD0</data>
      <data key="d2">9f5a8d86160d5d097bca174e2e2496c1</data>
      <data key="d3">1712</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9499</data>
      <data key="d7">287c0043bd3446e0b299193ac594aaac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIPOALANINEMIA">
      <data key="d0" />
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">9f5a8d86160d5d097bca174e2e2496c1</data>
      <data key="d3">1712</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9500</data>
      <data key="d7">af92864836fc484fa53702a79586fe60</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENFERMEDAD DE MCARDLE">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Enfermedad de McArdle, also known as Glycogen Storage Disease Type V, is a metabolic myopathy caused by a mutation in the muscle isoform of glycogen phosphorylase, leading to exercise intolerance and muscle symptoms.</data>
      <data key="d2">4464b0da291e94ba7b7f5b51134b4a75</data>
      <data key="d3">1758</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">12</data>
      <data key="d6">9501</data>
      <data key="d7">e64af2bf5dce45d9965056dec35ad3d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PYGM">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">PYGM is the gene responsible for encoding the muscle isoform of glycogen phosphorylase, which is mutated in McArdle disease.</data>
      <data key="d2">4464b0da291e94ba7b7f5b51134b4a75</data>
      <data key="d3">1758</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9502</data>
      <data key="d7">562b4285f80148d096d39b68aa7f05eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CK">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">CK, or creatine kinase, is an enzyme that is typically elevated in individuals with muscle damage, including those with McArdle disease.</data>
      <data key="d2">4464b0da291e94ba7b7f5b51134b4a75</data>
      <data key="d3">1758</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9503</data>
      <data key="d7">eff605d7f4ae46df8ebee1dd9d21ea35</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOREARM NON-ISQUEMIC TEST">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">The forearm non-ischemic test is a diagnostic procedure used to measure lactate and ammonia levels in patients suspected of having McArdle disease.</data>
      <data key="d2">4464b0da291e94ba7b7f5b51134b4a75</data>
      <data key="d3">1758</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9504</data>
      <data key="d7">bfcfb0a87fa84958b5f4c3dd3e91fbd2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CICLOERG&#211;METRO">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">A cicloerg&#243;metro is a device used to measure heart rate and exercise capacity, which can help confirm the second wind phenomenon in McArdle disease patients.</data>
      <data key="d2">4464b0da291e94ba7b7f5b51134b4a75</data>
      <data key="d3">1758</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9505</data>
      <data key="d7">4b1f3eca11c940eb97632d68643965a7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOPSIA MUSCULAR">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Muscle biopsy is a diagnostic procedure that can show focal accumulations of normal glycogen and assess enzyme activity in patients with McArdle disease.</data>
      <data key="d2">4464b0da291e94ba7b7f5b51134b4a75</data>
      <data key="d3">1758</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9506</data>
      <data key="d7">f4c7ed12513a461fa212aeae7966dcf3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EJERCICIO AER&#211;BICO LIGERO-MODERADO">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Light to moderate aerobic exercise is recommended for patients with McArdle disease to improve physical fitness and increase muscle oxidative capacity.</data>
      <data key="d2">4464b0da291e94ba7b7f5b51134b4a75</data>
      <data key="d3">1758</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">9507</data>
      <data key="d7">877abe09a3204e9eadd1ee323b5a7c2f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GSDVI">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Glycogen Storage Disease Type VI (GSDVI) is a rare metabolic disorder characterized by a mutation in the liver isoform of glycogen phosphorylase. This condition is similar to Glycogen Storage Disease Type IX (GSDIX) and necessitates dietary management to address liver-related issues. Individuals with GSDVI often experience symptoms such as hepatomegaly and hypoglycemia, particularly in early childhood.</data>
      <data key="d2">160d4259ba08a1bfcdc877b63a4c799d,24cc40d6e3b225945cd4d8c2484632e4</data>
      <data key="d3">1732</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">11</data>
      <data key="d6">9508</data>
      <data key="d7">24d4995824ea4245b09419674fc49011</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PYGL">
      <data key="d0">GENE</data>
      <data key="d1">The PYGL gene is responsible for encoding the liver isoform of glycogen phosphorylase, which is crucial for glycogenolysis.</data>
      <data key="d2">24cc40d6e3b225945cd4d8c2484632e4</data>
      <data key="d3">1732</data>
