    </node>
    <node id="BANS">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">BANS, which stands for the British Artificial Nutrition Survey, is a registry that focuses on estimating the prevalence of nutritional disorders in the UK. It has specifically published data regarding the prevalence of nutritional deficiencies (NED) and nutritional support (NPD). In 2011, BANS provided significant insights into the prevalence of NPD, contributing to the understanding of artificial nutrition needs within the British population.</data>
      <data key="d2">eab1daf48c7ca09f43fc115a3379cc10,efc0b55f4e91f02e4708b7cc43c64608</data>
      <data key="d3">1930</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">3</data>
      <data key="d6">16296</data>
      <data key="d7">cda5ebb087924107a48834c441ac2b19</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NADYA-SENPE">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">NADYA-SENPE is a specialized working group in Spain dedicated to home enteral nutrition. This group is responsible for maintaining various national registries that pertain to nutrition care, including annual registries of enteral and parenteral nutrition, which have been conducted since the late 1990s. Additionally, NADYA-SENPE is actively involved in research concerning health-related quality of life, particularly focusing on patients who receive home nutritional support. The group has also been collecting data on patients undergoing nutritional support therapy since 1992, specifically for those receiving NPD (Nutritional Parenteral Diet). Through these efforts, NADYA-SENPE plays a crucial role in advancing the understanding and management of nutritional care in Spain.</data>
      <data key="d2">4b0d0238a19e23ffee592c9d0817e226,eab1daf48c7ca09f43fc115a3379cc10,efc0b55f4e91f02e4708b7cc43c64608,fff76d1cd0e64233a5727a707d80e2b9</data>
      <data key="d3">1926</data>
      <data key="d4">2</data>
      <data key="d5">29</data>
      <data key="d6">16297</data>
      <data key="d7">9aaffb55d89e4d8898d05a1268e16439</data>
    </node>
