    </edge>
    <edge source="OBESIDAD" target="ANTIBIOTIC THERAPY">
      <data key="d8">7.0</data>
      <data key="d9">Studies have linked antibiotic therapy to an increase in obesity rates</data>
      <data key="d10">15ac197b57d904b3bf72905c4d946c0d</data>
      <data key="d11">591bb7982f7244cd8003947f21ac250c</data>
      <data key="d12">5891</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="OBESIDAD" target="MICROBIOTA">
      <data key="d8">9.0</data>
      <data key="d9">Obesidad, or obesity, is influenced by the composition and function of the microbiota in the gut. Research indicates that alterations in microbiota, particularly those caused by antibiotic use, have been associated with the development of obesity. This suggests a significant relationship between the gut microbiota and obesity, highlighting the importance of microbial health in the management and understanding of obesity.</data>
      <data key="d10">05360f0d7332f748b436b98d64e4eeb7,15ac197b57d904b3bf72905c4d946c0d</data>
      <data key="d11">1c19f4f5b3704e1ab88786e660d37b60</data>
      <data key="d12">5892</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="OBESIDAD" target="MICROBIOTA INTESTINAL">
      <data key="d8">24.0</data>
      <data key="d9">The entity "OBESIDAD" is closely related to "MICROBIOTA INTESTINAL," as the intestinal microbiota plays a significant role in the development of obesity and metabolic disorders. Research indicates that the composition of the intestinal microbiota is linked to the regulation of energy balance, which is crucial in the onset of obesity. Furthermore, the intestinal microbiota influences obesity development through its effects on metabolism and inflammation, highlighting its importance in understanding and potentially addressing obesity-related issues.</data>
      <data key="d10">01eb4b96cd8e6a886ebc313171922a77,0a78cb1d502baf0116e4326e7fbcae41,e189588f51b4bb84b3621a1f846d07bc</data>
      <data key="d11">3e1fc44bc2864b51ae0f6479c0c7328a</data>
      <data key="d12">5893</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="OBESIDAD" target="H&#193;BITOS ALIMENTARIOS">
      <data key="d8">1.0</data>
      <data key="d9">Los cambios en los h&#225;bitos alimentarios, como el aumento de grasas, contribuyen al incremento de la obesidad</data>
      <data key="d10">01eb4b96cd8e6a886ebc313171922a77</data>
      <data key="d11">23f792d032ff473fb8444dc2d36ef35f</data>
      <data key="d12">5894</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="OBESIDAD" target="ENFERMEDADES METAB&#211;LICAS">
      <data key="d8">9.0</data>
      <data key="d9">La obesidad es una de las principales enfermedades metab&#243;licas que afecta a la poblaci&#243;n mundial</data>
      <data key="d10">01eb4b96cd8e6a886ebc313171922a77</data>
      <data key="d11">d31c7f35fa4e4f8db18fc1e8f1e6d07c</data>
      <data key="d12">5895</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="OBESIDAD" target="FACTORES AMBIENTALES">
      <data key="d8">1.0</data>
      <data key="d9">Los factores ambientales, como la dieta y la actividad f&#237;sica, son determinantes clave en el aumento de la obesidad</data>
      <data key="d10">01eb4b96cd8e6a886ebc313171922a77</data>
      <data key="d11">1df9174fa06a48bcb14807f561cb7686</data>
      <data key="d12">5896</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="OBESIDAD" target="CITOQUINAS">
      <data key="d8">6.0</data>
      <data key="d9">Cytokines are produced in response to inflammation and are involved in the inflammatory processes associated with obesity.</data>
      <data key="d10">0a78cb1d502baf0116e4326e7fbcae41</data>
      <data key="d11">d4f461546fc643d59a912854e4532889</data>
      <data key="d12">5897</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="OBESIDAD" target="DIETAS VEGETARIANAS">
      <data key="d8">5.0</data>
      <data key="d9">Well-planned vegetarian diets can be associated with a lower risk of obesity</data>
      <data key="d10">62afbb83c3f69bd45bf24467ac5b47b8</data>
      <data key="d11">c37db93dd54d4e4f9730594d9ede760f</data>
      <data key="d12">5898</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="OBESIDAD" target="HIPERTENSI&#211;N">
      <data key="d8">1.0</data>
      <data key="d9">Obesity significantly increases the risk of developing hypertension.</data>
      <data key="d10">92ae465a11b49807321be06ec9f5bc5a</data>
      <data key="d11">2bb8543a634246af8ac00f07076b6a48</data>
      <data key="d12">5899</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="OBESIDAD" target="HYPERTENSION">
      <data key="d8">8.0</data>
      <data key="d9">Obesity is associated with a higher prevalence of hypertension compared to individuals with normal weight, indicating a strong relationship</data>
      <data key="d10">3fab4e796fd2145b2d0f62ffa60775aa</data>
      <data key="d11">372a5418c7484e6aad09bec448fdaf3e</data>
      <data key="d12">5900</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="OBESIDAD" target="LITIASIS RENAL">
      <data key="d8">19.0</data>
      <data key="d9">Obesidad, or obesity, is identified as a significant risk factor for the development of litiasis renal, commonly known as kidney stones. The relationship between obesity and kidney stone formation is well-established, with evidence suggesting that obesity can increase the likelihood of developing this condition. This connection highlights the importance of addressing obesity as a contributing factor in the prevention and management of litiasis renal.</data>
      <data key="d10">1ad59ffc3e39719e331a017fe64cd07d,9ea70909c43b9b7923a0fa217c0e7290,b703f173982cf68d36653cc0d9cc1fcb</data>
      <data key="d11">8f6af74899124da78298205fecac9cd0</data>
      <data key="d12">5901</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="OBESIDAD" target="HIPERURICEMIA">
      <data key="d8">15.0</data>
      <data key="d9">Obesidad and hiperuricemia are closely related conditions. Hiperuricemia, characterized by elevated levels of uric acid in the blood, is often associated with obesity. Individuals suffering from obesity are more likely to experience increased uric acid levels due to various metabolic factors. Additionally, obesity can impair the renal elimination of uric acid, further contributing to the development of hiperuricemia. This relationship highlights the interconnectedness of these two health issues, emphasizing the importance of managing obesity to mitigate the risk of elevated uric acid levels.</data>
      <data key="d10">1ad59ffc3e39719e331a017fe64cd07d,ab93481c5db482b6da2462b4687e4313</data>
      <data key="d11">53143b792fc547d8901f439fa87d08c5</data>
      <data key="d12">5902</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="OBESIDAD" target="NEFROLITIASIS">
      <data key="d8">7.0</data>
      <data key="d9">Obesidad is a risk factor for developing kidney stones due to its association with metabolic disorders</data>
      <data key="d10">a29197a0d36ee2c688c9c959435e999f</data>
      <data key="d11">71b4160a85004847b486036c9978fa4c</data>
      <data key="d12">5903</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="OBESIDAD" target="INSUFICIENCIA RENAL">
      <data key="d8">6.0</data>
      <data key="d9">Obesidad is linked to a higher risk of renal insufficiency due to metabolic changes.</data>
      <data key="d10">1ad59ffc3e39719e331a017fe64cd07d</data>
      <data key="d11">e04dc6366ea741ffaa8c48404d51ac31</data>
      <data key="d12">5904</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
