      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CVC" target="HEPARINA">
      <data key="d8">13.0</data>
      <data key="d9">CVC, or central venous catheters, are medical devices used to administer medications, fluids, and nutrients directly into the bloodstream. Heparin is an anticoagulant that is commonly used in conjunction with CVCs to manage complications that may arise from their use. Specifically, heparin helps prevent clotting during parenteral nutrition, ensuring that the catheter remains patent and functional. Thus, heparin plays a crucial role in enhancing the safety and effectiveness of CVCs in clinical settings.</data>
      <data key="d10">c06e5bcd7933ed338cd1257d11c709cf,fe3ebcedac0857680bcc85bb58dcb3e1</data>
      <data key="d11">c80fa235967846c89f04c39a640debd0</data>
      <data key="d12">10131</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CVC" target="UROQUINASA">
      <data key="d8">1.0</data>
      <data key="d9">Urokinase is a fibrinolytic agent used for treating catheter occlusions</data>
      <data key="d10">fe3ebcedac0857680bcc85bb58dcb3e1</data>
      <data key="d11">c35c2dad2ae946778206bd52676de91e</data>
      <data key="d12">10132</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CVC" target="TAUROLIDINA">
      <data key="d8">14.0</data>
      <data key="d9">CVC and Taurolidine are related in the context of infection prevention. Taurolidine is recommended for use in conjunction with central venous catheters (CVCs) to prevent infections. It serves as a prophylactic measure specifically aimed at reducing the risk of infections in patients who have CVCs.</data>
      <data key="d10">c06e5bcd7933ed338cd1257d11c709cf,e5313f692c0a8259c93be803a704413c</data>
      <data key="d11">000b590167d44eeda6619ea38fd8a738</data>
      <data key="d12">10133</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CVC" target="ALCOHOL AL 70%">
      <data key="d8">1.0</data>
