    </edge>
    <edge source="OSTEOPOROSIS" target="POBLACIONES MADURAS">
      <data key="d8">1.0</data>
      <data key="d9">Older populations are at higher risk for osteoporosis, making dietary and exercise recommendations crucial</data>
      <data key="d10">dea64d5eb807430bc52e8bed6e30c393</data>
      <data key="d11">fab1dfe9e9624f79b77acc7ca3f3a0be</data>
      <data key="d12">11007</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="OSTEOPOROSIS" target="ANOREXIA NERVOSA">
      <data key="d8">15.0</data>
      <data key="d9">Osteoporosis is a condition that is characterized by weakened bones and an increased risk of fractures. Anorexia nervosa, an eating disorder marked by an intense fear of gaining weight and a distorted body image, is associated with an increased risk of developing osteoporosis. This heightened risk is primarily due to the low body weight and nutritional deficiencies commonly seen in individuals with anorexia nervosa. Additionally, patients with anorexia nervosa experience low bone mass and hormonal changes that further contribute to the development of osteoporosis. Therefore, the interplay between anorexia nervosa and osteoporosis highlights the significant impact of nutritional status and body weight on bone health.</data>
      <data key="d10">203ff7a2671a44d48a1a326f469c5543,bb6a2caec3fa27e00fc1044b17147ccb</data>
      <data key="d11">161b28d5852444a4a702d6c64cd156f5</data>
      <data key="d12">11008</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="OSTEOPOROSIS" target="GSDIII">
      <data key="d8">1.0</data>
      <data key="d9">Osteoporosis can also be a long-term complication in patients with GSDIII</data>
      <data key="d10">b5e899a3def3da339630eb0d2bfe4af8</data>
      <data key="d11">5e34f9fa69bd4e7f96f0be0dc26a5aec</data>
      <data key="d12">11009</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="OSTEOPOROSIS" target="POST-LIVER TRANSPLANTATION">
      <data key="d8">1.0</data>
