    </edge>
    <edge source="DIABETES 2" target="WOLFSDORF JI">
      <data key="d8">7.0</data>
      <data key="d9">Wolfsdorf JI has contributed to clinical practice consensus on managing diabetes, including Diabetes 2</data>
      <data key="d10">9889a9000d5a82ffba9e0f6c92da918a</data>
      <data key="d11">5f3db46b53b14043b4f73f75b85e2377</data>
      <data key="d12">13065</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GLUCAGON" target="INSULIN">
      <data key="d8">26.0</data>
      <data key="d9">Glucagon and insulin are hormones that play crucial roles in regulating blood glucose levels in the body. They work in opposition to each other, with insulin lowering blood sugar levels and glucagon raising them. Together, these hormones maintain metabolic balance by ensuring that blood glucose levels remain stable. While they have opposing functions, their coordinated actions are essential for effective glucose regulation, highlighting the importance of both hormones in maintaining overall metabolic health.</data>
      <data key="d10">50fafa000d5f2830cbf66f3201657396,7b084f7afc5f3c66a528475ae4fa76b1,bcded135629c889cb0a10ae968743582,d4fb31e0ff471ed415f98bc11ca26d1d</data>
      <data key="d11">b998d5f10ef84e02ac76fe319a66706c</data>
      <data key="d12">13066</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GLUCAGON" target="HYPOGLYCEMIA">
      <data key="d8">29.0</data>
      <data key="d9">Glucagon is a key hormone that plays a crucial role in counteracting hypoglycemia, a condition characterized by abnormally low blood glucose levels. It is primarily responsible for raising blood glucose levels when they drop too low, making it essential in the management of hypoglycemia. Glucagon is released in response to hypoglycemic episodes, effectively increasing blood glucose levels to restore balance. However, it is important to note that glucagon's release is suppressed during episodes of hypoglycemia, indicating a complex regulatory mechanism at play. Overall, glucagon is vital for maintaining glucose homeostasis and preventing the adverse effects of hypoglycemia.</data>
      <data key="d10">46466da830644a88f74108645c668fd5,5b487245068b8e8b87ffcc04970aaff4,704cfd179507a977ecc0691a5bd2861e,7b084f7afc5f3c66a528475ae4fa76b1</data>
      <data key="d11">a39052f4520f45fda87b99eb337d479e</data>
      <data key="d12">13067</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GLUCAGON" target="CAD">
      <data key="d8">7.0</data>
      <data key="d9">Excess glucagon contributes to the development of CAD by promoting gluconeogenesis</data>
      <data key="d10">bcded135629c889cb0a10ae968743582</data>
      <data key="d11">33ad0d4a4b2e49bcac7998a54cc80ef5</data>
      <data key="d12">13068</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GLUCAGON" target="HORMONES">
      <data key="d8">9.0</data>
      <data key="d9">Glucagon is a hormone that raises blood glucose levels, counteracting the effects of insulin</data>
      <data key="d10">bcded135629c889cb0a10ae968743582</data>
      <data key="d11">266f181a1e87414a8f18020b23810e8d</data>
      <data key="d12">13069</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TYPE 1 DIABETES GENETICS CONSORTIUM" target="HLA DR-DQ HAPLOTYPES">
      <data key="d8">8.0</data>
      <data key="d9">The Type 1 Diabetes Genetics Consortium studies the association of HLA DR-DQ haplotypes with type 1 diabetes risk</data>
      <data key="d10">d0231ac4e41b0e826d80763b5b8c61e0</data>
      <data key="d11">8b8e3729e0684ee3966ba706fc4a01dc</data>
      <data key="d12">13070</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
