    </edge>
    <edge source="ADRENOLEUCODISTROFIA" target="HEXA-COSANOIC ACID">
      <data key="d8">9.0</data>
      <data key="d9">Hexacosanoic acid accumulates in patients with adrenoleucodistrofia, contributing to the disease's symptoms</data>
      <data key="d10">34299d74df25d13e56cd62aab766e618</data>
      <data key="d11">6f00e355f0b04a148518a91d3a078313</data>
      <data key="d12">14539</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ADRENOLEUCODISTROFIA" target="TETRA-COSANOIC ACID">
      <data key="d8">7.0</data>
      <data key="d9">Tetracosanoic acid is also accumulated in patients with adrenoleucodistrofia, although in smaller amounts</data>
      <data key="d10">34299d74df25d13e56cd62aab766e618</data>
      <data key="d11">065652010d1d4c51bcf0e9dbecfa1cc9</data>
      <data key="d12">14540</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ADRENOLEUCODISTROFIA" target="AGCML">
      <data key="d8">14.0</data>
      <data key="d9">Adrenoleucodistrofia (ALD) is a genetic disorder characterized by the breakdown of myelin, the protective sheath surrounding nerve fibers, leading to neurological impairment. In patients with ALD, a biochemical marker known as AGCML is monitored. Elevated levels of AGCML are indicative of this metabolic disorder, serving as a crucial indicator for the diagnosis and management of the condition.</data>
      <data key="d10">0745ce31d3922cbba1497dbe1d2845de,34299d74df25d13e56cd62aab766e618</data>
      <data key="d11">92805f882fd74a4aa72c7bfbfdb4e6d2</data>
      <data key="d12">14541</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ADRENOLEUCODISTROFIA" target="ALDP">
      <data key="d8">6.0</data>
      <data key="d9">The study of ALDP is crucial for diagnosing adrenoleucodistrofia</data>
      <data key="d10">34299d74df25d13e56cd62aab766e618</data>
      <data key="d11">eef8ddf186ca4edbbd5f58963ef0e2c7</data>
      <data key="d12">14542</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ADRENOLEUCODISTROFIA" target="FIBROBLASTS">
      <data key="d8">5.0</data>
      <data key="d9">Fibroblasts can be cultured to study the effects of adrenoleucodistrofia on cellular metabolism</data>
      <data key="d10">34299d74df25d13e56cd62aab766e618</data>
      <data key="d11">268f5544c4f64bb4bd78341891454402</data>
      <data key="d12">14543</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ADRENOLEUCODISTROFIA" target="PLASMA">
      <data key="d8">1.0</data>
      <data key="d9">Plasma analysis is used to diagnose adrenoleucodistrofia by measuring fatty acid levels</data>
      <data key="d10">34299d74df25d13e56cd62aab766e618</data>
      <data key="d11">936072b93d3344d280c413b25728023d</data>
      <data key="d12">14544</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ADRENOLEUCODISTROFIA" target="MORFOLOGICALLY">
      <data key="d8">7.0</data>
      <data key="d9">Morphological changes in the brain are a key aspect of the pathology of adrenoleucodistrofia</data>
      <data key="d10">34299d74df25d13e56cd62aab766e618</data>
      <data key="d11">4be868b5131a421cae12ffdeeee6081b</data>
      <data key="d12">14545</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ADRENOLEUCODISTROFIA" target="AXONOPATHY">
