    <edge source="TURNER SYNDROME" target="ANDROGEN INSENSITIVITY SYNDROME">
      <data key="d8">3.0</data>
      <data key="d9">Androgen insensitivity syndrome can occur in individuals with XY chromosomes, similar to the chromosomal issues seen in Turner syndrome.</data>
      <data key="d10">766fec9f74738cf0af53aee77d58dc78</data>
      <data key="d11">8d514bbe34364950b3ed650bba342d4f</data>
      <data key="d12">14870</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ANDROGEN INSENSITIVITY SYNDROME" target="XY INDIVIDUALS">
      <data key="d8">7.0</data>
      <data key="d9">XY individuals can be affected by androgen insensitivity syndrome, leading to female physical traits despite having male chromosomes.</data>
      <data key="d10">766fec9f74738cf0af53aee77d58dc78</data>
      <data key="d11">9352ec2c51d04e9ca166d7dfdb5da169</data>
      <data key="d12">14871</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ANDROGEN INSENSITIVITY SYNDROME" target="CARIOTYPE 46,XY">
      <data key="d8">1.0</data>
      <data key="d9">Individuals with a karyotype of 46,XY may experience androgen insensitivity syndrome, affecting their sexual development.</data>
      <data key="d10">766fec9f74738cf0af53aee77d58dc78</data>
      <data key="d11">1078cc25ae834fab83f3639dc642119c</data>
      <data key="d12">14872</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ANDROGEN INSENSITIVITY SYNDROME" target="GONADAL DYSGENESIS">
      <data key="d8">7.0</data>
      <data key="d9">Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome can result from gonadal dysgenesis, leading to atypical sexual development</data>
      <data key="d10">bc241ef500af250c0f0e0253bf5ae0f2</data>
      <data key="d11">953a0939cc46419696ac790351ea273a</data>
      <data key="d12">14873</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ANDROGEN INSENSITIVITY SYNDROME" target="5&#913;-REDUCTASE DEFICIENCY">
      <data key="d8">6.0</data>
      <data key="d9">5&#945;-Reductase Deficiency and Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome both involve issues with androgen response and sexual development</data>
      <data key="d10">bc241ef500af250c0f0e0253bf5ae0f2</data>
      <data key="d11">bcd69cdde8654dfcb5aac3412ab58d32</data>
      <data key="d12">14874</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HORMONE ANTIM&#220;LLERIAN" target="CELLS OF SERTOLI">
      <data key="d8">8.0</data>
      <data key="d9">Hormone antim&#252;llerian is produced by Sertoli cells, which are essential for male reproductive development.</data>
      <data key="d10">766fec9f74738cf0af53aee77d58dc78</data>
      <data key="d11">7be08f382756470d8a6515bc16c4e18a</data>
      <data key="d12">14875</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TESTOSTERONE" target="CELLS OF LEYDIG">
      <data key="d8">9.0</data>
      <data key="d9">Cells of Leydig produce testosterone, which is crucial for male sexual differentiation.</data>
      <data key="d10">766fec9f74738cf0af53aee77d58dc78</data>
      <data key="d11">e0718a4e91ae4f0dbd57229ee735f5b2</data>
      <data key="d12">14876</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TESTOSTERONE" target="DIHYDROTESTOSTERONE">
      <data key="d8">16.0</data>
      <data key="d9">Testosterone and dihydrotestosterone are closely related hormones that play crucial roles in male development. Testosterone is a primary male sex hormone that is converted into dihydrotestosterone (DHT), which is a more potent androgen. Dihydrotestosterone is essential for the development of male external genitalia, highlighting its importance in male sexual differentiation and reproductive health. Together, these hormones are vital for various physiological processes in males.</data>
      <data key="d10">766fec9f74738cf0af53aee77d58dc78,d4fb31e0ff471ed415f98bc11ca26d1d</data>
      <data key="d11">d41cfb4977264f6d8ccfdbedd42080d8</data>
      <data key="d12">14877</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TESTOSTERONE" target="POLYCYSTIC OVARY SYNDROME">
      <data key="d8">5.0</data>
      <data key="d9">Testosterone is used as a treatment for male transsexuals, and its effects can be relevant in the context of Polycystic Ovary Syndrome in women.</data>
      <data key="d10">dda58e50ce4bb9fa40e3c61e40e1b9c7</data>
      <data key="d11">27474d0091904ddf80c5a033409893bb</data>
      <data key="d12">14878</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TESTOSTERONE" target="SLEEP APNEA SYNDROME">
      <data key="d8">6.0</data>
      <data key="d9">Sleep Apnea Syndrome can have implications for patients undergoing testosterone treatment, as it may affect their health outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d10">dda58e50ce4bb9fa40e3c61e40e1b9c7</data>
      <data key="d11">f408adeca14e41149b16a58ce1e217f2</data>
      <data key="d12">14879</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TESTOSTERONE" target="ERYTHROCYTOSIS">
      <data key="d8">9.0</data>
      <data key="d9">Erythrocytosis is a potential adverse effect of testosterone treatment, indicating a direct relationship between the two.</data>
      <data key="d10">dda58e50ce4bb9fa40e3c61e40e1b9c7</data>
      <data key="d11">45bf61fcc6f3480989b4a2a276659118</data>
      <data key="d12">14880</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TESTOSTERONE" target="ENDOMETRIAL ATROPHY">
      <data key="d8">1.0</data>
      <data key="d9">Endometrial atrophy is a consequence of testosterone treatment in transsexual men, indicating a direct relationship.</data>
      <data key="d10">dda58e50ce4bb9fa40e3c61e40e1b9c7</data>
      <data key="d11">9aef5b7b67de461290cf15f759551a1c</data>
      <data key="d12">14881</data>
      <data key="d13">2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TESTOSTERONE" target="HORMONAL STUDY">
      <data key="d8">9.0</data>
      <data key="d9">Testosterone levels were a key focus of the hormonal study conducted for the patient.</data>
